// ESE 2025 Prelims Paper II, Mechanical Engineering, Series A, Q76–100 (MADE EASY unofficial key, checked)
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
(function () {
var K = "MADE EASY unofficial key";
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2025-76", year: 2025, no: 76, subject: "mechatronics", topic: "Mechatronics and robots", keySource: K,
q: `Consider the following statements regarding mechatronics and robots :<ol><li>Mechatronics is the synergistic combination of precision mechanical engineering, electronic control and systems thinking in the design of products and manufacturing processes.</li><li>All robots are not mechatronic systems, but all mechatronic systems are robots.</li><li>All machines that do not have any kind of autonomy in their behaviour, because they simply automatically act according to the inputs they receive from humans, are strictly pure mechatronic systems.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> This is the standard definition of mechatronics: mechanical engineering, electronic control and systems thinking combined from the design stage.</li><li><b>2 (wrong):</b> It is reversed: all robots are mechatronic systems, but not all mechatronic systems are robots (a camera autofocus or an anti-lock brake is mechatronic but not a robot).</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> A machine that only acts on inputs given by humans, with no autonomy of its own, is a pure mechatronic system; autonomy is what marks a robot.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Examples of pure mechatronic systems: CNC machines, washing machines and automatic doors, which sense and actuate but follow fixed commands.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 3.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Robot = mechatronic system + some autonomy.</p>` },

{ id: "2025-77", year: 2025, no: 77, subject: "mechatronics", topic: "Fuel cell voltage at rated load", keySource: K,
q: `How much voltage can be produced by a typical cell at full rated load?`,
opts: ["0·2 V to 0·3 V", "0·4 V to 0·5 V", "0·6 V to 0·7 V", "0·9 V to 1 V"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 0·6 V to 0·7 V</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The question is read as being about a fuel cell. A hydrogen fuel cell has a theoretical voltage of about 1·23 V and an open-circuit voltage of about 1 V. At full rated load, activation, ohmic and concentration losses bring it down to about 0·6–0·7 V, so cells are stacked in series to give a useful voltage.</p><div class="formula">V_cell = E₀ − activation − ohmic − concentration losses</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Theoretical 1.23 V; at full load about <b>0.6–0.7 V</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0.2–0.3 V is far below a working cell's voltage.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 0.4–0.5 V is below typical full-load voltage.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0.9–1 V is the open-circuit value, not at full load.</li></ul>
<p>Cell efficiency is roughly proportional to voltage: V/1·48 on the higher heating value, so 0·65 V corresponds to about 44%.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> The question does not say which cell; our reading and MADE EASY's key both give (c).</p>` },

{ id: "2025-78", year: 2025, no: 78, subject: "mechatronics", topic: "Multi-robot systems: graphs and consensus", keySource: K,
q: `Consider the following statements regarding graph theory and consensus :<ol><li>Graphs are often exploited for modelling the communication between robots in multi-robot systems.</li><li>Consensus problem is a well-known and widely studied problem in the field of decentralized control of multi-robot systems.</li><li>Rendezvous is the problem of controlling the robots in such a way that based on locally available quantities, their positions converge to a common value.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> In a communication graph, each robot is a node and each communication link an edge.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> Consensus, in which all robots agree on a common value using only neighbour information, is a central problem of decentralised control.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> Rendezvous is the consensus problem applied to position: using only local information, the robots' positions converge to one point.</li></ul>
<div class="formula">Consensus protocol: ẋ<sub>i</sub> = Σ<sub>j∈N<sub>i</sub></sub> (x<sub>j</sub> − x<sub>i</sub>), or ẋ = −Lx with L the graph Laplacian</div>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Convergence is guaranteed when the communication graph is connected.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Formation control adds fixed offsets to the same consensus idea.</p>` },

{ id: "2025-79", year: 2025, no: 79, subject: "mechatronics", topic: "Microsoft Robotics Developer Studio", keySource: K,
q: `Consider the following statements regarding Microsoft Robotics Developer Studio (MRDS) :<ol><li>MRDS was a freely available .NET-based programming environment for building robotic applications.</li><li>MRDS can be used by professional developers.</li><li>MRDS can be used by non-professional developers.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> MRDS was a freely available, .NET-based programming environment for building robot applications.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> It was aimed at professional developers.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> It was also aimed at non-professional developers and hobbyists.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> MADE EASY quotes the source text: MRDS served professional as well as non-professional developers and hobbyists, with a lightweight REST-style service-oriented run-time and visual programming and simulation tools, but because of vendor lock-in it never gained wide acceptance in the robotics research community.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> ROS (Robot Operating System) is the open framework that became the research standard instead.</p>` },

{ id: "2025-80", year: 2025, no: 80, subject: "mechatronics", topic: "Software component models", keySource: K,
q: `For software component model, the link between component internal ports (dashed interface) and component external ports (solid interface) is realized via`,
opts: ["communication patterns", "SmartSoft software component model", "agnostic model", "hidden dotted model"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) communication patterns</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In robotics component models such as SmartSoft, a component's internal (dashed) interface is linked to its external (solid) interface through communication patterns. In general, a publish/subscribe and a request/response pattern would be sufficient to cover all needs.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> The SmartSoft model is the overall framework in which the patterns are used, not the link itself.</li><li><b>(c)</b> "Agnostic model" refers to platform-independent modelling, not to linking ports.</li><li><b>(d)</b> "Hidden dotted model" is not a recognised term.</li></ul>
<p>Patterns separate the component's internal logic from the communication middleware, so that a component can be reused on a different middleware or platform.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> This is a narrow, source-based item; the explanation follows MADE EASY's note, which matched our answer.</p>` },

{ id: "2025-81", year: 2025, no: 81, subject: "mechatronics", topic: "Actuators in mechatronics", keySource: K, selfCorrected: true,
q: `Consider the following statements regarding actuators :<ol><li>The use of multiple extra actuators requires the development of new control strategies to cope with the diverse interactions that may arise compared with standard systems with a single actuator.</li><li>Two-degree-of-freedom actuators can also be designed to combine two motions, like the roto-translational direct drive motor.</li><li>Their response time is tens to hundreds of milliseconds, but they can be profitably used in many mechatronic applications.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> Several redundant actuators interact, so new control strategies are needed.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> Two-degree-of-freedom actuators combine two motions, such as a roto-translational direct-drive motor.</li><li><b>3 (correct, in its source context):</b> The source passage is about inexpensive actuators such as shape memory alloy (SMA) wires, which contract and relax according to their temperature. Their response time is of the order of tens to hundreds of milliseconds, yet they are useful in many applications.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Our first answer was (a), because "their" in statement 3 has no clear referent and tens of milliseconds seemed slow for actuators in general. MADE EASY quotes the source passage, which supports the statement, so we accepted (d).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> SMA wires: very high force per weight, silent, but slow and limited by cooling.</p>` },

{ id: "2025-82", year: 2025, no: 82, subject: "mechatronics", topic: "Intrinsic and extrinsic tactile sensing", keySource: K,
q: `Consider the following statements regarding intrinsic tactile sensing :<ol><li>It consists of one strain gauge detecting vertical force and derives both vertical force and position of centre of pressure.</li><li>Intrinsic sensing is based on a force/torque sensor placed within the mechanical structure of the sensing system.</li><li>Extrinsic sensing is based on sensors, which are often arranged in arrays that are mounted at or near the contact interface.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 2 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (wrong):</b> A single strain gauge measuring one vertical force cannot also give the position of the centre of pressure. Finding the contact location needs several force and moment components (the position follows from moment/force).</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> Intrinsic tactile sensing uses a force/torque sensor inside the mechanical structure, away from the contact.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> Extrinsic tactile sensing uses sensors, often in arrays, mounted at or near the contact interface.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Intrinsic sensing is simple and robust, but gives only overall contact data. Extrinsic arrays (tactile skins) give the detailed pressure distribution, with more wiring and fragility.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 2, 3.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Intrinsic: inside the structure. Extrinsic: on the surface.</p>` },

{ id: "2025-83", year: 2025, no: 83, subject: "mechatronics", topic: "Electroencephalography", keySource: K,
q: `Consider the following statements regarding simple electroencephalography (EEG) :<ol><li>A subjective index is obtained by a questionnaire, whereas an objective index is determined by a biosignal.</li><li>EEG is one of the biosignals used as indexes for determining preference.</li><li>Visualization of the mind status cannot be done by using EEG.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> Questionnaires give a subjective index; measured biosignals (EEG, heart rate, skin conductance) give an objective index.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> EEG, the record of electrical activity of the brain from scalp electrodes, is used as an index of preference.</li><li><b>3 (wrong):</b> EEG <i>is</i> used to visualise the state of mind, for example attention, relaxation or stress, from the power in its frequency bands.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> EEG bands: delta (below 4 Hz), theta (4–8 Hz), alpha (8–13 Hz, relaxed), beta (13–30 Hz, alert). Simple headsets with few electrodes have made these measurements practical in product evaluation.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Brain–computer interfaces use the same signals to control devices.</p>` },

{ id: "2025-84", year: 2025, no: 84, subject: "mechatronics", topic: "Next-generation motion control", keySource: K,
q: `Consider the following statements regarding next-generation motion control :<ol><li>Next-generation motion systems are inherently multi-variable since the flexible dynamical behaviour is generally not aligned with the motion DOFs.</li><li>Next-generation motion systems are envisaged to be designed with many actuators and sensors to actively control flexible dynamical behaviour, whereas traditionally, the number of inputs and outputs equals the number of motions DOFs.</li><li>A model-based design provides a systematic control design procedure for multi-variable systems.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> Lightweight, high-precision stages have flexible modes that do not line up with the motion axes, so each input affects several outputs: the system is multi-variable.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> Traditionally, inputs and outputs equal the motion degrees of freedom. New designs use extra actuators and sensors to actively control the flexible behaviour (over-actuation and over-sensing).</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> Model-based design (for example H∞ methods using a plant model) gives a systematic procedure for such multi-variable systems, where tuning loops one at a time no longer works.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> A typical application is the wafer stage of a lithography machine, which needs nanometre accuracy at high acceleration.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Rigid-body motion can be decoupled by simple transformations; flexible modes usually cannot.</p>` },

{ id: "2025-85", year: 2025, no: 85, subject: "mechatronics", topic: "Impedance and admittance", keySource: K,
q: `Consider the following statements regarding next-generation impedance control :<ol><li>A mechanical impedance at an interaction port can be defined as a dynamic operator that determines an output force in response to an input velocity at the same port.</li><li>A mechanical admittance is a dynamic operator that determines an output velocity in response to an input force.</li><li>For linear systems, admittance is proportional to impedance, and both can be represented as transfer functions in Laplace domain.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> Impedance Z: velocity in → force out: F(s) = Z(s)V(s).</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> Admittance Y: force in → velocity out: V(s) = Y(s)F(s).</li><li><b>3 (wrong):</b> For linear systems, admittance is the <i>inverse</i> of impedance, Y(s) = 1/Z(s), not proportional to it. Both are indeed transfer functions in the Laplace domain.</li></ul>
<div class="formula">Mass–spring–damper: Z(s) = ms + b + k/s</div>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Impedance control makes a robot behave like a chosen mass–spring–damper at its contact point, which is safer for interaction with people and stiff surfaces.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Compare electrical circuits: Z = V/I, Y = 1/Z, with force as voltage and velocity as current.</p>` },

{ id: "2025-86", year: 2025, no: 86, subject: "mechatronics", topic: "Component-based software engineering", keySource: K,
q: `Consider the following statements regarding component-based software engineering (CBSE) :<ol><li>It shifts the emphasis in system building from traditional requirements analysis, system design, and implementation to composing software systems from a mixture of reusable off-the-shelf and custom-built components.</li><li>CBSE is based on the explication of all relevant information about a component to make it usable by other software elements without the need to get in contact with the component provider.</li><li>Software components explicitly cannot consider reusable pieces of software.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> CBSE builds systems by composing reusable off-the-shelf and custom-built components.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> A component's interfaces and requirements are made explicit, so others can use it without contacting its provider.</li><li><b>3 (wrong):</b> Components are explicitly considered <i>reusable</i> pieces of software; reuse is the whole purpose of the approach.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Benefits: lower development effort, tested parts, and easier maintenance through clear interfaces. Robotics frameworks such as ROS and SmartSoft use this approach.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> A component is a unit of independent deployment with contractually specified interfaces.</p>` },

{ id: "2025-87", year: 2025, no: 87, subject: "mechatronics", topic: "Service-oriented architecture", keySource: K,
q: `Consider the following statements regarding service-oriented architectures (SOAs) :<ol><li>SOAs are the policies, practices, and frameworks that enable application functionality to be provided and consumed as sets of services published at a granularity relevant to the service consumers.</li><li>Services are the key entities performing communication between providers and consumers.</li><li>SOA is not related to policy, practice and frameworks.</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 only", "3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> This is the standard definition of an SOA.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> Services are the key entities through which providers and consumers communicate.</li><li><b>3 (wrong):</b> It directly contradicts statement 1: an SOA <i>is</i> a set of policies, practices and frameworks.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Services are loosely coupled and are found and used through published interfaces, regardless of the language or platform behind them. Web services and REST APIs are common examples.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, 2, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> When two statements contradict each other, at most one can be correct; here that eliminates (d).</p>` },

{ id: "2025-88", year: 2025, no: 88, subject: "mechatronics", topic: "Simultaneous localization and mapping", keySource: K,
q: `Consider the following statements regarding simultaneous localization and mapping (SLAM) :<ol><li>SLAM forms the backbone of mobile robotics, as it is a prerequisite for higher-level tasks such as path planning and navigation.</li><li>SLAM has received a lot of attention in the robotics community, and many algorithms that address different aspects of the problem have been proposed over the years.</li><li>The architecture of a SLAM algorithm can be divided into two main parts—the front-end and the back-end.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> A robot must know the map and its own position in it before it can plan paths and navigate.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> SLAM is heavily researched: EKF-SLAM, particle-filter methods (FastSLAM) and graph-based SLAM are examples.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> The front-end processes sensor data (feature extraction, data association, loop-closure detection). The back-end estimates the best trajectory and map (usually by graph optimisation).</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> SLAM is a chicken-and-egg problem: a map is needed to localise, and the position is needed to build the map, so both are estimated together.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Loop closure, recognising a place seen before, is what corrects accumulated drift.</p>` },

{ id: "2025-89", year: 2025, no: 89, subject: "mfg", topic: "Properties of moulding sand", keySource: K,
q: `Match the following lists :<br><b>List-I</b> P. Refractoriness; Q. Permeability; R. Cohesiveness; S. Adhesiveness<br><b>List-II</b> 1. Ability of sand to stick to other bodies; 2. Ability of sand grains to stick together; 3. Ability to allow gases, water vapour and air to pass through it; 4. It should be able to withstand high temperatures`,
opts: ["P-4, Q-3, R-2, S-1", "P-1, Q-2, R-3, S-4", "P-3, Q-1, R-4, S-2", "P-4, Q-1, R-2, S-3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) P-4, Q-3, R-2, S-1</h3><ul><li><b>P Refractoriness → 4:</b> withstands the molten metal's temperature without fusing.</li><li><b>Q Permeability → 3:</b> lets gases, steam and air escape; low permeability causes blow holes.</li><li><b>R Cohesiveness → 2:</b> grains stick together, giving the mould its strength.</li><li><b>S Adhesiveness → 1:</b> sand sticks to other bodies, such as the moulding box.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> mismatches all or most of the pairs.</li><li><b>(c)</b> mismatches all or most of the pairs.</li><li><b>(d)</b> swaps permeability and adhesiveness.</li><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Every pair matches.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Other properties: plasticity (takes the pattern's shape), collapsibility (allows the casting to contract and the sand to be removed) and green strength.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Cohesion: sand to sand. Adhesion: sand to something else.</p>` },

{ id: "2025-90", year: 2025, no: 90, subject: "mfg", topic: "Hot and cold working", keySource: K,
q: `Consider the following statements regarding hot and cold working :<ol><li>Cold working may be defined as plastic deformation of metals and alloys at a temperature above the recrystallization temperature for that metal or alloy.</li><li>Hot working may be explained as plastic deformation of metals and alloys at such a temperature at which recovery and recrystallization take place simultaneously with the strain hardening.</li><li>Recrystallization temperature is not a fixed temperature but is actually a temperature range.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (wrong):</b> Cold working is plastic deformation <i>below</i> the recrystallisation temperature; deforming above it is hot working.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> In hot working, recovery and recrystallisation occur along with strain hardening, so the metal does not harden overall.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> Recrystallisation occurs over a range of temperatures, depending on the amount of prior cold work, the time and the composition.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Recrystallisation temperature is roughly 0·3–0·5 T<sub>m</sub> (absolute). So lead, which recrystallises near room temperature, is effectively hot-worked at room temperature.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 2, 3.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Cold working: strength and finish up, ductility down. Hot working: low forces, but oxidation and poorer tolerances.</p>` },

{ id: "2025-91", year: 2025, no: 91, subject: "mfg", topic: "Red hardness", keySource: K,
q: `What is the property developed by the cutting tools due to addition of tungsten and molybdenum to high-carbon steel?`,
opts: ["Hardness", "White hardness", "Red hardness", "None of the above"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Red hardness</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Red hardness (hot hardness) is the ability to keep hardness at the high temperatures of cutting, when the tool may glow dull red. Tungsten and molybdenum form stable, hard carbides that resist softening, so high-speed steels keep their cutting ability up to about 600 °C. Plain high-carbon steel softens above about 250 °C.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Plain hardness at room temperature is already available in quenched high-carbon steel; it is not what these additions specifically give.</li><li><b>(b)</b> "White hardness" is not a recognised property.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Option (c) is correct.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Hot-hardness ladder: carbon steel &lt; HSS &lt; carbide &lt; ceramic &lt; CBN and diamond.</p>` },

{ id: "2025-92", year: 2025, no: 92, subject: "mfg", topic: "Types of fits", keySource: K,
q: `Match the following lists :<br><b>List-I</b> P. Clearance fit; Q. Interference fit; R. Transition fit<br><b>List-II</b> 1. The largest permissible diameter of the shaft is smaller than the diameter of the smallest hole; 2. The diameter of the largest permissible hole is greater than the diameter of the smallest shaft, and the diameter of the smallest hole is smaller than the diameter of the largest shaft; 3. The minimum permissible diameter of the shaft exceeds the maximum allowable diameter of the hole`,
opts: ["P-2, Q-3, R-1", "P-1, Q-3, R-2", "P-3, Q-1, R-2", "P-3, Q-2, R-1"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) P-1, Q-3, R-2</h3><ul><li><b>P Clearance fit → 1:</b> the largest shaft is still smaller than the smallest hole, so there is always a gap.</li><li><b>Q Interference fit → 3:</b> the smallest shaft is still larger than the largest hole, so force or shrinking is always needed.</li><li><b>R Transition fit → 2:</b> the tolerance zones overlap, so a given pair may have slight clearance or slight interference.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> each swap the definitions; only (b) puts all three correctly.</li><li><b>(c)</b> each swap the definitions; only (b) puts all three correctly.</li><li><b>(d)</b> each swap the definitions; only (b) puts all three correctly.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Every pair matches.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Examples in the hole-basis system: H7/g6 (clearance), H7/k6 (transition), H7/p6 (interference).</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Compare extremes: max shaft vs min hole, and min shaft vs max hole.</p>` },

{ id: "2025-93", year: 2025, no: 93, subject: "mfg", topic: "Sine bar", keySource: K,
q: `The formula for sine of angle (θ) formed between the upper surface of a sine bar and the surface plate (datum) is<br>where L = the distance between the centres of the rollers and h = the height difference between the two rollers.`,
opts: ["L/h", "h/L", "2h/L", "2L/h"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) h/L</h3><div class="formula">sin θ = h/L</div>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The two rollers and the slip-gauge stack form a right-angled triangle: the sine bar's centre distance L is the hypotenuse, and the difference in roller heights h is the side opposite θ.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> L/h is greater than 1, impossible for a sine.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 2h/L adds a factor that has no geometric basis.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 2L/h adds a factor that has no geometric basis.</li></ul>
<p>Example: L = 200 mm and θ = 30° need h = 100 mm of slip gauges. Sine bars become inaccurate above about 45°, because a small error in h then causes a larger error in θ.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Common sizes: L = 100, 200 or 250 mm.</p>` },

{ id: "2025-94", year: 2025, no: 94, subject: "mfg", topic: "FMS layouts and material handling", keySource: K,
q: `Match the following lists :<br><b>List-I (Layout configuration)</b> P. In-line layout; Q. Loop layout; R. Robot-centred layout<br><b>List-II (Typical material handling system)</b> 1. In-floor towline carts; 2. Rail-guided vehicle system; 3. Industrial robot`,
opts: ["P-2, Q-1, R-3", "P-1, Q-3, R-2", "P-3, Q-1, R-2", "P-1, Q-2, R-3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) P-2, Q-1, R-3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> From Groover's table of FMS layouts and material handling systems:</p>
<ul><li><b>P In-line → 2:</b> a rail-guided vehicle moves back and forth along a straight line of machines.</li><li><b>Q Loop → 1:</b> in-floor towline carts travel around a closed loop of workstations.</li><li><b>R Robot-centred → 3:</b> an industrial robot at the centre serves the machines placed around it.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> places the robot elsewhere, though a robot-centred cell plainly needs a robot.</li><li><b>(c)</b> places the robot elsewhere, though a robot-centred cell plainly needs a robot.</li><li><b>(d)</b> swaps the towline carts and the rail vehicle.</li><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Every pair matches.</li></ul>
<p>Groover's other layout is the ladder (or open-field) layout, which uses AGVs.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Layout follows from what the handler can do: rail = line, towline = loop, robot arm = circle.</p>` },

{ id: "2025-95", year: 2025, no: 95, subject: "mfg", topic: "Material requirements planning", keySource: K,
q: `Consider the following statements regarding material requirements planning (MRP) :<ol><li>MRP is a computational technique that converts the master schedule for end products into a detailed schedule for the raw materials and components used in the end products.</li><li>MRP is often thought of as a method of inventory control.</li><li>The distinction between independent demand and dependent demand is important in MRP.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Statement by statement (Groover)</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> MRP explodes the master production schedule, through the bill of materials, into dated requirements for each part and material.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> It is often considered an inventory-control method, since it orders only what is needed, when it is needed.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> End products have independent demand (from customers, forecast). Components have dependent demand, calculated from the end products. MRP applies to dependent-demand items.</li></ul>
<p>Inputs: master schedule, bill of materials and inventory records. Outputs: planned order releases and reschedule notices.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Example: one car needs 4 wheels. Cars are forecast; wheels are calculated.</p>` },

{ id: "2025-96", year: 2025, no: 96, subject: "mfg", topic: "Dummy activity", keySource: K,
q: `Which activity simply shows the logical relationship and does <b>not</b> consume any resource?`,
opts: ["Dummy", "Tail", "Head", "Cross"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Dummy</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A dummy activity, drawn as a dashed arrow in an activity-on-arrow network, shows a dependency between activities. It uses no time and no resources. Dummies are used to show logical relationships and to keep two activities from sharing the same start and end events.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> The tail is the start event of an activity, not an activity.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The head is the end event of an activity, not an activity.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 'Cross' is not a network activity type.</li></ul>
<p>Activity-on-node networks generally do not need dummies, because dependencies are shown directly by the links between nodes.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Dummy duration = 0, but it can still lie on the critical path.</p>` },

{ id: "2025-97", year: 2025, no: 97, subject: "mfg", topic: "Selective inventory control", keySource: K,
q: `Match the following lists :<br><b>List-I (Selective control technique)</b> P. ABC; Q. VED; R. XYZ<br><b>List-II (Basis of classification)</b> 1. Criticality of item; 2. Value of items in storage; 3. Annual consumption value`,
opts: ["P-2, Q-1, R-3", "P-1, Q-3, R-2", "P-3, Q-1, R-2", "P-3, Q-2, R-1"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) P-3, Q-1, R-2</h3><ul><li><b>P ABC → 3:</b> annual consumption value (price × annual usage). A items are about 10% of items and 70% of value.</li><li><b>Q VED → 1:</b> criticality: vital, essential or desirable (ESE 2022 Q52).</li><li><b>R XYZ → 2:</b> value of the stock held in storage, used to review and reduce inventory investment.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> swaps the bases of ABC and XYZ.</li><li><b>(b)</b> gives VED a basis other than criticality.</li><li><b>(d)</b> gives VED a basis other than criticality.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Every pair matches.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Other techniques: HML (unit price), FSN (rate of usage), SDE (difficulty of purchase), GOLF (source of supply).</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> ABC is based on consumption; XYZ on stock actually held.</p>` },

{ id: "2025-98", year: 2025, no: 98, subject: "mfg", topic: "Reliability analysis", keySource: K,
q: `Consider the following statements regarding reliability theory :<ol><li>Reliability analysis can be divided into two broad categories—(i) qualitative and (ii) quantitative.</li><li>Reliability engineering deals with the design and construction of systems and products, taking into account the reliability of their parts and components.</li><li>Reliability management deals with the various management issues in the context of managing the design, manufacture, and/or operation of reliable products and systems.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Reliability analysis.</p><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> Qualitative analysis looks at how failures happen (FMEA, fault trees without numbers). Quantitative analysis calculates probabilities and life (Weibull fits, reliability block diagrams).</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> Reliability engineering designs and builds systems, taking account of the reliability of their parts.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> Reliability management handles the managerial issues: the design, manufacture and operation of reliable products, along with costs and warranties.</li></ul>
<div class="formula">R(t) = e<sup>−λt</sup> (constant failure rate); MTBF = 1/λ; series system R = ΠR<sub>i</sub></div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Parallel redundancy: R = 1 − Π(1 − R<sub>i</sub>).</p>` },

{ id: "2025-99", year: 2025, no: 99, subject: "mfg", topic: "Bathtub curve", keySource: K,
q: `The failure rate function can have many different shapes. What type of shape it is where, in region A (decreasing failure rate), the failure is due to manufacturing and/or assembly errors (often referred to as teething problems), in region B (constant failure rate), the failure is purely due to chance (and is not affected by age), and in the final region C (increasing failure rate), failure is due to the aging effect?`,
opts: ["FMEA", "FTA", "Bathtub", "RBD"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Bathtub</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The failure rate curve falls in early life (infant mortality), stays flat in useful life and rises in the wear-out period, so it looks like the cross-section of a bathtub.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> FMEA (failure mode and effects analysis) is a systematic listing of how parts can fail and with what effects.</li><li><b>(b)</b> FTA (fault tree analysis) traces a top event down to its causes through logic gates.</li><li><b>(d)</b> RBD (reliability block diagram) shows how component reliabilities combine in series and parallel.</li></ul>
<p>In Weibull terms, the three regions correspond to shape parameter β &lt; 1, β = 1 and β &gt; 1. Burn-in testing removes early failures; preventive replacement avoids wear-out failures.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> The exponential law R = e<sup>−λt</sup> applies only to the flat middle region.</p>` },

{ id: "2025-100", year: 2025, no: 100, subject: "mechatronics", topic: "Vibration signal analysis", keySource: K,
q: `In which domains, the signals generated by vibration may be analyzed?`,
opts: ["Distance or time", "Time or frequency", "Speed or time", "Time or velocity"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Time or frequency</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Vibration signals are studied in the time domain (the waveform, with RMS, peak and crest-factor values) and in the frequency domain (the spectrum, found by the fast Fourier transform). The spectrum shows which frequencies are present, so a fault can be traced to its source: imbalance at 1× running speed, misalignment at 2×, gear-mesh and bearing-defect frequencies.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Distance velocitys are not analysis domains. Displacement and velocity are quantities that may be measured; time and frequency are the axes on which it is analysed.</li><li><b>(c)</b> speed velocitys are not analysis domains. Displacement and velocity are quantities that may be measured; time and frequency are the axes on which it is analysed.</li><li><b>(d)</b> velocitys are not analysis domains. Displacement and velocity are quantities that may be measured; time and frequency are the axes on which it is analysed.</li></ul>
<p>Time–frequency methods (wavelets, short-time FFT) are used for machines whose speed changes.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Time domain: is it bad? Frequency domain: what is causing it?</p>` }
);
})();

