// ESE 2025 Prelims Paper II, Mechanical Engineering, Series A, Q126–150 (MADE EASY unofficial key, checked)
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
(function () {
var K = "MADE EASY unofficial key";
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2025-126", year: 2025, no: 126, subject: "materials", topic: "Atoms per FCC unit cell", keySource: K,
q: `For the FCC crystal structure, total how many whole atoms may be assigned to a given unit cell, if there are eight corner atoms (N<sub>c</sub> = 8), six face atoms (N<sub>f</sub> = 6), and no interior atoms (N<sub>i</sub> = 0)?`,
opts: ["5", "4", "6", "3"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 4</h3><div class="formula">N = N<sub>i</sub> + N<sub>f</sub>/2 + N<sub>c</sub>/8</div>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Each corner atom is shared by 8 cells and each face atom by 2, so N = 0 + 6/2 + 8/8 = 3 + 1 = <b>4</b> (Callister).</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>N = 0 + 6/2 + 8/8.</li><li>= <b>4</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 5 adds a body-centre atom that FCC does not have.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 6 counts the face atoms whole.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 3 forgets the corner contribution.</li></ul>
<p>Compare: simple cubic 1, BCC 2, FCC 4, HCP 6 atoms per unit cell. FCC's coordination number is 12 and its packing factor 0·74.</p>
<p>Atomic packing factor check: with a = 2√2 R, APF = 4 × (4/3)πR³/(2√2 R)³ = 0·74, the densest possible packing of equal spheres. FCC metals have 12 slip systems and so are generally very ductile.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Examples of FCC metals: Cu, Al, Ni, Au, Ag, γ-Fe.</p>` },

{ id: "2025-127", year: 2025, no: 127, subject: "materials", topic: "Interaxial angles of crystal systems", keySource: K,
q: `Match the following lists :<br><b>List-I (Crystal systems)</b> P. Hexagonal; Q. Cubic; R. Monoclinic<br><b>List-II (Interaxial angles)</b> 1. α = γ = 90° ≠ β; 2. α = β = 90°, γ = 120°; 3. α = β = γ = 90°`,
opts: ["P-2, Q-3, R-1", "P-1, Q-3, R-2", "P-3, Q-1, R-2", "P-3, Q-2, R-1"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) P-2, Q-3, R-1</h3><ul><li><b>P Hexagonal → 2:</b> a = b ≠ c, α = β = 90°, γ = 120°.</li><li><b>Q Cubic → 3:</b> a = b = c, all angles 90°.</li><li><b>R Monoclinic → 1:</b> a ≠ b ≠ c, α = γ = 90° ≠ β.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> each misplace at least the hexagonal 120° angle, which belongs only to the hexagonal system.</li><li><b>(c)</b> each misplace at least the hexagonal 120° angle, which belongs only to the hexagonal system.</li><li><b>(d)</b> each misplace at least the hexagonal 120° angle, which belongs only to the hexagonal system.</li><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Every pair matches.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> (ESE 2024 Q38 asked the rhombohedral system: a = b = c, α = β = γ ≠ 90°.)</p>
<p>Seven crystal systems in order of decreasing symmetry: cubic, hexagonal, tetragonal, rhombohedral (trigonal), orthorhombic, monoclinic, triclinic. Together with the centring of points (P, I, F, C), they give the 14 Bravais lattices.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Triclinic is the least symmetric: all edges and all angles different.</p>` },

{ id: "2025-128", year: 2025, no: 128, subject: "power", topic: "Electrostatic precipitator corona", keySource: K,
q: `Which one of the following is an indication of negatively charged ionized gas molecules that travel from discharge electrodes to grounded collection electrodes?`,
opts: ["Corona", "Condenser", "Ejector", "Precipitator"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Corona</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In an electrostatic precipitator, a high negative voltage (about 30–100 kV) on thin discharge wires ionises the gas around them. This glowing ionised region, the corona, produces negative gas ions that travel towards the grounded collecting plates, charging the dust particles on the way; the particles then collect on the plates.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> A condenser condenses steam.</li><li><b>(c)</b> An ejector uses a jet to entrain and compress a fluid.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The precipitator is the whole device, not the ionisation effect.</li></ul>
<p>Precipitators remove over 99% of fly ash from boiler flue gas.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Collected dust is removed by rapping the plates into hoppers.</p>` },

{ id: "2025-129", year: 2025, no: 129, subject: "materials", topic: "Terminal solid solutions", keySource: K,
q: `The eutectic copper-silver and lead-tin phase diagrams have only two solid phases α and β; these are sometimes termed as`,
opts: ["intermediate solid solutions", "terminal solid solutions", "intermetallic compound solutions", "co-terminal solid solutions"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) terminal solid solutions</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In these eutectic systems, α is a solid solution rich in one metal and β rich in the other. Because each exists near one end (terminal) of the composition range, they are called terminal solid solutions (Callister).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Intermediate solid solutions occur at compositions between the two ends, as in Cu–Zn (β, γ phases).</li><li><b>(c)</b> Intermetallic compounds have a definite formula, such as Mg<sub>2</sub>Pb.</li><li><b>(d)</b> "Co-terminal" is not a term.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Pb–Sn: α (Pb-rich) and β (Sn-rich), with a eutectic at 183 °C.</p>` },

{ id: "2025-130", year: 2025, no: 130, subject: "materials", topic: "Tempering martensite", keySource: K,
q: `What properties of martensite may be enhanced and the internal stresses relieved by a heat treatment known as tempering?`,
opts: ["Ductility and toughness", "Brittleness and strength", "Ductility and malleability", "Ductility and hardness"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Ductility and toughness</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> As-quenched martensite is very hard but brittle, with high internal stresses. Tempering (reheating to about 250–650 °C) lets carbon come out as fine carbides, relieving stresses and greatly raising ductility and toughness, at some cost in hardness (Callister).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Tempering <i>reduces</i> brittleness, and strength falls slightly.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Malleability is not the property described for tempering.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Hardness <i>decreases</i> on tempering.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Higher tempering temperature: softer and tougher.</p>` },

{ id: "2025-131", year: 2025, no: 131, subject: "materials", topic: "Ferrous alloys", keySource: K,
q: `Consider the following statements regarding ferrous alloys :<ol><li>Ferrous alloys are those in which iron is the prime constituent.</li><li>Stainless steel is a low-alloy ferrous alloy.</li><li>Ferrous alloys are extremely versatile, in that they may be tailored to have a wide range of mechanical and physical properties.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> In ferrous alloys, iron is the main constituent (steels and cast irons).</li><li><b>2 (wrong):</b> Stainless steel is a <i>high</i>-alloy steel: at least 11% chromium, often with nickel.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> Ferrous alloys can be tailored to a very wide range of properties by composition and heat treatment (Callister).</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Drawbacks of ferrous alloys: high density, low electrical conductivity and poor corrosion resistance in most environments.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 3.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Low-alloy steels have under about 5% total alloying elements.</p>` },

{ id: "2025-132", year: 2025, no: 132, subject: "materials", topic: "Applications of cast irons", keySource: K,
q: `Match the following lists :<br><b>List-I</b> P. Gray iron; Q. Ductile (nodular) iron; R. Malleable iron; S. Compacted graphite iron<br><b>List-II</b> 1. General engineering service at normal and elevated temperature; 2. Miscellaneous soft iron castings in which strength is not a primary consideration; 3. Diesel engine blocks, exhaust manifolds, brake discs for high-speed train; 4. Pressure-containing parts such as valve and pump bodies`,
opts: ["P-2, Q-4, R-1, S-3", "P-1, Q-3, R-2, S-4", "P-3, Q-1, R-4, S-2", "P-1, Q-2, R-3, S-4"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) P-2, Q-4, R-1, S-3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> From Callister's table of cast irons:</p>
<ul><li><b>P Gray iron → 2:</b> soft grade (G1800): miscellaneous castings where strength is not the main need.</li><li><b>Q Ductile iron → 4:</b> pressure-containing parts such as valve and pump bodies.</li><li><b>R Malleable iron → 1:</b> general engineering service at normal and elevated temperatures.</li><li><b>S Compacted graphite iron → 3:</b> diesel engine blocks, exhaust manifolds and brake discs for high-speed trains.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> mismatches at least two irons; none gives CGI its engine-block use.</li><li><b>(c)</b> mismatches at least two irons; none gives CGI its engine-block use.</li><li><b>(d)</b> mismatches at least two irons; none gives CGI its engine-block use.</li><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Every pair matches.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> CGI has worm-like graphite, between gray (flakes) and ductile (nodules) iron.</p>` },

{ id: "2025-133", year: 2025, no: 133, subject: "materials", topic: "Non-ferrous alloys", keySource: K,
q: `Consider the following statements regarding non-ferrous alloys :<ol><li>Molybdenum alloys are used for extrusion dies and structural parts in space vehicles.</li><li>Tantalum is immune to chemical attack by virtually all environments at temperatures below 150 °C and is frequently used in applications requiring such a corrosion-resistant material.</li><li>Superalloys are used in aircraft turbine components, which must withstand exposure to severely oxidizing environments and high temperatures for reasonable time periods.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Statement by statement (Callister)</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> Molybdenum (a refractory metal) alloys: extrusion dies and structural parts in space vehicles.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> Tantalum is immune to chemical attack in almost all environments below 150 °C; used in chemical equipment and surgical implants.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> Superalloys (Ni, Co, Fe–Ni based) are used in aircraft turbines, facing severe oxidation and high temperature.</li></ul>
<p>Other refractory metals: niobium, tungsten (lamp filaments, X-ray tubes).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Refractory metals melt above about 2000 °C (W at 3410 °C).</p>` },

{ id: "2025-134", year: 2025, no: 134, subject: "materials", topic: "Nanostructured materials", keySource: K, provKey: "D",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding nanostructured materials :<ol><li>Nanostructured materials may be defined as those materials whose structural elements—clusters, crystallites or molecules have dimensions in the 1 nm to 100 nm range.</li><li>Clusters of atoms consisting of typically hundreds to thousands on nanometre (nm) scale are commonly called as nanoclusters.</li><li>Fullerenes and carbon nanotubes cannot be seen as curved pieces of graphite.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only (our answer)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Nanostructured materials.</p><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> Nanostructured materials have structural elements of 1–100 nm.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> Clusters of hundreds to thousands of atoms, nanometres across, are nanoclusters.</li><li><b>3 (wrong):</b> Fullerenes and carbon nanotubes <i>can</i> be seen as curved pieces of graphite: graphene sheets closed into spheres (with pentagons giving the curvature) or rolled into tubes.</li></ul>
<div class="note">MADE EASY's unofficial key gives (d), treating all three as true. We differ: standard descriptions present fullerenes and nanotubes as curved or closed graphite sheets (for example in reviews of graphitic structures), and MADE EASY's own note calls them "curved graphite" structures, which contradicts statement 3. We show (a).</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> C<sub>60</sub>: 12 pentagons and 20 hexagons, like a football.</p>` },

{ id: "2025-135", year: 2025, no: 135, subject: "materials", topic: "Creep test", keySource: K,
q: `Which one of the following tests is used to determine the continuing changes in the deformation of materials at elevated temperatures when stresses are below the yield point?`,
opts: ["Tensile test", "Hardness test", "Impact test", "Creep test"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Creep test</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Creep is slow, continuing deformation under constant stress below the yield point, at high temperature. A creep test holds a specimen at constant load and temperature and records strain against time, showing primary, secondary (steady) and tertiary creep up to rupture.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A tensile test loads to fracture in minutes; it does not measure time-dependent strain.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Hardness measures resistance to indentation.</li><li><b>(c)</b> An impact test measures energy absorbed in sudden fracture.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Steady-state creep rate and rupture time are the main design data.</p>` },

{ id: "2025-136", year: 2025, no: 136, subject: "materials", topic: "Isotropy, anisotropy and ductility", keySource: K,
q: `Match the following lists :<br><b>List-I</b> P. Isotropy; Q. Anisotropy; R. Ductility<br><b>List-II</b> 1. Physical properties are not dependent upon the direction in the body along which they are measured; 2. Property of a material which governs its ability to be deformed in processes; 3. Variation of physical property with the direction in a body along which the property is measured`,
opts: ["P-2, Q-3, R-1", "P-1, Q-3, R-2", "P-3, Q-1, R-2", "P-1, Q-2, R-3"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) P-1, Q-3, R-2</h3><ul><li><b>P Isotropy → 1:</b> properties are the same in all directions.</li><li><b>Q Anisotropy → 3:</b> properties vary with direction (single crystals, wood, rolled sheet, composites).</li><li><b>R Ductility → 2:</b> the ability to be deformed plastically, as in wire drawing and forming.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> each mismatch isotropy and anisotropy, or give ductility a direction definition.</li><li><b>(c)</b> each mismatch isotropy and anisotropy, or give ductility a direction definition.</li><li><b>(d)</b> each mismatch isotropy and anisotropy, or give ductility a direction definition.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Every pair matches.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Rolled sheet becomes anisotropic because grains align (texture); this causes earing in deep drawing. Ductility is usually measured by percentage elongation and reduction of area in a tensile test.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Polycrystalline metals with randomly oriented grains are nearly isotropic.</p>` },

{ id: "2025-137", year: 2025, no: 137, subject: "mech", topic: "Bulk modulus and Young's modulus", keySource: K,
q: `What is the relation between K and E? (where ν = Poisson's ratio, K = bulk modulus and E = Young's modulus of elasticity)`,
opts: ["K = E/3(1 − 2ν)", "K = E/2(1 − 2ν)", "K = E(1 + ν)/2", "K = E(1 − ν)/2"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) K = E/[3(1 − 2ν)]</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Under equal stress σ on three axes, each strain is σ(1 − 2ν)/E, so the volumetric strain is 3σ(1 − 2ν)/E. Then K = σ/ε<sub>v</sub> = E/[3(1 − 2ν)].</p><div class="formula">ε_v = 3σ(1 − 2ν)/E</div><div class="formula">K = σ/ε_v</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>K = σ/[3σ(1 − 2ν)/E].</li><li>= <b>E/3(1 − 2ν)</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Factor must be 3 (three equal principal stresses).</li><li><b>(c)</b> E/2(1 + ν) is G, the shear modulus form.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Not a standard elastic relation.</li></ul>
<p>As ν → 0·5, K → ∞: the material becomes incompressible (rubber).</p>
<p>Example: steel with E = 200 GPa and ν = 0·3 gives K = 200/(3 × 0·4) = 167 GPa and G = 200/2·6 = 77 GPa. Two independent constants fix all the others for an isotropic material.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> E = 2G(1 + ν) = 3K(1 − 2ν).</p>` },

{ id: "2025-138", year: 2025, no: 138, subject: "materials", topic: "Impact strength", keySource: K,
q: `Consider the following statements regarding impact strength of a material :<ol><li>Impact strength increases if the dimensions of the specimen are increased.</li><li>When the sharpness of the notch increases, the impact strength of the material required to cause failure also increases.</li><li>The angle of notch also improves impact strength after certain values.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> A larger specimen absorbs more energy to fracture, so the measured impact energy increases with size; that is why standard specimen dimensions are fixed.</li><li><b>2 (wrong):</b> A sharper notch concentrates stress, so <i>less</i> energy is needed to break the specimen: impact strength decreases.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> Beyond a certain angle, opening up the notch angle (making it blunter) improves the impact strength.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Impact tests (Charpy, Izod) use a standard V-notch of 45° and 0·25 mm root radius so that results can be compared.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 3.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Temperature matters too: BCC steels become brittle below their transition temperature.</p>` },

{ id: "2025-139", year: 2025, no: 139, subject: "mech", topic: "Unknown force from resultant", keySource: K,
q: `What is the magnitude of the third force P, if three forces of magnitudes 40 kN, 15 kN and P kN are acting at a point O. The angles made by 40 kN, 15 kN and P kN forces with X-axis are 60°, 120° and 240° respectively, and the magnitude and direction of the resultant force are 30·41 kN and 85·28°? (Take cos 85·28° = 0·08229)`,
opts: ["18 kN", "19 kN", "20 kN", "21 kN"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 20 kN</h3><div class="formula">ΣF<sub>x</sub> = R cos θ</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>ΣF<sub>x</sub> = 40 cos 60° + 15 cos 120° + P cos 240° = 20 − 7·5 − 0·5P.</li><li>R cos θ = 30·41 × 0·08229 = 2·50 kN.</li><li>12·5 − 0·5P = 2·5 → P = <b>20 kN</b>.</li><li>Check ΣF<sub>y</sub> = 34·64 + 12·99 − 17·32 = 30·31 kN; √(2·5² + 30·31²) = 30·41 kN ✓.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 18 gives resultants of 32·2, 31·3 and 29·5 kN.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 19 gives resultants of 32·2, 31·3 and 29·5 kN.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 21 kN gives resultants of 32·2, 31·3 and 29·5 kN.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Method: resolve every force into x and y components, add each set, and compare with the resultant's components. Here one component equation is enough, because it contains only the unknown P; the other equation serves as a check.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Use the component equation with the given cosine: it isolates P directly.</p>` },

{ id: "2025-140", year: 2025, no: 140, subject: "mech", topic: "Laws of friction", keySource: K,
q: `Consider the following statements regarding laws of friction :<ol><li>The limiting frictional force bears a constant ratio to the normal reaction between two surfaces.</li><li>The ratio between limiting friction and normal reaction is always greater when the two surfaces are in motion.</li><li>The limiting frictional force does not depend upon the shape and areas of the surfaces in contact.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> F<sub>limiting</sub> = μN: a constant ratio for given surfaces.</li><li><b>2 (wrong):</b> The ratio is slightly <i>less</i> when the surfaces are in motion: kinetic friction is lower than limiting static friction (μ<sub>k</sub> &lt; μ<sub>s</sub>).</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> Limiting friction is independent of the shape and apparent area of contact (ESE 2022 Q148).</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> These are Coulomb's laws of dry friction. They are approximations: at very high pressures or for very soft materials the friction does depend on area, and lubricated surfaces follow different laws altogether.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 3.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Angle of friction φ = tan<sup>−1</sup> μ.</p>` },

{ id: "2025-141", year: 2025, no: 141, subject: "mech", topic: "Centroids of standard areas", keySource: K,
q: `Match the following lists :<br><b>List-I (Shape)</b> P. Semicircular area; Q. Triangular area; R. Rectangular area; S. Parabolic area<br><b>List-II (Centroid ȳ)</b> 1. h/3; 2. 3b/8; 3. 4r/(3π); 4. h/2`,
opts: ["P-1, Q-2, R-3, S-4", "P-2, Q-1, R-4, S-3", "P-4, Q-3, R-2, S-1", "P-3, Q-1, R-4, S-2"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) P-3, Q-1, R-4, S-2</h3><ul><li><b>P Semicircle → 3:</b> 4r/(3π) from the diameter.</li><li><b>Q Triangle → 1:</b> h/3 from the base.</li><li><b>R Rectangle → 4:</b> h/2.</li><li><b>S Parabolic area → 2:</b> 3b/8 (for the half-parabolic spandrel/segment, measured from the axis).</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> mismatches the semicircle, whose 4r/3π is unique to circular shapes.</li><li><b>(b)</b> mismatches the semicircle, whose 4r/3π is unique to circular shapes.</li><li><b>(c)</b> mismatches the semicircle, whose 4r/3π is unique to circular shapes.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Every pair matches.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The parabolic-area value depends on which region is meant: for a semi-parabolic area of base b and height h, the centroid lies 3b/8 from the axis and 2h/5 from the base. Only the semicircle takes the distinctive form 4r/3π.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Quarter circle: 4r/3π from both straight edges.</p>` },

{ id: "2025-142", year: 2025, no: 142, subject: "mech", topic: "Kinematics from position function", keySource: K,
q: `What is the time required for a particle to reach a velocity of 72 m/s from its initial condition at t = 0, if the position coordinate of the particle which is confined to move along a straight line is given by s = 2t³ − 24t + 6, where s is measured in metres from a convenient origin and t is in seconds?`,
opts: ["4 s", "6 s", "8 s", "2 s"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 4 s</h3><div class="formula">v = ds/dt = 6t² − 24</div>
<p><b>Concept:</b> 6t² − 24 = 72 → t² = 16 → t = <b>4 s</b>.</p>
<p>At t = 0 the velocity is −24 m/s; it is zero at t = 2 s (where the particle reverses) and reaches 72 m/s at 4 s. Acceleration a = 12t = 48 m/s² at that instant.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(d)</b> 2 s is when v = 0.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 6 s gives 192 and 360 m/s.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 8 s gives 192 and 360 m/s.</li></ul>
<p>Displacement from t = 0 to t = 4 s: s(4) − s(0) = (128 − 96 + 6) − 6 = 32 m; but the particle first moves back to s = −26 m at t = 2 s, so the distance travelled is 32 + 32 = 64 m.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Meriam's example: differentiate once for velocity, twice for acceleration.</p>` },

{ id: "2025-143", year: 2025, no: 143, subject: "mech", topic: "Symbols for stress and strain", keySource: K,
q: `Match the following lists :<br><b>List-I (Name)</b> P. Tensile strain; Q. Tensile stress; R. Shear strain; S. Shear stress<br><b>List-II (Symbol)</b> 1. τ; 2. φ; 3. e; 4. σ`,
opts: ["P-1, Q-2, R-3, S-4", "P-2, Q-1, R-4, S-3", "P-3, Q-4, R-2, S-1", "P-3, Q-1, R-4, S-2"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) P-3, Q-4, R-2, S-1</h3><ul><li><b>P Tensile strain → 3:</b> e (or ε).</li><li><b>Q Tensile stress → 4:</b> σ.</li><li><b>R Shear strain → 2:</b> φ (or γ), the change in right angle.</li><li><b>S Shear stress → 1:</b> τ.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> gives τ or σ to strains, which are dimensionless.</li><li><b>(b)</b> gives τ or σ to strains, which are dimensionless.</li><li><b>(d)</b> gives τ or σ to strains, which are dimensionless.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Every pair matches.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Relations: σ = E e and τ = G φ.</p>
<p>Strains are ratios (change in length over original length, or an angle in radians), so they have no units. Stresses are force per unit area, in N/mm² or MPa.</p><p>Indian textbooks (Bansal, Rajput) use e for linear strain and φ for shear strain; Western texts use ε and γ. Both conventions keep σ for normal stress and τ for shear stress. Volumetric strain is written e<sub>v</sub>, and the relations between them are e<sub>v</sub> = 3e for equal triaxial strain and E = 2G(1 + μ).</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Greek σ, τ for stresses; e, φ (or ε, γ) for strains.</p>` },

{ id: "2025-144", year: 2025, no: 144, subject: "mech", topic: "Elongation of a rod", keySource: K,
q: `A rod 150 cm long and of diameter 2·0 cm is subjected to an axial pull of 20 kN. If the modulus of elasticity of the material of the rod is 2 × 10⁵ N/mm², what is the elongation of the rod?`,
opts: ["1·5/π mm", "1·6/π mm", "1·4/π mm", "1·8/π mm"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1·5/π mm</h3><div class="formula">δ = PL/(AE)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>P = 20,000 N; L = 1500 mm; A = π × 20²/4 = 100π mm²; E = 2 × 10⁵ N/mm²</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> δ = 20,000 × 1500/(100π × 2 × 10⁵) = 3 × 10⁷/(2π × 10⁷) = <b>1·5/π mm</b> ≈ 0·477 mm.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 1·6/π π mm do not follow from PL/AE with these data.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 1·4/π π mm do not follow from PL/AE with these data.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 1·8/ π mm do not follow from PL/AE with these data.</li></ul>
<p>Numerically, 1·5/π = 0·477 mm. Strain = σ/E = 63·7/200,000 = 3·18 × 10<sup>−4</sup>, and 3·18 × 10<sup>−4</sup> × 1500 mm gives the same 0·477 mm. ✓</p><p>Working in consistent units avoids slips: keep force in N, lengths in mm and E in N/mm², and the result comes out directly in mm. Mixing cm (the given length and diameter) with mm is the most common error in this kind of question.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Stress here = 20,000/(100π) = 63·7 N/mm², well within the elastic range.</p>` },

{ id: "2025-145", year: 2025, no: 145, subject: "mech", topic: "Principal planes", keySource: K,
q: `The planes on which the shear stress is zero are known as`,
opts: ["normal planes", "tangential planes", "orthogonal planes", "principal planes"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) principal planes</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Principal planes carry only normal stress, with zero shear. The normal stresses on them are the principal stresses (the maximum and minimum normal stresses). In two dimensions they are at right angles, found from tan 2θ<sub>p</sub> = 2τ<sub>xy</sub>/(σ<sub>x</sub> − σ<sub>y</sub>).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 'Normal planes' is not the defined name for zero-shear planes.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 'Tangential planes' is not a defined stress-analysis term.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 'Orthogonal' only means perpendicular; it is not a property of zero shear.</li></ul>
<p>Planes of maximum shear lie at 45° to the principal planes.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> On Mohr's circle, the principal stresses are where the circle cuts the σ axis.</p>` },

{ id: "2025-146", year: 2025, no: 146, subject: "mech", topic: "Normal stress on an oblique plane", keySource: K,
q: `What is the expression for normal stress when two perpendicular stresses are acting accompanied with a state of simple shear?`,
opts: ["p<sub>1</sub> cos²θ + p<sub>2</sub> sin²θ + q sin 2θ", "{(p<sub>1</sub> + p<sub>2</sub>)/2} sin 2θ + q cos 2θ", "p<sub>1</sub> sin²θ + p<sub>2</sub> cos²θ + q sin 2θ", "{(p<sub>1</sub> + p<sub>2</sub>)/2} cos 2θ + q sin 2θ"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) p<sub>1</sub> cos²θ + p<sub>2</sub> sin²θ + q sin 2θ</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> With cos²θ = (1 + cos 2θ)/2 and sin²θ = (1 − cos 2θ)/2, this equals the standard form:</p>
<div class="formula">σ<sub>n</sub> = (p<sub>1</sub> + p<sub>2</sub>)/2 + [(p<sub>1</sub> − p<sub>2</sub>)/2] cos 2θ + q sin 2θ</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> has the wrong trigonometric functions; it is not the normal stress.</li><li><b>(c)</b> swaps sin² and cos², which is correct only if θ is measured from the other plane.</li><li><b>(d)</b> omits the mean-stress term and uses the sum instead of the difference.</li></ul>
<p>Here θ is the angle of the oblique plane's normal from the direction of p<sub>1</sub>. The tangential (shear) stress on the same plane is [(p<sub>1</sub> − p<sub>2</sub>)/2] sin 2θ − q cos 2θ.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Our first answer was (a), as was MADE EASY's.</p>` },

{ id: "2025-147", year: 2025, no: 147, subject: "fm", topic: "Darcy–Weisbach equation", keySource: K,
q: `The equation commonly used for finding loss of head due to friction in pipes is`,
opts: ["Darcy-Weisbach equation", "Reynolds equation", "Navier-Stokes equation", "Hagen-Poiseuille equation"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Darcy-Weisbach equation</h3><div class="formula">h<sub>f</sub> = f L V²/(2 g D) (Darcy f), or 4f′LV²/(2gD) with the Fanning-type coefficient</div>
<p><b>Concept:</b> It applies to both laminar and turbulent flow, with f found from the Moody chart or the Colebrook equation.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> "Reynolds equation" is for lubricating films (or is the Reynolds number, a parameter).</li><li><b>(c)</b> Navier–Stokes is the general equation of viscous motion; it is not the practical formula used.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Hagen–Poiseuille gives the head loss only for laminar flow: h<sub>f</sub> = 32μVL/(ρgD²).</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> For laminar flow, Darcy f = 64/Re.</p>` },

{ id: "2025-148", year: 2025, no: 148, subject: "mech", topic: "SF and BM diagrams", keySource: K,
q: `Consider the following statements regarding shear force and bending moment diagrams :<ol><li>The shear force between any two vertical loads will be constant and hence the shear force diagram between two vertical loads will be horizontal.</li><li>The bending moment at the two supports of a simply supported beam and at the free end of a cantilever will be zero. But at the fixed end of the cantilever, there will be bending moment (or fixing moment).</li><li>The positive values of shear force and bending moment are plotted above the baseline, and negative values below the baseline.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Statement by statement (Bansal)</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> With only point loads, the shear force is constant between loads, so the SF diagram is horizontal there.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> The moment is zero at simple supports and at a cantilever's free end; the fixed end carries the fixing moment.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> By convention, positive values are plotted above the base line and negative values below.</li></ul>
<div class="formula">dM/dx = V; dV/dx = −w</div>
<p>With a uniformly distributed load between supports, the SF diagram becomes inclined and the BM diagram parabolic. Under a point load the SF jumps by the load's value and the BM has a kink.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Maximum BM occurs where the SF changes sign.</p>` },

{ id: "2025-149", year: 2025, no: 149, subject: "mech", topic: "Bending stress in a curved wire", keySource: K,
q: `A steel wire of 10 mm diameter is bent into a circular arc of 20 metres radius. What is the maximum stress induced in it? (Take E = 2 × 10⁵ N/mm²)`,
opts: ["40 N/mm²", "45 N/mm²", "50 N/mm²", "55 N/mm²"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 50 N/mm²</h3><div class="formula">σ/y = E/R → σ<sub>max</sub> = E y<sub>max</sub>/R</div>
<p><b>Concept:</b> σ = 2 × 10⁵ × 5/20,000 = <b>50 N/mm²</b>, with y<sub>max</sub> = 5 mm (half the diameter) and R = 20,000 mm.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>y_max = 5 mm, R = 20 000 mm.</li><li>σ = 2 × 10⁵ × 5/20 000 = <b>50 N/mm²</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 40 does not equal Ey/R.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 45 does not equal Ey/R.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 55 N/mm² does not equal Ey/R.</li><li>Using the full diameter for y would give 100 N/mm².</li></ul>
<p>This follows from simple bending theory, M/I = σ/y = E/R. The corresponding bending moment is M = EI/R = 2 × 10⁵ × (π × 10⁴/64)/20,000 = 4909 N mm.</p><p>Assumptions: the wire stays elastic (50 N/mm² is far below the yield strength of steel wire) and bends into a true circular arc, so the curvature, and with it the bending stress, is the same along its whole length.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> The stress does not depend on the moment of inertia here, only on curvature.</p>` },

{ id: "2025-150", year: 2025, no: 150, subject: "fm", topic: "Piezometric head", keySource: K,
q: `The sum of pressure head and potential head is termed as`,
opts: ["datum head", "velocity head", "piezometric head", "static head"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) piezometric head</h3><div class="formula">Piezometric head = p/ρg + z</div>
<p><b>Concept:</b> It is the level to which liquid would rise in a piezometer tube; the line joining these levels along a pipe is the hydraulic gradient line.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Datum (potential) head is z alone.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Velocity head is V²/2g.</li><li><b>(d)</b> "Static head" usually means the vertical height between water levels in a pumping system (suction + delivery heads).</li></ul>
<p>Total head = piezometric head + V²/2g; the total-energy line lies V²/2g above the hydraulic gradient line.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> HGL = pressure + elevation; EGL = HGL + velocity head.</p>` }
);
})();

