// ESE 2024 Prelims Paper II, Mechanical Engineering, Series A, Q1–25
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2024-1", year: 2024, no: 1, subject: "fm", topic: "Viscosity of liquids and gases",
q: `Consider the following statements :<ol><li>Viscosity of liquid increases with increase in temperature.</li><li>Viscosity of gas decreases with increase in temperature.</li><li>In liquids, cohesive forces predominate the molecular momentum transfer.</li><li>In gases, cohesive forces are small and the molecular momentum transfer predominates.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "3 and 4 only", "1, 2, 3 and 4"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 3 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (wrong):</b> Liquid viscosity <i>decreases</i> as temperature rises (water: 1·79 mPa s at 0 °C, 0·28 mPa s at 100 °C).</li><li><b>2 (wrong):</b> Gas viscosity <i>increases</i> as temperature rises (air: 17·2 μPa s at 0 °C, 21·8 μPa s at 100 °C).</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> In liquids the molecules are close together and cohesion dominates; heating weakens it, so viscosity falls.</li><li><b>4 (correct):</b> In gases cohesion is small; viscosity comes from molecules carrying momentum between layers, which increases with molecular speed (temperature).</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Statements 3 and 4 are the reason for the true behaviour that statements 1 and 2 state the wrong way round. (ESE 2021 Q146 tested the same idea.)</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, 2, which are incorrect; Omits 3, 4, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 4, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 3, 4.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 1, 2, which are incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Gas viscosity is nearly independent of pressure at ordinary pressures.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-2", year: 2024, no: 2, subject: "fm", topic: "Ideal plastic (Bingham) fluid",
q: `A fluid in which shear stress is more than the yield value and shear stress is proportional to the rate of shear strain is known as`,
opts: ["non-ideal plastic fluid", "Newtonian fluid", "non-Newtonian fluid", "ideal plastic fluid"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) ideal plastic fluid</h3><div class="formula">τ = τ<sub>y</sub> + μ<sub>p</sub>(du/dy) for τ &gt; τ<sub>y</sub></div>
<p><b>Concept:</b> An ideal plastic (Bingham plastic) does not flow until the shear stress exceeds a yield value τ<sub>y</sub>; beyond that, the excess shear stress is proportional to the rate of shear strain (a straight line on the rheogram, offset from the origin). Examples: toothpaste, drilling mud, sewage sludge.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A non-ideal plastic also has a yield stress, but its curve beyond yield is non-linear.</li><li><b>(b)</b> A Newtonian fluid has no yield stress: τ = μ du/dy through the origin.</li><li><b>(c)</b> "Non-Newtonian" is the broad family; the specific name for this behaviour is ideal plastic.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Rheogram: Newtonian (straight through origin), Bingham (straight, offset), pseudoplastic (concave down), dilatant (concave up).</p>` },

{ id: "2024-3", year: 2024, no: 3, subject: "fm", topic: "Shear stress in an oil film",
q: `Two horizontal plates are placed 1·25 cm apart, the space between them being filled with an oil of viscosity 15 poises. What is the shear stress in the oil if the upper plate is moved with a velocity of 2·5 m/s?`,
opts: ["280 N/m²", "260 N/m²", "300 N/m²", "250 N/m²"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 300 N/m²</h3><div class="formula">τ = μ du/dy = μ V/h (linear velocity profile)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>μ = 15 poise = 1·5 N s/m²; V = 2·5 m/s; h = 0·0125 m</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> τ = 1·5 × 2·5/0·0125 = <b>300 N/m²</b>.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>μ = 15 P = 1.5 N·s/m².</li><li>τ = 1.5 × 2.5/0.0125 = <b>300 N/m²</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 280 does not equal μV/h; using 15 N s/m² (forgetting the poise conversion) would give 3000 N/m².</li><li><b>(b)</b> 260 does not equal μV/h; using 15 N s/m² (forgetting the poise conversion) would give 3000 N/m².</li><li><b>(d)</b> 250 N/m² does not equal μV/h; using 15 N s/m² (forgetting the poise conversion) would give 3000 N/m².</li></ul>
<p>The plates are close together, so the flow is laminar Couette flow with a linear profile and the same shear stress everywhere in the gap.</p>
<p>Force needed per square metre of plate equals the shear stress (300 N/m²); the power to drag 1 m² of plate is τ × V = 300 × 2·5 = 750 W. A thicker oil film would reduce both in proportion.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> 1 poise = 0·1 N s/m².</p>` },

{ id: "2024-4", year: 2024, no: 4, subject: "fm", topic: "Capillary depression of mercury",
q: `What is the capillary rise in a glass tube of 2·0 mm diameter when immersed vertically in mercury? (Take surface tension of mercury as 0·52 N/m. The specific gravity of mercury is 13·6 and the angle of contact is 130°)`,
opts: ["−0·25 cm", "−0·65 cm", "−0·8 cm", "−0·5 cm"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) −0·5 cm</h3><div class="formula">h = 4 σ cos θ/(ρ g d)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>σ = 0·52 N/m; θ = 130° (cos θ = −0·643); ρ = 13,600 kg/m³; d = 0·002 m</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> h = 4 × 0·52 × (−0·643)/(13,600 × 9·81 × 0·002) = −1·337/266·8 = −0·0050 m = <b>−0·5 cm</b>.</p>
<p>The negative sign means capillary <i>depression</i>: mercury does not wet glass (θ &gt; 90°), so its level in the tube is below the outside level.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Numerator: 4 × 0.52 × (−0.643) = −1.337.</li><li>Denominator: 13 600 × 9.81 × 0.002 = 266.8.</li><li>h = −0.0050 m = <b>−0.5 cm</b> (depression).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> −0·25 cm uses the radius in place of the diameter in a 2σ/(ρgr) form wrongly, or omits a factor of 2.</li><li><b>(b)</b> −0·65 cm ignores the cos θ factor (which would give −0·78 cm).</li><li><b>(c)</b> −0·8 cm ignores the cos θ factor (which would give −0·78 cm).</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Water in clean glass (θ ≈ 0°) rises; mercury (θ ≈ 130–140°) is depressed.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-5", year: 2024, no: 5, subject: "fm", topic: "Piezometers and manometers",
q: `Consider the following statements :<ol><li>One end of piezometer is connected to the point where pressure is to be measured and the other end is open to the atmosphere.</li><li>Inverted U-tube differential manometer is used to measure high pressure.</li><li>U-tube differential manometer contains heavy liquid.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> A piezometer is a simple tube connected at one end to the point of measurement and open to the atmosphere at the other; the liquid rises to the pressure head.</li><li><b>2 (wrong):</b> An inverted U-tube differential manometer uses a <i>light</i> liquid (air or oil) at the top and measures <i>small</i> pressure differences.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> An ordinary U-tube differential manometer uses a heavy liquid (such as mercury) at the bottom, suited to larger pressure differences.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Piezometers cannot measure gas pressures, negative (vacuum) pressures, or high pressures (the tube would be too tall).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 3.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Heavy manometric liquid → large differences; light liquid in inverted tube → small differences.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-6", year: 2024, no: 6, subject: "fm", topic: "Hydrostatic force on a circular disc",
q: `A circular opening, 3 m diameter, in a vertical side of a tank is closed by a disc of 3 m diameter which can rotate about a horizontal diameter. What is the force on the disc if the head of water above the horizontal diameter is 5 m?`,
opts: ["277·4 kN", "324·4 kN", "311·2 kN", "346·7 kN"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 346·7 kN</h3><div class="formula">F = ρ g A h̄</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>A = (π/4) × 3² = 7·069 m²; h̄ = 5 m (the centroid of the disc lies on its horizontal diameter)</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> F = 9810 × 7·069 × 5 = <b>346·7 kN</b>.</p>
<p>The next part of this textbook problem asks for the torque to keep the disc closed. The centre of pressure lies below the centroid by I<sub>G</sub>/(A h̄) = (πd⁴/64)/(7·069 × 5) = 3·976/35·34 = 0·1125 m, so the torque about the diameter is F × 0·1125 = 39 kN m.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>A = (π/4) × 3² = 7.069 m², h̄ = 5 m.</li><li>F = 9810 × 7.069 × 5 = <b>346.7 kN</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 277·4 uses a wrong depth for the centroid (for example to the top of the disc).</li><li><b>(b)</b> 324·4 uses a wrong depth for the centroid (for example to the top of the disc).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 311·2 kN uses a wrong depth for the centroid (for example to the top of the disc).</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> The total force depends only on the centroid depth; the centre of pressure depends on the second moment of area.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-7", year: 2024, no: 7, subject: "fm", topic: "Flow-measuring devices",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding dynamics of fluid flow :<ol><li>Pitot tube is a device used to measure rate of flow of fluid through a pipe.</li><li>Orifice meter is a device used for measuring velocity of flow at any point in a pipe.</li><li>Venturi meter consists of converging part, throat and diverging part.</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 3", "2 only", "3 only", "1 and 2"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Flow-measuring devices.</p><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (wrong):</b> A pitot tube measures the <i>velocity</i> at a point, from the difference between stagnation and static pressure.</li><li><b>2 (wrong):</b> An orifice meter measures the <i>rate of flow</i> (discharge) through a pipe. Statements 1 and 2 have the two devices' functions swapped.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> A venturimeter has a short converging cone, a throat and a long, gradually diverging cone (to recover pressure with little loss).</li></ul>
<div class="formula">Venturi: Q = C<sub>d</sub> a<sub>1</sub>a<sub>2</sub>√(2gh)/√(a<sub>1</sub>² − a<sub>2</sub>²); C<sub>d</sub> ≈ 0·98 (orifice meter about 0·6)</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 3.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 1, 2, which are incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> The venturi loses much less head than an orifice plate, but is longer and costlier.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-8", year: 2024, no: 8, subject: "fm", topic: "Pressure gradient between fixed plates",
q: `What is the pressure gradient per metre width for the laminar flow of oil with a maximum velocity of 2 m/s between two horizontal parallel fixed plates which are 100 mm apart? (Take dynamic viscosity = 2·5 N-s/m²)`,
opts: ["−4000 N/m² per m", "−3700 N/m² per m", "−3500 N/m² per m", "−3200 N/m² per m"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) −4000 N/m² per m</h3><div class="formula">u<sub>max</sub> = (−dp/dx) t²/(8μ) → dp/dx = −8μ u<sub>max</sub>/t²</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>u<sub>max</sub> = 2 m/s; t = 0·1 m (gap); μ = 2·5 N s/m²</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> dp/dx = −8 × 2·5 × 2/0·01 = <b>−4000 N/m² per m</b>.</p>
<p>Equivalently: mean velocity ū = (2/3) u<sub>max</sub> = 1·333 m/s, and dp/dx = −12μū/t² = −12 × 2·5 × 1·333/0·01 = −4000 N/m³. The pressure falls in the direction of flow.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>dp/dx = −8 × 2.5 × 2/0.1².</li><li>= <b>−4000 N/m² per m</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> does not satisfy the plane Poiseuille relation with these data.</li><li><b>(c)</b> does not satisfy the plane Poiseuille relation with these data.</li><li><b>(d)</b> does not satisfy the plane Poiseuille relation with these data.</li></ul>
<p>Flow rate per metre width: q = ū t = 1·333 × 0·1 = 0·1333 m³/s per m. The velocity profile is parabolic, u = u<sub>max</sub>[1 − (2y/t)²], with y measured from the centreline.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Wall shear stress here = (−dp/dx) t/2 = 200 N/m².</p>` },

{ id: "2024-9", year: 2024, no: 9, subject: "fm", topic: "Kinetic energy and momentum correction factors",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding dynamics of fluid flow :<ol><li>Kinetic energy correction factor is defined as the ratio of kinetic energy of the flow per second based on actual velocity to kinetic energy of the flow per second based on average velocity.</li><li>Kinetic energy correction factor is defined as the ratio of kinetic energy of the flow per second based on average velocity to kinetic energy of the flow per second based on maximum velocity.</li><li>Momentum correction factor is defined as the ratio of momentum of the flow per second based on actual velocity to momentum of the flow per second based on average velocity.</li><li>Momentum correction factor is defined as the ratio of momentum of the flow per second based on average velocity to momentum of the flow per second based on maximum velocity.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 4", "1 and 3", "2 and 4", "2 and 3"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1 and 3</h3><div class="formula">α = (1/A)∫(u/V)³ dA &nbsp;&nbsp; β = (1/A)∫(u/V)² dA</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>u: actual local velocity; V: average velocity over the section</li></ul>
<p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> α = actual KE flux/KE flux computed with the average velocity.</li><li><b>2 (wrong):</b> The comparison is with the average velocity, not the maximum.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> β = actual momentum flux/momentum flux computed with the average velocity.</li><li><b>4 (wrong):</b> Again, maximum velocity is not the reference.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Values: laminar pipe flow α = 2, β = 4/3; turbulent pipe flow α ≈ 1·03–1·1, β ≈ 1·01–1·04; uniform flow α = β = 1.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 4, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 3.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 2, 4, which are incorrect; Omits 1, 3, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> α multiplies V²/2g in the energy equation; β multiplies ρQV in the momentum equation.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-10", year: 2024, no: 10, subject: "fm", topic: "Head loss by Darcy's formula",
q: `An oil is flowing through a pipe of diameter 300 mm with velocity of 5 m/s. What is the head loss to maintain the flow for a length of 1000 m? (Take coefficient of friction = 0·005)`,
opts: ["60·2 m", "72·4 m", "76·7 m", "84·9 m"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 84·9 m</h3><div class="formula">h<sub>f</sub> = 4 f L V²/(2 g D)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>f = 0·005 ("coefficient of friction", the Fanning-type factor used in Bansal's form); L = 1000 m; V = 5 m/s; D = 0·3 m</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> h<sub>f</sub> = 4 × 0·005 × 1000 × 25/(2 × 9·81 × 0·3) = 500/5·886 = <b>84·9 m</b>.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>h_f = 4 × 0.005 × 1000 × 5²/(2 × 9.81 × 0.3).</li><li>= 500/5.886 = <b>84.9 m</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 60.2 m would need f ≈ 0.00355.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 72.4 m would need f ≈ 0.00426.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 76.7 m would need f ≈ 0.00452.</li></ul>
<p>Indian texts write Darcy–Weisbach as h<sub>f</sub> = 4fLV²/(2gD), where f is the coefficient of friction; the Darcy friction factor is 4f (here 0·02).</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Head loss grows with V²: halving the velocity cuts it to a quarter.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-11", year: 2024, no: 11, subject: "fm", topic: "Non-Newtonian fluids",
q: `Consider the following statements :<ol><li>The apparent viscosity of a pseudoplastic fluid decreases with decrease in the shear rate.</li><li>The apparent viscosity of a dilatant fluid increases with increase in the shear rate.</li><li>Bingham plastic fluid requires a finite yield stress before beginning to flow.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 2 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Non-Newtonian fluids.</p><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (wrong):</b> A pseudoplastic (shear-thinning) fluid's apparent viscosity <i>decreases</i> as the shear rate <i>increases</i> (paints, blood, polymer solutions). With decreasing shear rate it increases.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> A dilatant (shear-thickening) fluid's apparent viscosity increases with shear rate (cornflour in water, wet sand).</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> A Bingham plastic does not flow until the shear stress exceeds a finite yield stress.</li></ul>
<div class="formula">Power law: τ = K (du/dy)<sup>n</sup>; n &lt; 1 pseudoplastic, n = 1 Newtonian, n &gt; 1 dilatant</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 2, 3.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Apparent viscosity μ<sub>app</sub> = τ/(du/dy) = K (du/dy)<sup>n−1</sup>.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-12", year: 2024, no: 12, subject: "mech", topic: "Dimensions of bulk modulus",
q: `The dimensional formula of bulk modulus of elasticity in MLT notation system is`,
opts: ["MLT<sup>−1</sup>", "M<sup>−1</sup>LT²", "ML<sup>−1</sup>T<sup>−1</sup>", "ML<sup>−1</sup>T<sup>−2</sup>"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) ML<sup>−1</sup>T<sup>−2</sup></h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Bulk modulus K = −Δp/(ΔV/V). The volumetric strain is dimensionless, so K has the dimensions of pressure (stress):</p>
<div class="formula">[K] = [force/area] = MLT<sup>−2</sup>/L² = ML<sup>−1</sup>T<sup>−2</sup></div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>[pressure] = MLT⁻²/L².</li><li>= <b>ML⁻¹T⁻²</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> MLT<sup>−1</sup> is momentum.</li><li><b>(b)</b> M<sup>−1</sup>LT² is the reciprocal of stress: the dimensions of compressibility (1/K), not of K itself.</li><li><b>(c)</b> ML<sup>−1</sup>T<sup>−1</sup> is dynamic viscosity.</li></ul>
<p>All elastic moduli (E, G, K) have the dimensions of stress: ML<sup>−1</sup>T<sup>−2</sup>, unit Pa.</p>
<p>Dimensional analysis check: pressure = force/area = (mass × acceleration)/area = M (L T<sup>−2</sup>)/L² = M L<sup>−1</sup> T<sup>−2</sup>. Energy per unit volume has the same dimensions, which is why stress can also be read as energy density.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Water's bulk modulus is about 2·2 GPa; steel's about 160 GPa.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-13", year: 2024, no: 13, subject: "mech", topic: "Sign conventions in stress analysis", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Consider the following statements regarding general conventions in stress analysis :<ol><li>Tensile stress is positive and compressive stress is negative.</li><li>A pair of shear stresses on parallel planes forming a clockwise couple is positive and a pair with counterclockwise couple is negative.</li><li>Clockwise angle is taken as negative and counterclockwise couple is positive.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> Normal stresses: tension positive, compression negative.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> In the Mohr's circle convention used in strength-of-materials texts, shear stresses forming a clockwise couple on the element are taken as positive, and counterclockwise as negative.</li><li><b>3 (wrong as written):</b> The angle convention is that angles measured counterclockwise are positive and clockwise negative; the statement mixes an "angle" with a "couple" and contradicts statement 2 by calling a counterclockwise <i>couple</i> positive.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Our first answer was (d), reading statement 3 as the usual angle convention. On re-reading, the statement speaks of a counterclockwise <i>couple</i> being positive, which conflicts with statement 2, so it cannot be correct alongside 1 and 2. We accepted the key's (a).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Always state the sign convention when drawing Mohr's circle: textbooks differ on the shear-stress sign.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-14", year: 2024, no: 14, subject: "mech", topic: "Principle of superposition",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding principle of superposition :<ol><li>The principle of superposition states that if a body is acted upon by a number of loads on various segments of the body, then the net effect on the body is the sum of the effects caused by each of the loads acting independently on the respective segment of the body.</li><li>The superposition principle applies to all parameters like stress, strain and deflection.</li><li>The superposition principle is applicable to materials with non-linear stress-strain characteristic, which do not follow Hooke's law.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> The net effect of several loads equals the sum of the effects of each load acting alone.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> It applies to stresses, strains, deflections and reactions, provided the system is linear.</li><li><b>3 (wrong):</b> Superposition needs <i>linearity</i>: linear-elastic material (Hooke's law) and small deformations that do not change the geometry. It does not apply to non-linear materials or large deflections.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Superposition is the basis of beam-deflection tables: complex loadings are broken into standard cases whose deflections are added (see ESE 2022 Q7).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Superposition fails for buckling, plastic deformation and contact (Hertz) problems, all of which are non-linear.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-15", year: 2024, no: 15, subject: "mech", topic: "Thermal stress in rails",
q: `Rails are laid such that they have no stress at 24 °C. What is the stress in the rails at 80 °C, when there is no allowance for expansion? (Assume coefficient of linear expansion = 11 × 10<sup>−6</sup> °C<sup>−1</sup> and Young's modulus of rails metal = 205 GPa)`,
opts: ["126·28 MPa", "251·84 MPa", "296·72 MPa", "325·35 MPa"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 126·28 MPa</h3><div class="formula">σ = E α ΔT</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>E = 205 × 10³ MPa; α = 11 × 10<sup>−6</sup>/°C; ΔT = 80 − 24 = 56 °C</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> σ = 205,000 × 11 × 10<sup>−6</sup> × 56 = <b>126·28 MPa</b> (compressive, since the rail is prevented from expanding).</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>ΔT = 56 °C.</li><li>σ = 205 000 × 11 × 10⁻⁶ × 56 = <b>126.28 MPa</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 251·84 MPa is twice the correct value.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 296·72 uses the full 80 °C or other slips.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 325·35 MPa uses the full 80 °C or other slips.</li></ul>
<p>Thermal stress does not depend on the rail length or cross-section, only on E, α and ΔT. Continuous welded rails are laid at a "stress-free temperature" chosen to keep stresses within limits.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> If the rails could expand partly (gap δ), σ = E(αLΔT − δ)/L.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-16", year: 2024, no: 16, subject: "mech", topic: "Pure shear: stresses on 45° planes",
q: `When a body is acted upon by pure shear stresses on two perpendicular planes, the planes inclined at 45° are subjected to`,
opts: ["a tensile stress of magnitude equal to that of the shear stress", "a compressive stress of magnitude equal to that of the shear stress", "a torsional stress of magnitude equal to that of the shear stress", "a bending stress of magnitude equal to that of the shear stress"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) a tensile stress of magnitude equal to that of the shear stress</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For pure shear (σ<sub>x</sub> = σ<sub>y</sub> = 0, shear τ), the normal stress on a plane at angle θ is σ<sub>θ</sub> = τ sin 2θ, and the shear on it is τ cos 2θ.</p>
<div class="formula">θ = 45°: σ = +τ (tensile), shear = 0; θ = 135°: σ = −τ (compressive), shear = 0</div>
<p>So the 45° planes are principal planes carrying a tensile stress equal to τ, with no shear; the perpendicular (135°) planes carry an equal compressive stress (Q24).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Compression acts on the other diagonal (135°), with the usual sign convention for positive shear.</li><li><b>(c)</b> "Torsional" and "bending" describe loadings, not stresses on a plane.</li><li><b>(d)</b> "Torsional" and "bending" describe loadings, not stresses on a plane.</li></ul>
<p>This is why brittle materials in torsion (for example chalk) break along 45° helices: they fail in tension across the tensile diagonal.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Mohr's circle for pure shear is centred at the origin with radius τ.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-17", year: 2024, no: 17, subject: "mech", topic: "Equal biaxial tension",
q: `A piece of material is subjected to same two perpendicular tensile stresses of 100 MPa each. What is the direct stress?`,
opts: ["90 MPa", "100 MPa", "96 MPa", "86 MPa"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 100 MPa</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For equal biaxial stress σ<sub>x</sub> = σ<sub>y</sub> = σ, the normal stress on any inclined plane is:</p>
<div class="formula">σ<sub>θ</sub> = (σ<sub>x</sub> + σ<sub>y</sub>)/2 + [(σ<sub>x</sub> − σ<sub>y</sub>)/2] cos 2θ = σ</div>
<p>So every plane carries a direct stress of <b>100 MPa</b>, and the shear stress on every plane is (σ<sub>x</sub> − σ<sub>y</sub>)/2 × sin 2θ = 0. Mohr's circle shrinks to a point.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>σ_x = σ_y = 100 MPa ⇒ second term = 0.</li><li>σ_θ = <b>100 MPa</b> on every plane.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Equal biaxial tension gives σ on every plane; no reduction to 90 MPa.</li><li><b>(c)</b> No reduction; Mohr's circle is a point.</li><li><b>(d)</b> No reduction; Mohr's circle is a point.</li></ul>
<p>This stress state occurs in the wall of a thin spherical pressure vessel.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Equal biaxial stress: no shear on any plane in that plane of stress (though out-of-plane shear is σ/2).</p>` },

{ id: "2024-18", year: 2024, no: 18, subject: "mech", topic: "Modulus of toughness",
q: `The strain energy per unit volume required to cause the material to rupture is called`,
opts: ["modulus of toughness", "modulus of rigidity", "resilience", "proof resilience"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) modulus of toughness</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The modulus of toughness is the strain energy per unit volume absorbed up to fracture: the whole area under the stress–strain curve (elastic + plastic parts).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Modulus of rigidity is G = τ/γ, an elastic constant.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Resilience is the elastic strain energy stored (and recovered on unloading).</li><li><b>(d)</b> Proof resilience is the maximum strain energy that can be stored without permanent set, i.e. up to the elastic limit. Per unit volume, it is the modulus of resilience σ<sub>e</sub>²/2E.</li></ul>
<p>Approximation: modulus of toughness ≈ (σ<sub>y</sub> + σ<sub>u</sub>)/2 × ε<sub>f</sub> for ductile metals.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Toughness needs both strength and ductility: a strong but brittle material has low toughness.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-19", year: 2024, no: 19, subject: "mech", topic: "Buckling of columns",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding buckling concept :<ol><li>Buckling can occur when the induced stresses are compressive such as in a column.</li><li>Buckling analysis uses the Young's modulus of the material and the moment of inertia of the column cross-section, as well as its length.</li><li>The load that buckles the column is called the crushing load.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> Buckling is an instability under compression; long, slender columns buckle sideways at loads far below their crushing strength.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> Euler's formula uses E, I and the effective length: P<sub>cr</sub> = π²EI/L<sub>e</sub>².</li><li><b>3 (wrong):</b> The load that buckles a column is the <i>critical</i> (buckling, crippling) load. The crushing load is the load at which a short column fails by crushing of the material: σ<sub>c</sub> × A.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Rankine's formula combines both: 1/P = 1/P<sub>crush</sub> + 1/P<sub>Euler</sub>, valid for all slenderness ratios.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Buckling depends on stiffness (E) and geometry, not on the material's strength.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-20", year: 2024, no: 20, subject: "design", topic: "Names of failure theories",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding theories of failures :<ol><li>Maximum principal strain theory is known as St. Venant's theory.</li><li>Maximum shear strain energy theory is known as Mises and Hencky theory.</li><li>Maximum strain energy theory is known as Guest and Tresca theory.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> Maximum principal strain theory: St Venant.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> Maximum shear strain energy (distortion energy) theory: Huber–von Mises–Hencky.</li><li><b>3 (wrong):</b> The maximum (total) strain energy theory is <i>Haigh's</i>. Guest and Tresca's theory is the maximum <i>shear stress</i> theory.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Full list: maximum principal stress (Rankine), maximum shear stress (Guest–Tresca), maximum principal strain (St Venant), maximum strain energy (Haigh), maximum distortion energy (von Mises–Hencky).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Tresca and von Mises are the two used for ductile metals; Rankine for brittle ones.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-21", year: 2024, no: 21, subject: "mech", topic: "Types of beams",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding beams :<ol><li>Beams with one end fixed and the other end simply supported are known as propped cantilevers.</li><li>Beams supported at more than two sections are known as fixed beams.</li><li>Beams with one end fixed and the other end free are known as cantilevers.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> One end fixed, the other simply supported: propped cantilever.</li><li><b>2 (wrong):</b> A beam on more than two supports is a <i>continuous</i> beam. A fixed (built-in) beam has both ends fixed.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> One end fixed, the other free: cantilever.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Propped cantilevers, fixed beams and continuous beams are statically indeterminate; they are solved with compatibility conditions (for example the three-moment equation for continuous beams).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 3.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> ESE 2021 Q82 asked the same definitions with fixed beam and cantilever swapped.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-22", year: 2024, no: 22, subject: "mech", topic: "Effective length and points of inflection",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding effective length of a column :<ol><li>The effective length is the distance between the points of inflection in the deformed shape of the column, which is referred to as the elastic curve.</li><li>At the inflection point, the moment does not change sign and the member is not expected to resist any moment.</li><li>At the transition point, the curvature is changed and it is called the contraflexure point.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> The effective length is the length between successive inflection points of the buckled shape (elastic curve): the length of an equivalent pin-ended column.</li><li><b>2 (wrong):</b> At an inflection point the bending moment is zero and <i>changes sign</i>; the member resists no moment there. The statement wrongly says it "does not change sign".</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> At this transition point the curvature reverses; it is called the point of contraflexure.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Effective lengths: both ends pinned L; both fixed L/2; one fixed, one pinned L/√2 ≈ 0·7L; one fixed, one free 2L.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 3.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Longer effective length → lower Euler load (∝ 1/L<sub>e</sub>²).</p>` },

{ id: "2024-23", year: 2024, no: 23, subject: "mech", topic: "Power transmitted by a shaft",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding power transmitted through a circular shaft :<ol><li>The stress and deformation induced in the shaft can be calculated by relating power to torque.</li><li>The power produced by a motor is rated in terms of shaft horsepower at a specified rotational speed.</li><li>Power is defined as the work done per unit time.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><div class="formula">P = T ω = 2π N T/60</div>
<p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> From the power and speed one finds the torque, and then the shear stress τ = 16T/πd³ and twist θ = TL/GJ.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> Motors are rated by their shaft power (kW or hp) at a stated speed.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> Power is the rate of doing work.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> For a given power, a shaft running faster carries less torque and can be smaller, which is why high-speed shafts are slimmer and gearboxes increase torque by reducing speed.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> 1 hp = 746 W (British) or 735·5 W (metric).</p>` },

{ id: "2024-24", year: 2024, no: 24, subject: "mech", topic: "Pure shear: stresses on 135° planes",
q: `When a body is acted upon by pure shear stresses on two perpendicular planes, the planes inclined at 135° are subjected to`,
opts: ["a tensile stress of same magnitude with no shear stress on these planes", "a compressive stress of same magnitude with no shear stress on these planes", "a torsional stress of same magnitude with no shear stress on these planes", "a bending stress of same magnitude with no shear stress on these planes"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) a compressive stress of same magnitude with no shear stress on these planes</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> With σ<sub>θ</sub> = τ sin 2θ for pure shear: at θ = 135°, sin 270° = −1, so σ = −τ (compressive), and the shear τ cos 270° = 0.</p>
<p>The two diagonal planes are principal planes: tension τ on the 45° plane (Q16) and compression τ on the 135° plane, with no shear on either.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Tension acts on the 45° plane, not the 135° plane.</li><li><b>(c)</b> "Torsional" and "bending" stresses are not stresses on a plane; it describe loading types.</li><li><b>(d)</b> "Torsional" and "bending" stresses are not stresses on a plane; it describe loading types.</li></ul>
<p>This is why thin-walled tubes in torsion buckle along 45° helical lines (the compressive diagonal), as seen in a twisted drinking can.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Pure shear = equal tension and compression at 45°: σ<sub>1</sub> = +τ, σ<sub>2</sub> = −τ.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-25", year: 2024, no: 25, subject: "materials", topic: "Resilience",
q: `Which one of the following is the ability of a material to regain its original shape on removal of the applied load?`,
opts: ["Proof resilience", "Resilience", "Modulus of resilience", "Gradual resilience"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Resilience</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Resilience is the ability of a material to absorb energy when strained elastically and to give it back, returning to its original shape when the load is removed. (In many texts, the property of regaining shape is called elasticity; among these options, resilience is the term that describes it.)</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Proof resilience is a specific quantity: the maximum strain energy stored up to the elastic limit.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Modulus of resilience is proof resilience per unit volume, σ<sub>e</sub>²/2E.</li><li><b>(d)</b> "Gradual resilience" is not a standard term (loads may be applied gradually, suddenly or with impact).</li></ul>
<p>Strain energy depends on how the load is applied: a suddenly applied load produces twice the stress of the same load applied gradually.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Springs are designed for high resilience: high elastic limit and moderate stiffness.</p>` }
);

// ---- q2024b.js ----
// ESE 2024 Prelims Paper II, Mechanical Engineering, Series A, Q26–50
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2024-26", year: 2024, no: 26, subject: "thermo", topic: "Extensive and intensive properties",
q: `Which one of the following properties is <b>not</b> an extensive property of a system?`,
opts: ["Energy", "Enthalpy", "Volume", "Temperature"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Temperature</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Extensive properties depend on the amount of matter: energy, enthalpy and volume all double if the system is doubled. Temperature does not change when the system is divided, so it is <b>intensive</b>.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Energy is extensive: it is proportional to the mass of the system.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Enthalpy is extensive: it is proportional to the mass.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Volume is extensive: it doubles when the system is doubled.</li></ul>
<p>Intensive properties: temperature, pressure, density, and all specific (per-unit-mass) properties such as v, u, h and s. The ratio of two extensive properties is intensive.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Test: split the system in half. Whatever stays the same is intensive.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-27", year: 2024, no: 27, subject: "thermo", topic: "Mixing of air in two insulated vessels",
q: `Two cylindrical vessels of 2 m³ each are inter-connected through a pipe with a valve in-between. Initially, the valve is closed and one vessel has 20 kg air while 4 kg of air is there in second vessel. Assuming the system to be at 27 °C temperature initially and perfectly insulated, what is the final pressure in vessels after the valve is opened to attain equilibrium?`,
opts: ["516·6 kPa", "51·66 kPa", "561·6 kPa", "56·16 kPa"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 516·6 kPa</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The combined system is rigid and insulated: Q = 0 and W = 0, so its internal energy is constant. For an ideal gas starting at the same temperature in both vessels, the final temperature is also 27 °C (300 K).</p>
<div class="formula">p = m R T/V</div>
<p>p = 24 × 0·287 × 300/4 = 2066·4/4 = <b>516·6 kPa</b>.</p>
<p>Initial pressures: vessel 1 = 20 × 0·287 × 300/2 = 861 kPa; vessel 2 = 172·2 kPa. The final pressure is their volume-weighted average.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>m = 24 kg, V = 4 m³, T = 300 K.</li><li>p = 24 × 0.287 × 300/4 = <b>516.6 kPa</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 51·66 kPa is ten times too small.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 561·6 kPa transposes digits.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 56·16 kPa transposes digits.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> This mixing is irreversible: entropy increases even though temperature and internal energy do not change.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-28", year: 2024, no: 28, subject: "thermo", topic: "Thermocouple principle",
q: `Thermoelectric thermometer works on the principle of`,
opts: ["Wheatstone bridge", "entropy", "Seebeck effect", "ohmmeter"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Seebeck effect</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A thermocouple (thermoelectric thermometer) has two dissimilar metal wires joined at both ends. When the junctions are at different temperatures, an emf proportional to the temperature difference appears in the circuit: the Seebeck effect. With the reference junction at a known temperature (often 0 °C), the measured emf gives the hot-junction temperature.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A Wheatstone bridge is used with resistance thermometers (RTDs, thermistors).</li><li><b>(b)</b> Entropy is a thermodynamic property, not a measuring principle.</li><li><b>(d)</b> An ohmmeter measures resistance.</li></ul>
<p>Related effects: Peltier (heat absorbed or released at a junction when current flows, used in thermoelectric coolers) and Thomson (heat in a single conductor with a temperature gradient).</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Common types: K (chromel–alumel, to 1250 °C), J (iron–constantan), T (copper–constantan), R and S (platinum–rhodium, to 1600 °C).</p>` },

{ id: "2024-29", year: 2024, no: 29, subject: "thermo", topic: "Isentropic efficiency of expansion",
q: `Isentropic expansion efficiency is expressed as`,
opts: ["Ideal work in expansion − Actual work in expansion", "Actual work in expansion + Ideal work in expansion", "Ideal work in expansion/Actual work in expansion", "Actual work in expansion/Ideal work in expansion"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Actual work in expansion/Ideal work in expansion</h3><div class="formula">η<sub>T</sub> = w<sub>actual</sub>/w<sub>isentropic</sub> = (h<sub>1</sub> − h<sub>2</sub>)/(h<sub>1</sub> − h<sub>2s</sub>)</div>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Irreversibilities (friction, turbulence) make the actual work of an expansion device (turbine, nozzle) less than the isentropic work, so the efficiency is the actual over the ideal.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A difference gives the lost work, not an efficiency.</li><li><b>(b)</b> A sum has no meaning here.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Ideal/actual is the form used for <i>compression</i>, where the actual work input exceeds the ideal.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Turbine: actual/ideal; compressor and pump: ideal/actual. Both give a value below 1.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-30", year: 2024, no: 30, subject: "power", topic: "Purpose of reheating",
q: `In the reheat cycle, the principal advantage of reheat is to increase the quality of steam at`,
opts: ["turbine exhaust", "turbine inlet", "feed pump inlet", "feed pump exhaust"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) turbine exhaust</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Raising the boiler pressure improves Rankine efficiency, but makes the steam wetter at the end of expansion; moisture above about 10–12% erodes the low-pressure blades. In reheating, steam partly expanded in the HP turbine is returned to the boiler and reheated at constant pressure, then expanded in the LP turbine. This moves the expansion line to the right on the T–s diagram, so the dryness fraction at the turbine exhaust is much higher.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Steam at the turbine inlet is already superheated; reheat does not change it.</li><li><b>(c)</b> At the feed pump the fluid is liquid water; steam quality is not involved.</li><li><b>(d)</b> At the feed pump the fluid is liquid water; steam quality is not involved.</li></ul>
<p>Reheat also raises net work per kg and, if the reheat temperature is high enough, slightly raises cycle efficiency.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Optimum reheat pressure is usually about 20–25% of the boiler pressure.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-31", year: 2024, no: 31, subject: "power", topic: "Ways to improve Rankine efficiency",
q: `Consider that the Rankine cycle efficiency is improved in the following ways :<ol><li>By reducing heat addition in boiler</li><li>By increasing steam turbine expansion work</li><li>By reducing feed pump work</li></ol>Which of the above are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><div class="formula">η = (W<sub>T</sub> − W<sub>P</sub>)/Q<sub>in</sub></div>
<p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1:</b> Reducing the heat that must be added in the boiler for the same net work (for example by regenerative feed heating, which preheats the feed water with bled steam) raises η.</li><li><b>2:</b> Increasing the turbine work (higher boiler pressure and temperature, lower condenser pressure, reheating) raises the numerator.</li><li><b>3:</b> Reducing pump work also raises net work, though the effect is small because pump work is only about 1% of turbine work.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Each change acts directly on the efficiency formula, with the other terms held constant, so all three are taken as correct.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Practical routes: superheat, reheat, regeneration and higher vacuum in the condenser.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-32", year: 2024, no: 32, subject: "fm", topic: "Absolute pressure from a mercury manometer",
q: `What is the actual pressure of air in the tank if the pressure of compressed air measured by manometer is 30 cm of mercury and atmospheric pressure is 101 kPa? (Take g = 9·78 m/s² and density of mercury at room temperature as 13550 kg/m³)`,
opts: ["140·76 GPa", "140·76 MPa", "140·76 kPa", "140·76 Pa"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 140·76 kPa</h3><div class="formula">p<sub>abs</sub> = p<sub>atm</sub> + ρ g h</div>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Gauge pressure = 13,550 × 9·78 × 0·30 = 39,756 Pa = 39·76 kPa. Absolute pressure = 101 + 39·76 = <b>140·76 kPa</b>.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>p_gauge = 13 550 × 9.78 × 0.30 = 39.76 kPa.</li><li>p_abs = 101 + 39.76 = <b>140.76 kPa</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> GPa Pa have the right digits but the wrong units.</li><li><b>(b)</b> MPa Pa have the right digits but the wrong units.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Pa have the right digits but the wrong units.</li></ul>
<p>A manometer reading is a gauge pressure: the pressure above atmospheric. "Actual" pressure means absolute pressure, so the atmospheric pressure is added.</p>
<p>The manometer liquid's density and the local gravity both matter: using 13,600 kg/m³ and 9·81 m/s² would give 40·0 kPa gauge, only slightly different. 30 cm of mercury is roughly 0·39 atm above atmospheric.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> This is Cengel's example on manometry; local g (9·78 m/s²) is used instead of 9·81.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-33", year: 2024, no: 33, subject: "thermo", topic: "Two-dimensional steady conduction",
q: `The equation for two-dimensional steady-state conduction without heat sources is`,
opts: ["d²T/dr² + q/k = 0", "d²T/dx² + (1/r) dT/dr = 0", "d²T/dx² = 0", "∂²T/∂x² + ∂²T/∂y² = 0"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) ∂²T/∂x² + ∂²T/∂y² = 0</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The general conduction equation is ∇²T + q̇/k = (1/α) ∂T/∂t. For two-dimensional, steady conduction (∂T/∂t = 0) with no heat generation (q̇ = 0) and constant k, it reduces to Laplace's equation in two dimensions.</p><div class="formula">∇²T + q̇/k = (1/α)∂T/∂t</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Steady: ∂T/∂t = 0; no source: q̇ = 0; 2-D.</li><li><b>∂²T/∂x² + ∂²T/∂y² = 0</b> (Laplace).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> is one-dimensional with heat generation (and in r without the cylindrical term).</li><li><b>(b)</b> mixes x and r: it is an incorrect form of the radial (cylindrical) equation.</li><li><b>(c)</b> is one-dimensional steady conduction without generation (a plane wall), whose solution is linear.</li></ul>
<p>Two-dimensional problems are solved by separation of variables, conduction shape factors, flux plotting or numerical finite-difference methods.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Laplace's equation also governs potential flow and electrostatics, which is why analogue methods were once used.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-34", year: 2024, no: 34, subject: "thermo", topic: "Rayleigh number",
q: `Which one of the following expressions is correct about the relation among Grashof number (Gr), Prandtl number (Pr) and Rayleigh number (Ra)?`,
opts: ["Ra = Gr Pr", "Gr = Ra Pr", "Ra = Gr − Pr", "Gr = Ra − Pr"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Ra = Gr Pr</h3><div class="formula">Ra = Gr × Pr = g β ΔT L³/(ν α)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>Gr = gβΔT L³/ν² (buoyancy/viscous forces); Pr = ν/α (momentum/thermal diffusivity)</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Natural-convection correlations are written as Nu = C Ra<sup>n</sup>: n = 1/4 for laminar flow (Ra &lt; 10⁹ on a vertical plate) and n = 1/3 for turbulent flow.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Gr = Ra Pr has Pr on the wrong side (Gr = Ra/Pr).</li><li><b>(c)</b> Dimensionless groups combine by multiplication, not subtraction.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Dimensionless groups combine by multiplication, not subtraction.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Ra ≈ 10⁹ marks the laminar–turbulent transition for free convection on a vertical plate.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-35", year: 2024, no: 35, subject: "thermo", topic: "Mixed convection criterion",
q: `An order-of-magnitude analysis of the free convection boundary layer equations will indicate a general criterion for determining whether free convection effects dominate. The criterion is that when`,
opts: ["Gr/Re² &lt; 10, then free convection is of primary importance", "Gr/Re² = 10, then free convection is of primary importance", "Gr/Re² &gt; 10, then free convection is of primary importance", "Gr/Re² &gt; 10, then forced convection is of primary importance"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Gr/Re² &gt; 10, then free convection is of primary importance</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Gr/Re² (the Richardson number) compares buoyancy forces to inertia forces (Holman):</p>
<ul><li>Gr/Re² ≫ 1 (taken as &gt; 10): free convection dominates.</li><li>Gr/Re² ≪ 1 (&lt; 0·1): forced convection dominates.</li><li>Gr/Re² ≈ 1: mixed convection; both must be considered.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A value below 10 includes the mixed and forced regimes.</li><li><b>(b)</b> A single value (= 10) cannot define a regime.</li><li><b>(d)</b> reverses the conclusion.</li></ul>
<div class="formula">Ri = Gr/Re² = g β ΔT L/V²</div>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Low air speeds over hot surfaces (for example electronic cooling) often fall in the mixed regime.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-36", year: 2024, no: 36, subject: "thermo", topic: "Gray body",
q: `If the monochromatic emissivity of a body is independent of wavelength, it is called`,
opts: ["transparent body", "reflective body", "black body", "gray body"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) gray body</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A gray body has a monochromatic (spectral) emissivity ε<sub>λ</sub> that is constant across all wavelengths and less than 1. Its emissive power is then a fixed fraction ε of the black-body emissive power at every wavelength: E = εσT⁴.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A transparent body transmits radiation (τ = 1).</li><li><b>(b)</b> A reflective (white, specular) body reflects radiation (ρ = 1).</li><li><b>(c)</b> A black body has ε<sub>λ</sub> = 1 at all wavelengths; it is the special limiting case of a gray body with ε = 1.</li></ul>
<p>Real surfaces are not exactly gray (for example white paint absorbs little visible light but much infrared), but the gray-body model simplifies radiation exchange calculations greatly.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> A body whose ε<sub>λ</sub> varies with wavelength is called a real (or selective) surface.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-37", year: 2024, no: 37, subject: "thermo", topic: "Kirchhoff's identity", dropped: true,
q: `Which one of the following equations is called Kirchhoff's identity?`,
opts: ["ε = E/E<sub>b</sub>", "ε = E<sub>b</sub>/E", "ε = ∞", "ε = 1"], ans: null,
analysis: `<h3>Dropped by UPSC (not scored)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> UPSC dropped this item. Kirchhoff's identity (Kirchhoff's law of thermal radiation) is usually stated as <b>ε = α</b>: at thermal equilibrium, the emissivity of a surface equals its absorptivity. None of the options states this.</p>
<p><b>The options</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> ε = E/E<sub>b</sub> is the <i>definition</i> of emissivity (emissive power relative to a black body at the same temperature). Combined with Kirchhoff's law it gives E/α = E<sub>b</sub>, which some texts call Kirchhoff's identity, so this option is the nearest.</li><li><b>(b)</b> ε = E<sub>b</sub>/E is inverted (it would exceed 1).</li><li><b>(c)</b> ε = ∞ is impossible; ε lies between 0 and 1.</li><li><b>(d)</b> ε = 1 applies only to a black body.</li></ul>
<p>The ambiguity between the definition of emissivity and Kirchhoff's law (ε = α) is the likely reason for the drop.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Kirchhoff: good absorbers are good emitters. A black body is both the best absorber and the best emitter.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-38", year: 2024, no: 38, subject: "materials", topic: "Rhombohedral crystal system",
q: `The interface angles of rhombohedral crystal systems are`,
opts: ["α = β = γ ≠ 90°", "α = β = γ = 90°", "α = β = 90° ≠ γ", "α = β = 90°; γ = 120°"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) α = β = γ ≠ 90°</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The rhombohedral (trigonal) system has a = b = c, with all three interaxial angles equal but not 90°: α = β = γ ≠ 90°. Its unit cell is a cube stretched or squashed along a body diagonal.</p><div class="formula">Rhombohedral: a = b = c, α = β = γ ≠ 90°</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Equal axes, equal angles not 90° ⇒ option (a).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> α = β = γ = 90° describes cubic, tetragonal and orthorhombic cells (different edge-length conditions).</li><li><b>(c)</b> α = β = 90° ≠ γ (with a ≠ b ≠ c) is monoclinic in some conventions.</li><li><b>(d)</b> α = β = 90°, γ = 120° (a = b ≠ c) is hexagonal.</li></ul>
<p>Seven crystal systems: cubic, tetragonal, orthorhombic, rhombohedral (trigonal), hexagonal, monoclinic and triclinic; together with lattice centrings they give 14 Bravais lattices.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Examples of rhombohedral structures: calcite, bismuth, antimony and α-alumina (in some descriptions).</p>` },

{ id: "2024-39", year: 2024, no: 39, subject: "materials", topic: "Properties of ionic compounds",
q: `Consider the following statements for the characteristics of ionic compounds :<ol><li>Ionic compounds are generally crystalline in nature and rigid.</li><li>They are generally non-conductors of electricity.</li><li>They are insoluble in organic solvents, but highly soluble in water.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> Strong, non-directional electrostatic attraction holds ions in a regular, rigid crystal lattice (hard, brittle, high melting point; NaCl melts at 801 °C).</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> In the solid, ions are fixed in place, so ionic solids do not conduct electricity. (They do conduct when molten or dissolved, as the ions become mobile.)</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> Polar water molecules surround and separate the ions (hydration), so many ionic compounds dissolve in water; non-polar organic solvents cannot do this.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Some ionic compounds (for example CaCO<sub>3</sub>, AgCl) are only sparingly soluble in water, but the general statement holds.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Ionic ceramics such as MgO and Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub> are good electrical insulators for the same reason.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-40", year: 2024, no: 40, subject: "materials", topic: "Properties of covalent compounds",
q: `Consider the following statements for the characteristics of covalent compounds :<ol><li>They can exist in all states of matter.</li><li>They are generally electric insulators.</li><li>They are insoluble in water, but soluble in non-polar solvents such as benzene and alcohol, etc.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 2 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> Covalent compounds exist as gases (CH<sub>4</sub>, CO<sub>2</sub>), liquids (water, benzene) and solids (sugar, diamond, polymers), because the forces between their molecules range from very weak to very strong.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> With electrons localised in bonds and no free ions, they are generally electrical insulators (graphite is a notable exception).</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> "Like dissolves like": non-polar covalent compounds dissolve in non-polar solvents (benzene, carbon tetrachloride) and are generally insoluble in water.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Statement 3 lists alcohol as a non-polar solvent, though alcohols are fairly polar; the general idea of the textbook statement is accepted.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Network covalent solids (diamond, SiC) are very hard and have very high melting points.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-41", year: 2024, no: 41, subject: "materials", topic: "Properties of X-rays",
q: `Consider the following statements :<ol><li>X-rays are not deflected by electric or magnetic fields.</li><li>They produce fluorescence in many substances.</li><li>They are highly penetrating and destructive on living tissues on excessive exposure.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> X-rays are electromagnetic waves carrying no charge, so electric and magnetic fields do not deflect them (unlike cathode rays).</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> They make many substances fluoresce (zinc sulphide, barium platinocyanide); this is how fluoroscopy screens work, and how Röntgen discovered them.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> They penetrate matter (less dense matter more easily) and ionise living tissue; overexposure damages cells and DNA.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Other properties: they travel in straight lines at the speed of light, ionise gases, blacken photographic film, and are diffracted by crystals (Bragg's law).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> X-ray wavelengths (0·01–10 nm) are similar to interatomic spacings, which makes crystal diffraction possible.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-42", year: 2024, no: 42, subject: "materials", topic: "Applications of X-rays in research",
q: `Consider the following statements :<br>The application of X-rays in scientific research is<ol><li>to investigate the structure of crystalline solids</li><li>to study complex organic compounds by analyzing their structures</li><li>to find the atomic numbers and energy levels to identify the elements</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 2 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> X-ray diffraction reveals crystal structures, lattice parameters, phases and residual stresses (Bragg).</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> X-ray crystallography determines the structure of complex organic and biological molecules, for example penicillin and vitamin B<sub>12</sub> (Hodgkin), and DNA (Franklin's diffraction photographs).</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> Characteristic X-ray spectra depend on atomic number (Moseley's law, √f ∝ Z), so they identify elements and reveal inner-shell energy levels; this is the basis of X-ray fluorescence and EDS analysis.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Engineering uses include radiography (finding defects in castings and welds) and X-ray stress measurement.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Moseley's law led to ordering the periodic table by atomic number rather than atomic mass.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-43", year: 2024, no: 43, subject: "materials", topic: "Effects of molybdenum in steel",
q: `Consider the following statements :<br>The influence of molybdenum element in steels<ol><li>imparts higher temperature strength</li><li>enhances resistance to creep</li><li>minimizes temperature brittleness</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 2 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> Molybdenum forms stable carbides and strengthens ferrite, giving strength at high temperatures (hot hardness).</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> It is the main alloying element for creep resistance (for example Cr–Mo boiler and steam-pipe steels such as 2¼Cr–1Mo).</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> It reduces temper embrittlement (the brittleness that develops when some steels are tempered in or slowly cooled through 375–575 °C).</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Molybdenum also raises hardenability strongly (see ESE 2021 Q86), improves pitting resistance in stainless steels (316 grade) and helps high-speed steels resist softening.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 2, 3, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Cr–Mo steels: high-temperature pressure vessels, piping and turbine casings.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-44", year: 2024, no: 44, subject: "materials", topic: "Tungsten high-speed steels",
q: `The alloying elements of tungsten-based tool steels are`,
opts: ["Cr, V, Co and W", "Cr and V only", "Cr, Ni and Mo", "Cr, W, V and Mo"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Cr, V, Co and W</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Tungsten (T-series) high-speed steels are alloyed with tungsten, chromium, vanadium and, in the super high-speed grades, cobalt. The classic 18-4-1 HSS (T1) contains 18% W, 4% Cr and 1% V; T4 to T15 add 5–12% Co for extra hot hardness.</p>
<p><b>Role of each element</b></p><ul><li>W: hard carbides and red-hardness (keeps hardness at cutting temperatures up to about 600 °C).</li><li>Cr: hardenability.</li><li>V: very hard vanadium carbides for wear resistance, and grain refinement.</li><li>Co: raises the temperature at which the steel softens.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Cr and V alone omit the defining tungsten.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Cr, Ni, Mo is a typical engineering alloy steel combination; nickel is not used in HSS.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Cr, W, V and Mo describes the <i>molybdenum</i> (M-series) high-speed steels, such as M2 (6W–5Mo–4Cr–2V).</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> M-series HSS are now more common than T-series because molybdenum is cheaper and lighter than tungsten.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-45", year: 2024, no: 45, subject: "materials", topic: "Disadvantages of the Laue method",
q: `Consider the following disadvantages of LAUE methods :<ol><li>Various reflections may make the appearance of the photograph complicated.</li><li>Due to the wide range of wavelengths, there is overlapping of diffraction images.</li><li>There is a variation of incident X-ray beam due to the large range of wavelengths.</li></ol>Which of the above disadvantages is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 3 only", "1 and 2 only", "3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The Laue method shines white (polychromatic) X-radiation on a stationary single crystal; each set of planes picks out the wavelength that satisfies Bragg's law and produces a spot.</p>
<p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1:</b> Many reflections appear at once, making the photograph complicated to interpret.</li><li><b>2:</b> Because a continuous range of wavelengths is used, different orders (and planes) can reflect to the same spot, so diffraction images overlap and d-spacings cannot be found directly.</li><li><b>3:</b> The incident intensity varies across the wavelength range of the white spectrum, so spot intensities cannot be compared simply.</li></ul>
<p>The Laue method is excellent for finding crystal orientation and symmetry, but not for measuring lattice parameters; the rotating-crystal and powder (Debye–Scherrer) methods, which use monochromatic X-rays, are used for that.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 1, 2, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Laue: white X-rays, fixed θ; rotating crystal: monochromatic, variable θ; powder: monochromatic, random orientations.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-46", year: 2024, no: 46, subject: "thermo", topic: "Optical pyrometer",
q: `Optical pyrometer works based on the principle of`,
opts: ["conduction only", "convection only", "radiation", "conduction and convection"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) radiation</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> An optical (disappearing-filament) pyrometer compares the brightness of the hot body's thermal radiation, at a narrow wavelength band (red light, about 0·65 μm), with that of a calibrated lamp filament. When the filament "disappears" against the image of the body, their temperatures match. No contact is needed.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Conduction-based instruments (thermocouples, RTDs) must touch the object.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Convection carries heat by fluid motion; it plays no part in a pyrometer's measurement.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Convection carries heat by fluid motion; it plays no part in a pyrometer's measurement.</li></ul>
<p>Optical pyrometers are used for very hot objects (about 700–3000 °C) such as molten metal, furnaces and kilns, where contact sensors would melt. The reading depends on the emissivity of the target, so a correction is applied for non-black surfaces.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Total-radiation pyrometers use the Stefan–Boltzmann law over all wavelengths; optical pyrometers use Planck's law at one wavelength.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-47", year: 2024, no: 47, subject: "materials", topic: "Heat treatment defects",
q: `Consider the following common defects in steel due to heat treatment :<ol><li>Warping</li><li>Oxidation</li><li>Quenching cracks</li></ol>Which of the above defects are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 Warping (distortion):</b> uneven heating or cooling and the volume change on transformation (martensite is about 4% larger than austenite) bend or twist parts, especially thin or non-symmetric ones.</li><li><b>2 Oxidation (scaling) and decarburisation:</b> heating in air forms scale on the surface and removes carbon from it, leaving a soft skin.</li><li><b>3 Quench cracks:</b> severe quenching creates high thermal and transformation stresses, which can crack the part, especially at sharp corners and changes in section.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Other defects: soft spots (uneven quenching), overheating and burning (grain growth or melting at grain boundaries), and residual stresses.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Remedies: controlled-atmosphere or salt-bath furnaces, milder quenchants (oil, polymer), martempering and generous radii.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-48", year: 2024, no: 48, subject: "mfg", topic: "Conditions for powder metallurgy",
q: `Consider the following conditions to be fulfilled before a powder metallurgy product can be made :<ol><li>It must be possible to form a continuously bonded matrix.</li><li>The metal in the powder form must be able to respond to solid phase welding.</li><li>The powders in which the basic materials are available must be sufficiently close packing under pressure.</li></ol>Which of the above conditions are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For a sound powder-metallurgy part:</p>
<ul><li><b>1:</b> The particles must form a continuous bonded matrix after sintering, giving the part its strength.</li><li><b>2:</b> The powder must respond to solid-state welding (diffusion bonding) during sintering below its melting point.</li><li><b>3:</b> The powder must pack closely under compaction pressure, to give an adequate green density and a dimensionally accurate part.</li></ul>
<p>Main steps: powder production (atomisation, reduction, electrolysis) → blending → compaction in dies → sintering (typically at 70–90% of the melting temperature) → secondary operations (sizing, infiltration, impregnation).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> P/M suits refractory metals (W, Mo), self-lubricating bearings (porous bronze), cemented carbides and high-volume small parts.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-49", year: 2024, no: 49, subject: "materials", topic: "Fatigue, creep and the Larson–Miller parameter",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding mechanical properties of metals :<ol><li>The phenomenon leading to fracture under repeated stresses having a maximum value less than the ultimate strength of material is called creep.</li><li>A time-temperature parameter used to predict stress rupture due to creep is called Larson-Miller parameter.</li><li>Time-dependent deformation of a material when subjected to a constant load or stress is called fatigue.</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 only", "3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (wrong):</b> Fracture under repeated (fluctuating) stresses below the ultimate strength is <i>fatigue</i>.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> The Larson–Miller parameter combines temperature and rupture time to predict creep-rupture life: LMP = T(C + log t<sub>r</sub>), with T in kelvin and C ≈ 20.</li><li><b>3 (wrong):</b> Time-dependent deformation under constant load or stress is <i>creep</i>. Statements 1 and 3 have fatigue and creep swapped.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The Larson–Miller parameter lets short, high-temperature tests predict long-term life at lower temperatures.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 2.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 1, 3, which are incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Creep matters above about 0·4 T<sub>m</sub> (absolute melting temperature).</p>` },

{ id: "2024-50", year: 2024, no: 50, subject: "materials", topic: "Slip, Hall–Petch and superplasticity",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding mechanical properties of metals :<ol><li>The process of atoms moving over each other during the permanent deformation of a metal is called slip.</li><li>An empirical equation that relates the strength of a metal to its grain size is known as Hall-Petch relationship.</li><li>The ability of some metals to deform plastically by 1000–2000 percent at high temperatures and low loading rates is called super-plasticity.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> Slip is the movement of planes of atoms over one another (by dislocation motion) during plastic deformation.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> Hall–Petch: σ<sub>y</sub> = σ<sub>0</sub> + k d<sup>−1/2</sup>; finer grains give higher yield strength because grain boundaries block dislocations.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> Superplastic alloys (very fine-grained, for example Ti–6Al–4V, Zn–22Al, some Al–Li alloys) can stretch by hundreds or thousands of per cent at high temperature (about 0·5 T<sub>m</sub>) and low strain rates.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Superplastic forming is used to make complex aerospace panels in one piece.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Grain refinement is the only strengthening method that also improves toughness.</p>` }
);

// ---- q2024c.js ----
// ESE 2024 Prelims Paper II, Mechanical Engineering, Series A, Q51–75
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2024-51", year: 2024, no: 51, subject: "icrac", topic: "Brake mean effective pressure of a diesel engine",
q: `The Rolls-Royce CV12 turbocharged four-stroke direct injection diesel engine has a displacement of 26·1 litres. The engine has a maximum output of 900 kW at 2300 r.p.m. What is the brake mean effective pressure?`,
opts: ["18 bar", "15 bar", "10·15 bar", "21 bar"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 18 bar</h3><div class="formula">bmep = P n<sub>R</sub>/(V<sub>d</sub> N), with n<sub>R</sub> = 2 for a four-stroke engine</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>P = 900 kW; V<sub>d</sub> = 26·1 L = 0·0261 m³ (total displacement); N = 2300/60 = 38·33 rev/s</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> bmep = 900 × 10³ × 2/(0·0261 × 38·33) = 1·8 × 10⁶ Pa = <b>18 bar</b>.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>N = 2300/60 = 38.33 rev/s.</li><li>bmep = 900 × 10³ × 2/(0.0261 × 38.33) = 1.8 × 10⁶ Pa = <b>18 bar</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(c)</b> 10·15 bar would correspond to lower power or the two-stroke formula wrongly applied with other errors (the two-stroke value would be 9 bar).</li><li><b>(b)</b> 15 bar would correspond to lower power or the two-stroke formula wrongly applied with other errors (the two-stroke value would be 9 bar).</li><li><b>(d)</b> 21 bar overstates the result.</li></ul>
<p>A bmep of 18 bar is typical of a highly turbocharged diesel; naturally aspirated diesels reach about 7–9 bar.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> This is Heywood's example of a tank engine (Challenger 2).</p>` },

{ id: "2024-52", year: 2024, no: 52, subject: "icrac", topic: "Overall (brake thermal) efficiency",
q: `A high-performance four-stroke SI engine has a compression ratio 10 : 1. The ideal air standard Otto cycle efficiency is 0·602. The indicated efficiency is 55 percent of the corresponding ideal air standard Otto cycle efficiency. The engine mechanical efficiency is 85 percent, and the volumetric efficiency is 90 percent. The calorific value of the fuel is 44 MJ/kg. What is the engine arbitrary overall efficiency?`,
opts: ["26·1%", "28·1%", "29·5%", "30·5%"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 28·1%</h3><div class="formula">η<sub>overall</sub> = η<sub>Otto</sub> × (relative efficiency) × η<sub>mech</sub></div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Indicated thermal efficiency = 0·55 × 0·602 = 0·331.</li><li>Brake (overall) efficiency = 0·331 × 0·85 = <b>0·281 = 28·1%</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The volumetric efficiency affects how much charge (and so power) the engine takes in, not how efficiently it converts fuel energy; the calorific value is needed only for fuel consumption.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 26·1% multiplies by the volumetric efficiency too (0·281 × 0·9 = 25·3%, near (a)).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 29·5% omits or misapply the mechanical efficiency.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 30·5% omits or misapply the mechanical efficiency.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Brake specific fuel consumption = 3600/(η × CV) = 3600/(0·281 × 44,000) = 0·291 kg/kWh.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-53", year: 2024, no: 53, subject: "icrac", topic: "Refrigeration capacity from condenser heat balance",
q: `The performance test of an air-conditioning unit rated as 140·7 kW (40 TR) seems to be indicating poor cooling. The test on heat rejection to atmosphere in its condenser shows the following : Cooling water flow rate : 4 L/s; Water temperatures : In 30 °C; Out 40 °C; Power input to motor : 48 kW (95% efficiency). What is the actual refrigeration capacity of the unit? (The specific heat capacity of water is 4·1868 kJ/kg-K)`,
opts: ["101·5 kW", "167·4 kW", "121·9 kW", "189·3 kW"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 121·9 kW</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> (This repeats ESE 2022 Q97, with the answer asked in kW.)</p>
<div class="formula">Q<sub>evaporator</sub> = Q<sub>condenser</sub> − W<sub>compressor</sub></div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Q<sub>c</sub> = 4 × 4·1868 × (40 − 30) = 167·47 kW.</li><li>W = 48 × 0·95 = 45·6 kW (work delivered to the compressor).</li><li>Q<sub>e</sub> = 167·47 − 45·6 = <b>121·9 kW</b> (34·7 TR, compared with the rated 40 TR).</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 167·4 kW is the condenser heat rejection itself.</li><li><b>(a)</b> 101·5 kW subtracts or add the wrong work.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 189·3 kW subtracts or add the wrong work.</li></ul>
<p>The unit's actual COP = 121·9/45·6 = 2·67, and it delivers only 87% of its rated capacity. Typical causes are an undercharged system, a fouled condenser or evaporator, or air in the system.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> The condenser rejects the evaporator heat plus the compressor work.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-54", year: 2024, no: 54, subject: "icrac", topic: "Specific humidity",
q: `If the partial pressure of water vapour for the mixture of dry air and water vapour is 12·79 mm Hg and its total pressure is 736 mm Hg, then what is the specific humidity?`,
opts: ["0·068 kg w.v./kg d.a.", "0·011 kg w.v./kg d.a.", "0·023 kg w.v./kg d.a.", "0·044 kg w.v./kg d.a."], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 0·011 kg w.v./kg d.a.</h3><div class="formula">ω = 0·622 p<sub>v</sub>/(p − p<sub>v</sub>)</div>
<p><b>Concept:</b> ω = 0·622 × 12·79/(736 − 12·79) = 7·955/723·21 = <b>0·0110 kg/kg dry air</b>.</p>
<p>The pressures can stay in mm Hg because only their ratio appears.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>ω = 0.622 × 12.79/(736 − 12.79).</li><li>= 7.955/723.21 = <b>0.011 kg/kg da</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(c)</b> 0·023 is about twice the correct value; times.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0·044 four times.</li><li><b>(a)</b> 0·068 is far too humid for these conditions.</li></ul>
<p>Specific humidity (humidity ratio) is the mass of water vapour per kg of dry air. It stays constant during sensible heating or cooling, which is why it is a convenient coordinate on the psychrometric chart.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> 12·79 mm Hg is the saturation pressure of water at 15 °C, so this air is saturated at 15 °C (dew point 15 °C).</p>` },

{ id: "2024-55", year: 2024, no: 55, subject: "icrac", topic: "Adiabatic compression work",
q: `If 1 m³ of a gas is compressed adiabatically (the ratio of specific heats = 1·4) from 1 bar to 5 bar in a reciprocating compressor, what is the work of compression?`,
opts: ["192543 N-m", "245361 N-m", "158643 N-m", "204050 N-m"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 204050 N-m</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For a reciprocating compressor (steady-flow work, including suction and delivery):</p>
<div class="formula">W = [γ/(γ − 1)] p<sub>1</sub>V<sub>1</sub> [(p<sub>2</sub>/p<sub>1</sub>)<sup>(γ−1)/γ</sup> − 1]</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>γ = 1·4; p<sub>1</sub> = 10⁵ Pa; V<sub>1</sub> = 1 m³; p<sub>2</sub>/p<sub>1</sub> = 5</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>(γ − 1)/γ = 0·2857; 5<sup>0·2857</sup> = 1·5838.</li><li>W = 3·5 × 10⁵ × 0·5838 = 2·043 × 10⁵ N m ≈ <b>204,050 N m</b> (about 204 kJ).</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(c)</b> 158,643 N m and similar values come from the closed-system (non-flow) formula p<sub>1</sub>V<sub>1</sub>[(r)<sup>0·286</sup> − 1]/(γ − 1) = 146 kJ, which omits the flow work.</li><li><b>(a)</b> 192,543 ms do not match either formula.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 245,361 N ms do not match either formula.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Isothermal compression between the same pressures would need only p<sub>1</sub>V<sub>1</sub> ln 5 = 161 kJ.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-56", year: 2024, no: 56, subject: "icrac", topic: "Thermostatic expansion valve: superheat setting",
q: `An R134a thermostatic expansion valve, not equipped with an external equalizer, has a superheat setting of 7 °C, while supplying the refrigerant to the evaporator at 0 °C. The power fluid is same as refrigerant. What is the difference in pressure in opposite sides of the diaphragm required to open the valve? (Consider evaporator pressure at 0 °C and 7 °C as 2·928 bar and 3·748 bar respectively)`,
opts: ["2·928 bar", "3·748 bar", "0·82 bar", "6·676 bar"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 0·82 bar</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In a thermostatic expansion valve, the bulb (charged with the same refrigerant) senses the suction-line temperature. Its pressure acts on top of the diaphragm; the evaporator pressure (internally equalised) acts underneath, together with the spring.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Evaporator at 0 °C: pressure below the diaphragm = 2·928 bar.</li><li>With 7 °C superheat, the bulb is at 7 °C: pressure above = 3·748 bar.</li><li>Difference = 3·748 − 2·928 = <b>0·82 bar</b>, which the spring is set to balance; any greater superheat opens the valve further.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 2·928 bar is the individual pressures.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 3·748 bar is the individual pressures.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 6·676 bar is their sum, which has no meaning here.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> An external equaliser is needed when the evaporator pressure drop is large, so that the valve senses the outlet pressure.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-57", year: 2024, no: 57, subject: "icrac", topic: "Carnot refrigerator COP",
q: `A machine working on a Carnot cycle operates between 305 K and 260 K. What is the COP when it is operated as a refrigerating machine?`,
opts: ["5·78", "4·35", "1·17", "6·78"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 5·78</h3><div class="formula">COP<sub>R</sub> = T<sub>L</sub>/(T<sub>H</sub> − T<sub>L</sub>)</div>
<p><b>Concept:</b> COP<sub>R</sub> = 260/(305 − 260) = 260/45 = <b>5·78</b>.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>COP = 260/(305 − 260).</li><li>= 260/45 = <b>5.78</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(d)</b> 6·78 is the COP as a heat pump: T<sub>H</sub>/(T<sub>H</sub> − T<sub>L</sub>) = 305/45 = COP<sub>R</sub> + 1.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 1·17 is the temperature ratio 305/260.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 4.35 would need T_L ≈ 255 K.</li></ul>
<p>As a heat engine between the same temperatures, the efficiency would be 1 − 260/305 = 14·8%.</p>
<p>The Carnot COP is the upper limit for any refrigerator between these temperatures. Real vapour-compression machines achieve roughly 40–60% of it.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> COP<sub>HP</sub> = COP<sub>R</sub> + 1 for the same machine.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-58", year: 2024, no: 58, subject: "icrac", topic: "Cold storage capacity",
q: `A cold storage plant is required to store fish. The fish is supplied at a temperature of 30 °C. The fish is stored in cold storage which is maintained at −8 °C. If the plant requires 75 kW to drive it, what is the capacity of the plant? (Assume actual COP of the plant as 0·3 of the Carnot COP)`,
opts: ["40·8 TR", "35·9 TR", "44·8 TR", "54·6 TR"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 44·8 TR</h3><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Carnot COP between 303 K and 265 K: 265/38 = 6·97.</li><li>Actual COP = 0·3 × 6·97 = 2·09.</li><li>Capacity = 2·09 × 75 = 156·9 kW.</li><li>In tons: 156·9/3·5 = <b>44·8 TR</b> (taking 1 TR = 3·5 kW, as many Indian texts do; with 3·517 kW it is 44·6 TR).</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The temperatures used are those of the warm surroundings (the fish supply at 30 °C) and the cold space (−8 °C).</p><div class="formula">COP_Carnot = T_L/(T_H − T_L)</div><div class="formula">Capacity = COP × W, 1 TR = 3.5 kW</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 40·8 uses T<sub>L</sub> = 273 K or 1 TR = 3·9 kW.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 35·9 TR uses T<sub>L</sub> = 273 K or 1 TR = 3·9 kW.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 54·6 TR omits the 0·3 factor partly.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Rajput's version of this example uses 1 TR = 210 kJ/min = 3·5 kW.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-59", year: 2024, no: 59, subject: "icrac", topic: "Relative and specific humidity",
q: `On a particular day, the atmospheric air records a partial pressure of water vapour 9·62 mm Hg and the saturation pressure of vapour is 31·8 mm Hg. The barometric pressure is observed to be 756 mm Hg. What are the relative humidity and the specific humidity respectively?`,
opts: ["30·25%, 0·008", "30·25%, 0·014", "35·87%, 0·014", "35·87%, 0·008"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 30·25%, 0·008</h3><div class="formula">φ = p<sub>v</sub>/p<sub>s</sub> &nbsp;&nbsp; ω = 0·622 p<sub>v</sub>/(p − p<sub>v</sub>)</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>φ = 9·62/31·8 = <b>30·25%</b>.</li><li>ω = 0·622 × 9·62/(756 − 9·62) = 5·984/746·38 = <b>0·0080 kg/kg dry air</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> pairs the right φ with a wrong ω.</li><li><b>(c)</b> has no basis from these pressures.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 35·87% has no basis from these pressures.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> 31·8 mm Hg is the saturation pressure at about 30 °C, so this is warm, fairly dry air (dew point about 10·6 °C).</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Relative humidity compares with saturation at the same temperature; specific humidity is the actual mass ratio.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-60", year: 2024, no: 60, subject: "icrac", topic: "Condenser water flow",
q: `A small water-cooled condenser uses mains water at 13 °C and heats this to 24 °C before it goes to waste. The evaporator duty is 4·2 kW and the motor output is 1·7 kW. What is the water mass flow?`,
opts: ["0·09 kg/s", "0·19 kg/s", "0·21 kg/s", "0·13 kg/s"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 0·13 kg/s</h3><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Heat rejected in the condenser = evaporator duty + compressor work = 4·2 + 1·7 = 5·9 kW.</li><li>ṁ = Q/(c ΔT) = 5·9/(4·187 × 11) = <b>0·128 kg/s</b> ≈ 0·13 kg/s.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0·09 kg/s uses only the evaporator duty (4·2/46·1 = 0·091).</li><li><b>(b)</b> 0·19 uses a smaller temperature rise.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0·21 kg/s uses a smaller temperature rise.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Assumes all motor output reaches the refrigerant as compression work (a hermetic compressor, where motor losses also go into the refrigerant, would add even more heat).</p><div class="formula">Q_cond = Q_evap + W</div><div class="formula">ṁ = Q/(cΔT)</div>
<p><b>Remember:</b> This is a Trott and Welch (<i>Refrigeration and Air-Conditioning</i>) worked example.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-61", year: 2024, no: 61, subject: "icrac", topic: "Water flow in an air heater coil",
q: `Air circulates at the rate of 68 kg/s and is to be heated from 16 °C to 34 °C. What is the water mass flow for an air heater coil having hot water entering at 85 °C and leaving at 74 °C? (Take specific heat of air at constant pressure as 1·02 kJ/kg-K and specific heat of water as 4·187 kJ/kg-K)`,
opts: ["19 kg/s", "17 kg/s", "27 kg/s", "14 kg/s"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 27 kg/s</h3><div class="formula">ṁ<sub>a</sub> c<sub>pa</sub> ΔT<sub>a</sub> = ṁ<sub>w</sub> c<sub>w</sub> ΔT<sub>w</sub></div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Heat to air = 68 × 1·02 × (34 − 16) = 1248·5 kW.</li><li>Water flow = 1248·5/(4·187 × 11) = <b>27·1 kg/s</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The specific heat of 1·02 kJ/kg K for "air" includes the moisture in it (humid specific heat); dry air alone is 1·005.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 19 uses the wrong temperature differences (for example the inlet water to inlet air difference).</li><li><b>(b)</b> 17 uses the wrong temperature differences (for example the inlet water to inlet air difference).</li><li><b>(d)</b> 14 kg/s uses the wrong temperature differences (for example the inlet water to inlet air difference).</li></ul>
<p>The heat duty here is 1·25 MW, and the coil must be sized for an LMTD based on the water (85 → 74 °C) and air (16 → 34 °C) temperatures: for counter flow, ΔT<sub>1</sub> = 51 °C and ΔT<sub>2</sub> = 58 °C, LMTD ≈ 54·4 °C.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Heat lost by the water = heat gained by the air (no losses to surroundings).</p>` },

{ id: "2024-62", year: 2024, no: 62, subject: "icrac", topic: "Fuel injected per cycle per cylinder",
q: `A six-cylinder four-stroke diesel engine develops 125 kW at 3000 r.p.m. Its brake-specific consumption is 200 gm/kWh. What is the quantity of fuel to be injected per cycle per cylinder? (The specific gravity of the fuel is 0·85)`,
opts: ["0·0726 cc/cycle", "0·0379 cc/cycle", "0·0981 cc/cycle", "0·0545 cc/cycle"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 0·0545 cc/cycle</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Fuel injected per cycle per cylinder.</p><div class="formula">Fuel rate = P × bsfc</div><div class="formula">Injections/s = cylinders × N/(2 × 60)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Fuel consumption = 125 × 0·2 = 25 kg/h = 6·944 × 10<sup>−3</sup> kg/s.</li><li>Injections per second: 6 cylinders × (3000/2)/60 = 150 per second (one injection per cylinder every two revolutions).</li><li>Fuel per injection = 6·944 × 10<sup>−3</sup>/150 = 4·63 × 10<sup>−5</sup> kg = 0·0463 g.</li><li>Volume = 0·0463/0·85 = <b>0·0545 cm³ per cycle</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 0.0379 cc would need about 8.7 kg/h less fuel than stated.</li><li><b>(a)</b> 0·0726 uses fewer cylinders or ignore the density.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0·0981 cc uses fewer cylinders or ignore the density.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Injected quantities per cycle are tiny (tens of mm³), so injection systems must be very precise.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-63", year: 2024, no: 63, subject: "tom", topic: "Time ratio of a slotted-lever quick-return mechanism",
q: `In a crank and slotted lever quick return motion mechanism, the distance between the fixed centres is 240 mm and the length of the driving crank is 120 mm. What is the time ratio of cutting stroke to return stroke?`,
opts: ["1", "0·5", "2", "3"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 2</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> At the two extreme positions of the slotted lever, the crank is perpendicular to the lever. The angle β turned by the crank during the return stroke satisfies:</p>
<div class="formula">cos(β/2) = crank radius/distance between fixed centres = 120/240 = 0·5</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>β/2 = 60° → β = 120° (return stroke).</li><li>Cutting stroke angle α = 360° − 120° = 240°.</li><li>Time ratio = α/β = 240/120 = <b>2</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 1 would mean equal strokes (no quick return).</li><li><b>(b)</b> 0·5 is the inverse ratio (return to cutting).</li><li><b>(d)</b> 3 would need cos(β/2) = cos 45° = 0·707.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> The crank turns uniformly, so the time ratio equals the ratio of crank angles.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-64", year: 2024, no: 64, subject: "tom", topic: "Ideal mechanical advantage of a four-bar chain",
q: `ABCD is a four-bar chain in which link AD is the driver, link BC is the driven, and DC is fixed. The driver rotates uniformly at a speed of 120 r.p.m. and the constant driving torque is 50 N-m. At a particular phase of the mechanism, the angular velocity of the driven link is 2 rad/s. What is the ideal mechanical advantage?`,
opts: ["5·26", "6·28", "8·98", "10"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 6·28</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For an ideal (frictionless) mechanism, input power = output power: T<sub>in</sub>ω<sub>in</sub> = T<sub>out</sub>ω<sub>out</sub>. So the mechanical advantage T<sub>out</sub>/T<sub>in</sub> equals the velocity ratio ω<sub>in</sub>/ω<sub>out</sub>.</p>
<div class="formula">MA = ω<sub>driver</sub>/ω<sub>driven</sub></div>
<p>ω<sub>driver</sub> = 2π × 120/60 = 12·566 rad/s → MA = 12·566/2 = <b>6·28</b>. The output torque at this instant is 50 × 6·28 = 314 N m.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>ω_in = 2π × 120/60 = 12.566 rad/s.</li><li>MA = 12.566/2 = <b>6.28</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 5·26 does not equal 12·566/2.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 8·98 does not equal 12·566/2.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 10 does not equal 12·566/2.</li></ul>
<p>The mechanical advantage varies through the cycle and becomes infinite at the toggle positions, where the driven link momentarily stops.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Toggle mechanisms (stone crushers, riveters) exploit this huge mechanical advantage.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-65", year: 2024, no: 65, subject: "tom", topic: "Oldham coupling sliding speed",
q: `The distance between two parallel shafts is 18 mm and they are connected by an Oldham's coupling. The driving shaft revolves at 160 r.p.m. What is the maximum speed of sliding of the tongue of the intermediate piece along its groove?`,
opts: ["0·415 m/s", "0·586 m/s", "0·302 m/s", "0·845 m/s"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 0·302 m/s</h3><div class="formula">v<sub>max</sub> = ω × d</div>
<p><b>Concept:</b> ω = 2π × 160/60 = 16·755 rad/s → v<sub>max</sub> = 16·755 × 0·018 = <b>0·302 m/s</b>.</p>
<p>(ESE 2023 Q90 asked the same with 25 mm and 240 rpm.) The intermediate disc's centre moves on a circle of diameter d (the shaft offset) at the shaft speed, so each tongue slides back and forth with a maximum speed of ωd.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>ω = 2π × 160/60 = 16.755 rad/s.</li><li>v = 16.755 × 0.018 = <b>0.302 m/s</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0·415 does not equal ωd.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 0·586 does not equal ωd.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0·845 m/s does not equal ωd.</li></ul>
<p>The sliding speed varies sinusoidally during each revolution, from zero to its maximum, twice per revolution. Friction at the tongues causes wear and power loss, so the coupling is used mainly at moderate speeds.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> An Oldham coupling transmits constant angular velocity between parallel, offset shafts.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-66", year: 2024, no: 66, subject: "tom", topic: "Friction power in a collar thrust bearing",
q: `In a thrust bearing, the external and the internal diameters of the contacting surfaces are 320 mm and 200 mm respectively. The total axial load is 80 kN. The shaft rotates at 400 r.p.m. Taking the coefficient of friction as 0·06, what is the power lost in overcoming the friction?`,
opts: ["26·602 kW", "21 kW", "44·23 kW", "46 kW"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 26·602 kW</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Assuming uniform pressure (a new bearing):</p>
<div class="formula">T = (2/3) μ W (R³ − r³)/(R² − r²)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>R = 0·16 m; r = 0·10 m; W = 80,000 N; μ = 0·06</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>(R³ − r³)/(R² − r²) = (0·004096 − 0·001)/(0·0256 − 0·01) = 0·003096/0·0156 = 0·19846 m.</li><li>T = (2/3) × 0·06 × 80,000 × 0·19846 = 635·1 N m.</li><li>P = 2πNT/60 = 2π × 400 × 635·1/60 = <b>26·60 kW</b>.</li></ol>
<p>With uniform wear (a worn-in bearing), T = μW(R + r)/2 = 624 N m and P = 26·14 kW, slightly less, as always.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 21 kW uses the outer radius alone or other errors.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 44·23 kW uses the outer radius alone or other errors.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 46 kW uses the outer radius alone or other errors.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Uniform pressure gives the higher (safer) friction torque, so it is used for design of power loss.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-67", year: 2024, no: 67, subject: "tom", topic: "Pitch-line velocity",
q: `The number of teeth of a spur gear is 30 and it rotates at 200 r.p.m. What is the pitch line velocity if it has a module of 2 mm?`,
opts: ["376·9 mm/s", "628·3 mm/s", "850·7 mm/s", "246·1 mm/s"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 628·3 mm/s</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> (This repeats ESE 2021 Q105.)</p>
<div class="formula">d = m T = 2 × 30 = 60 mm; v = π d N/60</div>
<p>v = π × 60 × 200/60 = 200π = <b>628·3 mm/s</b>.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>d = 2 × 30 = 60 mm.</li><li>v = π × 60 × 200/60 = <b>628.3 mm/s</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 376·9 uses wrong diameters or speeds.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 850·7 uses wrong diameters or speeds.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 246·1 mm/s uses wrong diameters or speeds.</li></ul>
<p>Pitch-line velocity is used to choose the gear quality and lubrication method, and in the dynamic factor of the gear strength equations.</p>
<p>Pitch-line velocity is the common peripheral speed of the two pitch circles where the gears roll on each other without slipping. It is the same for both gears in a pair.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Module = pitch diameter/number of teeth.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-68", year: 2024, no: 68, subject: "tom", topic: "Number of teeth from velocity ratio",
q: `Two spur gears have a velocity ratio of 1/3. If the driven gear has 72 teeth, then what is the number of teeth of the driver gear?`,
opts: ["72", "36", "24", "12"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 24</h3><div class="formula">Velocity ratio = N<sub>driven</sub>/N<sub>driver</sub> = T<sub>driver</sub>/T<sub>driven</sub></div>
<p><b>Concept:</b> 1/3 = T<sub>driver</sub>/72 → T<sub>driver</sub> = <b>24 teeth</b>.</p>
<p>The driven gear turns at one-third the speed of the driver, so it must have three times as many teeth.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 72 would give a velocity ratio of 1.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 36 gives 1/2; gives 1/6.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 12 gives 1/6.</li></ul>
<p>Since the teeth of meshing gears pass the contact point at the same rate, N<sub>1</sub>T<sub>1</sub> = N<sub>2</sub>T<sub>2</sub>. The gear ratio here is 72/24 = 3.</p><p>The velocity ratio is often defined in the other sense (driver speed/driven speed = 3); either way, the larger gear must be the slower one, and the smaller gear here is the driver.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Gear speeds are inversely proportional to the numbers of teeth.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-69", year: 2024, no: 69, subject: "tom", topic: "Teeth from centre distance and velocity ratio",
q: `The following data relate to two meshing gears : Velocity ratio = 1/3; Module = 4 mm; Centre distance = 200 mm. How many number of teeth are there on the gear wheels?`,
opts: ["90", "60", "45", "75"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 75</h3><div class="formula">C = m(T<sub>1</sub> + T<sub>2</sub>)/2 &nbsp;&nbsp; T<sub>2</sub> = 3T<sub>1</sub></div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>T<sub>1</sub> + T<sub>2</sub> = 2C/m = 2 × 200/4 = 100.</li><li>With T<sub>2</sub> = 3T<sub>1</sub>: 4T<sub>1</sub> = 100 → T<sub>1</sub> = 25 (pinion), T<sub>2</sub> = <b>75</b> (gear wheel).</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Pitch diameters: pinion 100 mm, gear 300 mm; check C = (100 + 300)/2 = 200 mm.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 90 does not satisfy both conditions.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 60 does not satisfy both conditions.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 45 would give a centre distance of only 120 mm with its mating pinion of 15 teeth.</li></ul>
<p>Both gears must have the same module to mesh. The centre distance equals the sum of the pitch radii: m T<sub>1</sub>/2 + m T<sub>2</sub>/2.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> "Gear wheel" means the larger of the pair; the smaller is the pinion.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-70", year: 2024, no: 70, subject: "tom", topic: "Simple epicyclic gear train",
q: `An epicyclic gear train consists of an arm and two gears A and B having 30 teeth and 40 teeth respectively. The arm rotates about the centre of gear A at a speed of 80 r.p.m. counterclockwise. What is the speed of gear B if gear A is fixed?`,
opts: ["140 r.p.m.", "180 r.p.m.", "120 r.p.m.", "200 r.p.m."], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 140 r.p.m.</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Tabular method: lock the train and turn it with the arm (+y), then give relative motions with the arm fixed (+x to A).</p><div class="formula">Tabular method: arm +y, A relative +x</div><div class="formula">N_B = y − x(T_A/T_B)</div>
<table><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Arm</th><th>A (30)</th><th>B (40)</th></tr><tr><td>Arm fixed, A +x</td><td>0</td><td>+x</td><td>−x × 30/40</td></tr><tr><td>Add +y to all</td><td>y</td><td>y + x</td><td>y − 0·75x</td></tr></table>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Arm: y = +80 rpm (counterclockwise).</li><li>A fixed: y + x = 0 → x = −80.</li><li>N<sub>B</sub> = 80 − 0·75 × (−80) = 80 + 60 = <b>140 rpm</b> counterclockwise.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(c)</b> 120 rpm comes from using the inverted tooth ratio (40/30) or adding the relative speed with the wrong sign.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 180 rpm comes from using the inverted tooth ratio (40/30) or adding the relative speed with the wrong sign.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 200 rpm comes from using the inverted tooth ratio (40/30) or adding the relative speed with the wrong sign.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Check: N<sub>B</sub> = N<sub>arm</sub>(1 + T<sub>A</sub>/T<sub>B</sub>) when A is fixed.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-71", year: 2024, no: 71, subject: "tom", topic: "Flywheel energy for a speed change",
q: `A flywheel with a mass of 3 kN has a radius of gyration of 1·6 m. What is the energy stored in the flywheel when its speed increases from 315 r.p.m. to 340 r.p.m.?`,
opts: ["757·5 kJ", "896·2 kJ", "412·1 kJ", "684·9 kJ"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 684·9 kJ</h3><div class="formula">ΔE = ½ I (ω<sub>2</sub>² − ω<sub>1</sub>²), I = m k²</div>
<p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Working (as keyed, treating the "mass of 3 kN" as 3000 kg)</b></p><ol class="work"><li>I = 3000 × 1·6² = 7680 kg m².</li><li>ω<sub>1</sub> = 2π × 315/60 = 32·99 rad/s; ω<sub>2</sub> = 35·60 rad/s.</li><li>ω<sub>2</sub>² − ω<sub>1</sub>² = 1267·6 − 1088·2 = 179·3.</li><li>ΔE = 0·5 × 7680 × 179·3 = 688·6 kJ ≈ <b>684·9 kJ</b> (the small difference is from rounding).</li></ol>
<p>If "3 kN" is taken as a weight, the mass would be 305·8 kg and ΔE only 70·2 kJ, not offered. The options are clearly built on a mass of 3000 kg (3 tonnes): the question writes "kN" where it means a mass.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 757.5 kJ would need k ≈ 1.68 m.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 896.2 kJ would need k ≈ 1.82 m.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 412.1 kJ would need k ≈ 1.24 m.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Short form: ΔE = I ω<sub>mean</sub>² × (ω<sub>2</sub> − ω<sub>1</sub>)/ω<sub>mean</sub> = I ω<sub>mean</sub> Δω.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-72", year: 2024, no: 72, subject: "tom", topic: "Flywheel kinetic energy after starting",
q: `A flywheel fitted to a steam engine has a mass of 800 kg. Its radius of gyration is 360 mm. The starting torque of the engine is 580 N-m and may be assumed constant. What is the kinetic energy of the flywheel after 12 seconds?`,
opts: ["233·27 kJ", "263·27 kJ", "333·27 kJ", "363·27 kJ"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 233·27 kJ</h3><div class="formula">α = T/I; ω = α t; KE = ½ I ω²</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>I = 800 × 0·36² = 103·68 kg m².</li><li>α = 580/103·68 = 5·594 rad/s².</li><li>ω after 12 s = 67·13 rad/s (641 rpm).</li><li>KE = 0·5 × 103·68 × 67·13² = <b>233·6 kJ</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Shortcut: KE = (Tt)²/(2I) = (580 × 12)²/(2 × 103·68) = 233·6 kJ, or KE = T × θ where θ = ½αt² = 402·8 rad.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 263·27 adds round amounts to the correct value.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 333·27 adds round amounts to the correct value.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 363·27 kJ adds round amounts to the correct value.</li></ul>
<p>Assumptions: the engine starts from rest, the starting torque stays constant and there is no friction or load, so all the work done by the torque goes into flywheel kinetic energy.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Constant torque from rest: KE grows with t², not t.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-73", year: 2024, no: 73, subject: "tom", topic: "Gyroscopic couple on an aircraft",
q: `An aeroplane makes a complete half circle of 50 metres radius, towards left, when flying at 200 km/h. The rotary engine and propeller of the plane have a mass of 400 kg and radius of gyration of 0·3 m. The engine rotates at 2400 r.p.m. clockwise when viewed from the rear. What is the gyroscopic couple on the aircraft?`,
opts: ["10 kN-m", "15 kN-m", "18 kN-m", "12 kN-m"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 10 kN-m</h3><div class="formula">C = I ω ω<sub>p</sub></div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>I = 400 × 0·3² = 36 kg m².</li><li>ω = 2π × 2400/60 = 251·3 rad/s.</li><li>ω<sub>p</sub> = v/R = (200/3·6)/50 = 1·111 rad/s.</li><li>C = 36 × 251·3 × 1·111 = 10,053 N m ≈ <b>10 kN m</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Effect (Khurmi's classic example): with the engine clockwise from the rear and the plane turning left, the gyroscopic couple raises the nose and dips the tail.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 15 ms use a wrong speed conversion or radius.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 18 ms use a wrong speed conversion or radius.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 12 kN ms use a wrong speed conversion or radius.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> The "half circle" wording does not change the couple; only the turning rate v/R matters.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-74", year: 2024, no: 74, subject: "tom", topic: "Height of a Watt governor",
q: `What is the vertical height of a Watt governor when it rotates at 60 r.p.m.?`,
opts: ["0·157 m", "0·192 m", "0·205 m", "0·248 m"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 0·248 m</h3><div class="formula">h = g/ω² = 895/N² m (N in rpm)</div>
<p><b>Concept:</b> h = 895/60² = 895/3600 = <b>0·2486 m</b>.</p>
<p>Derivation: for a simple Watt governor, balancing the moments of the ball's weight and centrifugal force about the pivot gives tan θ = ω²r/g and h = r/tan θ = g/ω².</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>h = 895/N².</li><li>= 895/3600 = <b>0.248 m</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0.157 m would need N ≈ 75 rpm.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 0.192 m would need N ≈ 68 rpm.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0.205 m would need N ≈ 66 rpm.</li></ul>
<p>Because h ∝ 1/N², the governor height becomes tiny at high speed (only 8·9 mm at 316 rpm), which is why Watt governors are limited to slow engines.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> The height does not depend on the mass of the balls or the arm lengths.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-75", year: 2024, no: 75, subject: "tom", topic: "Degrees of freedom of a spherical pair",
q: `The degree of freedom (DOF) for globular or spherical pair is`,
opts: ["1", "2", "−1", "3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A spherical (globular) pair, such as a ball-and-socket joint, allows rotation about three mutually perpendicular axes but no translation: <b>3 degrees of freedom</b>.</p>
<p><b>Degrees of freedom of pairs</b></p><ul><li>Turning (revolute), sliding (prismatic) and screw (helical) pairs: 1.</li><li>Cylindrical pair (rotation + sliding along the same axis): 2.</li><li>Spherical pair: 3.</li><li>Planar pair (flat surfaces sliding): 3.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> One DOF is a revolute/prismatic pair.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Two DOF is a cylindrical pair.</li><li><b>(c)</b> DOF cannot be negative for a pair.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Examples of spherical pairs: car steering ball joints, shoulder and hip joints, the ball joint of a rear-view mirror.</p>` }
);

// ---- q2024d.js ----
// ESE 2024 Prelims Paper II, Mechanical Engineering, Series A, Q76–100
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2024-76", year: 2024, no: 76, subject: "turbo", topic: "Specific speed of a turbine",
q: `A turbine develops 9000 kW when running at a speed of 150 r.p.m. and under a head of 30 m. What is the specific speed of the turbine?`,
opts: ["202 r.p.m.", "152 r.p.m.", "189 r.p.m.", "217 r.p.m."], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 202 r.p.m.</h3><div class="formula">N<sub>s</sub> = N √P/H<sup>5/4</sup> (P in kW, H in m)</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>√9000 = 94·87.</li><li>30<sup>1·25</sup> = 70·21.</li><li>N<sub>s</sub> = 150 × 94·87/70·21 = <b>202·7</b> (SI units).</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> A specific speed of about 200 indicates a medium-speed Francis turbine (Francis range about 60–300).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 152 would need N ≈ 112 rpm.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 189 would need N ≈ 140 rpm.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 217 would need N ≈ 161 rpm.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Specific speed is the speed of a geometrically similar turbine producing 1 kW under 1 m head. Ranges (SI): Pelton 10–35, Francis 60–300, Kaplan 300–1000; it is used to choose the turbine type for a site.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-77", year: 2024, no: 77, subject: "turbo", topic: "Unit quantities of turbines",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding unit quantities :<ol><li>Unit specific speed is defined as the speed of a turbine working under unit head.</li><li>Unit discharge is defined as the discharge passing through a turbine, which is working under a unit head.</li><li>Unit power is defined as the power developed by a turbine working under unit speed.</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 3", "2 and 3", "2 only", "3 only"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 2 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (wrong):</b> The speed of a turbine working under unit head is the <i>unit speed</i> N<sub>u</sub> = N/√H. "Specific speed" is a different quantity (for a similar turbine producing unit power under unit head).</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> Unit discharge Q<sub>u</sub> = Q/√H: the discharge of the turbine working under a head of 1 m.</li><li><b>3 (wrong):</b> Unit power P<sub>u</sub> = P/H<sup>3/2</sup> is the power developed under unit <i>head</i>, not unit speed.</li></ul>
<div class="formula">N<sub>u</sub> = N/√H; Q<sub>u</sub> = Q/√H; P<sub>u</sub> = P/H<sup>3/2</sup></div>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Unit quantities predict how the same turbine performs under a different head: for example N<sub>2</sub> = N<sub>1</sub>√(H<sub>2</sub>/H<sub>1</sub>).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, 3, which are incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 2.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Unit quantities apply to the same turbine; specific speed compares different (geometrically similar) turbines.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-78", year: 2024, no: 78, subject: "turbo", topic: "Characteristic curves of turbines",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding characteristic curves of hydraulic turbines :<ol><li>Main characteristic curves are obtained by maintaining a constant speed and a constant gate opening on a turbine.</li><li>Operating characteristic curves are obtained by maintaining a constant head on a turbine.</li><li>Constant efficiency curves are known as Muschel curves.</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2", "2 and 3", "2 only", "3 only"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Statement by statement (Bansal)</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (wrong):</b> Main (constant-head) characteristic curves are obtained by keeping the <i>head</i> constant and varying the speed (by changing the load) for several gate openings.</li><li><b>2 (wrong):</b> Operating characteristic curves are obtained by keeping both the head and the <i>speed</i> constant (as in a power station, where speed is fixed by the grid), varying the load.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> Constant-efficiency curves (iso-efficiency curves) are called Muschel (shell) curves, from their shell shape; they are drawn from the main characteristics.</li></ul>
<p>Muschel curves show the region of best efficiency and help choose a turbine's operating range.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, 2, which are incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Main: constant head; operating: constant head and speed; Muschel: constant efficiency.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-79", year: 2024, no: 79, subject: "turbo", topic: "Parts of a centrifugal pump",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding centrifugal pumps :<ol><li>The rotating part of a centrifugal pump is called runner.</li><li>Volute casing is spiral type in which area of flow increases gradually.</li><li>In vortex casing, a vortex chamber is introducing between the casing and impeller.</li><li>Foot valve is fitted at the lower end of suction pipe.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3 only", "2, 3 and 4 only", "1, 3 and 4 only", "1, 2, 3 and 4"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2, 3 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (wrong):</b> The rotating part of a centrifugal pump is the <i>impeller</i>. "Runner" is the rotating part of a turbine.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> In a volute casing, the flow area increases gradually around the impeller, reducing the velocity and converting kinetic energy into pressure.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> A vortex (whirlpool) casing adds a circular vortex chamber between the impeller and the volute, which reduces eddy losses and improves efficiency.</li><li><b>4 (correct):</b> A foot valve (a non-return valve, usually with a strainer) at the bottom of the suction pipe keeps the pipe and pump primed when the pump stops.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> A diffuser casing (guide vanes round the impeller) is the third type of casing, used in multistage pumps.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 4, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 2, 3, 4.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Centrifugal pumps must be primed: an impeller full of air develops very little pressure.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-80", year: 2024, no: 80, subject: "turbo", topic: "Heads of a centrifugal pump",
q: `Consider the following statements :<ol><li>The horizontal distance between the centreline of the pump and the water surface in the tank to which water is delivered is known as delivery head.</li><li>The sum of suction head and delivery head is known as static head.</li><li>The manometric head is defined as the head against which a centrifugal pump has to work.</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 3", "2 and 3", "1 only", "2 only"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Heads of a centrifugal pump.</p><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (wrong):</b> Delivery head is the <i>vertical</i> height of the delivery water surface above the pump centreline, not a horizontal distance.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> Static head H<sub>s</sub> = suction head h<sub>s</sub> + delivery head h<sub>d</sub>.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> Manometric head is the head against which the pump works: H<sub>m</sub> = h<sub>s</sub> + h<sub>d</sub> + h<sub>fs</sub> + h<sub>fd</sub> + V<sub>d</sub>²/2g (static head plus pipe friction losses plus exit velocity head).</li></ul>
<div class="formula">Manometric efficiency η<sub>mano</sub> = g H<sub>m</sub>/(V<sub>w2</sub>u<sub>2</sub>)</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 2, 3.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 2, 3, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Manometric head can also be found as the difference of the delivery and suction gauge readings (with velocity head correction).</p>` },

{ id: "2024-81", year: 2024, no: 81, subject: "turbo", topic: "Efficiencies of a centrifugal pump",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding efficiencies of a centrifugal pump :<ol><li>The ratio of the manometric head to the head imparted by the impeller to the water is known as manometric efficiency.</li><li>The ratio of the power available at the impeller to the power at the shaft of the centrifugal pump is known as overall efficiency.</li><li>The ratio of the power output of the pump to the power input to the pump is known as mechanical efficiency.</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 3", "2 and 3", "1 only", "3 only"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> Manometric efficiency η<sub>mano</sub> = H<sub>m</sub>/(V<sub>w2</sub>u<sub>2</sub>/g).</li><li><b>2 (wrong):</b> Power at the impeller/power at the shaft is the <i>mechanical</i> efficiency.</li><li><b>3 (wrong):</b> Power output (water power ρgQH<sub>m</sub>)/power input (shaft power) is the <i>overall</i> efficiency. Statements 2 and 3 have the names swapped.</li></ul>
<div class="formula">η<sub>overall</sub> = η<sub>mano</sub> × η<sub>mech</sub> (× η<sub>volumetric</sub>)</div>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Typical values for a good centrifugal pump: manometric efficiency about 0·80–0·90, mechanical efficiency about 0·95–0·98, overall efficiency about 0·75–0·85. The losses are hydraulic (friction, shock, eddies), mechanical (bearings, seals) and leakage.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, 3, which are incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> The same family of definitions applies to turbines, but inverted (hydraulic efficiency = power at runner/water power).</p>` },

{ id: "2024-82", year: 2024, no: 82, subject: "turbo", topic: "Absolute velocity at impeller outlet",
q: `The outlet velocity of flow and outlet velocity of whirl of an impeller of a centrifugal pump are 5 m/s and 12 m/s respectively. What is the resultant velocity at outlet?`,
opts: ["17 m/s", "7 m/s", "11 m/s", "13 m/s"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 13 m/s</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The velocity of flow (radial) and the velocity of whirl (tangential) are perpendicular components of the absolute outlet velocity.</p>
<div class="formula">V<sub>2</sub> = √(V<sub>f2</sub>² + V<sub>w2</sub>²) = √(25 + 144) = √169 = 13 m/s</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>V₂ = √(5² + 12²) = √169.</li><li>= <b>13 m/s</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 17 m/s adds the components arithmetically.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 7 m/s subtracts them.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 11 m/s is not the hypotenuse of 5 and 12.</li></ul>
<p>The whirl component sets the head imparted (Euler: H = V<sub>w2</sub>u<sub>2</sub>/g, with radial entry); the flow component sets the discharge Q = πD<sub>2</sub>B<sub>2</sub>V<sub>f2</sub>.</p>
<p>From the same triangle, the outlet angle of the absolute velocity is tan<sup>−1</sup>(5/12) = 22·6° to the tangent. The kinetic energy V<sub>2</sub>²/2g = 8·6 m of head leaving the impeller is partly converted to pressure in the volute.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> A 5-12-13 right triangle: a classic number set.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-83", year: 2024, no: 83, subject: "turbo", topic: "Nozzle efficiency and velocity coefficient",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding steam nozzles :<ol><li>Nozzle efficiency is defined as the ratio of the actual enthalpy drop to the isentropic enthalpy drop between the same pressures.</li><li>Velocity coefficient is defined as the ratio of the actual exit velocity to the exit velocity when the flow is isentropic between the same pressures.</li><li>Velocity coefficient is the square root of nozzle efficiency.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 2 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><div class="formula">η<sub>N</sub> = (h<sub>1</sub> − h<sub>2</sub>)/(h<sub>1</sub> − h<sub>2s</sub>); C<sub>v</sub> = V<sub>2</sub>/V<sub>2s</sub></div>
<p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> Nozzle efficiency = actual enthalpy drop/isentropic enthalpy drop.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> Velocity coefficient = actual exit velocity/isentropic exit velocity.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> Since V = √(2Δh) (with negligible inlet velocity), C<sub>v</sub> = √(Δh/Δh<sub>s</sub>) = √η<sub>N</sub>.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Typical values: η<sub>N</sub> about 0·90–0·95, so C<sub>v</sub> about 0·95–0·97. Friction in the nozzle reheats the steam, so less enthalpy is converted to kinetic energy.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> ESE 2023 Q31 used C<sub>v</sub>² = η<sub>N</sub> in reverse.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-84", year: 2024, no: 84, subject: "turbo", topic: "Steam turbine efficiencies",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding steam turbines :<ol><li>Blade efficiency is the ratio of the work done on the blade per second to the energy entering the blade per second.</li><li>Internal efficiency is the ratio of the heat converted into useful work to the total adiabatic heat drop.</li><li>Net efficiency is the ratio of brake thermal efficiency to diagram thermal efficiency.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 2 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> Blade (diagram) efficiency = work done on the blades/kinetic energy supplied to the blades, per second.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> Internal efficiency = heat actually converted to useful work (inside the turbine)/total isentropic (adiabatic) heat drop.</li><li><b>3 (wrong):</b> The ratio of brake thermal efficiency to indicated (or diagram) thermal efficiency is the <i>mechanical</i> efficiency. Net (overall) efficiency is the brake work over the heat supplied, or the product of the component efficiencies.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Other definitions: stage efficiency = work on blades/enthalpy drop in the stage = nozzle efficiency × blade efficiency; reheat factor = cumulative stage heat drop/overall isentropic drop (above 1).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Ratios of efficiencies give component efficiencies (mechanical, relative), not the net efficiency.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-85", year: 2024, no: 85, subject: "power", topic: "Bleeding and governing of turbines",
q: `Consider the following statements :<ol><li>Bleeding is the process of draining steam from the turbine at certain point during its expansion.</li><li>There is a usual practice in bleeding installation to allow the bled steam to mix with the preheated air, after that the mixture of steam and air proceeds to the boiler.</li><li>The objective of governing is to keep the turbine speed fairly constant irrespective of load.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 2 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> Bleeding is extracting part of the steam from intermediate stages of the turbine.</li><li><b>2 (wrong):</b> Bled steam is used to heat the <i>feed water</i> (in open or closed feed-water heaters), not mixed with preheated air going to the boiler. This is regenerative feed heating.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> Governing keeps the turbine speed (and so the generator frequency) nearly constant as the load changes, by controlling steam flow (throttle, nozzle or by-pass governing).</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Regenerative heating raises the mean temperature at which heat is added in the boiler, improving cycle efficiency (a 5–10% gain is common).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 3.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Open heaters mix bled steam and feed water directly; closed heaters exchange heat through tubes.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-86", year: 2024, no: 86, subject: "icrac", topic: "Indicated power of a compressor",
q: `The indicated work of a single-stage reciprocating air compressor is 250 kJ/min. What is the indicated power?`,
opts: ["250 kW", "0·24 kW", "15 kW", "4·16 kW"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 4·16 kW</h3><div class="formula">Power = work per unit time</div>
<p><b>Concept:</b> 250 kJ/min ÷ 60 s/min = <b>4·17 kW</b>.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>250 kJ/min ÷ 60 = <b>4.16 kW</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 250 kW treats kJ/min as kJ/s.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 0·24 kW is 1/4·17, an inverted ratio.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 15 kW is 250 × 60/1000 (wrong conversion).</li></ul>
<p>The shaft (brake) power would be the indicated power divided by the mechanical efficiency (for example 4·17/0·85 = 4·9 kW).</p>
<p>Indicated work comes from the area of the compressor's p–V (indicator) diagram multiplied by the number of cycles per minute. Isothermal efficiency compares the isothermal work with this indicated work.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> 1 kW = 60 kJ/min = 3600 kJ/h.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-87", year: 2024, no: 87, subject: "thermo", topic: "Thermal and isentropic efficiencies",
q: `Consider the following statements :<ol><li>Thermal efficiency is the ratio of the network output to the heat supplied.</li><li>Compressor isentropic efficiency is the ratio of the work output in isentropic compression to the ideal work required.</li><li>Turbine isentropic efficiency is the ratio of the actual work output to the isentropic work output.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> η<sub>th</sub> = W<sub>net</sub>/Q<sub>in</sub>.</li><li><b>2 (wrong):</b> Compressor isentropic efficiency = isentropic (ideal) work/<i>actual</i> work required: η<sub>C</sub> = (h<sub>2s</sub> − h<sub>1</sub>)/(h<sub>2</sub> − h<sub>1</sub>). The statement compares isentropic work with "ideal work", which are the same thing, and calls compression work an "output".</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> Turbine isentropic efficiency = actual work/isentropic work: η<sub>T</sub> = (h<sub>1</sub> − h<sub>2</sub>)/(h<sub>1</sub> − h<sub>2s</sub>).</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Typical values: gas turbine compressors 0·80–0·90, turbines 0·85–0·92.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 3.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> In both cases the ratio is arranged so that the efficiency is less than 1.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-88", year: 2024, no: 88, subject: "design", topic: "Ductile, brittle and static loading",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding modes of failure :<ol><li>A ductile material is one which has a relatively large tensile strain before fracture takes place.</li><li>A brittle material has a relatively small tensile strain before fracture.</li><li>A static load is defined as a force, which is gradually applied to a mechanical component and which changes its magnitude or direction with respect to time.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Statement by statement (Bhandari)</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> Ductile materials undergo large strain (more than about 5% elongation) before fracture: mild steel, copper, aluminium.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> Brittle materials fracture with little strain: cast iron, glass, ceramics.</li><li><b>3 (wrong):</b> A static load is applied gradually and does <i>not</i> change its magnitude or direction with time. Loads that vary with time are dynamic or fluctuating loads.</li></ul>
<p>Failure criteria follow: ductile parts under static load fail by yielding (design on yield strength), brittle parts by fracture (design on ultimate strength).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> ESE 2022 Q149 gave Mott's 5% elongation criterion for ductility.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-89", year: 2024, no: 89, subject: "design", topic: "Theories of elastic failure",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding theories of elastic failure :<ol><li>Experimental investigations suggest that maximum principal stress theory gives good predictions for brittle materials.</li><li>Maximum shear stress theory predicts that the yield strength in shear is equal to the yield strength in tension.</li><li>Maximum shear stress theory is also known as Coulomb, Tresca and Guest theory.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> The maximum principal stress theory agrees well with tests on brittle materials.</li><li><b>2 (wrong):</b> Tresca predicts S<sub>sy</sub> = 0·5 S<sub>yt</sub>, <i>half</i> the tensile yield strength. (S<sub>sy</sub> = S<sub>yt</sub> is the prediction of the maximum principal stress theory.)</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> The maximum shear stress theory is credited to Coulomb, Tresca and Guest.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Distortion energy (von Mises) predicts S<sub>sy</sub> = 0·577 S<sub>yt</sub>, closest to test data for ductile metals.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 3.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Bhandari uses exactly these names: "Coulomb, Tresca and Guest's theory".</p>` },

{ id: "2024-90", year: 2024, no: 90, subject: "design", topic: "Distortion energy theory",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding distortion energy theory :<ol><li>It is known as Huber-von Mises-Hencky theory.</li><li>The yield strength in shear is 0·577 times the yield strength in tension.</li><li>Experiments have shown that the distortion energy theory is in better agreement for predicting the failure of a brittle component than any other theory of failure.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Distortion energy theory.</p><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> The distortion energy theory is the Huber–von Mises–Hencky theory.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> In pure shear it predicts S<sub>sy</sub> = S<sub>yt</sub>/√3 = 0·577 S<sub>yt</sub>.</li><li><b>3 (wrong):</b> It gives the best agreement for <i>ductile</i> materials, which fail by yielding. Brittle materials are better described by the maximum principal stress or Mohr-type theories.</li></ul>
<div class="formula">σ<sub>e</sub> = √(σ<sub>1</sub>² + σ<sub>2</sub>² − σ<sub>1</sub>σ<sub>2</sub>) ≤ S<sub>yt</sub>/N (plane stress)</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> The theory ignores the hydrostatic part of the stress, since pure volume change does not cause yielding.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-91", year: 2024, no: 91, subject: "design", topic: "Low-cycle and high-cycle fatigue",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding low-cycle and high-cycle fatigue :<ol><li>Any fatigue failure, when the number of stress cycles is less than 1000, is called low-cycle fatigue.</li><li>Any fatigue failure, when the number of stress cycles is more than 1000, is called high-cycle fatigue.</li><li>The high-cycle fatigue involves plastic yielding at localized areas of the components.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Statement by statement (Bhandari)</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> Fatigue failure in fewer than about 1000 (10³) cycles is low-cycle fatigue.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> Failure after more than about 1000 cycles is high-cycle fatigue.</li><li><b>3 (wrong):</b> It is <i>low</i>-cycle fatigue that involves significant plastic yielding (strain-controlled, for example from thermal cycling or start–stop loading). High-cycle fatigue occurs at stresses below the yield strength, essentially elastic overall.</li></ul>
<p>Design: low-cycle fatigue uses strain–life (Coffin–Manson) methods; high-cycle fatigue uses stress–life (S–N) curves and the endurance limit (at about 10⁶–10⁷ cycles for steels).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Examples of low-cycle fatigue: pressure vessels cycled a few times a day, aircraft landing gear.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-92", year: 2024, no: 92, subject: "design", topic: "Permanent and separable joints",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding joints :<ol><li>Bolted joint is the example of permanent joint.</li><li>Cotter joint is the example of separable joint.</li><li>In hand riveting, a die is placed on the protruding end of the shank and blows are applied by a hammer.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 2 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (wrong):</b> A bolted joint is a <i>separable</i> (temporary) joint: it can be taken apart without damage. Permanent joints are riveted, welded, brazed, soldered and adhesive-bonded joints.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> A cotter joint (and a knuckle joint) is a separable joint.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> In hand riveting, a die (snap) is held on the protruding end of the shank and hammered to form the head, while a dolly supports the original head.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Separable joints include threaded fasteners, keys, cotters, pins and couplings.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 2, 3.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Permanent joints cannot be dismantled without destroying the joining element.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-93", year: 2024, no: 93, subject: "design", topic: "Types of transmission shafts",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding transmission shaft :<ol><li>Countershaft is a secondary shaft, which is driven by the main shaft and from which the power is supplied to a machine component.</li><li>Spindle consists of a number of shafts, which are connected in axial direction by means of couplings.</li><li>Jackshaft is an auxiliary shaft between two shafts that are used in transmission of power.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Statement by statement (Bhandari)</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> A countershaft is a secondary shaft driven by the main shaft, supplying power to a machine component.</li><li><b>2 (wrong):</b> A number of shafts joined end to end by couplings is a <i>line shaft</i>. A spindle is a short rotating shaft (such as a machine-tool spindle).</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> A jackshaft is an auxiliary (intermediate) shaft between two shafts used in transmitting power.</li></ul>
<p>(ESE 2022 Q48 gave the correct definition of the line shaft.) An axle, by contrast, supports rotating parts but transmits no useful torque.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 3.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Spindle: short, rotating, often carrying the tool or work.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-94", year: 2024, no: 94, subject: "design", topic: "Gear centre distance",
q: `A pair of spur gears consists of a 20 teeth pinion meshing with a 120 teeth gear. The module is 4 mm. What is the centre distance?`,
opts: ["210 mm", "280 mm", "310 mm", "325 mm"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 280 mm</h3><div class="formula">C = m(T<sub>p</sub> + T<sub>g</sub>)/2</div>
<p><b>Concept:</b> C = 4 × (20 + 120)/2 = 4 × 70 = <b>280 mm</b>.</p>
<p>Pitch diameters: pinion 80 mm, gear 480 mm; gear ratio 6:1.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>C = 4 × (20 + 120)/2.</li><li>= <b>280 mm</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 210 does not equal m(T<sub>p</sub> + T<sub>g</sub>)/2.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 310 does not equal m(T<sub>p</sub> + T<sub>g</sub>)/2.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 325 mm does not equal m(T<sub>p</sub> + T<sub>g</sub>)/2.</li></ul>
<p>A 20-tooth pinion with 20° full-depth teeth is above the minimum (about 18 teeth) needed to avoid interference with a large gear.</p>
<p>Standard tooth proportions for this pair: addendum = m = 4 mm, dedendum = 1·25m = 5 mm, so the outside diameters are 88 mm (pinion) and 488 mm (gear).</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Centre distance = sum of pitch radii.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-95", year: 2024, no: 95, subject: "design", topic: "Rated bearing life in revolutions",
q: `In a particular application, the expected life for 90% of the bearings is 8000 h. What is the rated bearing life when the shaft rotates at 1450 r.p.m.?`,
opts: ["607 million revolutions", "641 million revolutions", "696 million revolutions", "712 million revolutions"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 696 million revolutions</h3><div class="formula">L<sub>10</sub> (rev) = 60 N L<sub>10h</sub></div>
<p><b>Concept:</b> L<sub>10</sub> = 60 × 1450 × 8000 = 696 × 10⁶ rev = <b>696 million revolutions</b>.</p>
<p>The rated life L<sub>10</sub> is the life that 90% of a group of identical bearings will reach or exceed. It is then used to find the required dynamic load rating: C = P(L<sub>10</sub>/10⁶)<sup>1/k</sup> = P × 696<sup>1/3</sup> = 8·86P for a ball bearing.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>L = 60 × 1450 × 8000.</li><li>= 696 × 10⁶ rev = <b>696 million revolutions</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 607 revolutions use other speeds or hours.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 641 revolutions use other speeds or hours.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 712 million revolutions use other speeds or hours.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> 8000 h is a typical design life for machines used 8 hours a day (Bhandari's table).</p>` },

{ id: "2024-96", year: 2024, no: 96, subject: "design", topic: "Lubrication regimes",
q: `Consider the following statements :<ol><li>Zero film bearing is a bearing which operates without any lubricant.</li><li>Thin film lubrication describes a condition of lubrication, where two surfaces of the bearing in relative motion are completely separated by a film of fluid.</li><li>Hydrodynamic lubrication is defined as a system of lubrication in which the load-supporting fluid film is created by the shape and relative motion of the sliding surfaces.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Statement by statement (Bhandari)</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> A zero-film bearing operates without any lubricant (for example PTFE or nylon dry bearings).</li><li><b>2 (wrong):</b> Complete separation of the surfaces by a fluid film describes <i>thick-film</i> (full-film) lubrication. Thin-film (boundary) lubrication is when the film is too thin to prevent some contact between surface asperities.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> In hydrodynamic lubrication, the pressure in the film is generated by the wedge shape and the relative motion of the surfaces (as in a journal bearing), with no external pump.</li></ul>
<p>Hydrostatic lubrication, by contrast, uses an external pump to supply oil under pressure, so it supports load even at zero speed.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 3.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Stribeck curve: boundary → mixed → hydrodynamic lubrication as μN/p increases.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-97", year: 2024, no: 97, subject: "design", topic: "Functions of lubricants; MoS₂",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding lubricant :<ol><li>Molybdenum disulphide is semi-solid lubricant.</li><li>It is used to protect bearing from corrosion.</li><li>It is used to reduce wear.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 2 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (wrong):</b> Molybdenum disulphide (MoS<sub>2</sub>) is a <i>solid</i> lubricant, like graphite: its layered structure lets planes slide easily. Semi-solid lubricants are greases.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> Lubricants protect bearing surfaces from corrosion.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> They reduce friction and wear.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Other functions of a lubricant: carrying heat away (cooling), washing away wear debris, and sealing against dirt. Solid lubricants are used where oils fail: very high or low temperatures, vacuum, and heavy loads at low speed.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 2, 3.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> MoS<sub>2</sub> works in vacuum (spacecraft), whereas graphite needs moisture to lubricate well.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-98", year: 2024, no: 98, subject: "tom", topic: "Energy absorbed by a brake",
q: `A solid cast iron disc, 1130·97 kg mass, is used as a flywheel. The radius of gyration of the solid disc is 0·3535 m about its axis of rotation. It is rotating at an angular speed of 36·65 rad/s and brought to rest by means of a brake. The energy absorbed by the brake is`,
opts: ["61 kJ", "76 kJ", "94 kJ", "107 kJ"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 94 kJ</h3><div class="formula">E = ½ I ω² = ½ m k² ω²</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>I = 1130·97 × 0·3535² = 141·3 kg m².</li><li>E = 0·5 × 141·3 × 36·65² = 0·5 × 141·3 × 1343·2 = 94,900 J ≈ <b>94 kJ</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> All the flywheel's kinetic energy becomes heat in the brake. (For a solid disc, k = R/√2: R = 0·5 m, so the disc is 1 m in diameter; 36·65 rad/s = 350 rpm.)</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 61 uses the radius instead of k, or other errors.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 76 uses the radius instead of k, or other errors.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 107 kJ uses the radius instead of k, or other errors.</li></ul>
<p>If the brake stops the disc in, say, 10 s with constant torque, the braking torque is I ω/t = 141·3 × 36·65/10 = 518 N m, and the average power absorbed is 9·5 kW.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> This is a Mott example for brake sizing: the brake must absorb ½Iω² in the chosen stopping time.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-99", year: 2024, no: 99, subject: "tom", topic: "Functions of a flywheel",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding functions of flywheel :<ol><li>It stores and releases energy when needed during the work cycle.</li><li>It increases the power capacity of the electric motor or engine.</li><li>It reduces the amplitude of speed fluctuations.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Functions of a flywheel.</p><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> A flywheel stores kinetic energy when the supply exceeds demand and gives it back when demand exceeds supply.</li><li><b>2 (wrong):</b> A flywheel does not increase the power capacity of the motor or engine. It allows a smaller motor to be used for a peaky load (such as a punch press), because the motor needs to supply only the average power, but it cannot create power.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> By absorbing and releasing energy, it reduces the fluctuation of speed within each cycle.</li></ul>
<div class="formula">ΔE = I ω<sup>2</sup> C<sub>s</sub>, where C<sub>s</sub> = (ω<sub>max</sub> − ω<sub>min</sub>)/ω (coefficient of fluctuation of speed)</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 3.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Flywheel: energy reservoir within a cycle. Governor: controls the mean speed over many cycles.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-100", year: 2024, no: 100, subject: "tom", topic: "Governor versus flywheel",
q: `Consider the following statements :<ol><li>The governor controls the mean speed of the engine by varying the fuel supply to the engine.</li><li>The flywheel has no influence on the mean speed of the engine.</li><li>A flywheel may be used if the cyclic fluctuations of energy output are negligible.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> The governor senses speed changes caused by load changes and adjusts the fuel (or steam) supply, so it controls the mean speed.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> A flywheel only smooths fluctuations within a cycle; it does not change the mean speed, since it cannot respond to a lasting imbalance between supply and demand.</li><li><b>3 (wrong):</b> A flywheel is needed when the cyclic fluctuations of energy are <i>large</i> (as in single-cylinder engines and punch presses). If they were negligible, no flywheel would be necessary.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Comparison: a governor works intermittently, over many cycles, and deals with load changes; a flywheel works continuously, within each cycle, and deals with torque fluctuations.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Multi-cylinder engines have smoother torque and need smaller flywheels.</p>` }
);

