// ESE 2023 Prelims Paper II, Mechanical Engineering, Series A, Q126–150
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
(function () {
var C = ["Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I).", "Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true, but Statement (II) is <b>not</b> the correct explanation of Statement (I).", "Statement (I) is true, but Statement (II) is false.", "Statement (I) is false, but Statement (II) is true."];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2023-126", year: 2023, no: 126, subject: "mechatronics", topic: "Stepper motors: brushless construction",
q: `In stepper motors, which one of the following features eliminates the need for brushes and a commutator?`,
opts: ["It works without the need for a position sensor.", "There are no wires connected to the rotor.", "It does not generate large torque at low speed.", "It uses gears only."], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) There are no wires connected to the rotor.</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In a stepper motor, all the windings are on the stator; the rotor is a permanent magnet or a toothed soft-iron (variable reluctance) part. Since no current has to be fed to the rotor, there is no need for brushes or a commutator. The stator phases are switched electronically by the drive.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Open-loop operation without a position sensor is a separate advantage of steppers; it is not what removes the brushes.</li><li><b>(c)</b> is false in fact: steppers give their <i>highest</i> torque at low speeds (holding torque at standstill), and torque falls as speed rises.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Steppers do not need gears; gearing is optional.</li></ul>
<p>Types: permanent magnet, variable reluctance and hybrid (the most common, typically 1·8° per step, 200 steps per revolution).</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> No brushes means long life, low maintenance and no sparking.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-127", year: 2023, no: 127, subject: "mechatronics", topic: "Passive transducers",
q: `Which of the following types of transducers are also known as externally powered transducers?`,
opts: ["Self-generating transducers", "Passive transducers", "Active transducers", "Differential transducers"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Passive transducers</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Passive transducers need an external power (excitation) source to produce an output: their measured property, such as resistance, capacitance or inductance, changes with the input. Examples: strain gauges, RTDs, thermistors, potentiometers, LVDTs and capacitive sensors.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Self-generating transducers are active transducers; they need no external power.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Active transducers generate their own output; they are not externally powered.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Differential transducers (such as the LVDT) describe a configuration, not the power source.</li></ul>
<p>Note that the terminology differs between some textbooks; the usage here (passive = externally powered) is the one in Indian instrumentation texts.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> If it needs a battery or bridge supply to work, it is passive.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-128", year: 2023, no: 128, subject: "mechatronics", topic: "Controllability, observability and duality", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Consider the following statements :<ol><li>A multi-robot system whose dynamics are written is observable if and only if it is controllable.</li><li>Duality principle can be invoked to show that a multi-robot system is controllable if and only if it is observable.</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Both 1 and 2</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The duality principle (Kalman): the system (A, B, C) is controllable if and only if its dual (Aᵀ, Cᵀ, Bᵀ) is observable. In multi-robot (multi-agent) systems written with an interaction matrix, the dynamics are often symmetric (undirected interaction graphs, with the input and output sets chosen at the same agents). The system is then its own dual, and controllability and observability coincide.</p>
<ul><li><b>1:</b> For such a multi-robot formulation, observable if and only if controllable.</li><li><b>2:</b> This equivalence is shown by invoking the duality principle.</li></ul>
<p>Our first answer was (d): for a general linear system, controllability and observability are independent properties, and duality relates a system to its dual, not to itself. A search found the general duality result applied to multi-agent networks (for example in studies of leader-based multi-agent systems). Read in that context, as statements about a symmetric multi-robot model, both statements hold, so we accepted the key.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Omits 1, 2, which are also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Kalman tests: controllable if rank[B AB … A<sup>n−1</sup>B] = n; observable if rank[Cᵀ AᵀCᵀ … ] = n.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-129", year: 2023, no: 129, subject: "mechatronics", topic: "Automated guided vehicles",
q: `Which of the following are used in material handling systems for moving raw materials or partly finished goods from one workstation to another within a manufacturing system facility?`,
opts: ["Stationary robots", "Mobile robots", "Automated guided vehicles", "Robotic arms"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Automated guided vehicles</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Automated guided vehicles (AGVs) are driverless, battery-powered vehicles that carry raw materials, work in process and finished goods between workstations, guided by embedded wires, magnetic tape, laser reflectors or vision. They are a core material-handling element of flexible manufacturing systems.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Stationary robots load and unload parts at a station; they do not move goods between stations.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Robotic arms load and unload parts at a station; they do not move goods between stations.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 'Mobile robots' is a broad research term, not the standard material-handling system.</li></ul>
<p>Groover's AGV types: driverless trains (towing), pallet trucks and unit-load carriers.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Other handling equipment: conveyors, cranes and hoists, industrial trucks, and automated storage and retrieval systems (AS/RS).</p>` },

{ id: "2023-130", year: 2023, no: 130, subject: "mechatronics", topic: "Manipulator Jacobian",
q: `Consider the following statements :<ol><li>At the velocity level, the Manipulator Jacobian relates joint velocities to end effector velocities.</li><li>The Manipulator Jacobian is important in motion planning and for identifying singularities.</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Both 1 and 2</h3><div class="formula">ẋ = J(q) q̇</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>q̇: joint velocities; ẋ: end-effector linear and angular velocity (6 × 1); J(q): manipulator Jacobian, which depends on the configuration q</li></ul>
<p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> The Jacobian maps joint rates to end-effector velocities.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> It is used in motion planning (resolved-rate control, q̇ = J<sup>−1</sup>ẋ), in force control (joint torques τ = Jᵀ F) and for finding singularities, configurations where det J = 0 and the robot loses one or more directions of motion.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Near a singularity, small end-effector motions need very large joint speeds, so planners avoid these configurations (for example a fully stretched arm, or aligned wrist axes).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Omits 1, 2, which are also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> For a 2-link planar arm, det J = l<sub>1</sub>l<sub>2</sub> sin θ<sub>2</sub>: singular when the arm is fully stretched or folded (θ<sub>2</sub> = 0 or 180°).</p>` },

{ id: "2023-131", year: 2023, no: 131, subject: "mechatronics", topic: "Deflection of a robotic arm", doubt: true,
q: `A robotic arm made from steel has a length of 1·524 m, a breadth of 0·102 m, and a height of 0·1524 m. The payload is 444·82 kg. The density of steel, ρ, is 7·87 kg m<sup>−3</sup> and its Young's modulus, E, is 206·85 GPa. What is the deflection of the robotic arm due to the payload and the robotic link mass? (Consider moment of inertia of the arm, I, is 3 × 10<sup>−5</sup> m⁴, g = 9·81 m/s², and the robotic link mass is 1200 kg m<sup>−1</sup>)`,
opts: ["170 μm", "180 μm", "190 μm", "200 μm"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 170 μm (official key)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Model the arm as a cantilever with a tip payload W and a uniformly distributed self-weight w:</p>
<div class="formula">δ = W L³/(3EI) + w L⁴/(8EI)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>L = 1·524 m (5 ft); E = 206·85 GPa (30 × 10⁶ psi); I = bh³/12 = 0·102 × 0·1524³/12 = 3·0 × 10<sup>−5</sup> m⁴; EI = 6·206 × 10⁶ N m²</li></ul>
<p><b>Our check</b></p><ol class="work"><li>The data are converted from US units: 444·82 is 100 lbf in newtons. Taking the payload as W = 444·82 N: W L³/3EI = 444·82 × 3·539/(1·862 × 10⁷) = 84·6 μm.</li><li>Link weight w = ρAg = 7870 × 0·01554 × 9·81 = 1200 N/m (the printed density 7·87 kg/m³ and "1200 kg/m" are both unit slips): wL⁴/8EI = 1200 × 5·394/(4·965 × 10⁷) = 130·4 μm.</li><li>Total ≈ 215 μm.</li></ol>
<p>Taking 444·82 kg literally (4364 N) gives about 960 μm with the 1200 N/m link, and about 2·1 mm with 1200 kg/m. None of these readings gives the keyed 170 μm or any other option.</p>
<p><b>Other options</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 180 is likewise not reproduced.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 190 is likewise not reproduced.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 200 μm is likewise not reproduced.</li></ul>
<div class="note">In doubt (key followed). The data contain unit slips (density 7·87 kg/m³, payload in "kg", link mass in "kg/m"). The most consistent reading (100 lbf payload, link weight 1200 N/m) gives about 215 μm; we could not reproduce 170 μm. The official key gives (a).</div>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Robot links are made stiff (large I) because tip deflection grows with L³ and L⁴.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-132", year: 2023, no: 132, subject: "mechatronics", topic: "Robot control and actuators",
q: `Consider the following statements :<ol><li>Robots are normally controlled using microcomputers or microcontrollers.</li><li>The output from a robot needs to be transformed into usable forms using actuators.</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Both 1 and 2</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> Robot controllers are microprocessor-based: microcomputers, microcontrollers or embedded PCs run the kinematics, path planning and servo loops.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> The controller's output is a low-power electrical signal; actuators (electric motors, hydraulic and pneumatic cylinders, often through drives and amplifiers) turn it into usable mechanical motion and force at the joints and gripper.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> A robot as a mechatronic system: sensors (encoders, force and vision sensors) → controller (microcomputer) → actuators (servo or stepper motors, hydraulics) → mechanical structure.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Omits 1, 2, which are also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Electric servo drives dominate modern industrial robots; hydraulics are used for very heavy payloads.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-133", year: 2023, no: 133, subject: "mech", topic: "Law of sines for a two-member frame",
q: `In the frame as shown in figure, an external force P is applied at joint A. Which one of the equations is correct by using law of sines?<br><img class="fig" src="img/me/2023-133.png" alt="Members AB and AC meet at joint A; B and C are pinned to a vertical wall. AB makes 70° with the wall at B; AC makes 35° with the wall at C. A vertical load P acts down at A.">`,
opts: ["P/sin 55° = P<sub>AC</sub>/sin 35° = P<sub>AB</sub>/sin 110°", "P/sin 110° = P<sub>AB</sub>/sin 110° = P<sub>AC</sub>/sin 55°", "P/sin 35° = P<sub>AB</sub>/sin 35° = P<sub>AC</sub>/sin 110°", "P<sub>AC</sub>/sin 55° = P<sub>AB</sub>/sin 35° = P/sin 55°"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) P/sin 35° = P<sub>AB</sub>/sin 35° = P<sub>AC</sub>/sin 110°</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The three forces at joint A (P, the force in AB and the force in AC) are in equilibrium, so they form a closed force triangle. Because P is vertical, parallel to the wall BC, the force triangle is similar to the geometric triangle ABC: P corresponds to side BC, P<sub>AB</sub> to side AB and P<sub>AC</sub> to side AC.</p>
<p><b>Angles of triangle ABC</b></p><ol class="work"><li>At C: 35° (between AC and the wall).</li><li>At B: 180° − 70° = 110° (AB makes 70° with the wall above B, so the interior angle with BC below is 110°).</li><li>At A: 180° − 110° − 35° = 35°.</li></ol>
<p><b>Law of sines</b></p><p>Each side over the sine of the opposite angle: BC/sin A = AB/sin C = AC/sin B, so P/sin 35° = P<sub>AB</sub>/sin 35° = P<sub>AC</sub>/sin 110°. (So P<sub>AB</sub> = P and P<sub>AC</sub> = P sin 110°/sin 35° = 1·64P.)</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> uses 55° or pair the forces with the wrong angles.</li><li><b>(b)</b> uses 55° or pair the forces with the wrong angles.</li><li><b>(d)</b> uses 55° or pair the forces with the wrong angles.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Lami's theorem gives the same result using the angles between the forces themselves.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-134", year: 2023, no: 134, subject: "mech", topic: "Resultant of distributed line loads",
q: `Consider the following properties for line loads distributed along a plane curve :<ol><li>The magnitude of the resultant force is equal to the area under the load diagram.</li><li>The line of action of the resultant force passes through the centroid of the area under the load diagram.</li><li>The line of action of the resultant force is always vertically upward.</li></ol>Which of the above properties are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> The resultant R = ∫w dx, which is the area under the load-intensity diagram.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> Its line of action passes through the centroid of that area: x̄ = ∫x w dx/∫w dx.</li><li><b>3 (wrong):</b> The resultant acts in the direction of the loading. Gravity loads act vertically <i>downward</i>; other line loads (wind, fluid pressure) can act in any direction. It is not "always vertically upward".</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Example: a triangular load of peak w<sub>0</sub> over length L has resultant w<sub>0</sub>L/2, acting at L/3 from the larger end.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> This replacement is valid only for external effects (reactions); internal shear and moment must use the actual distribution.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-135", year: 2023, no: 135, subject: "mech", topic: "Maximum static friction",
q: `The 50-kg block as shown in figure is initially at rest on a horizontal plane. After that P was gradually increased from 0 to 150 N. What is the maximum static friction force? (μ<sub>s</sub> = 0·5, μ<sub>k</sub> = 0·2)<br><img class="fig" src="img/me/2023-135.png" alt="A block of weight W on a horizontal floor pulled by a horizontal force P">`,
opts: ["490·5 N", "245·25 N", "98·1 N", "25 N"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 245·25 N</h3><div class="formula">F<sub>s,max</sub> = μ<sub>s</sub> N</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>N = mg = 50 × 9·81 = 490·5 N (horizontal pull, so no change in N); μ<sub>s</sub> = 0·5</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Maximum (limiting) static friction = 0·5 × 490·5 = <b>245·25 N</b>.</p>
<p>Since P reaches only 150 N, which is less than 245·25 N, the block never moves: the actual friction force simply matches P, rising from 0 to 150 N. The kinetic coefficient (0·2) would matter only once sliding began (kinetic friction 98·1 N).</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>N = 50 × 9.81 = 490.5 N.</li><li>F_s,max = 0.5 × 490.5 = <b>245.25 N</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 490·5 N is the normal force (weight).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 98·1 N is the kinetic friction, μ<sub>k</sub>N.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 25 N is far below μ_sN.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Static friction is self-adjusting up to its limit μ<sub>s</sub>N; it does not always equal μ<sub>s</sub>N.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-136", year: 2023, no: 136, subject: "mech", topic: "Section modulus of a triangle",
q: `What is the section modulus (Z) for a triangular section of base width b and height h?`,
opts: ["bh²/12", "bh²/24", "bh³/12", "bh³/24"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) bh²/24</h3><div class="formula">Z = I/y<sub>max</sub></div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>I about the centroidal axis (parallel to the base) = bh³/36.</li><li>The centroid is h/3 from the base, so the farthest fibre (the apex) is at y<sub>max</sub> = 2h/3.</li><li>Z = (bh³/36)/(2h/3) = <b>bh²/24</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The base fibre gives a larger value, (bh³/36)/(h/3) = bh²/12, but the section modulus used for maximum bending stress is the smaller one, governed by the extreme (apex) fibre.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> bh²/12 is the section modulus of a rectangle-like value, not a triangle.</li><li><b>(c)</b> bh³/12 is the second moment of a rectangle.</li><li><b>(d)</b> bh³/24 has dimensions of I, not Z.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Rectangle Z = bh²/6; circle Z = πd³/32.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-137", year: 2023, no: 137, subject: "mech", topic: "Maximum bending moment: central point load",
q: `What is the maximum bending moment for the simply supported beam as shown in figure?<br><img class="fig" src="img/me/2023-137.png" alt="Simply supported beam AB of span L with a point load P at mid-span C">`,
opts: ["PL⁴/4", "PL²/4", "PL²/2", "PL/4"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) PL/4</h3><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>By symmetry, each reaction = P/2.</li><li>Bending moment at mid-span C = (P/2)(L/2) = <b>PL/4</b>, which is the maximum.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The bending moment diagram is a triangle: zero at the supports, rising linearly to PL/4 at the load. The shear force is +P/2 on the left half and −P/2 on the right, changing sign under the load, where the moment is greatest.</p><div class="formula">R = P/2</div><div class="formula">M_max = (P/2)(L/2)</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> PL⁴ is dimensionally wrong.</li><li><b>(b)</b> PL² is dimensionally wrong for a moment.</li><li><b>(c)</b> PL² is dimensionally wrong for a moment.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Central deflection of the same beam: PL³/(48EI).</p>` },

{ id: "2023-138", year: 2023, no: 138, subject: "mech", topic: "Beam with an applied couple: shear force",
q: `What is the maximum shear force for the simply supported beam loaded by a couple of moment T applied at point B as shown in figure?<br><img class="fig" src="img/me/2023-138.png" alt="Simply supported beam AC of span L with a concentrated couple T applied at B, distance a from A">`,
opts: ["T/L", "Ta/4", "TL²/2", "TL/4"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) T/L</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A pure couple has no net force, so the two reactions must form an equal and opposite couple: R<sub>A</sub> = −R<sub>C</sub> = T/L (one up, one down).</p><div class="formula">ΣM = 0 ⇒ R = T/L</div><div class="formula">V = constant = R</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Moments about A: R<sub>C</sub> × L + T = 0 → |R<sub>C</sub>| = T/L; then R<sub>A</sub> = −R<sub>C</sub>.</li><li>There are no transverse loads between the supports, so the shear force is constant along the whole beam: V = T/L.</li></ol>
<p>The bending moment varies linearly from zero at A to Ta/L just left of B, jumps by T at B, then returns linearly to zero at C. The largest moment is T × (larger of a, L − a)/L.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Ta/4 has units of moment × length, not force.</li><li><b>(d)</b> TL/4 has units of moment × length, not force.</li><li><b>(c)</b> TL²/2 is dimensionally wrong for a force.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> A couple causes a jump in the bending moment diagram but no change in the shear force diagram.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-139", year: 2023, no: 139, subject: "mech", topic: "Elongation of a surveyor's tape",
q: `A Surveyor's steel tape 30 m long has a cross-section of 15 mm × 0·75 mm. With this, line AB is measured as 150 m. If the force applied during measurement is 120 N more than the force applied at the time of calibration, what is the elongation? (Take modulus of elasticity for steel as 200 kN/mm²)`,
opts: ["4·400 mm", "3·375 mm", "2·125 mm", "1·600 mm"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1·600 mm</h3><div class="formula">δ = P L/(A E)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>P = 120 N (extra pull); L = 30 m = 30,000 mm (one tape length); A = 15 × 0·75 = 11·25 mm²; E = 2 × 10⁵ N/mm²</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> δ = 120 × 30,000/(11·25 × 2 × 10⁵) = 3·6 × 10⁶/2·25 × 10⁶ = <b>1·6 mm</b> per tape length.</p>
<p>Over the full 150 m line (5 tape lengths), the total error is 5 × 1·6 = 8 mm. Because the tape is stretched, it reads short: the true length of AB is 150 + 0·008 = 150·008 m. The keyed answer is the elongation of the 30 m tape itself.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 4·4 does not correspond to PL/AE for either 30 m or 150 m.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 3·375 does not correspond to PL/AE for either 30 m or 150 m.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 2·125 mm does not correspond to PL/AE for either 30 m or 150 m.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Tape corrections in surveying: temperature, pull (tension), sag and slope.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-140", year: 2023, no: 140, subject: "mech", topic: "Shear modulus (modulus of rigidity)",
q: `Which one of the following is defined as the ratio of shearing stress to shearing strain within elastic limit?`,
opts: ["Shear modulus", "Poisson's ratio", "Modulus of rigidity", "Young's modulus"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Modulus of rigidity (the key also accepts (a) Shear modulus)</h3><div class="formula">G = τ/γ (within the elastic limit)</div>
<p><b>Concept:</b> "Shear modulus" and "modulus of rigidity" are two names for the same constant G, so both (a) and (c) are correct. UPSC's final key accepts either answer.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Poisson's ratio = lateral strain/longitudinal strain (dimensionless).</li><li><b>(d)</b> Young's modulus = normal stress/normal strain.</li><li><b>(a)</b> Shear modulus is another name for the same quantity; the key lists (c) modulus of rigidity.</li></ul>
<p>Relation: E = 2G(1 + μ); for steel, G ≈ 80 GPa. G governs torsion (θ = TL/GJ) and the shear deflection of springs.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> When two options are synonyms, UPSC often accepts both in the final key.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-141", year: 2023, no: 141, subject: "mech", topic: "Error in treating a tapered bar as uniform",
q: `The extension of a bar uniformly tapering from a diameter of (d + a) to (d − a) in a length L is calculated by treating it as a bar of uniform cross-section of average diameter d. What is the percentage error?`,
opts: ["25 a²/d²", "50 a²/d²", "75 a²/d²", "100 a²/d²"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 100 a²/d²</h3><div class="formula">Tapered bar: δ = 4PL/(πE D<sub>1</sub>D<sub>2</sub>); uniform bar: δ′ = 4PL/(πE d²)</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>D<sub>1</sub>D<sub>2</sub> = (d + a)(d − a) = d² − a².</li><li>True extension δ = 4PL/[πE(d² − a²)]; approximate δ′ = 4PL/(πE d²).</li><li>Error = (δ − δ′)/δ = 1 − (d² − a²)/d² = a²/d².</li><li>Percentage error = <b>100 a²/d²</b> (the uniform-bar estimate is too small).</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 25a²/d² is a quarter of the correct percentage.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 50a²/d² is half of the correct percentage.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 75a²/d² is three quarters of the correct percentage.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Example: d = 50 mm, a = 5 mm → error = 100 × 25/2500 = 1%. The approximation is good for gentle tapers.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> The formula 4PL/(πE D<sub>1</sub>D<sub>2</sub>) comes from integrating dx/A(x) along a linearly tapering circular bar.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-142", year: 2023, no: 142, subject: "mech", topic: "Torque capacity of a solid shaft",
q: `What is the torque, if a shaft of 200 mm diameter can transmit safely and the shear stress is not to exceed 50 N/mm²?`,
opts: ["78·54 N-m", "78·54 kN-m", "152·45 kN-m", "152·45 N-m"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 78·54 kN-m</h3><div class="formula">T = (π/16) τ d³</div>
<p><b>Concept:</b> T = (π/16) × 50 × 200³ = 0·19635 × 50 × 8 × 10⁶ = 78·54 × 10⁶ N mm = <b>78·54 kN m</b>.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>T = (π/16) × 50 × 200³ N·mm.</li><li>= 78.54 × 10⁶ N·mm = <b>78.54 kN·m</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 78·54 N m has the right digits but the wrong unit (10⁶ N mm = 1 kN m, not 1 N m).</li><li><b>(c)</b> does not equal πτd³/16.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 152·45 does not equal πτd³/16.</li></ul>
<p>Derived from the torsion equation T/J = τ/r, with J = πd⁴/32 and r = d/2. At 100 rpm, this shaft could transmit P = 2πNT/60 = 822 kW.</p>
<p>The maximum shear stress occurs at the outer surface of the shaft and falls linearly to zero at the centre. A hollow shaft of the same weight transmits more torque, because material near the centre carries little stress.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Torque capacity grows with d³: doubling the diameter multiplies it by 8.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-143", year: 2023, no: 143, subject: "design", topic: "Thickness of a thin cylindrical shell",
q: `For the design of a thin cylindrical shell, if f<sub>a</sub> is allowable tensile stress for the material of the shell, thickness t of the cylindrical shell of a diameter d and internal pressure p, then the criterion for the thickness is`,
opts: ["t ≥ p/(2f<sub>a</sub>)", "t ≥ d/(2f<sub>a</sub>)", "t ≤ pd/(2f<sub>a</sub>)", "t ≥ pd/(2f<sub>a</sub>)"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) t ≥ pd/(2f<sub>a</sub>)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The governing stress in a thin cylinder is the hoop stress σ<sub>t</sub> = pd/(2t). It must not exceed the allowable stress: pd/(2t) ≤ f<sub>a</sub>, so t ≥ pd/(2f<sub>a</sub>).</p><div class="formula">σ_hoop = pd/(2t) ≤ f_a</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Rearranged: <b>t ≥ pd/(2f_a)</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> p/(2f<sub>a</sub>) ds/(2f<sub>a</sub>) are dimensionally wrong (it omit d or p).</li><li><b>(b)</b> ds/(2f<sub>a</sub>) are dimensionally wrong (it omit d or p).</li><li><b>(c)</b> has the inequality reversed: a thickness <i>below</i> pd/2f<sub>a</sub> would overstress the shell.</li></ul>
<p>Design codes add a joint efficiency η and a corrosion allowance C: t = pd/(2f<sub>a</sub>η) + C. The longitudinal stress pd/(4t) is half the hoop stress, so it does not govern.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Thin-shell theory applies when d/t is above about 20.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-144", year: 2023, no: 144, subject: "design", topic: "Lamé's theory assumptions",
q: `Consider the following assumptions for Lame's problem of stress distribution in the thick shells :<ol><li>The material of the shell is heterogeneous and isotropic.</li><li>Plane sections of the cylinder, perpendicular to the longitudinal axis, remain plane under the pressure.</li><li>The material of the shell is homogeneous and isotropic.</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 only", "3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 2 only"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 2 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (wrong):</b> Lamé assumes a <i>homogeneous</i> material (the same properties throughout), not heterogeneous.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> Plane transverse sections remain plane under pressure, so the longitudinal strain is uniform across the wall.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> The material is homogeneous and isotropic, and stressed within the elastic limit.</li></ul>
<div class="formula">σ<sub>r</sub> = A − B/r², σ<sub>t</sub> = A + B/r² (Lamé's equations)</div>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The hoop stress is greatest at the inner surface: for internal pressure only, σ<sub>t,max</sub> = p(r<sub>o</sub>² + r<sub>i</sub>²)/(r<sub>o</sub>² − r<sub>i</sub>²).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 2, 3, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 2, 3.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Thick shells are used when d/t is below about 20, as in hydraulic cylinders and gun barrels.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-145", year: 2023, no: 145, subject: "thermo", topic: "Free convection",
q: `<b>Statement (I):</b> Fluid motion produced due to change in density resulting from the temperature gradients is called free convection.<br><b>Statement (II):</b> The movement of fluid in free convection is due to the fact that the fluid particles in the immediate vicinity of the hot object become warmer than the surrounding fluid resulting in a local change of density.`,
opts: C, ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Both true; II explains I</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Free convection.</p><ul><li><b>Statement I (true):</b> Free (natural) convection is fluid motion caused by buoyancy forces from density differences, which result from temperature gradients, with no external fan or pump.</li><li><b>Statement II (true):</b> Fluid next to a hot surface warms, expands and becomes lighter (the printed "destiny" means density), so it rises and cooler fluid takes its place.</li><li><b>Link:</b> II describes the mechanism that produces the motion defined in I, so II explains I.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> would deny the causal link.</li><li><b>(c)</b> each reject a true statement.</li><li><b>(d)</b> each reject a true statement.</li><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Both true and II explains I (the keyed answer).</li></ul>
<div class="formula">Grashof number Gr = gβΔT L³/ν²; Nu = C(Gr Pr)<sup>n</sup></div>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Free convection coefficients are small (about 2–25 W/m² K in gases) compared with forced convection.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-146", year: 2023, no: 146, subject: "thermo", topic: "Heat and temperature difference",
q: `<b>Statement (I):</b> Heat is defined as the form of energy that is transferred between the system and surroundings due to the temperature difference between them.<br><b>Statement (II):</b> The temperature difference is the driving force or potential for heat transfer.`,
opts: C, ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Both true; II explains I</h3><ul><li><b>Statement I (true):</b> Heat is energy transferred across a system boundary because of a temperature difference.</li><li><b>Statement II (true):</b> Temperature difference is the driving potential for heat transfer, just as voltage drives current.</li><li><b>Link:</b> Heat is defined by its cause, the temperature difference; II states why that energy transfer happens, explaining I.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> would deny the link between the definition and its driving force.</li><li><b>(c)</b> each reject a true statement.</li><li><b>(d)</b> each reject a true statement.</li><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Both true and II explains I (the keyed answer).</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Rate equations all have this form: conduction q = kΔT/L, convection q = hΔT, radiation q = εσ(T<sub>1</sub>⁴ − T<sub>2</sub>⁴). With no temperature difference there is no heat transfer: thermal equilibrium.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Thermal resistance R = ΔT/Q, analogous to electrical resistance R = V/I.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-147", year: 2023, no: 147, subject: "icrac", topic: "Air-standard versus fuel-air cycles", selfCorrected: true,
q: `<b>Statement (I):</b> The simple air standard cycle analysis cannot predict the variation of thermal efficiency with mixture strength since air is assumed to be the working medium.<br><b>Statement (II):</b> Fuel air cycle analysis suggests that the thermal efficiency will deteriorate as the mixture supplied to an engine is enriched.`,
opts: C, ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Both true; II explains I</h3><ul><li><b>Statement I (true):</b> The air-standard cycle uses air alone with constant specific heats, so its efficiency (for example 1 − 1/r<sup>γ−1</sup> for Otto) depends only on the compression ratio. It cannot show any effect of the fuel–air ratio.</li><li><b>Statement II (true):</b> The fuel–air cycle includes the actual composition of the charge, variable specific heats and dissociation. It shows the efficiency falling as the mixture is made richer, since higher temperatures raise the specific heats and increase dissociation.</li><li><b>Link (as keyed):</b> The fuel–air result shows precisely the mixture-strength effect that air-standard analysis leaves out, because that effect comes from the working fluid's real properties. Statement II therefore explains why the simpler analysis, using air only, fails.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Our first answer was (b), treating II as a separate result. Considering that both statements deal with the same cause (the real properties of the working medium), the explanatory link holds, so we accepted the key's (a).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(c)</b> each reject a true statement.</li><li><b>(d)</b> each reject a true statement.</li><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Both true and II explains I (the keyed answer).</li><li><b>(b)</b> II is the correct explanation of I here.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Fuel–air cycle efficiency is roughly 75–80% of the air-standard value; actual engines reach about 85% of the fuel–air value.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-148", year: 2023, no: 148, subject: "mech", topic: "Lami's theorem and Varignon's theorem",
q: `<b>Statement (I):</b> When three concurrent forces are in equilibrium then each force is inversely proportional to the sine of the angle between the remaining two forces.<br><b>Statement (II):</b> The algebraic sum of moments of all forces about any point is equal to the moment of their resultant about that point.`,
opts: C, ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) I false, II true</h3><ul><li><b>Statement I (false):</b> Lami's theorem says each force is <i>directly</i> proportional to the sine of the angle between the other two: P/sin α = Q/sin β = R/sin γ. "Inversely proportional" makes it wrong.</li><li><b>Statement II (true):</b> This is Varignon's theorem (principle of moments).</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> all treat Statement I as true.</li><li><b>(b)</b> all treat Statement I as true.</li><li><b>(c)</b> all treat Statement I as true.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> I is false and II is true.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Lami's theorem follows from the sine rule applied to the closed force triangle; it applies only to three coplanar, concurrent forces in equilibrium.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Watch for the "inversely" trap: Lami is a direct proportion.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-149", year: 2023, no: 149, subject: "materials", topic: "Lateral contraction and Poisson's ratio", selfCorrected: true,
q: `<b>Statement (I):</b> When a material is subjected to a tensile strain, there is a simultaneous shortening of the cross-sectional dimensions perpendicular to the direction of the tensile strain.<br><b>Statement (II):</b> The ratio of the shortening strain to the tensile strain is called Poisson's ratio.`,
opts: C, ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Both true; II explains I</h3><ul><li><b>Statement I (true):</b> A bar stretched in tension contracts laterally: its width and thickness decrease.</li><li><b>Statement II (true):</b> Poisson's ratio ν = −(lateral strain)/(axial strain) quantifies this contraction.</li><li><b>Link (as keyed):</b> II takes the lateral shortening described in I and expresses it as a material constant; the key treats this as the explanation of I.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Our first answer was (b): a definition does not, strictly, explain why the contraction happens (the physical reason is the material's tendency to conserve volume as atomic bonds stretch). UPSC tends to read a statement that defines the quantity in I as its explanation, and the key gives (a); we accepted it.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(c)</b> each reject a true statement.</li><li><b>(d)</b> each reject a true statement.</li><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Both true and II explains I (the keyed answer).</li><li><b>(b)</b> II is the correct explanation of I here.</li></ul>
<p>Typical values: steel 0·3, aluminium 0·33, rubber about 0·5 (nearly incompressible), cork about 0.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Volumetric strain in uniaxial tension = ε(1 − 2ν).</p>` },

{ id: "2023-150", year: 2023, no: 150, subject: "materials", topic: "Toughness and impact energy", selfCorrected: true,
q: `<b>Statement (I):</b> Toughness is the ability of a material to absorb applied energy without failure.<br><b>Statement (II):</b> The energy absorption value from such tests is often called impact energy or impact resistance.`,
opts: C, ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Both true; II explains I</h3><ul><li><b>Statement I (true):</b> Toughness is a material's ability to absorb energy before fracture.</li><li><b>Statement II (true):</b> In impact tests (Charpy, Izod), the energy absorbed in breaking a notched specimen is called the impact energy (impact toughness or impact resistance).</li><li><b>Link (as keyed):</b> impact tests are how toughness is measured, and the "energy absorption value" of II is the toughness of I under impact loading.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Our first answer was (b), as II names a test value and does not explain I. The two statements come from one connected passage, where II continues the definition in I; the key gives (a), and we accepted it.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(c)</b> each reject a true statement.</li><li><b>(d)</b> each reject a true statement.</li><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Both true and II explains I (the keyed answer).</li><li><b>(b)</b> II is the correct explanation of I here.</li></ul>
<p>Static toughness is the area under the tensile stress–strain curve; impact toughness depends strongly on temperature for BCC steels (ductile-to-brittle transition).</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Charpy V-notch energy of 27 J is a common minimum requirement for structural steels.</p>` }
);
})();

// ---- q2022a.js ----
// ESE 2022 Prelims Paper II, Mechanical Engineering, Series A, Q1–25
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2022-1", year: 2022, no: 1, subject: "mech", topic: "Centroid and centre of gravity",
q: `Centroid of a body coincides with its centre of mass or its centre of gravity only if the material composing the body is`,
opts: ["uniform or homogeneous", "in equilibrium", "in static equilibrium", "translating with constant velocity"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) uniform or homogeneous</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The centroid is a purely geometric point (centre of the volume, area or line). The centre of mass weights each element by its density: x̄<sub>m</sub> = ∫x ρ dV/∫ρ dV. Only when ρ is constant does it cancel, giving x̄<sub>m</sub> = ∫x dV/∫dV, the centroid. The centre of gravity further coincides with the centre of mass when g is uniform over the body, as it is for ordinary engineering bodies.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Equilibrium is about the forces acting on the body; it has no effect on where its mass is located.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Equilibrium is about the forces acting on the body; it has no effect on where its mass is located.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Uniform translation likewise changes nothing about the mass distribution.</li></ul>
<p>Example: a bar half steel and half aluminium has its centroid at mid-length but its centre of mass shifted towards the steel end.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Centroid → geometry; centre of mass → density distribution; centre of gravity → weight distribution (gravity field).</p>` },

{ id: "2022-2", year: 2022, no: 2, subject: "mech", topic: "Thin cylinder: wall thickness",
q: `A cast-iron pipe of 750 mm diameter is used to carry water under a head of 60 m. What is the approximate thickness of the pipe if permissible stress is to be 20 MPa? (Take specific weight of water as 9·81 kN/m³)`,
opts: ["22 mm", "14 mm", "11 mm", "7 mm"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 11 mm</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A pipe under internal pressure is designed on the hoop (circumferential) stress, which is twice the longitudinal stress.</p>
<div class="formula">σ<sub>h</sub> = p d/(2t) → t = p d/(2σ<sub>h</sub>)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>p = γ H = 9·81 × 60 = 588·6 kN/m² = 0·5886 N/mm²; d = 750 mm; σ<sub>h</sub> = 20 N/mm²</li></ul>
<p>t = 0·5886 × 750/(2 × 20) = 441·45/40 = <b>11·04 mm</b>.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>p = 9.81 × 60 = 588.6 kN/m² = 0.5886 N/mm².</li><li>t = 0.5886 × 750/(2 × 20) = <b>11 mm</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 22 mm uses pd/σ (twice the correct value).</li><li><b>(d)</b> 7 mm comes from other slips; the longitudinal-stress formula pd/4σ would give 5·5 mm.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 14 mm comes from other slips; the longitudinal-stress formula pd/4σ would give 5·5 mm.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> d/t = 68 here, well above 20, so thin-cylinder theory is valid.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-3", year: 2022, no: 3, subject: "mech", topic: "Bending a plate to a radius",
q: `A 120 mm wide and 10 mm thick steel plate is bent into a circular arc of 8 m radius. What is the bending moment which will produce the maximum stress? (Take Young's modulus as 200 GPa)`,
opts: ["250 Nm", "212 Nm", "200 Nm", "172 Nm"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 250 Nm</h3><div class="formula">M/I = σ/y = E/R → M = E I/R</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>b = 120 mm; t = 10 mm; I = b t³/12 = 120 × 1000/12 = 10,000 mm⁴; E = 2 × 10⁵ N/mm²; R = 8000 mm</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>M = 2 × 10⁵ × 10⁴/8000 = 2·5 × 10⁵ N mm = <b>250 N m</b>.</li><li>Maximum stress σ = E y<sub>max</sub>/R = 2 × 10⁵ × 5/8000 = 125 N/mm².</li><li>Check: σ = M/Z = 2·5 × 10⁵/(120 × 100/6) = 125 N/mm². ✓</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 212 ms do not satisfy M = EI/R with these dimensions.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 200 ms do not satisfy M = EI/R with these dimensions.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 172 N ms do not satisfy M = EI/R with these dimensions.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Assumptions of simple bending: the material stays elastic (125 MPa is below the yield strength of structural steel, about 250 MPa), plane sections remain plane, and the radius of curvature (8 m) is very large compared with the depth (10 mm), so the curved-beam correction is negligible.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> The plate bends about its weak axis (thickness 10 mm in the depth direction).</p>` },

{ id: "2022-4", year: 2022, no: 4, subject: "mech", topic: "Support reactions of a simply supported beam",
q: `A simply supported beam of 8 m length carries three-point loads of 8 kN, 4 kN and 10 kN at 2 m, 5 m and 6 m respectively from the left end. What are the left and right support reactions respectively?`,
opts: ["12 kN and 10 kN", "9 kN and 11 kN", "11 kN and 9 kN", "10 kN and 12 kN"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 10 kN and 12 kN</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Support reactions of a simply supported beam.</p><div class="formula">ΣM_A = 0: R_B L = ΣWᵢxᵢ</div><div class="formula">ΣF_y = 0: R_A + R_B = ΣW</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Moments about the left support A: R<sub>B</sub> × 8 = 8 × 2 + 4 × 5 + 10 × 6 = 16 + 20 + 60 = 96 → R<sub>B</sub> = 12 kN.</li><li>Vertical equilibrium: R<sub>A</sub> = (8 + 4 + 10) − 12 = 10 kN.</li><li>Check, moments about B: R<sub>A</sub> × 8 = 8 × 6 + 4 × 3 + 10 × 2 = 80 → R<sub>A</sub> = 10 kN. ✓</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 12 and 10 kN has the reactions swapped; the heavier loads are nearer the right support, so R<sub>B</sub> is larger.</li><li><b>(b)</b> does not satisfy the moment equation.</li><li><b>(c)</b> does not satisfy the moment equation.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Always check with a second moment equation; it catches swapped reactions.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-5", year: 2022, no: 5, subject: "mech", topic: "Types of beam supports",
q: `Which one of the following is NOT used as support for beams?`,
opts: ["Roller support", "Hinged support", "Fixed support", "Independent support"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Independent support</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Standard beam supports, by the reactions they provide:</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Roller support: one reaction, perpendicular to the surface; free to rotate and to move along the surface.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Hinged (pinned) support: two reaction components (horizontal and vertical); free to rotate.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Fixed (built-in) support: three reactions (two forces and a moment); no translation or rotation.</li><li><b>(d)</b> "Independent support" is not a recognised support type.</li></ul>
<p>Other standard supports are the simple (knife-edge) support and the guided (sliding) support. Counting reactions against the 3 equations of planar equilibrium tells whether a beam is statically determinate.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Simply supported beam = hinge at one end + roller at the other (3 reactions, determinate).</p>` },

{ id: "2022-6", year: 2022, no: 6, subject: "mech", topic: "Efficiency of a pulley block with friction",
q: `The initial frictional resistance of an unloaded pulley block is 2·6 kN. The friction increases at the rate of 1·4 kN per 100 kN load lifted by the block. The velocity ratio is 18. The efficiency of the block at the load of 1200 kN is approximately`,
opts: ["64%", "77%", "85%", "87%"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 77%</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The effort is made up of the ideal effort plus the friction (expressed at the effort).</p>
<div class="formula">P = W/VR + F &nbsp;&nbsp; η = (W/VR)/P</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>W = 1200 kN; VR = 18; F = 2·6 + 1·4 × (1200/100) = 2·6 + 16·8 = 19·4 kN</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Ideal effort = 1200/18 = 66·67 kN.</li><li>Actual effort P = 66·67 + 19·4 = 86·07 kN.</li><li>η = 66·67/86·07 = 0·775 ≈ <b>77%</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 64% would need a much larger friction.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 85% neglects part of the friction (for example only the load-dependent 16·8 kN).</li><li><b>(d)</b> 87% neglects part of the friction (for example only the load-dependent 16·8 kN).</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Law of the machine: P = mW + C. Here m = 1/18 + 0·014 and C = 2·6 kN.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-7", year: 2022, no: 7, subject: "mech", topic: "Overhanging beam: deflection at the centre",
q: `The distance between the supports of a simply supported beam is L. The beam has two equal overhangs of length L/3 over each support. The beam carries a point load 2W at the centre and a point load W at each end. Deflection at the centre is`,
opts: ["1·8 mm", "7·2 mm", "0 mm", "3·6 mm"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 0 mm</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> By symmetry each support carries 2W. Use superposition on the span between the supports.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Central load 2W on span L: downward deflection = 2W L³/(48EI) = W L³/(24EI).</li><li>The end loads W on overhangs of L/3 apply hogging moments M = W L/3 at both supports; a uniform moment M over the span lifts the centre by M L²/(8EI) = (WL/3)L²/(8EI) = W L³/(24EI).</li><li>Net deflection at the centre = WL³/24EI − WL³/24EI = <b>0</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> gives numbers, but no E, I or loads are given; only zero can be found from the data, and the two effects cancel exactly.</li><li><b>(b)</b> gives numbers, but no E, I or loads are given; only zero can be found from the data, and the two effects cancel exactly.</li><li><b>(d)</b> gives numbers, but no E, I or loads are given; only zero can be found from the data, and the two effects cancel exactly.</li></ul>
<p>The centre does not move, but the beam is not straight: it sags under the central load and hogs near the supports.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Uniform moment M on a simply supported span: central deflection ML²/8EI; central point load P: PL³/48EI.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-8", year: 2022, no: 8, subject: "mech", topic: "Cantilever with UDL: maximum bending moment",
q: `The maximum bending moment at the fixed end in a cantilever of length L carrying a uniformly distributed load W per unit length across the whole span is`,
opts: ["WL²/2", "WL²/4", "WL³/4", "WL³/8"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) WL²/2</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The total load W L acts at the centroid of the load, L/2 from the fixed end.</p>
<div class="formula">M<sub>max</sub> = (W L)(L/2) = W L²/2 (hogging)</div>
<p>At a distance x from the free end, M = W x²/2: a parabola, zero at the free end and greatest at the fixed end. The shear force there is W L.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Total load wL acts at L/2 from the fixed end.</li><li>M_max = <b>WL²/2</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> WL²/4 is the simply supported beam central-load style value.</li><li><b>(c)</b> WL³ terms have wrong dimensions for a moment.</li><li><b>(d)</b> WL³ terms have wrong dimensions for a moment.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Simply supported beam with the same UDL: M<sub>max</sub> = WL²/8 at mid-span.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-9", year: 2022, no: 9, subject: "mech", topic: "Coefficient of friction",
q: `A body of weight 100 N is placed on a rough horizontal plane. If a horizontal force of 60 N just causes the body to slide over the horizontal plane, then the coefficient of friction between the body and the horizontal plane is`,
opts: ["0·6", "0·3", "0·2", "0·1"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 0·6</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> At the point of sliding, the applied force equals limiting friction μN; on a horizontal plane with a horizontal pull, N = W.</p>
<div class="formula">μ = F/N = 60/100 = 0·6</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>μ = 60/100 = <b>0.6</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 0·3 would need forces of 30, 20 and 10 N to start sliding.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0·2 would need forces of 30, 20 and 10 N to start sliding.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0·1 would need forces of 30, 20 and 10 N to start sliding.</li></ul>
<p>Angle of friction φ = tan<sup>−1</sup> 0·6 = 31°; the angle of repose of a plane with the same surfaces is also 31°. (The same question appeared in ESE 2021.)</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> If the pull were inclined at θ above horizontal, N = W − P sin θ and the force needed would fall.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-10", year: 2022, no: 10, subject: "fm", topic: "Power of a fire engine",
q: `A fire engine raises water at the rate of 6000 litres per minute through a height of 2 m and discharges it at 10 m/s. The H.P. of fire engine is nearly equal to (Take acceleration due to gravity as 9·81 m/s²)`,
opts: ["6·5", "7·2", "8·0", "9·5"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 9·5</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The engine gives the water both potential energy (lift) and kinetic energy (jet).</p>
<div class="formula">P = ρ g Q (h + V²/2g)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>Q = 6000 L/min = 0·1 m³/s; h = 2 m; V = 10 m/s → V²/2g = 5·10 m</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Total head = 2 + 5·10 = 7·10 m.</li><li>P = 1000 × 9·81 × 0·1 × 7·10 = 6962 W.</li><li>In metric horsepower (735·5 W): 6962/735·5 = <b>9·47 hp ≈ 9·5</b>. (British hp of 746 W gives 9·33.)</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 6·5 counts only part of the energy (the kinetic energy alone is 6·8 hp).</li><li><b>(b)</b> 7·2 counts only part of the energy (the kinetic energy alone is 6·8 hp).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 8.0 hp would correspond to about 5.9 kW.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Ignoring the jet velocity head would give only 2·7 hp: the kinetic energy dominates.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-11", year: 2022, no: 11, subject: "mech", topic: "Thin spherical vessel thickness",
q: `A spherical vessel has 1 m diameter. It is subjected to internal pressure of 1·5 N/mm². If maximum stress is not to exceed 200 N/mm² and joint efficiency is 80%, then the thickness of the plate required is`,
opts: ["3·20 mm", "4·21 mm", "5·22 mm", "2·34 mm"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 2·34 mm</h3><div class="formula">Thin sphere: σ = p d/(4 t η) → t = p d/(4 σ η)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>p = 1·5 N/mm²; d = 1000 mm; σ = 200 N/mm²; η = 0·8 (joint efficiency)</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> t = 1·5 × 1000/(4 × 200 × 0·8) = 1500/640 = <b>2·34 mm</b>.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(c)</b> 5·22 mm is close to the cylinder formula pd/(2ση) = 4·69 mm.</li><li><b>(a)</b> 3·20 uses other wrong combinations.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 4·21 mm uses other wrong combinations.</li></ul>
<p>A sphere has the same membrane stress pd/4t in every direction, half the hoop stress of a cylinder of the same diameter, so it needs half the thickness.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> That is why large pressure vessels and gas holders are often spherical.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-12", year: 2022, no: 12, subject: "mech", topic: "Poisson's ratio from G and K",
q: `A material has modulus of rigidity equal to 0·4 × 10⁵ N/mm² and bulk modulus equal to 0·75 × 10⁵ N/mm². The Poisson's ratio is`,
opts: ["0·2736", "0·1927", "0·3121", "0·4376"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 0·2736</h3><div class="formula">μ = (3K − 2G)/[2(3K + G)]</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>K = 0·75 × 10⁵ N/mm²; G = 0·4 × 10⁵ N/mm² (working in units of 10⁵)</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>3K − 2G = 2·25 − 0·8 = 1·45.</li><li>2(3K + G) = 2 × 2·65 = 5·30.</li><li>μ = 1·45/5·30 = <b>0·2736</b>.</li><li>Check: E = 9KG/(3K + G) = 2·7/2·65 = 1·019 × 10⁵ N/mm², and E = 2G(1 + μ) = 0·8 × 1·2736 = 1·019 ✓.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 0·1927 does not satisfy both relations E = 2G(1 + μ) = 3K(1 − 2μ).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0·3121 does not satisfy both relations E = 2G(1 + μ) = 3K(1 − 2μ).</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0·4376 does not satisfy both relations E = 2G(1 + μ) = 3K(1 − 2μ).</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Range check: for isotropic materials 0 &lt; μ &lt; 0·5. A value of 0·27 is typical of steels and cast irons. If K were much larger than G, μ would approach 0·5 (nearly incompressible, like rubber).</p><p><b>Remember:</b> The relation comes from eliminating E between E = 2G(1 + μ) and E = 3K(1 − 2μ).</p>` },

{ id: "2022-13", year: 2022, no: 13, subject: "materials", topic: "Lever rule: proeutectoid ferrite and pearlite",
q: `For a 99·65 wt% Fe-0·35 wt% C alloy at a temperature just below the eutectoid, the fractions of the proeutectoid ferrite and pearlite are respectively`,
opts: ["0·44 and 0·56", "0·56 and 0·44", "0·044 and 0·056", "0·056 and 0·044"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 0·56 and 0·44</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Just below the eutectoid temperature (727 °C), all austenite of eutectoid composition has become pearlite, so the lever rule is applied across the tie line from α (0·022% C) to the eutectoid point (0·76% C).</p>
<div class="formula">W<sub>p</sub> = (C<sub>0</sub> − 0·022)/(0·76 − 0·022) &nbsp;&nbsp; W<sub>α′</sub> = (0·76 − C<sub>0</sub>)/(0·76 − 0·022)</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>C<sub>0</sub> = 0·35 wt% C.</li><li>W<sub>p</sub> = 0·328/0·738 = 0·44.</li><li>W<sub>α′</sub> = 0·41/0·738 = 0·56.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> swaps the two fractions; a hypoeutectoid steel with less than half the eutectoid carbon must be mostly ferrite.</li><li><b>(c)</b> does not add up to 1, so it cannot be phase fractions.</li><li><b>(d)</b> does not add up to 1, so it cannot be phase fractions.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> This is a standard Callister problem; the total ferrite (proeutectoid + eutectoid) would be (6·70 − 0·35)/(6·70 − 0·022) = 0·95.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-14", year: 2022, no: 14, subject: "materials", topic: "Mechanical behaviour of Fe–C microstructures",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding the mechanical behavior of iron-carbon alloys :<ol><li>Martensitic steels are most ductile.</li><li>Tempered martensite is relatively brittle.</li><li>Fine pearlite is more brittle than coarse pearlite.</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 3 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (wrong):</b> Martensite is the hardest, strongest and <i>most brittle</i> microstructure of a steel; it has negligible ductility.</li><li><b>2 (wrong):</b> Tempering relieves internal stresses and precipitates fine carbides, so tempered martensite is tough and reasonably ductile, while keeping high strength.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> Fine pearlite has more phase-boundary area, which restricts dislocation motion: it is harder and stronger, but less ductile (more brittle) than coarse pearlite.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Ductility order (most to least): spheroidite &gt; coarse pearlite &gt; fine pearlite &gt; bainite &gt; tempered martensite &gt; martensite (Callister's summary table).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 3.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 1, 2, which are incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Strength and ductility usually trade off; tempered martensite gives the best combination of the two.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-15", year: 2022, no: 15, subject: "materials", topic: "HSLA steels",
q: `Which one of the following contain other alloying elements such as copper, vanadium, nickel, and molybdenum in combined concentrations as high as 10 wt%, and possess higher strengths than the plain low-carbon steels?`,
opts: ["Alloy steels", "Medium carbon steels", "Stainless steels", "High-strength, low-alloy steels"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) High-strength, low-alloy steels</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> This is Callister's description of HSLA steels: low-carbon steels containing elements such as Cu, V, Ni and Mo (up to about 10 wt% combined) that give much higher strength than plain low-carbon steel, while remaining ductile, formable and machinable. They are strengthened largely by fine precipitates and grain refinement, and some have better corrosion resistance.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> "Alloy steels" is a broad category, not this specific family.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Medium-carbon steels (0·25–0·60% C) are strengthened mainly by heat treatment (quenching and tempering).</li><li><b>(c)</b> Stainless steels need at least 11–12% Cr for corrosion resistance; the defining element is chromium.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> HSLA uses: bridges, towers, pressure vessels and vehicle frames, where weight savings matter.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-16", year: 2022, no: 16, subject: "materials", topic: "Copper alloys: tin bronze",
q: `Which one of the following alloys is most suitable for applications in bearings, bushings, piston rings, steam fittings and gears?`,
opts: ["Cartridge brass", "Tin bronze", "Leaded yellow brass", "Beryllium copper"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Tin bronze</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Callister's table of copper alloys lists for cast tin bronze (C90500, about 88% Cu, 10% Sn, 2% Zn): bearings, bushings, piston rings, steam fittings and gears. Tin bronzes combine good strength, wear resistance and corrosion resistance.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Cartridge brass (70Cu–30Zn): ammunition cases, automotive radiator cores, lamp fixtures.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Leaded yellow brass: free-machining parts such as plumbing fittings, valves and furniture hardware.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Beryllium copper: springs, bellows, firing pins, non-sparking tools and bushings (it is a precipitation-hardened, very strong alloy), but it is not the listed alloy for steam fittings and piston rings.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Phosphor bronze (with a little P) is another tin bronze used for springs and bearings.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-17", year: 2022, no: 17, subject: "materials", topic: "Purpose of annealing",
q: `In general, annealing is carried out to`,
opts: ["increase softness", "increase stresses", "decrease ductility", "decrease toughness"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) increase softness</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Annealing is heating to a suitable temperature, holding, and slow cooling (usually in the furnace). Its purposes are to relieve internal stresses, increase softness, ductility and toughness, refine grain structure and improve machinability.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Annealing <i>removes</i> residual stresses (stress-relief annealing).</li><li><b>(c)</b> It <i>increases</i> ductility, which is why it is used between stages of cold working (process annealing).</li><li><b>(d)</b> It increases, rather than decreases, toughness.</li></ul>
<p>Types: full annealing (above A<sub>3</sub>, furnace cooled: coarse pearlite), process annealing (below A<sub>1</sub> for cold-worked parts), stress relief, spheroidising.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Normalising is similar but air-cooled, giving finer pearlite and slightly higher strength.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-18", year: 2022, no: 18, subject: "materials", topic: "Determining phase compositions",
q: `Consider the following statements related to compute the equilibrium concentrations of the two phases :<ol><li>A tie line is constructed across the two-phase region at the temperature of the alloy.</li><li>The intersections of the tie line and the phase boundaries on either side are noted.</li><li>Perpendiculars are dropped from these intersections to the horizontal composition axis, from which the composition of each of the respective phases is read.</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 only", "3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> These are exactly Callister's three steps for finding the compositions of the two phases at a point in a two-phase region:</p>
<ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> Draw the tie line (isotherm) across the two-phase region at the alloy temperature.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> Note where it meets the phase boundaries on each side.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> Drop perpendiculars to the composition axis and read the composition of each phase.</li></ul>
<p>To find the phase <i>amounts</i>, the lever rule is used on the same tie line: the fraction of a phase is the length of the opposite arm divided by the total length. (ESE 2021 asked the complementary question on the lever rule.)</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 2, 3, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 1, 2, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> In a single-phase region, the phase composition equals the alloy composition; no tie line is needed.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-19", year: 2022, no: 19, subject: "materials", topic: "Microconstituents containing α-ferrite and Fe₃C",
q: `In which of the following microconstituents, α-Ferrite + Fe<sub>3</sub>C phases are present?<ol><li>Spheroidite</li><li>Coarse pearlite</li><li>Fine pearlite</li></ol>Select the correct answer using the code given below :`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> All three are two-phase mixtures of ferrite and cementite; they differ only in the shape and size of the cementite:</p>
<ul><li><b>1 Spheroidite:</b> sphere-like Fe<sub>3</sub>C particles in a continuous α-ferrite matrix.</li><li><b>2 Coarse pearlite:</b> thick alternating lamellae of α and Fe<sub>3</sub>C (transformation just below 727 °C).</li><li><b>3 Fine pearlite:</b> thin lamellae (transformation near 540 °C).</li></ul>
<p>Bainite and tempered martensite also consist of α + Fe<sub>3</sub>C. Martensite is different: a single-phase, body-centred tetragonal supersaturated solution of carbon in iron.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Same phases, different morphology → very different properties (see Q14).</p>` },

{ id: "2022-20", year: 2022, no: 20, subject: "materials", topic: "Resilience",
q: `Which one of the following is the capacity of a material to absorb energy when it is deformed elastically and then, upon unloading, to have this energy recovered?`,
opts: ["Resilience", "Ductility", "Brittleness", "Hardness"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Resilience</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Resilience is the energy stored elastically and recovered on unloading. The modulus of resilience (energy per unit volume up to yield) is the area under the elastic part of the stress–strain curve:</p>
<div class="formula">U<sub>r</sub> = σ<sub>y</sub>²/(2E)</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Ductility is the amount of plastic deformation before fracture (% elongation, % reduction in area).</li><li><b>(c)</b> Brittleness is fracture with little or no plastic deformation.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Hardness is resistance to localised plastic deformation (indentation).</li></ul>
<p>Toughness, by contrast, is the total energy absorbed up to fracture (elastic + plastic), the whole area under the curve.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Spring materials need high resilience: high yield strength and moderate E.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-21", year: 2022, no: 21, subject: "materials", topic: "Vickers indenter",
q: `What is the shape of indenter for vickers microhardness testing technique?`,
opts: ["Sphere", "Diamond cone", "Diamond pyramid", "Cube"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Diamond pyramid</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The Vickers test uses a square-based diamond pyramid with 136° between opposite faces. The diagonals of the square impression are measured.</p>
<div class="formula">HV = 1·854 F/d² (F in kgf, d = mean diagonal in mm)</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A sphere (hardened steel or tungsten carbide ball) is the Brinell indenter, and the Rockwell B indenter.</li><li><b>(b)</b> A diamond cone (120° Brale) is the Rockwell C indenter.</li><li><b>(d)</b> No standard test uses a cube indenter.</li></ul>
<p>The Knoop microhardness test uses an elongated (rhombic) diamond pyramid, suited to thin layers and brittle materials. Vickers and Knoop are both used with small loads for microhardness.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Because the geometry is similar at all sizes, Vickers hardness is nearly independent of load.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-22", year: 2022, no: 22, subject: "materials", topic: "Corrosion penetration rate",
q: `Corrosion penetration rate is directly proportional to`,
opts: ["Exposed specimen area", "Density", "Weight loss after exposure time", "Exposure time"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Weight loss after exposure time</h3><div class="formula">CPR = K W/(ρ A t)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>W: weight loss after exposure time t; ρ: density; A: exposed area; K: unit constant (534 for mpy with W in mg, ρ in g/cm³, A in in², t in h; 87·6 for mm/yr with A in cm²)</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Exposed area is in the denominator of CPR = KW/(ρAt).</li><li><b>(b)</b> Density is in the denominator of CPR = KW/(ρAt).</li><li><b>(d)</b> Exposure time is in the denominator of CPR = KW/(ρAt).</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Physical meaning: weight loss divided by density gives the volume lost; divided by area gives the thickness lost; divided by time gives the rate of thinning.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> A CPR below about 0·5 mm/yr is generally acceptable for most applications (Callister).</p>` },

{ id: "2022-23", year: 2022, no: 23, subject: "materials", topic: "Reducing galvanic corrosion",
q: `Which one of the following measures is correct to reduce the effects of galvanic corrosion?`,
opts: ["It uses cathode area as large as possible", "It avoids an unfavorable anode-to-cathode surface area ratio", "It uses an anode area as small as possible", "It electrically insulates similar metals from each other"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) It avoids an unfavorable anode-to-cathode surface area ratio</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Callister's measures against galvanic corrosion: (1) if dissimilar metals must be joined, choose two close together in the galvanic series; (2) avoid an unfavourable anode-to-cathode area ratio, i.e. use as large an anode area as possible; (3) electrically insulate <i>dissimilar</i> metals from each other; (4) connect a third, more anodic metal (cathodic protection).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> describes the <i>unfavourable</i> case: a small anode and large cathode concentrate the corrosion current on the anode, which then corrodes very fast.</li><li><b>(c)</b> describes the <i>unfavourable</i> case: a small anode and large cathode concentrate the corrosion current on the anode, which then corrodes very fast.</li><li><b>(d)</b> It is <i>dissimilar</i> metals that should be insulated; similar metals do not form a galvanic couple.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Classic example: steel rivets (small anode) in copper plates corrode rapidly; copper rivets in steel plates are far less harmful.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-24", year: 2022, no: 24, subject: "materials", topic: "Forms of corrosion",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding corrosion :<ol><li>The formation of a film of atoms or molecules on the surface of an anode so that corrosion is slowed down, is called passivation.</li><li>Local corrosion attack resulting from the formation of small anodes on a metal surface, is known as intergranular corrosion.</li><li>Preferential corrosion occuring at grain boundaries or at regions adjacent to the grain boundaries, is called pitting corrosion.</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 only", "3 only"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> Passivation: a thin, adherent oxide film (for example Cr<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub> on stainless steel, Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub> on aluminium) slows or stops further corrosion.</li><li><b>2 (wrong):</b> Localised attack at small anodic sites, forming holes, is <i>pitting</i> corrosion.</li><li><b>3 (wrong):</b> Preferential attack along grain boundaries is <i>intergranular</i> corrosion (for example in sensitised stainless steels, where chromium carbides deplete Cr near the boundaries). Statements 2 and 3 have the names swapped.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Callister's eight forms of corrosion: uniform, galvanic, crevice, pitting, intergranular, selective leaching, erosion–corrosion and stress corrosion.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, 3, which are incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Weld decay in stainless steel is intergranular; it is prevented with low-carbon (L) grades or Ti/Nb-stabilised grades.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-25", year: 2022, no: 25, subject: "tom", topic: "Natural frequency of a cantilever with end mass",
q: `A cantilever beam of negligible mass has a mass m at its free end. If the length of the cantilever is halved, what is the factor by which its natural frequency is increased?`,
opts: ["√8 times", "√6 times", "√5 times", "√3 times"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) √8 times</h3><div class="formula">k = 3EI/L³ &nbsp;&nbsp; ω<sub>n</sub> = √(k/m) ∝ L<sup>−3/2</sup></div>
<ul class="sym"><li>k: stiffness at the tip (force per unit tip deflection); m: tip mass; E, I unchanged</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Halving L multiplies the stiffness by 2³ = 8.</li><li>ω<sub>n</sub> therefore increases by √8 = 2√2 ≈ 2·83 times.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> √6 would need f ∝ L^(−3/2) with a different length ratio.</li><li><b>(c)</b> √5 is inconsistent with f ∝ L^(−3/2).</li><li><b>(d)</b> √3 would need a stiffness ratio of 3.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Equivalent form: ω<sub>n</sub> = √(g/δ), with static deflection δ = mgL³/3EI, which falls to one-eighth when L is halved.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> For a simply supported beam with a central mass, k = 48EI/L³; the same L<sup>−3/2</sup> scaling applies.</p>` }
);

// ---- q2022b.js ----
// ESE 2022 Prelims Paper II, Mechanical Engineering, Series A, Q26–50
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2022-26", year: 2022, no: 26, subject: "tom", topic: "Kinematic pairs: classification",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding kinematic pairs :<ol><li>When a pair has a point or line contact between the links, it is known as lower pair.</li><li>When the elements of a pair are held together mechanically, it is known as closed pair.</li><li>If two mating links have a turning as well as sliding motion between them, they form a screw pair.</li><li>When two links of a pair are in contact either due to force of gravity, they constitute an unclosed pair.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["2 and 4 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 3 and 4 only", "2, 3 and 4"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 2, 3 and 4</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (wrong):</b> Point or line contact makes a <i>higher</i> pair (cam and follower, gear teeth, ball bearings). A lower pair has surface (area) contact (turning, sliding and screw pairs).</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> In a closed pair, the elements are held together mechanically (for example a pin in a hole, or a screw in a nut).</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> In a screw (helical) pair, the links have turning and sliding motion together, linked by the thread lead (Rattan's definition).</li><li><b>4 (correct):</b> An unclosed pair is kept in contact by gravity or a spring (for example a cam and follower held by a spring, or a body resting on a surface).</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Classification bases: by type of contact (lower/higher), by type of relative motion (sliding, turning, rolling, screw, spherical) and by mechanical constraint (closed/unclosed).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 2, 4, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 2, 3, 4.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> A screw pair has one degree of freedom: rotation and translation are not independent.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-27", year: 2022, no: 27, subject: "tom", topic: "Gyroscopic couple on a ship's turbine rotor",
q: `The turbine rotor of a ship has a mass of 3500 kg. It has a radius of gyration of 0·45 m and a speed of 3000 rpm clockwise when looking from stern. If the ship is steering to the left on a curve of 100 m radius at a speed of 36 km/h, then the gyroscopic couple is`,
opts: ["22·27 kN-m", "21·27 kN-m", "12·47 kN-m", "11·47 kN-m"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 22·27 kN m</h3><div class="formula">C = I ω ω<sub>p</sub> &nbsp;&nbsp; I = m k² &nbsp;&nbsp; ω<sub>p</sub> = v/R</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>m = 3500 kg; k = 0·45 m; N = 3000 rpm; v = 36 km/h = 10 m/s; R = 100 m (radius of the turn)</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>I = 3500 × 0·45² = 708·75 kg m².</li><li>ω = 2π × 3000/60 = 314·16 rad/s.</li><li>ω<sub>p</sub> = 10/100 = 0·1 rad/s.</li><li>C = 708·75 × 314·16 × 0·1 = 22,266 N m = <b>22·27 kN m</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Effect: with the rotor clockwise from the stern and the ship turning left (to port), the gyroscopic reaction raises the bow and depresses the stern.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 21·27 is an arithmetic slip; ms use a wrong I or ω<sub>p</sub>.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 12·47 ms use a wrong I or ω<sub>p</sub>.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 11·47 kN ms use a wrong I or ω<sub>p</sub>.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Rule: steering does not roll the ship; it pitches it (bow up or down depending on the turn and rotor direction).</p>` },

{ id: "2022-28", year: 2022, no: 28, subject: "tom", topic: "Types of governors",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding governors :<ol><li>A Wilson governor is a spring-controlled governor in which the vertical arms of the bell-crank lever are fitted with spring balls.</li><li>A Hartung governor is a spring-loaded type governor in which two bell-crank levers are pivoted at the ends of two arms which rotate with the spindle.</li><li>In a spring-controlled gravity governor, two bell-crank levers are pivoted on the moving sleeve.</li><li>In a Watt governor, a pair of balls (masses) is attached to a spindle with the help of links.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "3 and 4 only", "1, 3 and 4 only", "1, 2, 3 and 4"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 3 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Statement by statement (Rattan's definitions)</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (wrong):</b> A governor whose bell-crank vertical arms carry spring-loaded balls pressing against the frame is the <i>Hartung</i> governor. The Wilson–Hartnell governor has two balls connected directly by springs.</li><li><b>2 (wrong):</b> Two bell-crank levers pivoted at the ends of two arms rotating with the spindle, with a central spring on the sleeve, describes the <i>Hartnell</i> governor.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> In the spring-controlled gravity governor, two bell-crank levers are pivoted on the moving sleeve; their weight and a spring control the motion.</li><li><b>4 (correct):</b> The Watt governor is the simplest pendulum governor: two balls attached to the spindle by links (arms).</li></ul>
<p>Watt governor height: h = 895/N² m (N in rpm), so it is only practical at low speeds; spring-loaded governors work at high speeds.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, 2, which are incorrect; Omits 3, 4, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 3, 4.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 1, 2, which are incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Remember: Hartnell → central spring on the sleeve; Hartung → springs on the balls; Wilson–Hartnell → springs joining the balls.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-29", year: 2022, no: 29, subject: "tom", topic: "D'Alembert's principle",
q: `Which one of the following principles states that the inertia forces and couples, and external forces and torques on a body together give statical equilibrium?`,
opts: ["D'Alembert principle", "Paul Ehrlich principle", "David Hilbert principle", "Edward Jenner principle"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) D'Alembert principle</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> D'Alembert's principle turns a dynamics problem into a statics problem: adding the inertia force −m a<sub>G</sub> and the inertia couple −I<sub>G</sub> α to the external forces and torques gives a system in "dynamic equilibrium":</p>
<div class="formula">ΣF − m a<sub>G</sub> = 0 &nbsp;&nbsp; ΣM<sub>G</sub> − I<sub>G</sub> α = 0</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Paul Ehrlich was a physician and immunologist ("magic bullet" in chemotherapy).</li><li><b>(c)</b> David Hilbert was a mathematician (Hilbert spaces, Hilbert's problems).</li><li><b>(d)</b> Edward Jenner pioneered smallpox vaccination.</li></ul>
<p>The principle is the basis of dynamic force analysis of engine mechanisms (for example finding crank-pin forces and crankshaft torque).</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Inertia force acts through the centre of mass, opposite to its acceleration.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-30", year: 2022, no: 30, subject: "tom", topic: "Cycloid, epicycloid and hypocycloid",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding gears :<ol><li>A cycloid is the locus of a point on the circumference of a circle that rolls without slipping on a fixed straight line.</li><li>A hypocycloid is the locus of a point on the circumference of a circle that rolls without slipping on the circumference of another circle.</li><li>An epicycloid is the locus of a point on the circumference of a circle that rolls without slipping inside the circumference of another circle.</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 3 only", "2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 only"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> A cycloid is traced by a point on a circle rolling on a straight line.</li><li><b>2 (wrong):</b> A circle rolling <i>on</i> (outside) another circle traces an <i>epicycloid</i>. A hypocycloid needs rolling <i>inside</i>.</li><li><b>3 (wrong):</b> Rolling <i>inside</i> another circle gives a <i>hypocycloid</i>. Statements 2 and 3 have the names swapped.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> In cycloidal gears, the addendum (face) of a tooth is an epicycloid and the dedendum (flank) a hypocycloid, generated by the same rolling circle so that the teeth mesh with a constant velocity ratio.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 2, 3, which are incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> If the rolling circle's diameter is half the pitch circle's, the hypocycloid becomes a straight radial line (radial flanks).</p>` },

{ id: "2022-31", year: 2022, no: 31, subject: "tom", topic: "Cam angles",
q: `Which one of the following is the angle through which the cam turns during the time the follower rises?`,
opts: ["Angle of ascent", "Angle of dwell", "Angle of descent", "Angle of action"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Angle of ascent</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Cam angles over one revolution:</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Angle of ascent (rise): cam rotation while the follower rises.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Angle of dwell: cam rotation while the follower stays at rest (at the top or bottom).</li><li><b>(c)</b> Angle of descent (return): cam rotation while the follower falls.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Angle of action: the total rotation during which the follower moves = ascent + dwell (at the top) + descent.</li></ul>
<p>Example: a cam with rise 120°, dwell 30°, return 90° and dwell 120° has an angle of action of 240°.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> The follower displacement diagram (displacement vs cam angle) is drawn using these angles.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-32", year: 2022, no: 32, subject: "tom", topic: "Cam nomenclature",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding cams :<ol><li>Base circle is the smallest circle tangent to the cam profile (contour) drawn from the centre of rotation of a radial cam.</li><li>Pitch curve is the curve drawn by the trace point assuming that the cam is fixed, and the trace point of the follower rotates around the cam.</li><li>Pitch circle is the circle passing through the pitch point and concentric with the base circle.</li><li>The smallest circle drawn tangent to the pitch curve is known as the prime circle.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 3 only", "2 and 4 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2, 3 and 4"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2, 3 and 4</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> All four are standard definitions (Rattan, Khurmi):</p>
<ul><li><b>1:</b> Base circle: the smallest circle, centred on the cam axis, tangent to the cam profile; its size governs the cam's overall size.</li><li><b>2:</b> Pitch curve: the path of the trace point (roller centre or knife edge) relative to the cam, found by holding the cam fixed and rotating the follower around it (kinematic inversion).</li><li><b>3:</b> Pitch circle: the circle through the pitch point (the point of maximum pressure angle), concentric with the base circle.</li><li><b>4:</b> Prime circle: the smallest circle tangent to the pitch curve; for a roller follower its radius = base-circle radius + roller radius.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 2, 4, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 1, 3, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 1, 4, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3, 4.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> For a knife-edge follower, the pitch curve is the cam profile itself and the prime circle is the base circle.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-33", year: 2022, no: 33, subject: "tom", topic: "Hartmann and Bobillier constructions",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding acceleration analysis :<ol><li>A graphical method to find the location of the centre of curvature of the locus of a point on a moving body, is known as Bobillier construction.</li><li>A graphical method by which inflection circle can be drawn without requiring the curvatures of the centrodes, is known as Hartmann construction.</li><li>Bobillier theorem states that the angle subtended by one of the rays with the centrode tangent is equal to negative of the angle subtended by the other ray with the collineation axis.</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 3 only", "3 only", "2 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Advanced kinematics (Uicker–Pennock–Shigley, Rattan) uses the inflection circle and the Euler–Savary equation to find the curvature of point paths.</p>
<p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (wrong):</b> Locating the centre of curvature of the path of a point of a moving body, using the centrode curvature, is the <i>Hartmann</i> construction.</li><li><b>2 (wrong):</b> Drawing the inflection circle without knowing the centrode curvatures, using two pairs of conjugate points, is the <i>Bobillier</i> construction. Statements 1 and 2 have the names swapped.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> Bobillier's theorem: the angle between the centrode tangent and one ray equals the negative of the angle between the other ray and the collineation axis.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 3.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 1, 2, which are incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> The collineation axis joins the instantaneous centre to the intersection of the two rays (lines through conjugate point pairs).</p>` },

{ id: "2022-34", year: 2022, no: 34, subject: "tom", topic: "Grashof's criterion",
q: `Consider the following parameters :<br>s = length of the shortest link,<br>l = length of the longest link,<br>p = length of one of the intermediate length links,<br>q = length of the other intermediate length links;<br>According to the Grashof's criteria, if s + l &gt; p + q, then the category of four-bar mechanism is`,
opts: ["Double crank", "Crank-rocker", "Change point", "Triple rocker"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Triple rocker</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Grashof's law decides whether any link of a four-bar chain can rotate fully.</p>
<ul><li>s + l &lt; p + q (Grashof chain): the shortest link can rotate fully. Fixing a link next to the shortest gives a crank-rocker; fixing the shortest gives a double crank (drag link); fixing the link opposite the shortest gives a double rocker.</li><li>s + l = p + q: change-point mechanism (links can line up; the motion becomes uncertain at those positions).</li><li>s + l &gt; p + q (non-Grashof): no link can make a full revolution, whichever link is fixed. All inversions are <b>triple-rocker</b> mechanisms.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A double crank needs s + l < p + q with the shortest link fixed.</li><li><b>(b)</b> A crank-rocker needs s + l < p + q with a link next to the shortest fixed.</li><li><b>(c)</b> A change point needs s + l = p + q.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Check with numbers: links 4, 5, 6, 9 → 4 + 9 = 13 &gt; 11, so this is a triple rocker.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-35", year: 2022, no: 35, subject: "tom", topic: "Gyroscopic couple on a spinning disc",
q: `A uniform disc of 150 mm diameter has mass of 5 kg. It is mounted centrally in bearings which maintain its axle in a horizontal plane. The disc spins about its axle with a constant speed of 1000 rpm, while the axle precesses uniformly about the vertical at 60 rpm. The gyroscopic couple acting on the disc is approximately`,
opts: ["6·5 Nm", "7·2 Nm", "8·5 Nm", "9·2 Nm"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 9·2 N m</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Gyroscopic couple on a spinning disc.</p><div class="formula">C = I ω ω<sub>p</sub> &nbsp;&nbsp; I = ½ m r² (uniform disc about its axis)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>m = 5 kg; r = 0·075 m; ω = 2π × 1000/60 = 104·72 rad/s (spin); ω<sub>p</sub> = 2π × 60/60 = 6·283 rad/s (precession)</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>I = 0·5 × 5 × 0·075² = 0·01406 kg m².</li><li>C = 0·01406 × 104·72 × 6·283 = <b>9·25 N m</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 6·5 ms use a wrong moment of inertia or rounded speeds.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 7·2 ms use a wrong moment of inertia or rounded speeds.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 8·5 N ms use a wrong moment of inertia or rounded speeds.</li><li>Using I = m r² (a ring) would double the answer, to 18·5 N m.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> The couple acts in the vertical plane containing the axle; the bearings feel equal and opposite vertical reactions C/(bearing span).</p>` },

{ id: "2022-36", year: 2022, no: 36, subject: "tom", topic: "Dynamic balance of rotating masses",
q: `A system of rotating masses is in dynamic balance when`,
opts: ["there does not exist any resultant centrifugal force only", "there does not exist any resultant couple only", "there does not exist any resultant centrifugal force as well as resultant couple", "there exists both resultant centrifugal force and resultant couple"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) there does not exist any resultant centrifugal force as well as resultant couple</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Conditions for balancing masses rotating in several planes:</p>
<ul><li>Static balance: Σ m r = 0 (vector sum), so the centre of mass lies on the axis and there is no resultant centrifugal force.</li><li>Dynamic balance: in addition, Σ m r l = 0 (moments about a reference plane), so there is no resultant couple either.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> No resultant force alone is only <i>static</i> balance; a rotor can be statically balanced and still rock its bearings (for example two equal masses 180° apart in different planes).</li><li><b>(b)</b> No couple but a resultant force still loads the bearings.</li><li><b>(d)</b> describes a fully unbalanced system.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Any rotor can be dynamically balanced with two masses in two chosen planes (two-plane balancing).</p>` },

{ id: "2022-37", year: 2022, no: 37, subject: "design", topic: "Wear rating of a drum brake",
q: `Wear rating of a long shoe drum brake is`,
opts: ["directly proportional to friction power", "directly proportional to brake shoe area", "inversely proportional to friction torque on drum", "inversely proportional to friction power"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) directly proportional to friction power</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Mott rates brakes and clutches for wear by the friction power per unit area of the friction material:</p>
<div class="formula">WR = P<sub>f</sub>/A = (T ω)/A</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>P<sub>f</sub>: friction power (rate of energy dissipation); A: area of the brake lining; T: friction torque; ω: drum speed</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Wear rating is <i>inversely</i> proportional to the shoe area; more area spreads the energy.</li><li><b>(c)</b> It is <i>directly</i> proportional to friction torque (through P<sub>f</sub> = Tω).</li><li><b>(d)</b> is the reverse of the correct relation.</li></ul>
<p>Mott gives typical limits: about 0·04 hp/in² for frequent, severe service, up to 0·4 hp/in² for occasional light duty.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> The quantity p v (pressure × rubbing speed) is used in the same way for bearings and brakes.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-38", year: 2022, no: 38, subject: "design", topic: "Required basic dynamic load rating",
q: `What is the required basic dynamic load rating, C, for a ball bearing to carry a radial load of 550 kg from a shaft rotating at 500 rpm that is part of an assembly conveyor in a manufacturing plant? (Take the design life as 1 × 10⁹ rev)`,
opts: ["5000 kg", "5500 kg", "4000 kg", "4500 kg"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 5500 kg</h3><div class="formula">L = (C/P)<sup>k</sup> × 10⁶ rev → C = P (L<sub>d</sub>/10⁶)<sup>1/k</sup></div>
<ul class="sym"><li>P = 550 kg (radial load); L<sub>d</sub> = 10⁹ rev (design life); k = 3 for ball bearings</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>L<sub>d</sub>/10⁶ = 1000.</li><li>1000<sup>1/3</sup> = 10.</li><li>C = 550 × 10 = <b>5500 kg</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The speed 500 rpm is needed only to convert the life into hours: 10⁹/(500 × 60) = 33,333 h, typical for machines in continuous service.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 5000 is below the required rating; choosing them would shorten the life (for example C = 5000 gives (5000/550)³ = 751 million rev).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 4000 is below the required rating; choosing them would shorten the life (for example C = 5000 gives (5000/550)³ = 751 million rev).</li><li><b>(d)</b> 4500 kg is below the required rating; choosing them would shorten the life (for example C = 5000 gives (5000/550)³ = 751 million rev).</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> This is Mott's worked example; in practice you then pick a catalogue bearing with C ≥ 5500 kg.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-39", year: 2022, no: 39, subject: "design", topic: "L10 life of a ball bearing",
q: `A catalog lists the basic dynamic load rating for a ball bearing to be 8000 kg for a rated life of 1 × 10⁶ rev. What is the expected L<sub>10</sub> life of the bearing if it is subjected to a load of 4000 kg? (Take k = 3 for ball bearing)`,
opts: ["8 × 10⁶ rev", "6 × 10⁶ rev", "4 × 10⁶ rev", "2 × 10⁶ rev"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 8 × 10⁶ rev</h3><div class="formula">L<sub>10</sub> = (C/P)<sup>k</sup> × 10⁶ rev</div>
<p><b>Concept:</b> L<sub>10</sub> = (8000/4000)³ × 10⁶ = 2³ × 10⁶ = <b>8 × 10⁶ rev</b>.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>(8000/4000)³ = 8.</li><li>L₁₀ = <b>8 × 10⁶ rev</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(d)</b> 2 × 10⁶ rev assumes life is proportional to the load ratio (k = 1).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 4 × 10⁶ rev uses k = 2.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 6 × 10⁶ would need an exponent of 2.58.</li></ul>
<p>L<sub>10</sub> is the life that 90% of a group of identical bearings will reach or exceed (10% failure). Halving the load multiplies the life by 8 for ball bearings, and by 2<sup>10/3</sup> ≈ 10 for roller bearings (k = 10/3).</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Bearing life is very sensitive to load: a 10% overload cuts ball-bearing life by about 25%.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-40", year: 2022, no: 40, subject: "design", topic: "Failure theory for ductile materials",
q: `Which one of the following static loading failure theories is used for ductile materials?`,
opts: ["Maximum shear stress theory", "Maximum normal stress theory", "Coulomb-mohr theory", "Modified mohr theory"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Maximum shear stress theory</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Ductile materials fail by yielding, which is controlled by shear. The maximum shear stress (Tresca) and distortion energy (von Mises) theories are therefore used for them; Tresca is the slightly more conservative of the two.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Maximum normal stress (Rankine) theory suits brittle materials.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Coulomb–Mohr (internal friction) theory suits brittle materials with unequal tensile and compressive strengths.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Modified Mohr theory suits brittle materials.</li></ul>
<p>Mott's guide: ductile material, static load → distortion energy (most accurate) or maximum shear stress (conservative); brittle → modified Mohr.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Tresca: τ<sub>max</sub> = S<sub>y</sub>/(2N). von Mises: σ′ = √(σ<sub>1</sub>² − σ<sub>1</sub>σ<sub>2</sub> + σ<sub>2</sub>²) ≤ S<sub>y</sub>/N.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-41", year: 2022, no: 41, subject: "design", topic: "Shaft length, deflection and critical speed",
q: `In shaft rigidity and dynamic considerations, shorter shaft lengths`,
opts: ["increase deflections and reduce critical speeds", "increase deflections and raise critical speeds", "reduce deflections and reduce critical speeds", "reduce deflections and raise critical speeds"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) reduce deflections and raise critical speeds</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Shaft deflection under a load grows with the cube of the span (for example δ = WL³/48EI for a central load). A shorter shaft is much stiffer, so it deflects less, and since the critical speed is related to stiffness by ω<sub>c</sub> = √(g/δ), its critical speed rises.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> describes the effect of <i>longer</i> shafts.</li><li><b>(b)</b> mixes the effects: deflection and critical speed always move in opposite directions.</li><li><b>(c)</b> mixes the effects: deflection and critical speed always move in opposite directions.</li></ul>
<p>Mott's design advice: keep shafts short, place bearings close to the loads, and use larger diameters where stiffness matters (gears, for correct meshing; machine tools, for accuracy).</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Halving the span reduces central deflection 8 times and raises the critical speed by √8 ≈ 2·8 times.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-42", year: 2022, no: 42, subject: "design", topic: "Improving wear of brakes",
q: `Wear performance of the brakes can be improved to`,
opts: ["keep the pressure between the friction material and the material of the disc or drum as high as practical", "specify friction materials that have low bonding strength between constituent particles", "specify friction materials that have relatively high adhesion when in contact with the disc or drum material", "provide high hardness on the surface of the disc or drum by heat treatment"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) provide high hardness on the surface of the disc or drum by heat treatment</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Mott's guidelines for good wear performance of brakes and clutches: keep the contact pressure <i>low</i>; use friction materials with <i>high</i> bonding strength between their particles; choose materials with <i>low</i> adhesion to the mating disc or drum; give the disc or drum a hard surface (heat treatment) and a fine finish; and keep the operating temperature down.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> High pressure increases wear; pressure should be kept as <i>low</i> as practical.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Low bonding strength lets particles tear out: faster wear.</li><li><b>(c)</b> High adhesion promotes adhesive wear and material transfer.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Each wrong option is the reverse of one of Mott's recommendations.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-43", year: 2022, no: 43, subject: "design", topic: "Parameters for rating clutches and brakes",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding the parameters involved in the rating of clutches and brakes :<ol><li>Torque required to accelerate or decelerate the system.</li><li>Time required to accomplish the speed change.</li><li>The cycling rate is required.</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["2 only", "1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Mott lists the parameters involved in rating a clutch or brake:</p>
<ul><li><b>1:</b> The torque required to accelerate or decelerate the system.</li><li><b>2:</b> The time required for the speed change.</li><li><b>3:</b> The cycling rate: the number of on–off cycles per unit time (it decides the heat to be dissipated).</li></ul>
<p>The others in Mott's list: the inertia of the rotating or translating parts, the environment (temperature, cooling), the energy-dissipation (heat) capability of the unit, physical size and configuration, actuation method, expected life and cost.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 1, 3, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Torque to accelerate: T = Wk² Δn/(308 t) in Mott's US units, or T = I Δω/t in SI.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-44", year: 2022, no: 44, subject: "design", topic: "Minimising the gear load-distribution factor",
q: `In the design of spur gear, the load-distribution factor can be minimized by specifying which of the following?<ol><li>Accurate teeth</li><li>Narrow face widths</li><li>Long shaft spans between bearings</li></ol>Select the correct answer using the code given below :`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The load-distribution factor K<sub>m</sub> accounts for uneven sharing of load across the face width. Mott lists ways to minimise it: accurate teeth (high quality number), narrow face widths, gears placed midway between bearings, <i>short</i> shaft spans between bearings, large shaft diameters (high stiffness), rigid housings, and gears with small runout.</p>
<p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> Accurate teeth share load more evenly.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> A narrow face is less sensitive to misalignment.</li><li><b>3 (wrong):</b> <i>Long</i> spans increase shaft deflection and misalignment; short spans are needed.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Usual face-width limits: 8m &lt; F &lt; 16m (module m), often F ≈ 12m.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-45", year: 2022, no: 45, subject: "design", topic: "Fatigue criteria: graphical method", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Which one of the following fatigue failure criterias is used in graphical method equation?`,
opts: ["Soderberg fatigue failure criteria", "Goodman fatigue failure criteria", "Smith diagram fatigue failure criteria", "Gerber fatigue failure criteria"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Smith diagram fatigue failure criteria</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The Soderberg, Goodman and Gerber criteria are written as equations relating alternating and mean stress:</p>
<div class="formula">Soderberg: σ<sub>a</sub>/S<sub>e</sub> + σ<sub>m</sub>/S<sub>y</sub> = 1/N &nbsp;&nbsp; Goodman: σ<sub>a</sub>/S<sub>e</sub> + σ<sub>m</sub>/S<sub>u</sub> = 1/N &nbsp;&nbsp; Gerber: Nσ<sub>a</sub>/S<sub>e</sub> + (Nσ<sub>m</sub>/S<sub>u</sub>)² = 1</div>
<p>The Smith diagram is a graphical construction: it plots the maximum and minimum stresses that can be sustained against the mean stress, so the allowable stress range is read directly from the chart. It is the criterion that is used as a graphical method.</p>
<p><b>Option by option</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Soderberg: straight line from S<sub>e</sub> to S<sub>y</sub>; the most conservative.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Goodman: straight line from S<sub>e</sub> to S<sub>u</sub>; the most commonly used in design (Mott recommends it).</li><li><b>(c)</b> Smith diagram: the graphical representation (the key's answer).</li><li><b>(d)</b> Gerber: parabola from S<sub>e</sub> to S<sub>u</sub>; fits test data best for ductile metals.</li></ul>
<p>Our first attempt chose (b) Goodman, because Goodman lines are usually drawn on a diagram. Checking the definitions, the Smith diagram is the criterion that is itself a graphical method, while the others are equation-based, so we accepted the key.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Haigh diagram: σ<sub>a</sub> vs σ<sub>m</sub>; Smith diagram: σ<sub>max</sub> and σ<sub>min</sub> vs σ<sub>m</sub>. Both carry the same information.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-46", year: 2022, no: 46, subject: "design", topic: "One-way clutches: backstopping", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Which one of the following is used in case in which the clutch runs free when the machine is being driven in the intended direction?`,
opts: ["Overrunning", "Backstopping", "Under running", "Front stopping"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Backstopping</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Mott describes three uses of one-way (freewheel) clutches:</p>
<ul><li><b>Backstopping:</b> the clutch runs free while the machine is driven in the intended direction, but locks if the drive stops and the load tries to reverse it. Examples: inclined conveyors, bucket elevators and hoists, which must not run backwards.</li><li><b>Overrunning:</b> the clutch transmits torque from the driver, but lets the driven member run faster than the driver (for example a bicycle freewheel, or a starter motor drive).</li><li><b>Indexing:</b> an oscillating input gives intermittent one-way rotation of the output.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> In overrunning, the clutch drives in one direction and lets the driven member run faster; it is not a reverse-rotation stop.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 'Under running' is not a standard clutch term.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 'Front stopping' is not a standard clutch term.</li></ul>
<p>Our first attempt chose (a), since both involve running free. Mott's definition matches this question's wording exactly to backstopping, so we accepted the key.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> A backstop is fixed to the frame on one side, so it acts as a one-way brake.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-47", year: 2022, no: 47, subject: "design", topic: "Torque in a band brake",
q: `In a band brake, the tension in the band decreases from the value P<sub>1</sub> at the pivot side of the band to P<sub>2</sub> at the lever side. If r is the radius of the drum, then the net torque on the drum is`,
opts: ["(P<sub>1</sub> − P<sub>2</sub>)/r", "(P<sub>1</sub> − P<sub>2</sub>)r", "(P<sub>1</sub> + P<sub>2</sub>)/r", "(P<sub>1</sub> + P<sub>2</sub>)r"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) (P<sub>1</sub> − P<sub>2</sub>)r</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The two band tensions act tangentially to the drum at radius r in opposite senses; the friction torque is the difference of their moments.</p>
<div class="formula">T = (P<sub>1</sub> − P<sub>2</sub>) r &nbsp;&nbsp; P<sub>1</sub>/P<sub>2</sub> = e<sup>fθ</sup></div>
<ul class="sym"><li>f: coefficient of friction; θ: angle of wrap (radians)</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>The two tensions act at radius r in opposite senses.</li><li>T = <b>(P₁ − P₂)r</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> divides by r, which gives force per length, not torque.</li><li><b>(c)</b> divides by r, which gives force per length, not torque.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The sum of tensions gives the resultant load on the drum shaft (for 180° wrap), not the torque.</li></ul>
<p>Maximum pressure on the lining: p<sub>max</sub> = P<sub>1</sub>/(r w), with w the band width.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> A band brake is self-energising when the drum turns so as to pull the band tighter at the lever end.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-48", year: 2022, no: 48, subject: "design", topic: "Types of transmission shafts",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding transmission shafts :<ol><li>Counter shaft is secondary shaft which is driven by the main shaft from which the power is supplied to a machine component.</li><li>Jack shaft is an intermediate shaft between two shafts that is used in transmission of power.</li><li>A line shaft consists of a number of shafts, which are connected in an axial direction by means of couplings.</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> All three are Bhandari's definitions of transmission shafts:</p>
<ul><li><b>1:</b> A countershaft is a secondary shaft driven by the main shaft, from which power goes to a machine component.</li><li><b>2:</b> A jack shaft is an intermediate shaft between two shafts, used for transmitting power.</li><li><b>3:</b> A line shaft is a long shaft made of several shafts joined end to end by couplings, driving several machines (for example in old workshops, through belts).</li></ul>
<p>Shafts are distinguished from axles (which carry bending loads but transmit no torque, like a railway wagon axle) and spindles (short rotating shafts, as in machine tools).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 1, 3, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Transmission shafts carry combined bending and torsion; they are designed with the ASME code or equivalent-moment methods.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-49", year: 2022, no: 49, subject: "mfg", topic: "Forward slip in rolling",
q: `In the rolling process, if V<sub>r</sub> is the velocity of roll surface, V<sub>0</sub> is the velocity of material at the entrance to the deformation zone and V<sub>1</sub> is the velocity of material at the exit of the rolls, then the forward slip is`,
opts: ["V<sub>r</sub>/(V<sub>1</sub> − V<sub>r</sub>) × 100 percent", "V<sub>r</sub>/(V<sub>r</sub> − V<sub>0</sub>) × 100 percent", "(V<sub>1</sub> − V<sub>r</sub>)/V<sub>r</sub> × 100 percent", "(V<sub>r</sub> − V<sub>0</sub>)/V<sub>r</sub> × 100 percent"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) (V<sub>1</sub> − V<sub>r</sub>)/V<sub>r</sub> × 100 percent</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Volume constancy means the strip speeds up as it thins: it enters slower than the roll surface (V<sub>0</sub> &lt; V<sub>r</sub>) and leaves faster (V<sub>1</sub> &gt; V<sub>r</sub>). At one point, the neutral (no-slip) point, strip and roll speeds are equal.</p>
<div class="formula">Forward slip = (V<sub>1</sub> − V<sub>r</sub>)/V<sub>r</sub> &nbsp;&nbsp; Backward slip = (V<sub>r</sub> − V<sub>0</sub>)/V<sub>r</sub></div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(d)</b> is the <i>backward</i> slip, at the entry side.</li><li><b>(a)</b> is inverted ratios with no physical meaning.</li><li><b>(b)</b> is inverted ratios with no physical meaning.</li></ul>
<p>Friction acts towards the neutral point from both sides; the friction hill of roll pressure peaks there.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Volume constancy: b h<sub>0</sub> V<sub>0</sub> = b h<sub>1</sub> V<sub>1</sub> (width constant in flat rolling).</p>` },

{ id: "2022-50", year: 2022, no: 50, subject: "mfg", topic: "Interference (force) fits",
q: `Which one of the following fits is used for high-strength assemblies where high resulting pressures are required?`,
opts: ["Light drive fit", "Medium drive fit", "Heavy drive fit", "Force fit"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Force fit</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> ANSI classes of interference (FN) fits, in increasing tightness:</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> FN1, light drive fit: light assembly pressures, permanent assemblies; thin sections or long fits, and cast-iron external members.</li><li><b>(b)</b> FN2, medium drive fit: ordinary steel parts, or shrink fits on light sections.</li><li><b>(c)</b> FN3, heavy drive fit: heavier steel parts, or shrink fits in medium sections.</li><li><b>(d)</b> FN4 and FN5, force fits: parts that can be highly stressed, giving high-strength assemblies with high interface pressures; shrink fits are used where pressing would need impractical forces.</li></ul>
<p>The holding power of an interference fit comes from the contact pressure p, which is found from the interference δ and the elastic properties (Lamé's equations for thick cylinders).</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Torque capacity of an interference fit: T = 2π r² L f p.</p>` }
);

