window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2022-51", year: 2022, no: 51, subject: "mfg", topic: "PERT time estimates",
q: `In PERT, which one of the following is the estimator expects that he may come across some sort of uncertainties and many a time the things will go right?`,
opts: ["Optimistic time", "Pessimistic time", "Likely time", "Unlikely time"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Likely time</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> PERT uses three time estimates for each activity:</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Optimistic time t<sub>o</sub>: the shortest time, if everything goes well with no difficulties.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Pessimistic time t<sub>p</sub>: the longest time, if things go badly (excluding major catastrophes).</li><li><b>(c)</b> Most likely time t<sub>m</sub>: the time under normal conditions, where the estimator expects some uncertainties but things will often go right. This matches the question.</li><li><b>(d)</b> "Unlikely time" is not a PERT estimate.</li></ul>
<div class="formula">t<sub>e</sub> = (t<sub>o</sub> + 4t<sub>m</sub> + t<sub>p</sub>)/6 &nbsp;&nbsp; σ = (t<sub>p</sub> − t<sub>o</sub>)/6</div>
<p>The weighting comes from the β distribution assumed for activity times; the most likely time is its mode.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Example: t<sub>o</sub> = 4, t<sub>m</sub> = 6, t<sub>p</sub> = 14 days → t<sub>e</sub> = 7 days, σ = 1·67 days.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-52", year: 2022, no: 52, subject: "mfg", topic: "VED analysis",
q: `In which one of the following, the criticality of the item is most important than the cost factor of the item?`,
opts: ["ABC analysis", "VED analysis", "p system", "q system"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) VED analysis</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> VED (Vital, Essential, Desirable) classifies inventory by how critical an item is to production: a vital spare whose shortage stops the plant must always be stocked, whatever its cost.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> ABC analysis classifies items by annual consumption value (cost), not criticality.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The p system (periodic review) is an ordering policy, not a criticality ranking.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The q system (fixed order quantity, reorder point) is an ordering policy, not a criticality ranking.</li></ul>
<p>VED is especially used for spare parts. It is often combined with ABC in a matrix (for example a "C–V" item is cheap but vital, so a generous stock is kept).</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Other classifications: FSN (fast, slow, non-moving: by usage rate), HML (high, medium, low unit price), SDE (scarce, difficult, easy to buy).</p>` },

{ id: "2022-53", year: 2022, no: 53, subject: "mfg", topic: "Core prints",
q: `Which one of the following is the projections on a pattern that is used to make recesses in the mould to locate the core?`,
opts: ["Sprue", "Core print", "Gate", "Riser"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Core print</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Core prints are extra projections on the pattern. They form recesses (core seats) in the mould, in which the ends of the core rest, so the core is located and supported in the right position.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The sprue is the vertical channel through which molten metal is poured down into the mould.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The gate is the channel through which metal enters the mould cavity from the runner.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The riser (feeder) is a reservoir that supplies liquid metal to make up for shrinkage during solidification.</li></ul>
<p>When a core cannot be fully supported by core prints (for example a long horizontal core), chaplets (small metal supports that fuse into the casting) are used.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Core prints are not part of the casting; they are painted a different colour on the pattern.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-54", year: 2022, no: 54, subject: "mfg", topic: "Permanent mould casting: disadvantage",
q: `Which one of the following is a disadvantage of permanent mold or gravity die casting?`,
opts: ["The surface of casting becomes hard due to chilling effect", "Good surface finish and surface details are obtained", "The process requires more labor", "Fast rate of production can be attained"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) The surface of casting becomes hard due to chilling effect</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In permanent (metal) moulds the metal is chilled rapidly at the mould wall. In cast irons this can produce a hard, white-iron skin, which is difficult to machine; this is listed as a disadvantage (Rao, Rajput).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Good surface finish and fine detail are <i>advantages</i> of metal moulds.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The process needs <i>less</i> labour than sand casting (no new mould for each casting).</li><li><b>(d)</b> Fast production rates are an advantage.</li></ul>
<p>Other disadvantages: high mould cost (economical only in large quantities), limited size and shape complexity, and suitability mainly for low-melting alloys (Al, Mg, Cu, Zn). Advantages also include close tolerances and fine-grained, stronger castings.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Mould coatings and preheating are used to reduce chilling and help metal flow.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-55", year: 2022, no: 55, subject: "tom", topic: "Critical speed of a shaft",
q: `In critical speed of a light shaft having a single disc without damping, the critical speed of the shaft is`,
opts: ["equal to the natural frequency of the system in longitudinal vibration", "equal to the natural frequency of the system in torsional vibration", "equal to the natural frequency of lateral vibration of the shaft", "no relationship to any of the natural frequency systems"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) equal to the natural frequency of lateral vibration of the shaft</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A disc mounted with a small eccentricity e whirls at radius y = ω² e/(ω<sub>n</sub>² − ω²). The deflection becomes unbounded (without damping) when ω equals ω<sub>n</sub> = √(k/m), the natural frequency of <i>transverse</i> (lateral) vibration of the shaft–disc system.</p>
<div class="formula">ω<sub>c</sub> = √(k/m) = √(g/δ)</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Longitudinal vibration is along the axis and involves different (much higher) stiffness.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Torsional vibration involves twisting; its natural frequency is unrelated to whirling.</li><li><b>(d)</b> There is a direct relationship, as shown above.</li></ul>
<p>Above the critical speed, the disc centre moves towards the axis of rotation and the shaft runs more smoothly; turbines are often run above their first critical speed.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> N<sub>c</sub> (rpm) ≈ 946/√δ, with δ in mm (static deflection).</p>` },

{ id: "2022-56", year: 2022, no: 56, subject: "mfg", topic: "Chip types: built-up edge",
q: `Which one of the following is formed due to large friction and stronger adhesion between chips and tool face?`,
opts: ["Continuous chip", "Discontinuous chip", "Continuous chip with built-up edge", "Discontinuous chip with built-up edge"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Continuous chip with built-up edge</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> When machining ductile metals at low cutting speed, high friction and strong adhesion (pressure welding) at the chip–tool interface make layers of work material stick to the tool tip. This built-up edge grows, becomes unstable and breaks away periodically, spoiling the surface finish.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Plain continuous chips form with ductile metals at high speed, a sharp tool, large rake angle and good lubrication (low friction).</li><li><b>(b)</b> Discontinuous chips form with brittle materials (cast iron, brass) or at very low speeds and large feeds.</li><li><b>(d)</b> is not a standard chip classification.</li></ul>
<p>Built-up edge is reduced by higher cutting speeds, larger rake angles, cutting fluids and smoother tool faces.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Built-up edge slightly protects the tool but worsens surface finish and dimensional accuracy.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-57", year: 2022, no: 57, subject: "mfg", topic: "Pioneers of quality management",
q: `Total Quality Management (TQM) and Quality assurance are the responsibility of everyone involved in designing and manufacturing of the product. Who among the following pioneers has NOT been quality control heightened?`,
opts: ["Deming", "Taguchi", "Juran", "B. F. Skinner"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) B. F. Skinner</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> B. F. Skinner was a behavioural psychologist (operant conditioning), not a quality pioneer.</p>
<p><b>The quality pioneers</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> W. Edwards Deming: statistical process control, the 14 points of management and the PDCA (Plan–Do–Check–Act) cycle; very influential in post-war Japan.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Genichi Taguchi: robust design and the quality loss function L = k(y − m)².</li><li><b>(c)</b> Joseph Juran: the quality trilogy (planning, control, improvement), "fitness for use" and the Pareto principle applied to quality.</li></ul>
<p>Others: Philip Crosby (zero defects, "quality is free"), Kaoru Ishikawa (cause-and-effect diagram, quality circles), Armand Feigenbaum (total quality control) and Walter Shewhart (control charts).</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Quality is designed in, not inspected in: the central idea of TQM.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-58", year: 2022, no: 58, subject: "mfg", topic: "Single-point tool angles: end relief",
q: `Which one of the following is the angle between the planes of end flank immediately below the end cutting edge and line perpendicular to the base and right angle to the axis?`,
opts: ["Back rake angle", "Side rake angle", "End relief angle", "Side relief angle"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) End relief angle</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The end relief (end clearance) angle is between the end flank, just below the end cutting edge, and a line perpendicular to the base at right angles to the tool axis. It stops the end flank rubbing on the machined surface.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Back rake angle: slope of the tool face measured in a plane parallel to the tool axis.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Side rake angle: slope of the tool face measured perpendicular to the tool axis.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Side relief angle: between the side flank, below the side cutting edge, and a line perpendicular to the base, measured at right angles to the axis.</li></ul>
<p>ASA tool signature order: back rake, side rake, end relief, side relief, end cutting edge angle, side cutting edge angle, nose radius (for example 8-14-6-6-6-15-1).</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Relief angles are small (about 5–8°): too small causes rubbing, too large weakens the cutting edge.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-59", year: 2022, no: 59, subject: "mfg", topic: "Stepless drives in machine tools",
q: `Consider the following statements related to stepless drive of machine tools :<ol><li>The spindle speeds available are fixed, it is not possible to use optimum cutting speeds with any of the workpiece diameters.</li><li>Charging [changing] the axial distance of the discs will vary the point of contact between the belt and disc.</li><li>The surface finish achieved will not be uniform.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The statements come from a textbook passage on why stepless drives are needed and how they work:</p>
<ul><li><b>1:</b> With a stepped drive, the spindle speeds are fixed, so the optimum cutting speed cannot be used for every workpiece diameter. This is the reason for using stepless drives.</li><li><b>2:</b> In a variable-pitch (PIV-type) pulley drive, moving the cone discs axially changes the radius at which the belt contacts them, so the speed ratio changes smoothly.</li><li><b>3:</b> With fixed speed steps, the cutting speed changes with diameter, for example in facing, so the surface finish is not uniform. A stepless drive keeps the cutting speed constant.</li></ul>
<p>Statements 1 and 3 describe the limitations of stepped drives that stepless drives overcome; statement 2 describes the stepless mechanism. Read as a passage on stepless drives, all three are taken as correct, as the key gives.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Stepless drives: variable-speed belts (PIV), hydraulic drives, and DC or VFD-controlled AC motors.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-60", year: 2022, no: 60, subject: "design", topic: "Machine vibration protection and isolators",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding machine vibration :<ol><li>If the mechanical stresses are below the acceptable safe working stress levels of the materials involved, no direct protection methods are required.</li><li>If the stresses exceed the safe levels, corrective measures such as stiffening, reduction of inertia and bending moment effects, and incorporation of further support members, as well as possible uses of isolators, may be required.</li><li>Shock isolators differ from vibration isolators in that shock requires a stiffer spring and a higher natural frequency for the resilient element.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Machine vibration protection and isolators.</p><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> If vibration stresses stay below the safe working stress, the structure tolerates them and no direct protection is needed (other than for comfort or noise).</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> If stresses exceed safe levels, remedies include stiffening, reducing inertia and bending-moment effects, adding supports and fitting isolators.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> A shock isolator must store a large burst of energy over a short time, so it uses a stiffer spring with a higher natural frequency than a vibration isolator. A vibration isolator needs a soft spring, with a natural frequency well below the excitation frequency (ω/ω<sub>n</sub> &gt; √2) to reduce transmissibility.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Transmissibility T = 1/|1 − r²| (undamped); isolation begins only when r = ω/ω<sub>n</sub> &gt; √2.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-61", year: 2022, no: 61, subject: "mechatronics", topic: "Microcontrollers, buses and microcomputers",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding microprocessors and microcontrollers :<ol><li>A microcontroller is a single, very large-scale integrated chip that contains programmable electronic components.</li><li>Address bus carries the signals relating to control actions.</li><li>A microcomputer consists of a central processing unit, I/O interface and a memory block.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> A microcontroller puts the CPU, memory (RAM, ROM/flash), I/O ports, timers and often ADCs on one VLSI chip: a "computer on a chip" for embedded control.</li><li><b>2 (wrong):</b> The address bus carries the <i>address</i> of the memory location or I/O device being accessed. Control signals (read, write, clock, interrupt) travel on the <i>control bus</i>.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> A microcomputer consists of a CPU (microprocessor), memory and input/output interfaces, linked by buses.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Bus summary: data bus (bidirectional, carries data words), address bus (one-way from the CPU; n lines address 2<sup>n</sup> locations), control bus (timing and control signals).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 3.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> A 16-bit address bus can address 2¹⁶ = 65,536 (64 K) locations.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-62", year: 2022, no: 62, subject: "mechatronics", topic: "Microprocessor instructions",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding microprocessor instructions :<ol><li>Compare instruction reads the contents of a particular memory location and copied to a specific register in the processor.</li><li>Jumps instruction changes the sequence in which the program is being carried out.</li><li>Decrement instruction subtracts 1 from the contents of a specified location.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Statement by statement (Bolton, <i>Mechatronics</i>)</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (wrong):</b> Reading a memory location and copying it to a register is the <i>load</i> instruction. A compare instruction compares a value with a register's contents (by subtraction without storing the result) and sets the status flags.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> Jump (branch) instructions change the order in which the program is executed, conditionally or unconditionally.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> Decrement subtracts 1 from the contents of a specified location or register (increment adds 1).</li></ul>
<p>Instruction groups: data transfer (load, store, move), arithmetic (add, subtract, increment, decrement, compare), logical (AND, OR, NOT, shift, rotate) and program control (jump, branch, call, return, halt).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 2, 3.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Compare + conditional jump together form the basis of loops and decisions in assembly language.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-63", year: 2022, no: 63, subject: "mechatronics", topic: "Characteristics of transducers",
q: `Which one of the following statements is correct regarding characteristic parameters used in transducers?`,
opts: ["Span is the deviation of the true value from the desired value", "Precision is defined as the degree of exactness for which an instrument is designed or intended to perform", "Sensitivity is the difference in the output for a given input when the value is approached from the opposite direction", "Hysteresis of an instrument is defining as the ratio of the magnitude of the output signal to the magnitude of the input signal"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Precision is the degree of exactness for which an instrument is designed or intended to perform: how closely repeated readings of the same quantity agree (Rangan, Sarma and Mani).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Span is the algebraic difference between the upper and lower limits of the range (for example 0–200 °C has a span of 200 °C). The deviation of the measured value from the true value is the error.</li><li><b>(c)</b> describes <i>hysteresis</i>: the difference in output for the same input approached from opposite directions.</li><li><b>(d)</b> describes <i>sensitivity</i> (output/input, the slope of the calibration curve). Options (c) and (d) have the two definitions swapped.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Accuracy = closeness to the true value; precision = closeness of repeated readings to each other.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-64", year: 2022, no: 64, subject: "mechatronics", topic: "Dynamic characteristics of sensors",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding dynamic quantities in sensors and transducers :<ol><li>The maximum amount by which the moving parts move beyond the steady state is known as over shoot.</li><li>An output whose magnitude does not repeat with time is known as transient.</li><li>An output whose magnitude has a definite repeating time cycle is called steady state periodic.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> Overshoot is the maximum amount by which the moving system (pointer, output) goes beyond its final steady value; it is usually expressed as a percentage of that value.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> A transient is an output whose magnitude does not repeat with time; it dies away after a change in input.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> A steady-state periodic output repeats in a definite time cycle (for example the response to a sinusoidal input after transients have died out).</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Other dynamic characteristics: speed of response, measuring lag (time delay), fidelity (faithful reproduction of the input) and dynamic error.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> An instrument with damping ratio about 0·6–0·7 gives a fast response with small overshoot.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-65", year: 2022, no: 65, subject: "mechatronics", topic: "Encoders",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding encoders :<ol><li>An encoder is a device that provides a digital output in response to a linear or angular displacement.</li><li>A digital optical encoder is a device that converts motion into a sequence of digital pulses.</li><li>An incremental encoder produces equally spaced pulses from one or more concentric tracks on the code disk.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> Encoders give a digital output for a linear or angular displacement (Bolton).</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> An optical encoder uses a light source, a slotted or patterned disc and photodetectors to turn motion into a train of digital pulses.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> An incremental encoder has one or more tracks (typically A and B in quadrature, plus an index Z) of equally spaced windows; counting pulses gives displacement, and the phase of A relative to B gives direction.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> An absolute encoder, by contrast, has several concentric tracks forming a unique binary (often Gray) code for each position, so it gives position directly even after power loss.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Resolution of an incremental encoder with N slots = 360°/N (×4 with quadrature decoding).</p>` },

{ id: "2022-66", year: 2022, no: 66, subject: "mechatronics", topic: "Types of accelerometers",
q: `Which one of the following statements is NOT correct regarding accelerometers?`,
opts: ["In displacement seismic accelerometer, the displacement of seismic mass is measured by displacement transducer itself", "In strain gauge accelerometer, the seismic mass is placed on a cantilever beam placed inside the housing", "In potentiometric accelerometer, in a spring-mass-damper system, the mass is connected with the wiper arm of the potentiometer", "The LVDT accelerometer consist of one primary and four secondary windings which are placed on either side of a central core"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> An LVDT has <b>one primary and two secondary</b> windings, placed symmetrically on either side of the primary, with a movable core. In an LVDT accelerometer the seismic mass is (or carries) the core, so the differential output of the two secondaries measures the mass displacement, which is proportional to acceleration.</p>
<p><b>The correct statements</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> In a seismic accelerometer, the relative displacement of the seismic mass is measured by a displacement transducer; below the natural frequency it is proportional to acceleration.</li><li><b>(b)</b> In a strain-gauge accelerometer, the mass sits at the end of a cantilever inside the housing; gauges on the beam measure the bending strain.</li><li><b>(c)</b> In a potentiometric accelerometer, the mass of the spring–mass–damper system is attached to the wiper arm of a potentiometer.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Seismic accelerometers need ω<sub>n</sub> well above the frequency being measured; vibrometers (displacement pickups) need ω<sub>n</sub> well below it.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-67", year: 2022, no: 67, subject: "mechatronics", topic: "Flow control valves",
q: `Which one of the following valves restricts or throttles the fluid in a particular direction to influence the volumetric flow of the fluid?`,
opts: ["Check valve", "Flow control valve", "Quick exhaust valve", "Sequence valve"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Flow control valve</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A flow control valve throttles the flow (often in one direction only, with a bypass check valve for free return flow) to control the volumetric flow rate, and hence the speed of an actuator such as a cylinder.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A check (non-return) valve lets fluid flow freely in one direction and blocks it in the other; it does not throttle.</li><li><b>(c)</b> A quick exhaust valve dumps air from a cylinder directly to atmosphere, increasing cylinder speed.</li><li><b>(d)</b> A sequence valve opens a second circuit only after the pressure in the first reaches a set value, to make operations happen in order.</li></ul>
<p>Speed control can be meter-in (throttling the supply) or meter-out (throttling the exhaust); meter-out gives steadier motion with overrunning loads.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Pressure-compensated flow control valves keep the flow constant despite load changes.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-68", year: 2022, no: 68, subject: "mechatronics", topic: "PLC control instructions",
q: `Which one of the following instructions is conditional instruction which allow the user to change the order in which the processor scans the program?`,
opts: ["Sequence instruction", "Communications instruction", "Control instruction", "Arithmetic instruction"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Control instruction</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> PLC (program) control instructions such as jump (JMP) and label (LBL), master control reset (MCR), subroutine call (JSR) and return are conditional instructions: when their rung is true, they change the order in which the processor scans the program, for example skipping a block of rungs.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Sequencer instructions step outputs through a fixed pattern (like a drum switch); they do not change the scan order.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Communication instructions send and receive data between PLCs or devices.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Arithmetic instructions (add, subtract, multiply, divide) process data.</li></ul>
<p>PLC scan cycle: read inputs → execute program rungs in order → update outputs → housekeeping and communications.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Jumps are used to skip parts of the program that are not needed, reducing scan time.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-69", year: 2022, no: 69, subject: "mechatronics", topic: "Set-point signals",
q: `Which one of the following signals are external commands signals provided to the controller?`,
opts: ["Control signals", "Controlled signals", "Disturbance signals", "Setpoint signals"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Setpoint signals</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The set point (reference input) is the external command telling the controller the desired value of the controlled variable (for example a room temperature of 22 °C). The controller compares it with the feedback signal to form the error.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Control signals (manipulated variables) are the controller's <i>outputs</i> to the actuator.</li><li><b>(b)</b> The controlled signal is the output of the plant (the variable being controlled).</li><li><b>(c)</b> Disturbances are unwanted inputs entering the plant (for example a door opening), not commands.</li></ul>
<div class="formula">Error e = set point − measured value; controller output u = f(e) (for example PID)</div>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Closed-loop systems reject disturbances through feedback; open-loop systems cannot.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-70", year: 2022, no: 70, subject: "mechatronics", topic: "Robot spatial resolution",
q: `Which one of the following is the smallest increment of movement into which the robot can divide its work volume?`,
opts: ["Spatial resolution of a robot", "Accuracy of a robot", "Repeatability of a robot", "Compliance of a robot"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Spatial resolution of a robot</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Groover defines spatial resolution as the smallest increment of movement into which the robot can divide its work volume. It depends on the control resolution (bits in the position encoder or control register) and the mechanical inaccuracies of joints and drives.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Accuracy is the robot's ability to reach a commanded target point in the work volume; Groover approximates it as half the control resolution plus the mechanical inaccuracy.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Repeatability is the ability to return to a previously taught point (usually better than accuracy).</li><li><b>(d)</b> Compliance is the displacement of the end effector under an applied force (the inverse of stiffness).</li></ul>
<div class="formula">Control resolution = range of joint/(2<sup>n</sup> − 1) for an n-bit controller</div>
<p><b>Remember:</b> A 12-bit control on a 1 m axis gives about 0·24 mm control resolution.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-71", year: 2022, no: 71, subject: "mechatronics", topic: "Tactile array sensors",
q: `Which one of the following sensors is a special type of force sensor composed of a matrix of force-sensing elements?`,
opts: ["Touch sensor", "Tactile array sensor", "Range sensor", "Proximity sensor"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Tactile array sensor</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A tactile array sensor is a matrix (grid) of small force-sensing elements, like an artificial skin. It measures the distribution of contact force over an area, so it can tell the shape, size and position of an object in a robot gripper.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A touch sensor gives a simple binary signal: contact or no contact (a limit switch, for example).</li><li><b>(c)</b> A range sensor measures distance to an object without contact (ultrasonic, laser triangulation).</li><li><b>(d)</b> A proximity sensor detects that an object is near (inductive, capacitive, optical), usually as an on/off signal.</li></ul>
<p>Groover groups tactile sensors into touch sensors and force sensors; tactile arrays belong to the force-sensor group.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Force–torque sensors at the robot wrist measure the total force; tactile arrays measure how it is distributed.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-72", year: 2022, no: 72, subject: "mechatronics", topic: "Robot end effectors",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding robot end effectors :<ol><li>Magnetic gripper can be a very feasible means of handling ferrous materials.</li><li>Hooks can be used as end effectors to handle containers of parts and to load and unload parts hanging from overhead conveyors.</li><li>Scoops and ladles can be used to handle certain materials in liquid or powder form.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> All three appear in Groover's list of gripper types:</p>
<ul><li><b>1:</b> Magnetic grippers (electromagnets or permanent magnets) handle ferrous parts quickly and can grip parts of different sizes; a drawback is residual magnetism and picking up more than one sheet.</li><li><b>2:</b> Hooks handle containers of parts and load and unload parts hanging from overhead conveyors.</li><li><b>3:</b> Scoops and ladles handle liquids and powders (for example molten metal in foundries), though control of the amount is limited.</li></ul>
<p>Other gripper types: mechanical (finger) grippers, vacuum cups (for flat, smooth surfaces such as glass and sheet metal), adhesive grippers (for fabrics) and expandable bladders (for gripping inside holes).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> End effectors are either grippers (to hold parts) or tools (welding gun, spray gun, drill).</p>` },

{ id: "2022-73", year: 2022, no: 73, subject: "fm", topic: "Power to drag a plate over an oil film",
q: `A flat plate of area 1·5 × 10⁶ mm² is pulled with a speed of 0·4 m/s relative to another plate located at a distance of 0·15 mm from it. What is the power required to maintain this speed, if the fluid separating them is having viscosity as 1 poise?`,
opts: ["160 W", "158 W", "145 W", "130 W"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 160 W</h3><div class="formula">τ = μ V/h &nbsp;&nbsp; F = τ A &nbsp;&nbsp; P = F V</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>μ = 1 poise = 0·1 N s/m²; V = 0·4 m/s; h = 0·15 × 10<sup>−3</sup> m; A = 1·5 × 10⁶ mm² = 1·5 m²</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>τ = 0·1 × 0·4/(0·15 × 10<sup>−3</sup>) = 266·7 N/m².</li><li>F = 266·7 × 1·5 = 400 N.</li><li>P = 400 × 0·4 = <b>160 W</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Assumption: the film is thin, so the velocity profile is linear (Couette flow), and the fluid is Newtonian.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 158 is not produced by any consistent unit conversion; the result is exactly 160 W.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 145 is not produced by any consistent unit conversion; the result is exactly 160 W.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 130 W is not produced by any consistent unit conversion; the result is exactly 160 W.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> P = μ A V²/h: power rises with the square of speed and falls as the gap widens.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-74", year: 2022, no: 74, subject: "thermo", topic: "Gas constant from specific weight",
q: `A gas weighs 16 N/m³ at 25 °C and at an absolute pressure 0·25 N/mm². What is the gas constant approximately? (Take acceleration due to gravity as 9·81 m/s²)`,
opts: ["514·68 Nmkg<sup>−1</sup> K<sup>−1</sup>", "542·55 Nmkg<sup>−1</sup> K<sup>−1</sup>", "562·68 Nmkg<sup>−1</sup> K<sup>−1</sup>", "592·55 Nmkg<sup>−1</sup> K<sup>−1</sup>"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 514·68 N m/kg K</h3><div class="formula">p = ρ R T → R = p/(ρ T), with ρ = w/g</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>w = 16 N/m³ (specific weight); p = 0·25 N/mm² = 2·5 × 10⁵ N/m²; T = 25 + 273 = 298 K</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>ρ = 16/9·81 = 1·631 kg/m³.</li><li>R = 2·5 × 10⁵/(1·63 × 298) = 2·5 × 10⁵/485·7 = <b>514·7 N m/kg K</b> (with ρ rounded to 1·63; unrounded, 514·4).</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 542·55 would need lower temperatures or a smaller density; for example T = 273 K would give 561·5 N m/kg K.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 562·68 would need lower temperatures or a smaller density; for example T = 273 K would give 561·5 N m/kg K.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 592·55 would need lower temperatures or a smaller density; for example T = 273 K would give 561·5 N m/kg K.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Molar mass check: M = 8314/514·7 = 16·2 kg/kmol, close to methane (CH<sub>4</sub>, R = 518 J/kg K).</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> R = R̄/M with R̄ = 8314 J/kmol K.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-75", year: 2022, no: 75, subject: "fm", topic: "Differential manometer",
q: `A pipe contains an oil of specific gravity 0·9. A differential manometer connected at the two points A and B shows a difference in mercury level as 15 cm. What is the difference of pressure at the two points? (Take acceleration due to gravity as 9·81 m/s², specific gravity of mercury as 13·6 and density of water as 1000 kg/m³)`,
opts: ["18688 N/m²", "19688 N/m²", "15688 N/m²", "17866 N/m²"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 18688 N/m²</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For points at the same level, with the pipe fluid (oil) above the mercury in both limbs:</p>
<div class="formula">p<sub>A</sub> − p<sub>B</sub> = g x (ρ<sub>Hg</sub> − ρ<sub>oil</sub>)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>x = 0·15 m (mercury level difference); ρ<sub>Hg</sub> = 13,600 kg/m³; ρ<sub>oil</sub> = 900 kg/m³</li></ul>
<p>p<sub>A</sub> − p<sub>B</sub> = 9·81 × 0·15 × (13,600 − 900) = 9·81 × 0·15 × 12,700 = <b>18,688 N/m²</b>.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>= 9.81 × 0.15 × (13 600 − 900).</li><li>= <b>18 688 N/m²</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 19,688 N/m² is close to ρ<sub>Hg</sub> g x = 20,012 N/m², which ignores the oil column.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 15,688 uses wrong density differences.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 17,866 N/m² uses wrong density differences.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> The oil column of height x in the other limb is what reduces the effective mercury density to (13·6 − 0·9).</p>` }
);

// ---- q2022d.js ----
// ESE 2022 Prelims Paper II, Mechanical Engineering, Series A, Q76–100
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2022-76", year: 2022, no: 76, subject: "fm", topic: "Weight in air from apparent weight in water",
q: `A body of dimensions 1·5 m × 1·0 m × 2 m, weighs 1962 N in water. What is the weight of the body in air? (Take acceleration due to gravity as 9·81 m/s²)`,
opts: ["31392 N", "23392 N", "14392 N", "46392 N"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 31392 N</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Archimedes: weight in water = weight in air − buoyant force (weight of water displaced).</p>
<div class="formula">W<sub>air</sub> = W<sub>water</sub> + ρ g V</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>V = 1·5 × 1·0 × 2 = 3 m³; ρ g = 9810 N/m³; W<sub>water</sub> = 1962 N</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Buoyant force = 9810 × 3 = 29,430 N.</li><li>W<sub>air</sub> = 1962 + 29,430 = <b>31,392 N</b>.</li><li>Specific gravity of the body = 31,392/29,430 = 1·067, so it sinks, as the question implies.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 23,392 corresponds to wrong volumes or to subtracting the buoyancy instead of adding it.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 14,392 corresponds to wrong volumes or to subtracting the buoyancy instead of adding it.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 46,392 N corresponds to wrong volumes or to subtracting the buoyancy instead of adding it.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Weighing a body in air and in water is the classic way to find its volume and specific gravity.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-77", year: 2022, no: 77, subject: "fm", topic: "Continuity equation: missing velocity component",
q: `The following cases represent the two velocity components, v = 2y²; w = 2xyz. What is the third component of velocity such that they satisfy the continuity equation?`,
opts: ["−4xy − x²y + f(y, z)", "−3xy − x³y + f(y, z)", "−5xy − 2x²y + f(y, z)", "−4xy − 3x²y + f(y, z)"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) −4xy − x²y + f(y, z)</h3><div class="formula">Incompressible continuity: ∂u/∂x + ∂v/∂y + ∂w/∂z = 0</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>∂v/∂y = 4y; ∂w/∂z = 2xy.</li><li>∂u/∂x = −(4y + 2xy).</li><li>Integrating with respect to x: u = −4xy − x²y + f(y, z).</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The "constant" of integration can be any function of y and z, since its x-derivative is zero.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> −3xy − x³y gives ∂u/∂x = −3y − 3x²y.</li><li><b>(c)</b> −5xy − 2x²y gives −5y − 4xy.</li><li><b>(d)</b> −4xy − 3x²y gives −4y − 6xy. None of these cancels 4y + 2xy.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Check: differentiate your u with respect to x and add the other two derivatives; the sum must be zero.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-78", year: 2022, no: 78, subject: "fm", topic: "Forced vortex in a rotating tank",
q: `An open circular tank of 20 cm diameter and 100 cm long contains water upto a height of 60 cm. The tank is rotated about its vertical axis at 300 rpm. What is the depth of parabola formed at the free surface of water? (Take acceleration due to gravity as 9·81 m/s²)`,
opts: ["50·28 cm", "55·28 cm", "65·36 cm", "69·36 cm"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 50·28 cm</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Forced vortex in a rotating tank.</p><div class="formula">Forced vortex: Z = ω² R²/(2g)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>ω = 2π × 300/60 = 31·416 rad/s; R = 0·1 m (tank radius); Z = height of the paraboloid from its vertex to the rim</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Z = 31·416² × 0·1²/(2 × 9·81) = 986·96 × 0·01/19·62 = 0·503 m = <b>50·3 cm</b>.</li><li>The volume of a paraboloid is half that of the circumscribing cylinder, so the surface rises Z/2 = 25·15 cm at the wall and falls 25·15 cm at the centre: wall level 85·15 cm, centre level 34·85 cm.</li><li>85·15 cm &lt; 100 cm, so no water spills.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 55·28 uses wrong speeds or the diameter in place of the radius (which would give 201 cm).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 65·36 uses wrong speeds or the diameter in place of the radius (which would give 201 cm).</li><li><b>(d)</b> 69·36 cm uses wrong speeds or the diameter in place of the radius (which would give 201 cm).</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> The depth of the parabola depends only on ω and R, not on the initial water level (as long as nothing spills or the bottom is not exposed).</p>` },

{ id: "2022-79", year: 2022, no: 79, subject: "fm", topic: "Velocity in a two-dimensional source flow",
q: `What is the velocity of flow at radius of 0·8 m, if the water is flowing radially outward in a horizontal plane from a source at a strength of 12 m²/s?`,
opts: ["9·55 m/s", "4·77 m/s", "2·38 m/s", "5·62 m/s"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 2·38 m/s</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For a two-dimensional source of strength q (volume flow per unit depth), continuity across a circle of radius r gives:</p>
<div class="formula">V<sub>r</sub> = q/(2π r)</div>
<p>V<sub>r</sub> = 12/(2π × 0·8) = 12/5·027 = <b>2·387 m/s</b>.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>V_r = 12/(2π × 0.8).</li><li>= <b>2.39 m/s</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 4·77 m/s divides by π r (forgets the 2).</li><li><b>(a)</b> 9·55 m/s divides by π r/2.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 5.62 m/s would need r ≈ 0.34 m.</li></ul>
<p>Stream function ψ = qθ/2π; velocity potential φ = (q/2π) ln r. The velocity falls as 1/r and is infinite at the source itself, a mathematical singularity.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> A sink is the same with a negative q (flow radially inwards).</p>` },

{ id: "2022-80", year: 2022, no: 80, subject: "fm", topic: "Pitot-static tube velocity",
q: `A pitot-static tube is used to measure the velocity of water in a pipe. The stagnation pressure head is 6 m and static pressure head is 5 m. What is the velocity of flow assuming the coefficient of tube equal to 0·98? (Take acceleration due to gravity as 9·81 m/s²)`,
opts: ["1·24 m/s", "2·68 m/s", "3·56 m/s", "4·34 m/s"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 4·34 m/s</h3><div class="formula">V = C<sub>v</sub> √(2 g h), with h = stagnation head − static head</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>h = 6 − 5 = 1 m (dynamic head); C<sub>v</sub> = 0·98</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> V = 0·98 × √(2 × 9·81 × 1) = 0·98 × 4·429 = <b>4·34 m/s</b>.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 1·24 uses the wrong head or omit the factor 2 in 2gh (√(9·81) × 0·98 = 3·07 m/s).</li><li><b>(b)</b> 2·68 uses the wrong head or omit the factor 2 in 2gh (√(9·81) × 0·98 = 3·07 m/s).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 3·56 m/s uses the wrong head or omit the factor 2 in 2gh (√(9·81) × 0·98 = 3·07 m/s).</li></ul>
<p>At the stagnation point the velocity head is fully converted to pressure, so (stagnation − static) head = V²/2g.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> A pitot-static tube measures the local (point) velocity, not the mean velocity in the pipe.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-81", year: 2022, no: 81, subject: "fm", topic: "Laminar pipe flow: wall shear and pressure gradient",
q: `A fluid of viscosity 0·7 Ns/m² and specific gravity 1·3 is flowing through a circular pipe of diameter 100 mm. The maximum shear stress at the pipe wall is given as 196·2 N/m². What is the pressure gradient of the flow?`,
opts: ["7848 N/m² per m", "−7848 N/m² per m", "−9848 N/m² per m", "9848 N/m² per m"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) −7848 N/m² per m</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A force balance on a fluid cylinder in fully developed pipe flow gives a shear stress that varies linearly with radius:</p>
<div class="formula">τ = (−∂p/∂x) r/2 → τ<sub>0</sub> = (−∂p/∂x) R/2</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>τ<sub>0</sub> = 196·2 N/m² (maximum, at the wall); R = 0·05 m</li></ul>
<p>∂p/∂x = −2τ<sub>0</sub>/R = −2 × 196·2/0·05 = <b>−7848 N/m² per m</b>. The pressure falls in the flow direction.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>∂p/∂x = −2 × 196.2/0.05.</li><li>= <b>−7848 N/m² per m</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> +7848 has the wrong sign: pressure must decrease along the flow to overcome friction.</li><li><b>(c)</b> ±9848 are arithmetic slips.</li><li><b>(d)</b> ±9848 are arithmetic slips.</li></ul>
<p>The viscosity and specific gravity are not needed for the gradient; they would be used next, for example to find the mean velocity: ū = (−∂p/∂x) R²/(8μ) = 7848 × 0·0025/5·6 = 3·50 m/s.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> The relation τ = (−dp/dx)r/2 holds for both laminar and turbulent flow; only the velocity profile differs.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-82", year: 2022, no: 82, subject: "fm", topic: "Laminar flow between fixed parallel plates",
q: `An oil of viscosity 10 poise flows between two parallel fixed plates which are kept at a distance of 50 mm apart. What is the rate of flow of oil between the plates if the drop of pressure in a length of 1·2 m be 0·3 N/cm² and the width of the plate is 200 mm?`,
opts: ["6·2 litre/s", "3·2 litre/s", "8·2 litre/s", "5·2 litre/s"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 5·2 litre/s</h3><div class="formula">Plane Poiseuille flow: ū = (−dp/dx) t²/(12μ) &nbsp;&nbsp; Q = ū b t</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>μ = 10 poise = 1 N s/m²; t = 0·05 m (gap); b = 0·2 m (width); Δp = 0·3 N/cm² = 3000 N/m² over L = 1·2 m</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>−dp/dx = 3000/1·2 = 2500 N/m³.</li><li>ū = 2500 × 0·05²/(12 × 1) = 0·521 m/s.</li><li>Q = 0·521 × 0·2 × 0·05 = 5·21 × 10<sup>−3</sup> m³/s = <b>5·2 L/s</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Check that the flow is laminar: for an oil of specific gravity about 0·9, Re = ρūt/μ ≈ 900 × 0·521 × 0·05/1 ≈ 23, far below the transition value.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 6·2 comes from wrong unit conversions (poise or N/cm²).</li><li><b>(b)</b> 3·2 comes from wrong unit conversions (poise or N/cm²).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 8·2 L/s comes from wrong unit conversions (poise or N/cm²).</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Maximum velocity at mid-gap = 1·5 × mean velocity for flow between parallel plates (2 × mean for a pipe).</p>` },

{ id: "2022-83", year: 2022, no: 83, subject: "fm", topic: "Power lost in a journal bearing",
q: `A shaft of diameter 0·35 m rotates at 200 rpm inside a sleeve 100 mm long. The dynamic viscosity of lubricating oil in the 2 mm gap between sleeve and shaft is 8 poises. What is the power lost in the bearing?`,
opts: ["0·59 kW", "0·69 kW", "0·88 kW", "0·91 kW"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 0·59 kW</h3><div class="formula">τ = μ V/t &nbsp;&nbsp; F = τ π D L &nbsp;&nbsp; P = F V</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>μ = 8 poise = 0·8 N s/m²; D = 0·35 m; L = 0·1 m; t = 0·002 m; N = 200 rpm</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Surface speed V = π D N/60 = π × 0·35 × 200/60 = 3·665 m/s.</li><li>τ = 0·8 × 3·665/0·002 = 1466 N/m².</li><li>Area = π × 0·35 × 0·1 = 0·1100 m² → F = 161·2 N.</li><li>P = 161·2 × 3·665 = 591 W = <b>0·59 kW</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 0·69 uses the gap or the viscosity wrongly converted.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0·88 uses the gap or the viscosity wrongly converted.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0·91 kW uses the gap or the viscosity wrongly converted.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Torque = F × D/2 = 28·2 N m. This is Petroff's approach: it assumes the shaft is concentric in the sleeve, with a linear velocity profile across the thin film.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> P = μ π² D³ N² L/(3600 t) in SI units: power loss grows with D³ and N².</p>` },

{ id: "2022-84", year: 2022, no: 84, subject: "fm", topic: "Falling-sphere viscometer (Stokes' law)",
q: `A sphere of diameter 2 mm falls 150 mm in 20 seconds in a viscous liquid. The density of the sphere is 7500 kg/m³ and of liquid is 900 kg/m³. What is the coefficient of viscosity of the liquid? (Take acceleration due to gravity as 9·81 m/s²)`,
opts: ["22·31 poise", "25·62 poise", "19·17 poise", "32·21 poise"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 19·17 poise</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> At terminal velocity, weight = buoyancy + Stokes drag (3π μ d V):</p>
<div class="formula">μ = g d² (ρ<sub>s</sub> − ρ)/(18 V)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>d = 0·002 m; ρ<sub>s</sub> = 7500 kg/m³; ρ = 900 kg/m³; V = 0·15/20 = 0·0075 m/s</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Numerator: 9·81 × 4 × 10<sup>−6</sup> × 6600 = 0·2590.</li><li>Denominator: 18 × 0·0075 = 0·135.</li><li>μ = 1·918 N s/m² = <b>19·18 poise</b>.</li><li>Check: Re = ρVd/μ = 900 × 0·0075 × 0·002/1·918 = 0·007 ≪ 1, so Stokes' law is valid.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 22·31 ignores the buoyancy (which would give 21·8 poise) or use other slips.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 25·62 ignores the buoyancy (which would give 21·8 poise) or use other slips.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 32·21 poise ignores the buoyancy (which would give 21·8 poise) or use other slips.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> 1 N s/m² = 10 poise.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-85", year: 2022, no: 85, subject: "thermo", topic: "Volumetric heat generation in a wire",
q: `The resistance wire of a 1200 W hair dryer is 80 cm long and has a diameter of 0·3 cm. What is the rate of heat generation in the wire per unit volume?`,
opts: ["212 W/cm³", "312 W/cm³", "512 W/cm³", "412 W/cm³"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 212 W/cm³</h3><div class="formula">ġ = Ė/V = Ė/(π D² L/4)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>Ė = 1200 W; D = 0·3 cm; L = 80 cm</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>V = π × 0·09/4 × 80 = 5·655 cm³.</li><li>ġ = 1200/5·655 = <b>212 W/cm³</b> (2·12 × 10⁸ W/m³).</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The heat flux at the wire surface is Ė/(πDL) = 1200/(π × 0·3 × 80) = 15·9 W/cm².</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 312 comes from using the radius as the diameter or other arithmetic errors.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 512 comes from using the radius as the diameter or other arithmetic errors.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 412 W/cm³ comes from using the radius as the diameter or other arithmetic errors.</li></ul>
<p>Why it matters: heat generation per unit volume (ġ) is the quantity that enters the heat conduction equation. For a given power, a thinner wire has a much larger ġ and therefore a larger temperature difference between its centre and surface (ΔT = ġr<sub>0</sub>²/4k).</p><p><b>Remember:</b> This is Cengel's Example 2-3: all electrical energy is assumed converted to heat in the wire.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-86", year: 2022, no: 86, subject: "thermo", topic: "Heat conduction equation for a heated wire",
q: `A 2-kW resistance heater wire with thermal conductivity 15 W/m.°C, diameter 0·4 cm, and length 50 cm is used to boil the water by immersing it in water. Assuming the variation of the thermal conductivity of the wire with temperature to be negligible. What is the differential equation that describes the variation of the temperature in the wire during steady operation?`,
opts: ["(1/r) d/dr (r dT/dr) + ġ/k = 0", "(1/2r) dT/dr (r dT/dr) + ġ/k = 0", "(1/r) d/dr (dT/dr) + ġ/k = 0", "(1/r) d/dr (r dT/dr) = 0"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) (1/r) d/dr (r dT/dr) + ġ/k = 0</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For a long cylinder with constant k, steady state and one-dimensional radial conduction, the general heat equation reduces to:</p>
<div class="formula">(1/r) d/dr (r dT/dr) + ġ/k = 0</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>ġ: volumetric heat generation = 2000/(π × 0·002² × 0·5) = 3·18 × 10⁸ W/m³; k = 15 W/m °C</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> has a non-linear product of derivatives and a factor 1/2r: not a valid form.</li><li><b>(c)</b> omits the r inside the derivative, which is what accounts for the growing area in cylindrical coordinates.</li><li><b>(d)</b> omits the heat generation term; it applies to a pipe wall with no generation.</li></ul>
<p>Solving with dT/dr = 0 at r = 0 and T = T<sub>s</sub> at r<sub>0</sub> gives T(r) = T<sub>s</sub> + ġ(r<sub>0</sub>² − r²)/(4k), used in Q88.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> This is Cengel's Example 2-7. The sphere version is (1/r²) d/dr (r² dT/dr) + ġ/k = 0.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-87", year: 2022, no: 87, subject: "thermo", topic: "Electric power for boiling on a wire",
q: `An electric current is passed through a wire 1 mm in diameter and 10 cm long. The wire is submerged in liquid water at atmospheric pressure and the current is increased until the water boils. For this situation, h is 5000 W/m².°C and the water temperature will be 100 °C. How much electric power must be supplied to the wire to maintain the wire surface at 114 °C?`,
opts: ["21·99 W", "32·39 W", "42·39 W", "52·36 W"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 21·99 W</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> At steady state, all the electrical power is convected from the wire surface into the boiling water.</p>
<div class="formula">Ė = h A (T<sub>s</sub> − T<sub>∞</sub>), A = π D L</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>h = 5000 W/m² °C; D = 0·001 m; L = 0·1 m; T<sub>s</sub> = 114 °C; T<sub>∞</sub> = 100 °C</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>A = π × 0·001 × 0·1 = 3·142 × 10<sup>−4</sup> m².</li><li>Ė = 5000 × 3·142 × 10<sup>−4</sup> × 14 = <b>21·99 W</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 32·39 uses wrong areas or temperature differences.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 42·39 uses wrong areas or temperature differences.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 52·36 W uses wrong areas or temperature differences.</li></ul>
<p>The 14 °C excess temperature lies in the nucleate-boiling regime for water, where h is high (several thousand W/m² °C).</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> This is Holman's Example 1-4.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-88", year: 2022, no: 88, subject: "thermo", topic: "Centre temperature of a heated wire",
q: `A 2-kW resistance heater wire whose thermal conductivity is 15 W/m.°C has a diameter of 4 mm and a length of 0·5 m, is used to boil the water. If the outer surface temperature of resistance wire is 105 °C, what is the temperature at the centre of the wire?`,
opts: ["136 °C", "126 °C", "146 °C", "156 °C"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 126 °C</h3><div class="formula">T<sub>0</sub> = T<sub>s</sub> + ġ r<sub>0</sub>²/(4k)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>ġ = Ė/V; r<sub>0</sub> = 0·002 m; k = 15 W/m °C; T<sub>s</sub> = 105 °C</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>V = π × 0·002² × 0·5 = 6·283 × 10<sup>−6</sup> m³ → ġ = 2000/6·283 × 10<sup>−6</sup> = 3·183 × 10⁸ W/m³.</li><li>ΔT = 3·183 × 10⁸ × 4 × 10<sup>−6</sup>/(4 × 15) = 21·2 °C.</li><li>T<sub>0</sub> = 105 + 21·2 = <b>126·2 °C</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The temperature profile is parabolic, with the maximum at the centreline. Equivalent form: ΔT = Ė/(4πkL) = 2000/(4π × 15 × 0·5) = 21·2 °C; the result does not depend on the diameter.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 136 uses ġr²/2k (the slab or wrong formula) or wrong radius values.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 146 uses ġr²/2k (the slab or wrong formula) or wrong radius values.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 156 °C uses ġr²/2k (the slab or wrong formula) or wrong radius values.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> This is Cengel's Example 2-17, a continuation of Q86.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-89", year: 2022, no: 89, subject: "thermo", topic: "Thermal contact conductance",
q: `The thermal contact conductance at the interface of two 1-cm-thick aluminum plates is measured to be 11,000 W/m².°C. What is the thickness of the aluminum plate whose thermal resistance is equal to the thermal resistance of the interface between the plates? (Take the thermal conductivity of aluminum at room temperature as 237 W/m.°C)`,
opts: ["3·15 cm", "5·13 cm", "1·28 cm", "2·15 cm"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 2·15 cm</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Set the conduction resistance of a plate of thickness L equal to the contact resistance (both per unit area):</p>
<div class="formula">L/k = 1/h<sub>c</sub> → L = k/h<sub>c</sub></div>
<p>L = 237/11,000 = 0·0215 m = <b>2·15 cm</b>.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 3·15 does not equal k/h<sub>c</sub>.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 5·13 does not equal k/h<sub>c</sub>.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 1·28 cm does not equal k/h<sub>c</sub>.</li></ul>
<p>So the thin interface between two 1 cm plates resists heat flow as much as 2·15 cm of solid aluminium: more than both plates together. Contact resistance arises because real surfaces touch only at peaks; the gaps are filled with air.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Contact resistance is reduced by higher contact pressure, smoother surfaces and thermal grease or soft metal foils. (Cengel's Example 3-5.)</p>` },

{ id: "2022-90", year: 2022, no: 90, subject: "thermo", topic: "Validity of one-dimensional fin analysis",
q: `In the design of a fin, the error involved in one dimensional fin analysis is negligible (less than about 1%) when`,
opts: ["hδ/k &lt; 0·2", "hk/δ &lt; 0·2", "hδ/k &lt; 2", "hk/δ &lt; 2"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) hδ/k &lt; 0·2</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> One-dimensional fin analysis assumes the temperature varies only along the fin, not across its thickness. Cengel states the error is negligible (under about 1%) when the fin Biot number is small:</p>
<div class="formula">Bi = h δ/k &lt; 0·2</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>δ: characteristic thickness of the fin (taken as the plate thickness t for rectangular fins, or the diameter D for pin fins); h: convection coefficient; k: fin conductivity</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>1-D fin analysis valid (error &lt; 1 %) when <b>hδ/k &lt; 0.2</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> hk/δ is not the Biot number (k must be in the denominator).</li><li><b>(d)</b> Wrong grouping and wrong limit.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Limit 2 is far too large for the 1 % error.</li></ul>
<p>In practice fins are thin and conductive (aluminium, copper), so Bi is usually far below 0·2.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Fins are also only worthwhile when hA/kP is small: their effectiveness should be at least 2.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-91", year: 2022, no: 91, subject: "thermo", topic: "Lumped system analysis",
q: `Lumped system analysis assumes a uniform temperature distribution throughout the body, which will be the case only when the thermal resistance of the body to heat conduction is`,
opts: ["3", "2", "1", "0"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 0</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Cengel: lumped system analysis assumes a uniform temperature throughout the body, which is exactly true only when the body's internal conduction resistance is zero (infinite conductivity). In practice it is used when that resistance is small compared with the external convection resistance:</p>
<div class="formula">Bi = h L<sub>c</sub>/k = (L<sub>c</sub>/k)/(1/h) ≤ 0·1</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>L<sub>c</sub> = V/A<sub>s</sub>: characteristic length</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Lumped analysis has no spatial variation, so no spatial dimensions remain (0), not 3.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Temperature depends on time only; zero space dimensions.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Even 1-D variation is excluded in lumped analysis.</li></ul>
<p>Lumped solution: (T − T<sub>∞</sub>)/(T<sub>i</sub> − T<sub>∞</sub>) = e<sup>−bt</sup>, with b = hA<sub>s</sub>/(ρVc<sub>p</sub>). With Bi ≤ 0·1, the error is under about 5%.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Small metal balls and thermocouple junctions are classic lumped bodies.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-92", year: 2022, no: 92, subject: "thermo", topic: "Factors affecting the convection coefficient",
q: `Which one of the following is NOT a fluid property for convection heat transfer coefficient?`,
opts: ["Dynamic viscosity", "Thermal conductivity", "Density", "Rate of convection"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Rate of convection</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Cengel: the convection heat transfer coefficient h depends on fluid properties (dynamic viscosity μ, thermal conductivity k, density ρ and specific heat c<sub>p</sub>), the fluid velocity, the surface geometry and roughness, and the type of flow (laminar or turbulent).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Viscosity sets the boundary-layer thickness.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Conductivity controls conduction across the thin fluid layer at the wall.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Density enters the Reynolds number (and buoyancy in natural convection).</li><li><b>(d)</b> The rate of convection, Q = hAΔT, is the <i>result</i>, not a fluid property.</li></ul>
<div class="formula">Nu = h L/k = f(Re, Pr), Re = ρVL/μ, Pr = μc<sub>p</sub>/k</div>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Because h depends on so many factors, it is found from experiments and correlations rather than tables.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-93", year: 2022, no: 93, subject: "thermo", topic: "Turbulent flat-plate boundary layer",
q: `In friction coefficient, the boundary layer thickness (δ), the local friction coefficient (C<sub>f</sub>) at location x and Reynolds number (Re) at location x for turbulent flow over a flat plate are`,
opts: ["δ = 5x/Re<sub>x</sub><sup>0·5</sup>; C<sub>f</sub> = 0·664/Re<sub>x</sub><sup>0·5</sup>", "δ = 0·382x/Re<sub>x</sub><sup>0·5</sup>; C<sub>f</sub> = 0·0592/Re<sub>x</sub><sup>0·5</sup>", "δ = 0·382x/Re<sub>x</sub><sup>1/5</sup>; C<sub>f</sub> = 0·0592/Re<sub>x</sub><sup>1/5</sup>", "δ = 5x/Re<sub>x</sub><sup>1/5</sup>; C<sub>f</sub> = 0·664/Re<sub>x</sub><sup>1/5</sup>"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) δ = 0·382x/Re<sub>x</sub><sup>1/5</sup>; C<sub>f</sub> = 0·0592/Re<sub>x</sub><sup>1/5</sup></h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Using the 1/7-power velocity profile and an empirical wall shear law, the turbulent flat-plate results are (Cengel, for 5 × 10⁵ ≤ Re<sub>x</sub> ≤ 10⁷):</p>
<div class="formula">δ/x = 0·38/Re<sub>x</sub><sup>1/5</sup> &nbsp;&nbsp; C<sub>f,x</sub> = 0·059/Re<sub>x</sub><sup>1/5</sup></div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 5x/Re<sup>½</sup> and 0·664/Re<sup>½</sup> are the <i>laminar</i> (Blasius) results.</li><li><b>(b)</b> combines the turbulent coefficients with the laminar exponent ½.</li><li><b>(d)</b> combines the laminar coefficients with the turbulent exponent 1/5.</li></ul>
<p>The turbulent boundary layer grows as x<sup>4/5</sup>, much faster than the laminar one (x<sup>1/2</sup>).</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Average turbulent friction coefficient over length L: C<sub>f</sub> = 0·074/Re<sub>L</sub><sup>1/5</sup>.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-94", year: 2022, no: 94, subject: "thermo", topic: "Hydrodynamic entrance region",
q: `The region from the tube inlet to the point at which the boundary layer merges at the centreline, is called`,
opts: ["Hydrodynamic entry length", "Thermal entrance region", "Hydrodynamic entrance region", "Thermal entry length"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Hydrodynamic entrance region</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Cengel: the region from the tube inlet to the point where the velocity boundary layer merges at the centreline is the hydrodynamic entrance region; the <i>length</i> of this region is the hydrodynamic entry length. Beyond it, the flow is hydrodynamically fully developed (the velocity profile no longer changes).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Hydrodynamic entry length is the <i>length</i> of the region, not the region itself.</li><li><b>(b)</b> The thermal entrance region and length refer to the growth of the <i>thermal</i> boundary layer.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The thermal entrance region and length refer to the growth of the <i>thermal</i> boundary layer.</li></ul>
<div class="formula">Laminar: L<sub>h</sub> ≈ 0·05 Re D; L<sub>t</sub> ≈ 0·05 Re Pr D = Pr L<sub>h</sub>. Turbulent: L ≈ 10 D</div>
<p><b>Remember:</b> For oils (Pr ≫ 1) the thermal entry length is much longer than the hydrodynamic one.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-95", year: 2022, no: 95, subject: "thermo", topic: "Dittus–Boelter equation",
q: `What is the traditional expression for calculation of heat transfer in fully developed turbulent flow in smooth tubes that recommended by Dittus Boelter?`,
opts: ["Nu<sub>d</sub> = 0·023 (Re<sub>d</sub><sup>0·8</sup>)(Pr)<sup>n</sup>", "Nu<sub>d</sub> = 0·023 (Re<sub>d</sub><sup>0·4</sup>)(Pr)<sup>2n</sup>", "Nu<sub>d</sub> = 0·023 (Re<sub>d</sub><sup>0·8</sup>)(Pr)<sup>2n</sup>", "Nu<sub>d</sub> = 0·023 (Re<sub>d</sub><sup>0·4</sup>)(Pr)<sup>n</sup>"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Nu<sub>d</sub> = 0·023 Re<sub>d</sub><sup>0·8</sup> Pr<sup>n</sup></h3><div class="formula">n = 0·4 for heating the fluid; n = 0·3 for cooling the fluid</div>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Range of validity: 0·7 ≤ Pr ≤ 160, Re<sub>d</sub> &gt; 10,000, L/D &gt; 10, moderate temperature differences; properties at the bulk mean temperature.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Re<sup>0·4</sup> confuses the Reynolds exponent with the Prandtl exponent.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Re<sup>0·4</sup> confuses the Reynolds exponent with the Prandtl exponent.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Pr<sup>2n</sup> doubles the Prandtl exponent.</li></ul>
<p>For better accuracy, the Gnielinski correlation is used; for large property variations, the Sieder–Tate correlation (with a viscosity correction).</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Since Nu ∝ Re<sup>0·8</sup>, doubling the velocity in turbulent flow raises h by about 74%.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-96", year: 2022, no: 96, subject: "thermo", topic: "Fouling factors",
q: `Which one of the following is having the highest value of fouling factor?`,
opts: ["Sea water", "Refrigerating liquid", "Fuel oil", "Industrial air"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Fuel oil</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Representative fouling factors R<sub>f</sub> (m² °C/W) from Cengel's table:</p>
<ul><li>Distilled water, sea water, river water, boiler feedwater: below 50 °C 0·0001; above 50 °C 0·0002.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Fuel oil: 0·0009 (highest of the four).</li><li><b>(b)</b> Refrigerating liquids: 0·0002.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Industrial air: 0·0004.</li><li>Steam (oil-free): 0·0001; quenching oil: 0·0007; alcohol vapours: 0·0001.</li></ul>
<p>A higher fouling factor means more deposit resistance, so the heat exchanger must be oversized. Fouling comes from scaling, corrosion, biological growth and deposits of particles or tars.</p>
<div class="formula">1/U<sub>dirty</sub> = 1/U<sub>clean</sub> + R<sub>f,i</sub>/(A<sub>i</sub>/A) + R<sub>f,o</sub></div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Sea water (below 50 °C) ≈ 0.0001 m²K/W – low.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Refrigerating liquid ≈ 0.0002 m²K/W.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Fuel oil ≈ 0.0009 m²K/W – the highest of these.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Industrial air ≈ 0.0004 m²K/W.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Fuel oils leave heavy, tarry deposits; this is why they top the table.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-97", year: 2022, no: 97, subject: "icrac", topic: "Actual capacity from a condenser heat balance",
q: `The performance test of an air conditioning unit rated as 140·7 kW (40 TR) seems to be indicating poor cooling. The test on heat rejection to atmosphere in its condenser shows the following : cooling water flow rate – 4 L/s; water temperature – inlet 30 °C and outlet 40 °C; power input to motor – 48 kW (95% efficiency); What is the actual refrigerating capacity of the unit? (Take C<sub>w</sub> = 4·1868)`,
opts: ["34·7 TR", "45·6 TR", "52·6 TR", "48·6 TR"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 34·7 TR</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Energy balance on the refrigerant: heat rejected in the condenser = refrigerating effect + compressor work.</p>
<div class="formula">Q<sub>e</sub> = Q<sub>c</sub> − W</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Q<sub>c</sub> = ṁ c<sub>w</sub> ΔT = 4 × 4·1868 × 10 = 167·47 kW.</li><li>Work reaching the compressor W = 48 × 0·95 = 45·6 kW.</li><li>Q<sub>e</sub> = 167·47 − 45·6 = 121·87 kW.</li><li>In tons: 121·87/3·517 = <b>34·65 TR</b>, compared with the rated 40 TR.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 45·6 TR is the shaft power number misread as tons.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 52·6 TR adds the work instead of subtracting it.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 48·6 TR uses the full 48 kW and other slips.</li></ul>
<p>The unit's COP is 121·87/45·6 = 2·67; the shortfall points to problems such as low refrigerant charge or a fouled condenser.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> 1 TR = 3·517 kW (211 kJ/min).</p>` },

{ id: "2022-98", year: 2022, no: 98, subject: "thermo", topic: "Intensive properties",
q: `Which one of the following is the intensive property in thermodynamic system?`,
opts: ["Pressure", "Enthalpy", "Internal energy", "Entropy"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Pressure</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> An intensive property does not depend on the size (mass) of the system: dividing the system into parts leaves it unchanged. Pressure, temperature and density are intensive.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Enthalpy H is extensive: it is proportional to the mass.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Internal energy U is extensive: it is proportional to the mass.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Entropy S is extensive: it is proportional to the mass.</li></ul>
<p>Dividing an extensive property by mass gives a specific property (h, u, s, v), which is intensive. Test: imagine a partition splitting the system in two; properties that stay the same in each half are intensive.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> State postulate: two independent intensive properties fix the state of a simple compressible system.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-99", year: 2022, no: 99, subject: "icrac", topic: "Average cooling rate from melting ice",
q: `100 kg of ice at −5 °C is placed in a bunker to cool some vegetables. 24 hours later, the ice has melted into water at 10 °C. What is the average rate of cooling in kJ/h provided by the ice? (Take specific heat of ice is 1·94 kJ/kg.K; Specific heat of water is 4·1868 kJ/kg.K; latent heat of fusion of ice at 0 °C is 335 kJ/kg)`,
opts: ["1611 kJ/h", "1811 kJ/h", "1711 kJ/h", "1911 kJ/h"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1611 kJ/h</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Working (heat absorbed by 100 kg)</b></p><div class="formula">Q = m(c_iceΔT₁ + L + c_wΔT₂)</div><div class="formula">Rate = Q/t</div><ol class="work"><li>Warming ice from −5 to 0 °C: 100 × 1·94 × 5 = 970 kJ.</li><li>Melting at 0 °C: 100 × 335 = 33,500 kJ.</li><li>Warming water from 0 to 10 °C: 100 × 4·1868 × 10 = 4186·8 kJ.</li><li>Total = 38,656·8 kJ over 24 h → <b>1610·7 kJ/h</b>.</li></ol>
<p>That is about 0·45 kW, or 0·127 TR: the latent heat provides 87% of the cooling.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 1811 adds extra heat that is not in the data.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 1711 adds extra heat that is not in the data.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 1911 kJ/h adds extra heat that is not in the data.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Ice-based cooling is mostly latent: 335 kJ/kg is equivalent to warming water by 80 °C.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-100", year: 2022, no: 100, subject: "icrac", topic: "Methods of producing low temperatures",
q: `Which of the following principles and processes involved in the production of low temperatures are correct?<ol><li>Adiabatic demagnetization</li><li>Thermoelectric cooling</li><li>Reversible adiabatic expansion of a gas</li><li>Irreversible adiabatic expansion of a real gas</li></ol>Select the correct answer using the code given below :`,
opts: ["1 and 3 only", "1, 3 and 4 only", "1, 2 and 3 only", "1, 2, 3 and 4"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2, 3 and 4</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Arora lists all four as methods of producing low temperatures:</p>
<ul><li><b>1:</b> Adiabatic demagnetisation of a paramagnetic salt reaches temperatures below 1 K.</li><li><b>2:</b> Thermoelectric (Peltier) cooling: a current through a junction of dissimilar materials absorbs heat.</li><li><b>3:</b> Reversible adiabatic (isentropic) expansion of a gas in an expander or turbine produces cooling while doing work (air cycle, Claude cycle).</li><li><b>4:</b> Irreversible adiabatic expansion (throttling) of a real gas below its inversion temperature cools it by the Joule–Thomson effect (Linde liquefaction).</li></ul>
<p>Other methods include vapour compression and vapour absorption cycles, steam-jet refrigeration and vortex tubes.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 2, 4, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 4, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3, 4.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Throttling an ideal gas gives no temperature change; the Joule–Thomson cooling needs real-gas behaviour.</p>` }
);

// ---- q2022e.js ----
// ESE 2022 Prelims Paper II, Mechanical Engineering, Series A, Q101–125
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2022-101", year: 2022, no: 101, subject: "icrac", topic: "Aircraft refrigeration",
q: `Which one of the following is used in aircraft refrigeration?`,
opts: ["Vapor compression cycle refrigeration", "Gas cycle refrigeration", "Vapor absorption cycle refrigeration", "Steam ejector cycle refrigeration"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Gas cycle refrigeration</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Aircraft cabins are cooled by air (gas, reversed Brayton/Bell–Coleman) cycles. Compressed air is bled from the engine compressor (or rammed in), cooled in a heat exchanger with ambient air, and expanded in a cooling turbine before being supplied to the cabin.</p>
<p><b>Why air cycles suit aircraft</b></p><ul><li>Air is the refrigerant and is also the cabin air: no leaks of toxic or flammable refrigerants and no separate air-handling system.</li><li>Low equipment weight per ton of refrigeration, which matters more in aircraft than the low COP.</li><li>Pressurised air is already available from the engine compressor.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Vapour compression is heavier and needs a separate refrigerant circuit, so it is unsuitable for aircraft.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Vapour absorption systems are bulky and need a heat source, so they are unsuitable for aircraft.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Steam-ejector systems need steam and are bulky, so they are unsuitable for aircraft.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Common air-cycle systems: simple, bootstrap, regenerative and reduced-ambient.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-102", year: 2022, no: 102, subject: "icrac", topic: "Ice plant refrigeration capacity",
q: `An ammonia ice plant operates between a condenser temperature of 35 °C and an evaporator temperature of −15 °C. It produces 10 tons of ice per day from water at 30 °C to ice at −5 °C. Assume simple saturation cycle, what is the capacity of the refrigeration plant? (Take Specific heat of ice is 1·94 kJ/kg.K; Specific heat of water is 4·1868 kJ/kg.K; latent heat of fusion of ice at 0 °C is 335 kJ/kg)`,
opts: ["54·43 kW", "64·32 kW", "74·52 kW", "84·23 kW"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 54·43 kW</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Working (heat to be removed per kg)</b></p><div class="formula">q = c_w(30) + L + c_ice(5)</div><div class="formula">Capacity = ṁ q</div><ol class="work"><li>Cooling water 30 → 0 °C: 4·1868 × 30 = 125·6 kJ/kg.</li><li>Freezing at 0 °C: 335 kJ/kg.</li><li>Cooling ice 0 → −5 °C: 1·94 × 5 = 9·7 kJ/kg.</li><li>Total = 470·3 kJ/kg.</li><li>Mass rate: 10 t/day = 10,000/86,400 = 0·1157 kg/s.</li><li>Capacity = 0·1157 × 470·3 = <b>54·43 kW</b> (15·5 TR).</li></ol>
<p>The condenser and evaporator temperatures are needed only for the next steps (refrigerant flow, power and COP from ammonia tables); they do not change the capacity.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 64·32 includes heat that is not in the data, or use the wrong mass rate.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 74·52 includes heat that is not in the data, or use the wrong mass rate.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 84·23 kW includes heat that is not in the data, or use the wrong mass rate.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> This is Arora's ice-plant example.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-103", year: 2022, no: 103, subject: "icrac", topic: "Effect of condenser pressure",
q: `Consider the following statements in a vapor compression refrigeration system :<br>It is observed that an increase in condenser pressure, similarly results in<ol><li>a decrease in the refrigerating capacity</li><li>an increase in power consumption</li><li>an increase in volumetric efficiency</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 2 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> With the evaporator pressure fixed, raising the condenser pressure (and temperature) has these effects:</p>
<ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> The liquid entering the expansion valve is hotter, so more flashes to vapour; the refrigerating effect per kg falls. The volumetric efficiency also falls (see 3), so less refrigerant is pumped and the capacity drops.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> The compressor must work against a higher pressure ratio: more work per kg and higher power, so the COP falls.</li><li><b>3 (wrong):</b> A higher pressure ratio makes the clearance gas re-expand over a longer part of the stroke, so the volumetric efficiency <i>decreases</i>: η<sub>v</sub> = 1 + C − C(p<sub>d</sub>/p<sub>s</sub>)<sup>1/n</sup>.</li></ul>
<p>The discharge temperature also rises, which can harm lubricating oil and valves.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Keep condensers clean and well ventilated: every 1 °C rise in condensing temperature cuts the COP by roughly 2–3%.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-104", year: 2022, no: 104, subject: "icrac", topic: "Refrigerant numbering: R114",
q: `Which one of the following is the designation for dichloro-tetrafluoro-ethane refrigerant used in refrigeration system?`,
opts: ["R114", "R116", "R113", "R118"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) R114</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Ethane-based refrigerants are numbered R(m − 1)(n + 1)(p), for the formula C<sub>m</sub>H<sub>n</sub>F<sub>p</sub>Cl<sub>q</sub>, with the chlorine atoms filling the remaining bonds (n + p + q = 2m + 2).</p><div class="formula">R(m − 1)(n + 1)(p) for C_mH_nF_pCl_q</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Dichlorotetrafluoroethane: C<sub>2</sub>Cl<sub>2</sub>F<sub>4</sub>: m = 2, n = 0 (no H), p = 4, q = 2.</li><li>Number = (2 − 1)(0 + 1)(4) = <b>114</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> R116 is hexafluoroethane, C<sub>2</sub>F<sub>6</sub>.</li><li><b>(c)</b> R113 is trichlorotrifluoroethane, C<sub>2</sub>Cl<sub>3</sub>F<sub>3</sub>.</li><li><b>(d)</b> R118 does not exist: an ethane can carry at most 6 fluorine atoms.</li></ul>
<p>Check with R134a: (1)(3)(4) → C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>2</sub>F<sub>4</sub>, tetrafluoroethane.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> R114 is a CFC, now phased out under the Montreal Protocol because of its ozone depletion potential.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-105", year: 2022, no: 105, subject: "icrac", topic: "Flammable refrigerants",
q: `Which of the following are highly explosive and flammable in a refrigeration system?<ol><li>Methane</li><li>Butane</li><li>R134a</li></ol>Select the correct answer using the code given below :`,
opts: ["2 and 3 only", "1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> Methane (R50) is a hydrocarbon: highly flammable (ASHRAE safety class A3).</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> Butane (R600, isobutane R600a) is also a highly flammable hydrocarbon (A3), although R600a is widely used in small domestic refrigerators with small charges.</li><li><b>3 (wrong):</b> R134a is non-flammable and of low toxicity (class A1).</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> ASHRAE 34 safety classes: a letter for toxicity (A lower, B higher) and a number for flammability (1 none, 2L mildly flammable, 2 flammable, 3 highly flammable). Ammonia is B2L; R32 and R1234yf are A2L.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Hydrocarbons have very low global-warming potential, so they are returning in small sealed systems with strict charge limits.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-106", year: 2022, no: 106, subject: "icrac", topic: "COP of a hydrocarbon refrigerator",
q: `A hydrocarbon refrigerator operating on equal proportions of propane and isobutane by mass works on simple saturation cycle. The condenser and evaporator pressure are 14 bar and 1·4 bar. What is the approximate theoretical COP of the system? (Take properties : h<sub>1</sub> = 545 kJ/kg; h<sub>3</sub> = 341·15 kJ/kg = h<sub>4</sub>; S<sub>1</sub> = 2·47 kJ/kgK = S<sub>2</sub>; h<sub>2</sub> = 661 kJ/kg and T<sub>2</sub> = 63 °C)`,
opts: ["1·8", "5·4", "3·6", "2·7"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1·8</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> States of the simple saturation cycle: 1 saturated vapour at the compressor inlet; 2 superheated vapour after isentropic compression (s<sub>2</sub> = s<sub>1</sub>); 3 saturated liquid leaving the condenser; 4 after throttling (h<sub>4</sub> = h<sub>3</sub>).</p>
<div class="formula">COP = (h<sub>1</sub> − h<sub>4</sub>)/(h<sub>2</sub> − h<sub>1</sub>)</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Refrigerating effect = 545 − 341·15 = 203·85 kJ/kg.</li><li>Compressor work = 661 − 545 = 116 kJ/kg.</li><li>COP = 203·85/116 = <b>1·76 ≈ 1·8</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 5·4 is too high for a pressure ratio of 10 (14/1·4).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 3·6 is too high for a pressure ratio of 10 (14/1·4).</li><li><b>(d)</b> 2·7 ≈ heat rejected/work = 319·85/116 = 2·76, which is the heat-pump COP (= COP<sub>R</sub> + 1).</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Heat rejected per kg = h<sub>2</sub> − h<sub>3</sub> = 319·85 kJ/kg.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-107", year: 2022, no: 107, subject: "icrac", topic: "Limits of single-refrigerant low-temperature systems",
q: `The use of a single refrigerant in a simple vapor compression cycle for the production of low temperatures is limited by which of the following reasons?<ol><li>Solidification temperature of the refrigerant</li><li>Extremely low pressures in the evaporator and large suction volumes if a high-boiling refrigerant is selected</li><li>Very high-pressure ratio, therefore a low coefficient of performance</li></ol>Select the correct answer using the code given below :`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Arora lists these limitations of a single-stage, single-refrigerant system for very low temperatures:</p>
<ul><li><b>1:</b> The refrigerant must not freeze at the evaporator temperature.</li><li><b>2:</b> A high-boiling refrigerant (one that condenses at moderate pressures) has a very low evaporator pressure at low temperatures, with very large specific volume of suction vapour, needing huge compressors and risking air leakage into the system.</li><li><b>3:</b> A low-boiling refrigerant (one suited to the low evaporator temperature) has a very high condensing pressure, so the pressure ratio is very high and the COP and volumetric efficiency are low.</li></ul>
<p>Solutions: multi-stage compression with intercooling, and cascade systems, in which a low-temperature cycle (for example with R23 or ethane) rejects heat to the evaporator of a high-temperature cycle (for example with ammonia or R404A).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Cascade systems are used for −70 to −100 °C applications such as blood plasma storage and environmental test chambers.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-108", year: 2022, no: 108, subject: "icrac", topic: "Capillary tube length (stepwise method)", doubt: true,
q: `A capillary tube in a one-ton R22 air conditioner has a bore of 2·3 mm. Saturated liquid from the condenser enters at a temperature of 48 °C and flows adiabatically through the tube until its temperature is 5 °C. The friction factor is given as f = 0·32/Re<sup>0·25</sup>. What is the length of the capillary tube? (Take intermediate sections at 40 °C, 30 °C, 20 °C and 10 °C and the mass flow rate of the refrigerant as 0·02417 kg/s)`,
opts: ["13·41 kg.s<sup>−1</sup>.m<sup>−1</sup>", "16·42 kg.s<sup>−1</sup>.m<sup>−1</sup>", "19·42 kg.s<sup>−1</sup>.m<sup>−1</sup>", "22·46 kg.s<sup>−1</sup>.m<sup>−1</sup>"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 13·41 (official key)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Method (Arora, Stoecker): divide the tube into sections between the given temperatures and apply, to each section, conservation of mass, energy and momentum.</p><div class="formula">G = ṁ/A</div><div class="formula">h + V²/2 = h₁, V = Gv (section-wise)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>Mass flux G = ṁ/A = 0·02417/(π × 0·0023²/4) = 5817 kg/s m² (constant along the tube)</li><li>Energy: h + V²/2 = h<sub>1</sub> (adiabatic), with V = G v and h = h<sub>f</sub> + x h<sub>fg</sub>, v = v<sub>f</sub> + x v<sub>fg</sub>; solve for quality x at each temperature</li><li>Momentum: (p<sub>1</sub> − p<sub>2</sub>) − G(V<sub>2</sub> − V<sub>1</sub>) = f<sub>m</sub> (ΔL/D)(ρ<sub>m</sub>V<sub>m</sub>²/2), with f = 0·32/Re<sup>0·25</sup> and Re = VD/ν at the section mean</li></ul>
<p><b>Our check</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Using R22 saturation data at 48, 40, 30, 20, 10 and 5 °C, the qualities rise from 0 to about 0·25 and the velocity from about 5 to about 60 m/s.</li><li>Summing the section lengths gives a total of the order of 0·6 m with f as a Darcy factor (shorter still with a Fanning factor).</li></ol>
<p>That is a typical capillary length, but it matches none of the options, which are also printed with the unit kg s<sup>−1</sup> m<sup>−1</sup>, not a length unit.</p>
<p><b>Other options</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 16·42 is likewise not reproducible from the given data.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 19·42 is likewise not reproducible from the given data.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 22·46 is likewise not reproducible from the given data.</li></ul>
<div class="note">In doubt (key followed). We could not reproduce any option: a stepwise mass–energy–momentum calculation with standard R22 properties gives a length of roughly 0·6 m, and the options' unit (kg s<sup>−1</sup> m<sup>−1</sup>) does not fit a length. The question or options appear to be garbled; the official key gives (a).</div>
<p><b>Remember:</b> In practice, capillary tubes are sized from manufacturers' charts and then fine-tuned by testing.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-109", year: 2022, no: 109, subject: "thermo", topic: "Minimum compression work",
q: `The work required for compressing one kg of air is minimum if the compression is carried out`,
opts: ["adiabatically", "isothermally", "isentropically", "isochorically"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) isothermally</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For a steady-flow compressor, work = ∫v dp. Cooling the gas during compression keeps its specific volume small at each pressure, so the area ∫v dp is the least for isothermal compression (pv = const).</p>
<div class="formula">W<sub>iso</sub> = p<sub>1</sub>v<sub>1</sub> ln(p<sub>2</sub>/p<sub>1</sub>) &nbsp;&nbsp; W<sub>poly</sub> = [n/(n − 1)] p<sub>1</sub>v<sub>1</sub>[(p<sub>2</sub>/p<sub>1</sub>)<sup>(n−1)/n</sup> − 1]</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Adiabatic compression raises the temperature and needs more work (largest area on the p–V diagram).</li><li><b>(c)</b> Isentropic compression is reversible adiabatic and needs more work than isothermal.</li><li><b>(d)</b> An isochoric process involves no volume change, so it is not compression at all.</li></ul>
<p>Example, air from 1 to 8 bar: isothermal work is about 2·08 p<sub>1</sub>v<sub>1</sub>, against about 2·85 p<sub>1</sub>v<sub>1</sub> for isentropic.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Isothermal efficiency = isothermal work/actual work. Intercooling in multistage compressors moves the process towards isothermal.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-110", year: 2022, no: 110, subject: "turbo", topic: "Torque converter",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding torque converter :<ol><li>A torque converter is a modified form of fluid coupling.</li><li>A reciprocating pump is used in a torque converter.</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> A torque converter is a fluid coupling with an extra fixed member, the stator (reactor), between the pump and turbine. The stator redirects the fluid, so the output torque can be greater than the input torque (torque multiplication, typically 2–3 times at stall).</li><li><b>2 (wrong):</b> The driving element is a <i>centrifugal</i> (radial-flow) pump impeller, not a reciprocating pump; the driven element is a turbine runner.</li></ul>
<div class="formula">Coupling: T<sub>out</sub> = T<sub>in</sub>; Converter: T<sub>out</sub> = T<sub>in</sub> + T<sub>stator</sub></div>
<p><b>Concept:</b> In a fluid coupling, the efficiency equals the speed ratio (η = N<sub>t</sub>/N<sub>p</sub>), because torque in equals torque out. A torque converter's efficiency peaks at an intermediate speed ratio, which is why modern converters lock up or freewheel the stator at high speed.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Torque converters are used in automatic transmissions of cars, buses and earth-moving machinery.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-111", year: 2022, no: 111, subject: "turbo", topic: "Pump model–prototype speed",
q: `A one fourth scale model of a pump was tested in a laboratory at 1000 rpm. The head developed and power input at the best efficiency point were found to be 7 m and 25 kW respectively. What is the speed of the prototype if the prototype pump has to operate against a head of 36 m?`,
opts: ["467 rpm", "567 rpm", "667 rpm", "767 rpm"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 567 rpm</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For geometrically similar pumps at the same point of operation, the head coefficient gH/(N²D²) is the same:</p>
<div class="formula">H ∝ N² D² → N<sub>p</sub> = N<sub>m</sub> (D<sub>m</sub>/D<sub>p</sub>) √(H<sub>p</sub>/H<sub>m</sub>)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>D<sub>m</sub>/D<sub>p</sub> = 1/4; N<sub>m</sub> = 1000 rpm; H<sub>m</sub> = 7 m; H<sub>p</sub> = 36 m</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>√(36/7) = 2·268.</li><li>N<sub>p</sub> = 1000 × 0·25 × 2·268 = <b>567 rpm</b>.</li><li>Prototype power: P ∝ N³D⁵ → P<sub>p</sub> = 25 × (0·567)³ × 4⁵ = 25 × 0·1823 × 1024 = 4667 kW.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 467 comes from wrong exponents (for example using D ratio squared).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 667 comes from wrong exponents (for example using D ratio squared).</li><li><b>(d)</b> 767 rpm comes from wrong exponents (for example using D ratio squared).</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Affinity laws: Q ∝ ND³, H ∝ N²D², P ∝ N³D⁵.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-112", year: 2022, no: 112, subject: "turbo", topic: "Force components on a turbine runner",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding Euler turbine equation :<ol><li>The axial component produces a bending of the shaft which is taken by the journal bearings.</li><li>The axial force only can cause the rotation of the runner and produce work.</li><li>The tangential force only can cause the rotation of the runner and produce work.</li><li>The radial component produces a bending of the shaft which is taken by the journal bearings.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "3 and 4 only", "2 and 4 only"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 3 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The force of the fluid on the runner can be split into three components:</p>
<ul><li><b>Axial:</b> along the shaft, producing an axial thrust taken by <i>thrust</i> bearings. So statement <b>1 is wrong</b> (it is not bending), and <b>2 is wrong</b> (the axial force does no work on the rotating runner).</li><li><b>Tangential:</b> the only component with a moment about the axis, so it alone turns the runner and does work: <b>3 correct</b>. Torque = ṁ(V<sub>w1</sub>r<sub>1</sub> − V<sub>w2</sub>r<sub>2</sub>).</li><li><b>Radial:</b> towards or away from the axis, producing bending of the shaft taken by <i>journal</i> bearings: <b>4 correct</b>.</li></ul>
<div class="formula">Euler equation: work per unit mass = u<sub>1</sub>V<sub>w1</sub> − u<sub>2</sub>V<sub>w2</sub></div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, 2, which are incorrect; Omits 3, 4, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 4, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 3, 4.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Only the whirl (tangential) components of velocity appear in the Euler equation.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-113", year: 2022, no: 113, subject: "turbo", topic: "Turbine selection by head",
q: `Which one of the following turbines is the most popularly used one in the medium head range of 60 m – 300 m?`,
opts: ["Kaplan turbine", "Francis turbine", "Pelton turbine", "Deriaz turbine"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Francis turbine</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Approximate head ranges for hydraulic turbines:</p>
<ul><li><b>(c)</b> Pelton wheel (impulse): high heads, above about 250–300 m (up to 1800 m); specific speed about 10–35.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Francis (mixed-flow reaction): medium heads, about 30–300 m (up to 600 m); specific speed about 60–300. It is the most widely used type.</li><li><b>(a)</b> Kaplan (axial-flow, adjustable blades): low heads, below about 60 m (2–70 m); specific speed 300–1000.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Deriaz (diagonal flow, adjustable blades): medium heads of about 20–100 m; also usable as a pump-turbine, but far less common than Francis.</li></ul>
<div class="formula">Specific speed N<sub>s</sub> = N√P/H<sup>5/4</sup></div>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Turbine choice follows specific speed: low N<sub>s</sub> → Pelton; medium → Francis; high → Kaplan or propeller.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-114", year: 2022, no: 114, subject: "turbo", topic: "Turbine types and flow directions",
q: `Match the following :<br><b>List I (Turbine types)</b> A. Deriaz turbine; B. Propeller turbine; C. Francis turbine; D. Pelton turbine<br><b>List II (Flow directions)</b> 1. Mixed flow; 2. Tangential flow; 3. Axial flow; 4. Diagonal flow`,
opts: ["A-4, B-3, C-1, D-2", "A-3, B-4, C-2, D-1", "A-2, B-1, C-4, D-3", "A-1, B-2, C-3, D-4"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) A-4, B-3, C-1, D-2</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Matching</b></p><ul><li><b>A Deriaz → 4 diagonal flow:</b> the water flows at an angle (about 45°) to the axis; the blades are adjustable, like a Kaplan's.</li><li><b>B Propeller → 3 axial flow:</b> water flows parallel to the axis; Kaplan is an axial propeller turbine with adjustable blades.</li><li><b>C Francis → 1 mixed flow:</b> modern Francis runners take water in radially and discharge it axially.</li><li><b>D Pelton → 2 tangential flow:</b> the jet strikes the buckets tangentially to the runner circle.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> each mismatch at least the Pelton turbine, which is the only tangential-flow machine.</li><li><b>(c)</b> each mismatch at least the Pelton turbine, which is the only tangential-flow machine.</li><li><b>(d)</b> each mismatch at least the Pelton turbine, which is the only tangential-flow machine.</li><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Every pair matches.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Older inward-radial-flow Francis turbines were called "radial"; the modern ones are mixed flow.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-115", year: 2022, no: 115, subject: "turbo", topic: "Kaplan turbine flow ratio",
q: `A Kaplan turbine plant develops 2000 kW under a head of 8 m while running at 100 rpm. The discharge is 250 m³/s. The tip diameter of the runner is 6·5 m and the hub to tip ratio is 0·43. What is the flow ratio based on tip speed? (Take acceleration due to gravity as 9·81 m/s²)`,
opts: ["0·43", "0·58", "0·62", "0·73"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 0·73</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The flow ratio is the axial (flow) velocity divided by the spouting velocity √(2gH).</p>
<div class="formula">V<sub>f</sub> = Q/[π(D<sub>o</sub>² − D<sub>b</sub>²)/4] &nbsp;&nbsp; ψ = V<sub>f</sub>/√(2gH)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>D<sub>o</sub> = 6·5 m; D<sub>b</sub> = 0·43 × 6·5 = 2·795 m; Q = 250 m³/s; H = 8 m</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Flow area = (π/4)(42·25 − 7·81) = 27·05 m².</li><li>V<sub>f</sub> = 250/27·05 = 9·24 m/s.</li><li>√(2gH) = √(156·96) = 12·53 m/s.</li><li>ψ = 9·24/12·53 = <b>0·74</b> ≈ 0·73.</li></ol>
<p>The tip speed is u = π × 6·5 × 100/60 = 34·0 m/s, giving a speed ratio u/√(2gH) = 2·71, typical of Kaplan turbines (1·5–3). The phrase "based on tip speed" refers to the speed-ratio companion figure; the flow ratio itself uses √(2gH).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0·43 is the hub ratio. uses the full circle area (V<sub>f</sub> = 7·53 m/s gives 0·60).</li><li><b>(b)</b> 0·58 uses the full circle area (V<sub>f</sub> = 7·53 m/s gives 0·60).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0·62 uses the full circle area (V<sub>f</sub> = 7·53 m/s gives 0·60).</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Overall efficiency here = 2000/(9·81 × 250 × 8) = 10%, an unrealistically low value; the data serve only the velocity calculation.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-116", year: 2022, no: 116, subject: "turbo", topic: "Jet propulsion of a ship",
q: `The water in a jet-propelled boat is drawn amid-ship and discharged at the back with an absolute velocity of 15 m/s. The boat speed is 30 km/hr. The cross-sectional area of the jet at the back is 0·03 m². What is the efficiency of the jet propulsion?`,
opts: ["68·5%", "51·3%", "45·9%", "39·4%"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 45·9%</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Bansal's result for a ship drawing water in amidships (the water enters with the ship's velocity relative to the ship) and ejecting it at the back:</p>
<div class="formula">η = 2 V u/(V + u)²</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>V = 15 m/s (jet velocity); u = 30 km/h = 8·333 m/s (boat speed)</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>V + u = 23·33 m/s → (V + u)² = 544·4.</li><li>2Vu = 2 × 15 × 8·333 = 250.</li><li>η = 250/544·4 = <b>0·459 = 45·9%</b>.</li></ol>
<p>The jet area (0·03 m²) is needed only to find the thrust and power, e.g. mass flow = 1000 × 0·03 × 23·33 = 700 kg/s based on the jet velocity relative to the ship.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 68·5% comes from other forms of the efficiency (for example 2u/(V + 2u) = 52·6%, or the formula for water taken in at the bow).</li><li><b>(b)</b> 51·3% comes from other forms of the efficiency (for example 2u/(V + 2u) = 52·6%, or the formula for water taken in at the bow).</li><li><b>(d)</b> 39.4 % would need V ≈ 19 m/s.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Jet-propulsion efficiency is highest when the jet velocity is only slightly larger than the ship's speed, but then the thrust per unit flow is small.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-117", year: 2022, no: 117, subject: "turbo", topic: "Surging in compressors",
q: `Surge is a condition which occurs`,
opts: ["in forward movement of aircraft", "beyond the stable limit of operation", "in forward motion of aircraft with speed above sonic velocity", "due to the blast of air produced in a compressor"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) beyond the stable limit of operation</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> On a compressor characteristic (pressure ratio against flow), operation is stable to the right of the peak. If the flow is reduced past the peak (the surge line), the compressor can no longer maintain the delivery pressure: flow reverses briefly, pressure falls, forward flow resumes and the cycle repeats. This violent oscillation, with noise and vibration, is surge.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Forward motion of the aircraft, subsonic or supersonic, is not itself surge; ram effects change the inlet conditions only.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Forward motion of the aircraft, subsonic or supersonic, is not itself surge; ram effects change the inlet conditions only.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Surge is a flow instability of the whole system, not a "blast of air".</li></ul>
<p>Prevention: blow-off or bleed valves, variable inlet guide vanes and stators, multi-spool compressors, and control systems that keep operation away from the surge line (surge margin).</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Stalling is local flow separation on blades; surge is a system-wide instability that stalling can lead to (see Q118).</p>` },

{ id: "2022-118", year: 2022, no: 118, subject: "turbo", topic: "Stalling in axial compressors",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding stalling in axial compressor :<ol><li>The flow separation occurring on the suction side of the blade is called negative stalling.</li><li>The flow separation occurring on the pressure side of the blade is called positive stalling.</li><li>The rotating stall decreases the effectiveness of the compressor.</li><li>The delivery pressure significantly drops with large stalling leading to flow reversal.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "3 and 4 only", "1 and 4 only"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 3 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Stalling in axial compressors.</p><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (wrong):</b> Separation on the suction (convex) side, from too large a positive incidence at low flow, is <i>positive</i> stalling.</li><li><b>2 (wrong):</b> Separation on the pressure (concave) side, from negative incidence at high flow, is <i>negative</i> stalling. Statements 1 and 2 have the names swapped.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> In rotating stall, stalled cells move around the annulus (at about 40–70% of rotor speed), reducing pressure rise and efficiency and causing blade vibration.</li><li><b>4 (correct):</b> With extensive stalling, the delivery pressure falls sharply and the flow can reverse: the start of surge.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, 2, which are incorrect; Omits 3, 4, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 4, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 3, 4.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Positive stalling is the dangerous one, since it occurs at low flow, near the surge line.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-119", year: 2022, no: 119, subject: "fm", topic: "Work saved by air vessels",
q: `In a double acting pump, the work saved by fitting air vessels is about`,
opts: ["19·2%", "29·2%", "39·2%", "50·2%"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 39·2%</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Without air vessels, the pipe friction losses follow the sinusoidal velocity variation; with air vessels, the pipe velocity is nearly uniform at its mean value. Comparing the work against friction (Bansal):</p>
<ul><li>Single-acting pump: the friction work with an air vessel is only about 15·2% of that without one, so about <b>84·8%</b> of the friction work is saved.</li><li>Double-acting pump: the mean velocity is twice as large, and the saving works out to about <b>39·2%</b>.</li></ul>
<div class="formula">Friction head without vessel: average (2/3) h<sub>f,max</sub>; with vessel (uniform flow): h<sub>f</sub> at the mean velocity</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Without air vessel the velocity is sinusoidal; with it, uniform at the mean.</li><li>For a double-acting pump the saving is <b>39.2 %</b> (Bansal).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 19.2 % is far below the double-acting saving.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 29.2 % is below the double-acting saving.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 50.2 % exceeds the textbook figure.</li></ul>
<p>Air vessels also allow higher pump speeds without separation in the suction pipe, and give a more uniform discharge.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Remember the pair: 84·8% (single acting) and 39·2% (double acting).</p>` },

{ id: "2022-120", year: 2022, no: 120, subject: "fm", topic: "Reciprocating pump discharge with slip",
q: `A single acting reciprocating pump has a bore of 200 mm and a stroke of 350 mm and runs at 45 rpm. The suction head is 8 m and the delivery head is 20 m. What is the theoretical discharge of water if slip is 12%? (Take acceleration due to gravity as 9·81 m/s²)`,
opts: ["7·260 l/s", "6·956 l/s", "3·456 l/s", "4·586 l/s"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 7·260 L/s</h3><div class="formula">Q<sub>th</sub> = A L N/60 (single acting) &nbsp;&nbsp; Q<sub>act</sub> = Q<sub>th</sub>(1 − slip)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>A = π × 0·2²/4 = 0·031416 m²; L = 0·35 m; N = 45 rpm; slip = 12%</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Q<sub>th</sub> = 0·031416 × 0·35 × 45/60 = 8·247 × 10<sup>−3</sup> m³/s = 8·247 L/s.</li><li>With 12% slip: Q = 8·247 × 0·88 = <b>7·257 L/s</b> ≈ 7·260 L/s.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The question calls this the "theoretical" discharge, but it is in fact the actual discharge after slip; 8·25 L/s is the swept-volume (theoretical) value. The heads are needed only for the power: P = ρgQ<sub>th</sub>(H<sub>s</sub> + H<sub>d</sub>) = 9810 × 0·008247 × 28 = 2·27 kW.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 6·956 uses the wrong speed, a radius in place of a diameter, or the double-acting formula reversed.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 3·456 uses the wrong speed, a radius in place of a diameter, or the double-acting formula reversed.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 4·586 L/s uses the wrong speed, a radius in place of a diameter, or the double-acting formula reversed.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Negative slip (actual &gt; theoretical) can occur with long suction pipes and high speeds.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-121", year: 2022, no: 121, subject: "power", topic: "Ideal processes of the Rankine cycle",
q: `Which one of the following statements is NOT correct regarding Rankine cycle?`,
opts: ["For the steam boiler, the ideal process would be a reversible constant pressure heating process of water to form steam", "For the turbine, the ideal process would be a reversible adiabatic expansion of steam", "For the condenser, it would be a reversible constant pressure heat rejection as the steam condenses till it becomes saturated liquid", "For the pump, the ideal process would be the reversible adiabatic expansion of the liquid ending at the final pressure"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In the pump, the ideal process is the reversible adiabatic <b>compression</b> of the liquid to the boiler pressure, not expansion. Pump work w<sub>p</sub> = v<sub>f</sub>(p<sub>boiler</sub> − p<sub>condenser</sub>) is small because liquid has a tiny specific volume.</p>
<p><b>The correct statements</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Boiler: reversible constant-pressure heating of the water into (superheated) steam.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Turbine: reversible adiabatic (isentropic) expansion of steam.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Condenser: reversible constant-pressure heat rejection until the steam becomes saturated liquid.</li></ul>
<div class="formula">η<sub>Rankine</sub> = [(h<sub>1</sub> − h<sub>2</sub>) − (h<sub>4</sub> − h<sub>3</sub>)]/(h<sub>1</sub> − h<sub>4</sub>)</div>
<p><b>Remember:</b> The Rankine cycle avoids the Carnot cycle's impractical wet-vapour compression by condensing fully and pumping only liquid.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-122", year: 2022, no: 122, subject: "power", topic: "Condensers and vacuum efficiency",
q: `Which one of the following statements is NOT correct?`,
opts: ["In surface condensers, the cooling water and exhaust steam do not come in direct contact with each other", "The vacuum efficiency is the ratio of ideal vacuum to actual vacuum", "The Hot well is a sump between the condenser and the boiler where the condensate coming from the condenser is collected", "Condenser is a closed vessel heat exchanger in which the steam coming from turbine is condensed using a supply of cooling water at atmospheric temperature"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Vacuum efficiency is the ratio of the <b>actual</b> vacuum at the steam inlet of the condenser to the <b>ideal</b> vacuum (the vacuum that would exist if only steam were present, i.e. barometric pressure minus the saturation pressure of steam at the condensate temperature). The statement inverts it; it is always less than 1 because of air leakage.</p>
<div class="formula">η<sub>vacuum</sub> = actual vacuum/ideal vacuum</div>
<p><b>The correct statements</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> In surface condensers, cooling water flows in tubes and steam outside; the two do not mix, so the condensate can be reused as boiler feed.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The hot well collects condensate between the condenser and the boiler feed pump.</li><li><b>(d)</b> A condenser is a closed heat exchanger that condenses turbine exhaust using cooling water.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Condenser efficiency = actual rise in cooling-water temperature/(saturation temperature at condenser pressure − water inlet temperature).</p>` },

{ id: "2022-123", year: 2022, no: 123, subject: "power", topic: "Fuel analysis and calorific value",
q: `Which one of the following statements is correct?`,
opts: ["Ultimate analysis is the determination of the percentages of fixed carbon, volatile matter, moisture and ash in fuel", "The percentage of each constituent element in the fuel such as carbon, hydrogen, sulphur, oxygen, nitrogen, and ash, is determined by proximate analysis", "Calorific value of solid or liquid fuel is defined as the heat evolved by the complete combustion of unit mass of fuel", "Proximate analysis value of gaseous fuel is expressed as heat developed by the complete combustion of one cubic metre of gas at standard temperature and pressure"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The calorific value of a solid or liquid fuel is the heat released by complete combustion of unit mass (1 kg) of the fuel, in kJ/kg.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Fixed carbon, volatile matter, moisture and ash are found by <i>proximate</i> analysis, not ultimate.</li><li><b>(b)</b> The elemental percentages (C, H, S, O, N, ash) are found by <i>ultimate</i> analysis, not proximate. Statements (a) and (b) are swapped.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Heat from complete combustion of 1 m³ of gas at standard conditions defines the <i>calorific value</i> of a gaseous fuel (kJ/m³), not its proximate analysis.</li></ul>
<p>Higher calorific value (HCV) includes the latent heat of the water formed; lower (LCV) excludes it: LCV = HCV − 9H × 2442 kJ/kg (H = mass fraction of hydrogen).</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Ultimate analysis is used for combustion (air) calculations; proximate analysis for judging coal quality and behaviour.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-124", year: 2022, no: 124, subject: "power", topic: "Orsat apparatus and bomb calorimeter",
q: `Which one of the following statements is NOT correct?`,
opts: ["A simple and convenient apparatus used for the volumetric analysis of dry flue gases is known as orsat apparatus", "The calorific values of liquid fuels are generally determined by using an orsat apparatus", "The calorific values of solid and liquid fuels are generally determined by using a bomb calorimeter", "In orsat apparatus, potassium hydroxide used as absorbent to absorb carbon dioxide"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The Orsat apparatus analyses the <i>composition</i> of flue gases; it cannot measure calorific value. Calorific values of solid and liquid fuels are measured with a bomb calorimeter (and of gaseous fuels with a Junker's or Boys' gas calorimeter).</p>
<p><b>The correct statements</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The Orsat apparatus gives the volumetric analysis of dry flue gas.</li><li><b>(c)</b> In a bomb calorimeter, a weighed fuel sample burns in oxygen at high pressure in a sealed steel bomb immersed in water; the water's temperature rise gives the higher calorific value.</li><li><b>(d)</b> KOH solution absorbs CO<sub>2</sub> in the Orsat apparatus.</li></ul>
<p>Orsat absorbents, in order: KOH for CO<sub>2</sub>, alkaline pyrogallol for O<sub>2</sub>, ammoniacal cuprous chloride for CO; N<sub>2</sub> is found by difference. The analysis is on a dry basis because the water vapour condenses at room temperature.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> CO<sub>2</sub> must be absorbed first, because pyrogallol also absorbs CO<sub>2</sub>.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-125", year: 2022, no: 125, subject: "power", topic: "Boiler terms and types",
q: `Which one of the following statements is NOT correct?`,
opts: ["Grate is the platform in the furnace of boiler upon which fuel is burnt and it is made of cast iron bars", "The removal of the mud and other impurities of water from the lowest part of the boiler is termed as blowing off", "Cochran boiler is one of the best types of vertical multi-tubular boiler", "Babcock and Wilcox boiler is a fire tube boiler"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The Babcock and Wilcox boiler is a <b>water-tube</b> boiler: water flows in inclined tubes connecting a steam drum with headers, while the hot gases pass outside the tubes. It is a horizontal, externally fired, stationary, natural-circulation boiler.</p>
<p><b>The correct statements</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The grate is the platform in the furnace on which solid fuel burns; it is made of cast-iron bars with air spaces.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Blowing off removes sludge, mud and impurities from the lowest part of the boiler through the blow-off cock.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The Cochran boiler is a compact vertical multi-tubular (fire-tube) boiler.</li></ul>
<p>Fire-tube boilers: Cochran, Lancashire, Cornish, locomotive, Scotch marine. Water-tube boilers: Babcock and Wilcox, Stirling, La Mont, Benson, Loeffler.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Water-tube boilers handle higher pressures and capacities, because the small tubes withstand pressure better than a large shell.</p>` }
);

// ---- q2022f.js ----
// ESE 2022 Prelims Paper II, Mechanical Engineering, Series A, Q126–150
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
(function () {
var C = ["Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is <b>NOT</b> the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Statement (I) is true but Statement (II) is false", "Statement (I) is false but Statement (II) is true"];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2022-126", year: 2022, no: 126, subject: "power", topic: "Boiler classification",
q: `Which one of the following statements is correct?`,
opts: ["In water tube boiler, water surrounds the tubes and hot gases are inside the tubes", "The boilers which produce steam at pressures of 10 bar and below are called high pressure boiler", "Lancashire boilers are externally fired boiler", "Stirling boilers are externally fired boiler"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Stirling boilers are externally fired boiler</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The Stirling boiler is a water-tube boiler with bent tubes connecting steam and water drums; its furnace is outside the boiler shell (external firing), like other water-tube boilers.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> describes a <i>fire-tube</i> boiler. In a water-tube boiler, water is inside the tubes and the hot gases outside.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Boilers producing steam at 10 bar or below are <i>low-pressure</i> boilers; high-pressure boilers work at about 80 bar and above (modern ones are supercritical, above 221 bar).</li><li><b>(c)</b> The Lancashire boiler is <i>internally</i> fired: the furnace is in two large flue tubes inside the shell.</li></ul>
<p>Internally fired: Lancashire, Cornish, Cochran, locomotive. Externally fired: Babcock and Wilcox, Stirling.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Internally fired boilers lose less heat, since the furnace is surrounded by water.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-127", year: 2022, no: 127, subject: "power", topic: "Auxiliary consumption from plant efficiencies",
q: `In a power plant, the efficiencies of the electric generator, turbine (mechanical), boiler, cycle and overall plant are 0·97, 0·95, 0·92, 0·42 and 0·33 respectively. What percentage of the total electricity generated is consumed in running the auxiliaries?`,
opts: ["9·29%", "8·50%", "7·32%", "6·76%"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 7·32%</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The product of the component efficiencies gives the efficiency based on <i>gross</i> generation; the stated overall efficiency is based on the <i>net</i> output after auxiliaries.</p><div class="formula">Aux fraction = 1 − η_net/η_gross</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Gross efficiency = 0·97 × 0·95 × 0·92 × 0·42 = 0·3561.</li><li>Net efficiency = 0·33.</li><li>Fraction of generated power used by auxiliaries = (0·3561 − 0·33)/0·3561 = 1 − 0·33/0·3561 = <b>0·0732 = 7·32%</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 9·29% arises from dividing by the wrong base (for example (0·3561 − 0·33)/0·33 = 7·9%) or from omitting one of the efficiencies.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 8·50% arises from dividing by the wrong base (for example (0·3561 − 0·33)/0·33 = 7·9%) or from omitting one of the efficiencies.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 6·76% arises from dividing by the wrong base (for example (0·3561 − 0·33)/0·33 = 7·9%) or from omitting one of the efficiencies.</li></ul>
<p>Auxiliaries include boiler feed pumps, cooling-water pumps, forced and induced draught fans, coal mills and ash handling; typically 6–10% of generation in coal plants.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Net heat rate = 3600/η<sub>net</sub> kJ/kWh = 10,900 kJ/kWh here.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-128", year: 2022, no: 128, subject: "power", topic: "Why the ideal regenerative cycle is impracticable",
q: `An ideal cycle is impracticable because`,
opts: ["there is an ideal fluid available which is considered as working fluid", "it is not possible to transfer heat of expanding steam to the fluid in the turbine itself", "it is always possible to transfer heat of expanding steam to the fluid in the turbine itself", "there is no possibility of excessive humidity in low pressure stages of the turbine"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) it is not possible to transfer heat of expanding steam to the fluid in the turbine itself</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The context is the ideal regenerative Rankine cycle: feed water would be heated reversibly by flowing around the turbine casing, picking up heat from the expanding steam, so that heat addition from outside occurs only at the highest temperature. Its efficiency would equal Carnot's. It is impracticable because:</p>
<ul><li>It is not possible in practice to transfer heat from the expanding steam to the feed water inside the turbine (heat exchange through a turbine casing would be far too slow and complex).</li><li>The steam, having given up heat, would become very wet at the low-pressure stages, causing blade erosion.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> is the reverse of the true reasons.</li><li><b>(c)</b> is the reverse of the true reasons.</li><li><b>(d)</b> In fact, excessive moisture in the low-pressure stages <i>would</i> occur; this is another reason the ideal cycle is impracticable.</li></ul>
<p>Practical solution: bleed steam from the turbine at several points to heat the feed water in open or closed feedwater heaters.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Regeneration raises the mean temperature of heat addition, which raises efficiency.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-129", year: 2022, no: 129, subject: "power", topic: "High-pressure boilers and pressures",
q: `Match the following :<br><b>List I (Boiler Type)</b> A. La Mont Boiler; B. Loeffler Boiler; C. Benson Boiler; D. Velox Boiler<br><b>List II (Pressure (kg/cm²))</b> 1. 84; 2. 170; 3. 135; 4. 230`,
opts: ["A-2, B-1, C-3, D-4", "A-1, B-2, C-4, D-3", "A-2, B-3, C-4, D-1", "A-3, B-2, C-1, D-4"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) A-2, B-3, C-4, D-1</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Matching (Rajput, Nag)</b></p><ul><li><b>A La Mont → 2 (170 kg/cm²):</b> forced-circulation water-tube boiler; a pump circulates water about 8–10 times the evaporation rate.</li><li><b>B Loeffler → 3 (about 135–140 kg/cm²):</b> water is evaporated by mixing with superheated steam in a drum, avoiding salt deposits in tubes.</li><li><b>C Benson → 4 (230 kg/cm²):</b> once-through boiler working above the critical pressure (221 bar), so there is no drum and no boiling.</li><li><b>D Velox → 1 (about 84 kg/cm²):</b> uses gas velocities above sound velocity (pressurised combustion, gas turbine-driven compressor) for very high heat transfer rates.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> gives Benson 135 and Velox 230: wrong, as only the Benson boiler is supercritical.</li><li><b>(b)</b> gives La Mont 84 and Loeffler 170, and Velox 135.</li><li><b>(d)</b> gives Benson 84, far below its supercritical pressure.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Benson is the only supercritical boiler in the list; that fixes C-4 straight away.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-130", year: 2022, no: 130, subject: "power", topic: "Air required for combustion",
q: `What is the amount of air required to burn one kg of fuel and product of combustion for a fuel the percentage composition of which is given as C = 70%, H<sub>2</sub> = 30%?`,
opts: ["16·54 Kg", "17·54 Kg", "18·54 Kg", "19·54 Kg"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 18·54 kg</h3><div class="formula">Theoretical O<sub>2</sub> = (8/3)C + 8(H − O/8) + S &nbsp;&nbsp; Air = O<sub>2</sub>/0·23 (air contains 23% O<sub>2</sub> by mass)</div>
<p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Working (per kg of fuel)</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Carbon: C + O<sub>2</sub> → CO<sub>2</sub>: 12 kg C needs 32 kg O<sub>2</sub> → 0·7 × 8/3 = 1·867 kg O<sub>2</sub>.</li><li>Hydrogen: 2H<sub>2</sub> + O<sub>2</sub> → 2H<sub>2</sub>O: 1 kg H needs 8 kg O<sub>2</sub> → 0·3 × 8 = 2·4 kg O<sub>2</sub>.</li><li>Total O<sub>2</sub> = 4·267 kg.</li><li>Air = 4·267/0·23 = <b>18·55 kg</b>.</li></ol>
<p>Products: CO<sub>2</sub> = 0·7 × 11/3 = 2·567 kg; H<sub>2</sub>O = 0·3 × 9 = 2·7 kg; N<sub>2</sub> = 0·77 × 18·55 = 14·28 kg; total 19·55 kg (= 1 kg fuel + 18·55 kg air, by mass balance).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(d)</b> 19·54 kg is the mass of the <i>products</i>, not the air.</li><li><b>(a)</b> 16·54 uses a wrong oxygen fraction in air.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 17·54 kg uses a wrong oxygen fraction in air.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> With 23·2% O<sub>2</sub> by mass, the air would be 18·39 kg; the options use 23%.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-131", year: 2022, no: 131, subject: "power", topic: "Improving Rankine cycle efficiency",
q: `Which one of the following statements is correct?`,
opts: ["The Rankine cycle efficiency can be improved by increasing the average temperature at which heat is rejected", "The Rankine cycle efficiency can be improved by decreasing/reducing the temperature at which heat is rejected", "If the steam is superheated before allowing it to expand the Rankine cycle efficiency may be decreased", "The thermal efficiency of the Rankine cycle can be amply improved by increasing the condenser pressure"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Like any heat engine, η ≈ 1 − T<sub>L,mean</sub>/T<sub>H,mean</sub>: efficiency rises when the mean temperature of heat addition rises or the temperature of heat rejection falls.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <i>Raising</i> the heat-rejection temperature lowers the efficiency.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Superheating raises the mean temperature of heat addition, so it <i>increases</i> efficiency (and reduces moisture at the turbine exit).</li><li><b>(d)</b> Raising the condenser pressure raises the heat-rejection temperature, which <i>reduces</i> efficiency. Lowering it (higher vacuum) improves efficiency.</li></ul>
<p>Ways to improve Rankine efficiency: lower condenser pressure, superheating, higher boiler pressure (with superheat to limit moisture), reheating and regenerative feed heating.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> The condenser temperature cannot go below the cooling-water temperature: about 0·04–0·1 bar in practice.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-132", year: 2022, no: 132, subject: "power", topic: "Rankine cycle processes",
q: `Match the following :<br><b>List I</b> A. Boiler to Turbine process; B. Turbine to Condenser process; C. Condenser to Pump process; D. Pump to Boiler process<br><b>List II</b> 1. Reversible adiabatic expansion in the turbine; 2. Constant pressure transfer of heat in the condenser; 3. Reversible adiabatic pumping process in the feed pump; 4. Constant pressure transfer of heat in the boiler`,
opts: ["A-3, B-4, C-1, D-2", "A-4, B-3, C-2, D-1", "A-2, B-1, C-4, D-3", "A-1, B-2, C-3, D-4"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) A-1, B-2, C-3, D-4</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Number the states 1 (boiler exit/turbine inlet), 2 (turbine exit/condenser inlet), 3 (condenser exit/pump inlet) and 4 (pump exit/boiler inlet). Each List I entry names the process that starts at that state:</p>
<ul><li><b>A</b> from boiler to turbine exit (1–2): reversible adiabatic expansion in the turbine → <b>1</b>.</li><li><b>B</b> from turbine to condenser exit (2–3): constant-pressure heat rejection in the condenser → <b>2</b>.</li><li><b>C</b> from condenser to pump exit (3–4): reversible adiabatic pumping in the feed pump → <b>3</b>.</li><li><b>D</b> from pump to boiler exit (4–1): constant-pressure heat addition in the boiler → <b>4</b>.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> scrambles the sequence; the cycle must run expansion → condensation → pumping → heating.</li><li><b>(b)</b> scrambles the sequence; the cycle must run expansion → condensation → pumping → heating.</li><li><b>(c)</b> scrambles the sequence; the cycle must run expansion → condensation → pumping → heating.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Every pair matches.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Two isentropic processes (turbine, pump) and two isobaric processes (boiler, condenser) make up the ideal Rankine cycle.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-133", year: 2022, no: 133, subject: "renew", topic: "Fuel cell operating temperatures",
q: `Which one of the following fuel cells has lowest operating temperature?`,
opts: ["PEMFC", "MCFC", "PAFC", "SOFC"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) PEMFC</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Typical operating temperatures:</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> PEMFC (proton exchange membrane): about 60–100 °C, using a hydrated polymer membrane (Nafion). Lowest; quick start-up, so it suits vehicles.</li><li><b>(c)</b> PAFC (phosphoric acid): about 150–200 °C.</li><li><b>(b)</b> MCFC (molten carbonate): about 600–700 °C.</li><li><b>(d)</b> SOFC (solid oxide): about 700–1000 °C.</li></ul>
<p>Alkaline fuel cells (AFC, about 60–90 °C) are also low-temperature; they were used in the Apollo and Space Shuttle programmes. High-temperature cells (MCFC, SOFC) can reform hydrocarbon fuels internally and need no platinum catalyst.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> The membrane must stay hydrated, which limits PEMFCs to below 100 °C.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-134", year: 2022, no: 134, subject: "renew", topic: "Yaw control of wind turbines",
q: `Adjusting the nacelle about the vertical axis to bring the rotor facing wind is known as`,
opts: ["Pitch control", "Hub control", "Rotor control", "Yaw control"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Yaw control</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Yaw control turns the nacelle (with the rotor) about the vertical tower axis to keep the rotor facing the wind. Large turbines use a wind vane and motor-driven yaw drives; small turbines use a tail vane.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Pitch control rotates each blade about its own long axis to change the angle of attack.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 'Hub control' is not a standard turbine-control term.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 'Rotor control' is not a standard turbine-control term.</li></ul>
<p>Power regulation methods: pitch control, stall control (passive, by blade design), active stall, and yawing the rotor out of the wind (furling) in small machines.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Vertical-axis turbines (such as Darrieus) need no yaw control: they accept wind from any direction.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-135", year: 2022, no: 135, subject: "renew", topic: "Ammonia dissociation in distributed solar collectors",
q: `In a distributed collector solar thermal electric power plant, the heat collected in collectors is used to dissociate ammonia into nitrogen and hydrogen at approximately`,
opts: ["300 atm pressure", "150 atm pressure", "100 atm pressure", "40 atm pressure"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 300 atm pressure</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In the thermochemical (ammonia) scheme described by G. D. Rai, solar heat from distributed parabolic-dish collectors dissociates ammonia at about 300 atm: 2NH<sub>3</sub> + heat → N<sub>2</sub> + 3H<sub>2</sub>. The gases are carried in pipes at ambient temperature to a central plant, where they recombine in a synthesis reactor. The heat of reaction released there raises steam for the turbine, and the ammonia returns to the collectors.</p><div class="formula">2NH₃ + heat ⇌ N₂ + 3H₂</div>
<p><b>Why the scheme</b></p><ul><li>Energy travels as chemical energy, so pipes need no insulation and there are no heat losses in transport.</li><li>The gases can be stored, giving round-the-clock generation.</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Dissociation in the solar collectors at about <b>300 atm</b> (G. D. Rai).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 150 atm is half the dissociation pressure quoted.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 100 atm is too low for this scheme.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 40 atm is too low for this scheme.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> This reaction is the reverse of the Haber process, which also runs at a few hundred atmospheres.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-136", year: 2022, no: 136, subject: "renew", topic: "Solar thermal water pumps",
q: `Solar thermal water pumps work on`,
opts: ["Rankine cycle", "Otto cycle", "Carnot cycle", "Diesel cycle"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Rankine cycle</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Solar thermal pumps use flat-plate or concentrating collectors to evaporate a low-boiling organic working fluid (for example R-11, R-113, butane). The vapour drives a small engine or turbine, which runs the pump; the vapour is condensed (often by the pumped water) and returned. This is an organic Rankine cycle.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> The Otto cycle is an internal-combustion cycle; a solar pump uses an external heat source.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The Diesel cycle is an internal-combustion cycle.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The Carnot cycle is an ideal reference, not a practical engine cycle.</li></ul>
<p>Collector temperatures of only 70–90 °C give low cycle efficiencies (a few per cent), which is why solar PV pumps have largely replaced solar thermal pumps.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Organic fluids are used because water would need much higher temperatures to give useful vapour pressures.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-137", year: 2022, no: 137, subject: "renew", topic: "Solar concentration ratios",
q: `The concentration ratio of as high a value as 3000 can be obtained by`,
opts: ["modified flat plate collector", "compound parabolic concentrator", "cylindrical parabolic concentrator", "central tower receiver"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) central tower receiver</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Concentration ratio = aperture area/absorber area. Typical values (Sukhatme, Rai):</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Flat plate (with booster mirrors): about 1–2.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Compound parabolic concentrator (CPC): about 3–10 (non-tracking or seasonal adjustment).</li><li><b>(c)</b> Cylindrical parabolic trough (line focus, one-axis tracking): about 10–80.</li><li>Paraboloid dish (point focus, two-axis tracking): up to about 1000–3000.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Central tower receiver (a field of two-axis tracking heliostats): up to about 3000.</li></ul>
<p>Higher concentration gives higher receiver temperatures (over 1000 °C for towers), which allows efficient power cycles.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> The thermodynamic limit for point-focus concentration on earth is about 46,000 (set by the sun's angular size).</p>` },

{ id: "2022-138", year: 2022, no: 138, subject: "renew", topic: "Solar altitude (inclination) angle",
q: `The angle between the sun's ray and its projection on a horizontal surface is known as`,
opts: ["Inclination angle", "Zenith angle", "Solar azimuth angle", "Hour angle"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Inclination angle</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The solar altitude angle α, also called the inclination angle (G. D. Rai), is the angle between the sun's rays and their projection on a horizontal surface.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> The zenith angle θ<sub>z</sub> is between the sun's rays and the <i>vertical</i>: θ<sub>z</sub> = 90° − α.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The solar azimuth angle is measured in the horizontal plane, between the projection of the sun's rays and the north–south line.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The hour angle ω is the angular displacement of the sun from the local meridian, at 15° per hour (zero at solar noon).</li></ul>
<div class="formula">sin α = cos θ<sub>z</sub> = sin φ sin δ + cos φ cos δ cos ω</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>φ: latitude; δ: declination; ω: hour angle</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> At solar noon, α = 90° − |φ − δ|.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-139", year: 2022, no: 139, subject: "renew", topic: "Insolation",
q: `The total solar radiation received at any point on the earth's surface is the sum of the direct and diffuse radiation and is referred as`,
opts: ["Total diffuse radiation", "Insolation", "Total radiation", "Total specular radiation"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Insolation</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> G. D. Rai: the total (global) solar radiation received at a point on the earth's surface, the sum of the beam (direct) and diffuse components, is referred to as insolation (incident solar radiation).</p>
<div class="formula">I<sub>T</sub> = I<sub>b</sub> R<sub>b</sub> + I<sub>d</sub> R<sub>d</sub> + (I<sub>b</sub> + I<sub>d</sub>) ρ R<sub>r</sub> (on a tilted surface)</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Diffuse radiation is only the scattered part.</li><li><b>(c)</b> "Total radiation" is a generic phrase; the defined term in the source is insolation (also called global radiation).</li><li><b>(d)</b> Specular (mirror-like) reflection is not a component of incident solar radiation.</li></ul>
<p>Measurement: global radiation with a pyranometer; beam radiation with a pyrheliometer (see Q143).</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> India receives about 4–7 kWh/m² per day of insolation.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-140", year: 2022, no: 140, subject: "renew", topic: "Solar constant",
q: `The rate at which solar energy arrives at the top of the atmosphere is called`,
opts: ["Total energy", "Radiation", "Solar constant", "Radiation constant"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Solar constant</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The solar constant I<sub>sc</sub> is the rate at which solar energy arrives at the top of the atmosphere, on a surface normal to the sun's rays, at the mean earth–sun distance: about 1367 W/m² (WMO), recently revised to about 1361 W/m².</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 'Total energy' is a generic term, not the defined flux at the top of the atmosphere.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 'Radiation' is a generic term.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 'Radiation constant' usually refers to the Stefan–Boltzmann constant σ.</li></ul>
<p>Extraterrestrial radiation varies by about ±3·3% over the year because of the earth's elliptical orbit: I<sub>0</sub> = I<sub>sc</sub>[1 + 0·033 cos(360n/365)].</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> About 70% of extraterrestrial radiation typically reaches the ground on a clear day at high sun.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-141", year: 2022, no: 141, subject: "renew", topic: "Solar ponds",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding solar ponds :<ol><li>It is possible to produce electricity from a solar pond by using a special 'High temperature' heat engine coupled to an electric generator.</li><li>In a large solar pond, the thermal capacitance and resistance can be made large enough to retain the heat in the bottom layer from summer to winter and the pond can therefore be used for heating buildings in the winter.</li><li>A solar pond is an ingenious collector, which uses water as its top cover.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (wrong):</b> The bottom of a salt-gradient pond reaches only about 70–90 °C, so electricity is produced with a special <i>low-temperature</i> heat engine (organic Rankine cycle), as at Ein Bokek in Israel.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> A large pond has enough thermal mass and insulation (the non-convecting gradient layer) to store heat seasonally, from summer to winter, for space heating.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> The pond is a large collector in which the upper water layers act as the transparent, insulating "cover", while the dark bottom absorbs radiation.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Layers: an upper convective zone (fresh water), a non-convective salinity-gradient zone (the insulator, which stops convection because the denser salty water stays at the bottom) and a lower convective storage zone (concentrated brine).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 2, 3.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> India's 6000 m² solar pond at Bhuj supplied process heat to a dairy.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-142", year: 2022, no: 142, subject: "renew", topic: "Vertical-axis wind turbines",
q: `Which one of the following is a vertical axis wind mill?`,
opts: ["Darrieus type wind mill", "Propellor type wind mill", "Sail type wind mill", "Multi blade type wind mill"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Darrieus type wind mill</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The Darrieus rotor has curved (egg-beater) or straight (H-type) aerofoil blades turning about a vertical axis. It uses lift, accepts wind from any direction (no yaw control) and has the generator at ground level, but it is not self-starting.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Propeller-type (two- or three-blade) turbines are horizontal-axis; they are the modern utility-scale design.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Sail-type windmills (cloth sails on a horizontal shaft) are horizontal-axis.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Multi-blade (American farm) windmills are horizontal-axis, with high starting torque for water pumping.</li></ul>
<p>The other common vertical-axis machine is the Savonius rotor (S-shaped, drag-type, self-starting, low efficiency).</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Horizontal-axis turbines reach higher power coefficients (about 0·45–0·5) than Darrieus rotors (about 0·35–0·4).</p>` },

{ id: "2022-143", year: 2022, no: 143, subject: "renew", topic: "Pyranometer and pyrheliometer",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding solar radiation measurement :<ol><li>Pyranometer collimates the radiation to determine the beam intensity as a function of incident angle.</li><li>Pyranometer measures the total hemi-spherical solar radiation.</li><li>Pyrheliometer collimates the radiation to determine the beam intensity as a function of incident angle.</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 2 only", "3 only"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (wrong):</b> Collimating the radiation to measure the beam component is what a <i>pyrheliometer</i> does, not a pyranometer.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> A pyranometer (with a hemispherical glass dome over a black thermopile, for example the Eppley PSP) measures total (global) radiation from the whole hemisphere. With a shading ring that blocks the sun's disc, it measures diffuse radiation only.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> A pyrheliometer uses a collimating tube aimed at the sun (with a tracking mount) to measure the direct beam (normal incidence) intensity.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Sunshine recorders (Campbell–Stokes) measure the hours of bright sunshine.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 2, 3, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 2, 3.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Pyr<b>heli</b>ometer → <b>heli</b>os, the sun's direct beam; pyr<b>ano</b>meter → the whole sky (Greek <i>ano</i>, "above").</p>` },

{ id: "2022-144", year: 2022, no: 144, subject: "renew", topic: "Heat collection element in parabolic troughs",
q: `Which one of the following materials is used for heat collection element in parabolic trough plants of solar central receiver system?`,
opts: ["Copper", "Mild steel", "Stainless steel", "Aluminum"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Stainless steel</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The heat collection element (HCE, receiver tube) of a parabolic trough is a stainless-steel absorber tube with a selective cermet coating (high solar absorptance, low thermal emittance), enclosed in an evacuated glass envelope to cut convection losses. Heat transfer oil flows inside at about 390 °C.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Copper conducts well but is costly and has lower strength at high temperature; it suits low-temperature flat-plate collectors.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Mild steel oxidises and is less compatible with the vacuum seals and coatings at 400 °C.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Aluminium loses strength at these temperatures (it melts at 660 °C).</li></ul>
<p>Stainless steel combines strength at high temperature, corrosion resistance, compatibility with the coatings and a good glass-to-metal seal.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Examples: the SEGS plants in California, and Godawari Green Energy's 50 MW plant in Rajasthan.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-145", year: 2022, no: 145, subject: "fm", topic: "Centre of pressure and principle of moments",
q: `<b>Statement (I):</b> Centre of pressure is calculated by using the "Principle of Moments".<br><b>Statement (II):</b> The moment of the resultant force about an axis is equal to the sum of moments of the components about the same axis.`,
opts: C, ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Both true; II explains I</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Centre of pressure and principle of moments.</p><ul><li><b>Statement I (true):</b> The depth of the centre of pressure on a submerged surface is found by equating the moment of the total pressure force (F × h*) to the sum of the moments of the elemental pressure forces (∫ρgh dA × h) about the free-surface line.</li><li><b>Statement II (true):</b> This is the principle of moments (Varignon's theorem).</li><li><b>Link:</b> II is exactly the principle used in I. Hence (a).</li></ul>
<div class="formula">h* = h̄ + I<sub>G</sub>/(A h̄) (vertical plane surface)</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> would deny the link, but II is the stated basis of the calculation.</li><li><b>(c)</b> each reject a true statement.</li><li><b>(d)</b> each reject a true statement.</li><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Both true and II explains I (the keyed answer).</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> The centre of pressure is always below the centroid of a vertical submerged surface, because pressure grows with depth.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-146", year: 2022, no: 146, subject: "thermo", topic: "Heat and work as boundary phenomena", selfCorrected: true,
q: `<b>Statement (I):</b> Heat and work are boundary phenomenon and recognized only when they cross the boundary of a system.<br><b>Statement (II):</b> Heat and work depend on the path followed by the system during a process.`,
opts: C, ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Both true; II explains I</h3><ul><li><b>Statement I (true):</b> Heat and work are energy in transit, recognised only as they cross the system boundary; a system contains energy, not heat or work.</li><li><b>Statement II (true):</b> Heat and work are path functions (inexact differentials, δQ and δW).</li><li><b>Link (as keyed):</b> Because heat and work are not properties of the state, they exist only during a process, i.e. while crossing the boundary, and their amount depends on that process. Their path dependence is the reason they cannot be stored as properties and are recognised only at the boundary. The key takes II as explaining I.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> treats the two as unrelated facts; this was our first answer.</li><li><b>(c)</b> each reject a true statement.</li><li><b>(d)</b> each reject a true statement.</li><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Both true and II explains I (the keyed answer).</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Our first attempt chose (b), treating I and II as two separate characteristics. Textbook treatments (Nag) link them: since heat and work are path functions and not point functions (properties), they are transient, boundary phenomena. We accepted the key's (a).</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Contrast: ΔU = Q − W depends only on the end states, although Q and W separately depend on the path.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-147", year: 2022, no: 147, subject: "icrac", topic: "Vapour absorption system",
q: `<b>Statement (I):</b> The vapour absorption system uses heat energy to change the condition of the refrigerant from the evaporator.<br><b>Statement (II):</b> The load variations do not affect the performance of a Vapour absorption system.`,
opts: C, ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Both true; II does not explain I</h3><ul><li><b>Statement I (true):</b> In a vapour absorption system, the compressor is replaced by an absorber, pump and generator. Refrigerant vapour from the evaporator is absorbed (for example ammonia in water), pumped as liquid to high pressure, and driven off again by <i>heat</i> in the generator.</li><li><b>Statement II (true):</b> Absorption systems keep their performance (COP) reasonably well at part load; load variations have little effect, unlike reciprocating compressors.</li><li><b>Link:</b> I explains <i>how</i> the system works (heat-operated); II is a separate operational advantage. II does not explain I, so (b).</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> would make II the reason for I.</li><li><b>(c)</b> each reject a true statement.</li><li><b>(d)</b> each reject a true statement.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Both true; II does not explain I.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Other advantages: few moving parts (only a small pump), quiet operation, and the ability to use waste heat or solar heat. Disadvantages: low COP (about 0·6–0·7 single effect) and bulk.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Common pairs: NH<sub>3</sub>–H<sub>2</sub>O (refrigeration below 0 °C) and H<sub>2</sub>O–LiBr (air conditioning).</p>` },

{ id: "2022-148", year: 2022, no: 148, subject: "mech", topic: "Laws of dry friction",
q: `<b>Statement (I):</b> For moderate speed, the force of friction remains nearly constant and decrease slightly with increase of speed.<br><b>Statement (II):</b> Friction is dependent on extent of area but independent on normal reaction.`,
opts: C, ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) I true, II false</h3><ul><li><b>Statement I (true):</b> A law of kinetic friction: for moderate speeds, the friction force is nearly constant, decreasing slightly as speed increases.</li><li><b>Statement II (false):</b> The laws of dry (Coulomb) friction say the opposite: friction is <i>independent</i> of the apparent area of contact and <i>proportional</i> to the normal reaction (F = μN).</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> treats II as true.</li><li><b>(b)</b> treats II as true.</li><li><b>(d)</b> rejects I, which is a standard law.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> I is true and II is false.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Physical reason: real contact happens only at surface asperities, whose total true area is proportional to the load, not to the apparent area. Other laws: limiting friction acts opposite to impending motion; static friction is slightly greater than kinetic friction; friction depends on the nature of the surfaces.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> These laws fail for very soft materials, very high pressures and lubricated surfaces.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-149", year: 2022, no: 149, subject: "materials", topic: "Percent elongation and ductility",
q: `<b>Statement (I):</b> The percent elongation is assumed to be based on a gage length of 2·00 in unless some other gage length is specifically indicated.<br><b>Statement (II):</b> Theoretically, a material is considered ductile if its percent elongation is greater than 5% (lower values indicate brittleness).`,
opts: C, ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Both true; II does not explain I</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Percent elongation and ductility.</p><ul><li><b>Statement I (true):</b> Mott: the percent elongation is based on a 2·00 in (50 mm) gauge length unless another is stated. Elongation depends on gauge length because necking is localised, so the gauge length must be specified.</li><li><b>Statement II (true):</b> Mott's design criterion: a material is ductile if its elongation exceeds 5%; below 5% it is treated as brittle.</li><li><b>Link:</b> I is a testing convention; II is a classification rule. Neither explains the other, so (b).</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> would make the ductility criterion the reason for the gauge-length convention.</li><li><b>(c)</b> each reject a true statement.</li><li><b>(d)</b> each reject a true statement.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Both true; II does not explain I.</li></ul>
<div class="formula">% elongation = (L<sub>f</sub> − L<sub>0</sub>)/L<sub>0</sub> × 100</div>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Indian/ISO practice uses a proportional gauge length L<sub>0</sub> = 5·65√A<sub>0</sub> (= 5d for round bars).</p>` },

{ id: "2022-150", year: 2022, no: 150, subject: "mfg", topic: "Mechanization",
q: `<b>Statement (I):</b> Mechanization means something is done or operated by machinery as well as by hand.<br><b>Statement (II):</b> Mechanization of the manufacturing means milestone oriented trend towards minimizing the human efforts to the extent of its possibility, by adopting mechanical and electrical means or methods for automating the different manufacturing processes.`,
opts: C, ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) I false, II true</h3><ul><li><b>Statement I (false):</b> Mechanization means something is done or operated by machinery and <i>not</i> by hand. The words "as well as by hand" make it incorrect.</li><li><b>Statement II (true):</b> Mechanization of manufacturing is a milestone-oriented trend towards minimising human effort as far as possible, by adopting mechanical and electrical means for the different processes.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> all treat I as true.</li><li><b>(b)</b> all treat I as true.</li><li><b>(c)</b> all treat I as true.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> I is false and II is true.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Stages of development: manual work → mechanization (machines provide power and motion, humans control them) → automation (machines also control themselves, through feedback, sensors and programs) → computer-integrated manufacturing.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Mechanization replaces human muscle; automation also replaces human control and decision-making.</p>` }
);
})();

// ---- q2021a.js ----
// ESE 2021 Prelims Paper II, Mechanical Engineering, Series A, Q1–25
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2021-1", year: 2021, no: 1, subject: "renew", topic: "Pumped hydro storage",
q: `In various solar energy storage systems, pumped hydro-electric storage system falls under which one of the following categories?`,
opts: ["Thermal energy storage", "Electrical energy storage", "Mechanical energy storage", "Electromagnetic energy storage"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Mechanical energy storage</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Pumped hydro stores energy as the <b>gravitational potential energy</b> of water pumped to an upper reservoir (E = ρgVh). It is released through turbines when needed. Since the stored form is mechanical (potential) energy, it is classed with flywheels and compressed-air storage as mechanical storage. Round-trip efficiency is about 70–85%.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Thermal storage uses sensible or latent heat (water tanks, molten salt, PCMs).</li><li><b>(b)</b> Electrical storage keeps energy as electricity itself (capacitors, supercapacitors); batteries are electrochemical.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Electromagnetic storage means superconducting magnetic energy storage (SMES).</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Pumped hydro provides over 90% of the world's grid-scale energy storage capacity.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-2", year: 2021, no: 2, subject: "renew", topic: "Solar constant",
q: `What is the standard value of solar constant adopted by World Radiation Centre?`,
opts: ["1192 W/m²", "1084 W/m²", "1927 W/m²", "1367 W/m²"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1367 W/m²</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The solar constant is the solar irradiance on a surface normal to the sun's rays, outside the atmosphere, at the mean Sun–Earth distance. The World Radiation Centre (Davos) adopted 1367 W/m² (±1%), the value used in most solar-energy texts (Duffie and Beckman, Sukhatme). Recent satellite measurements give about 1361 W/m².</p><div class="formula">G_sc = solar irradiance at mean Sun–Earth distance (WRC: 1367 W/m²)</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>WRC adopted value: <b>1367 W/m²</b> (±1 %).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 1192 W/m² is below the extraterrestrial value; it is closer to ground-level clear-sky irradiance.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 1084 W/m² is about the irradiance at aphelion-like conditions on the ground, not the constant.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 1927 W/m² is far above any measured value.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Extra-terrestrial irradiance varies by about ±3·3% over the year: I<sub>0n</sub> = I<sub>sc</sub>[1 + 0·033 cos(360n/365)].</p>` },

{ id: "2021-3", year: 2021, no: 3, subject: "renew", topic: "Savonius rotor tip speed ratio",
q: `What is the tip speed ratio of savonius wind turbine rotor?`,
opts: ["1", "3", "5", "7"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The Savonius rotor is a drag-type vertical-axis machine of two or more S-shaped scoops. Because it is pushed by drag, its blades cannot move faster than the wind: its optimum tip speed ratio is about 0·8–1, with a low power coefficient (about 0·15–0·2) but high starting torque.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> TSR 3 belongs to multi-blade/low-speed HAWTs approaching moderate speed.</li><li><b>(c)</b> TSR 5 is typical of three-blade HAWTs.</li><li><b>(d)</b> TSR 7 is typical of fast two/three-blade HAWTs.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Higher solidity → lower optimum TSR and higher starting torque; low solidity → high TSR and high efficiency.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-4", year: 2021, no: 4, subject: "renew", topic: "Solidity of multi-blade rotors",
q: `What is the solidity of American multiblade wind turbine rotor?`,
opts: ["0·4", "0·7", "0·9", "1"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 0·7</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Solidity is the ratio of total blade area to the swept area. The American multi-blade (farm windmill) rotor, with 12–24 broad blades, has a high solidity of about 0·7, which gives high starting torque at low wind speeds, suited to water pumping, with a low tip speed ratio (about 1).</p><div class="formula">Solidity σ = total blade area/swept area</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Multi-blade farm windmill: 12–24 broad blades ⇒ σ ≈ <b>0.7</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0.4 is too low for a 12–24 blade rotor.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0.9 would almost fully cover the swept area.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Solidity 1 would mean a solid disc.</li><li>Modern three-blade turbines have solidity of only about 0·05–0·1.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Solidity σ = N c/(πR) for N blades of mean chord c on radius R.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-5", year: 2021, no: 5, subject: "renew", topic: "Energy density of bio-ethanol",
q: `The energy density of Bio-ethanol is`,
opts: ["8·3 MJ/kg", "14·6 MJ/kg", "26·9 MJ/kg", "34·7 MJ/kg"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 26·9 MJ/kg</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Ethanol (C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH) has a higher heating value of about 29·7 MJ/kg and a lower heating value of about 26·8–27 MJ/kg. Its oxygen content makes it lower than petrol (about 44–46 MJ/kg); ethanol gives about two-thirds of petrol's energy per litre.</p><div class="formula">Energy density = heating value per kg</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Ethanol LHV ≈ 26.8–27 MJ/kg ⇒ <b>26.9 MJ/kg</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 8.3 MJ/kg is too low; it is typical of wet biomass residues.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 14.6 MJ/kg is typical of air-dry wood, not ethanol.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 34.7 MJ/kg is close to biodiesel (about 37 MJ/kg), not ethanol.</li></ul>
<p>Ethanol already contains oxygen (about 35% by mass), so less energy is released per kilogram when it burns; in return it burns cleaner and has a high octane number (about 108).</p><p><b>Remember:</b> India's ethanol blending programme targets E20 (20% ethanol in petrol).</p>` },

{ id: "2021-6", year: 2021, no: 6, subject: "renew", topic: "Composition of producer gas",
q: `The percentage of hydrogen in producer gas is`,
opts: ["34%", "27%", "18%", "8%"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 18%</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Producer gas is made by partial combustion of coal, coke or biomass with air (and some steam). Typical composition by volume: CO 20–30%, H<sub>2</sub> 10–20% (often quoted as about 18%), CH<sub>4</sub> 1–3%, CO<sub>2</sub> 5–10%, N<sub>2</sub> about 50%. Its calorific value is low (about 4–6 MJ/m³) because of the nitrogen from air.</p><div class="formula">Producer gas ≈ CO 20–30 %, H₂ 10–20 %, N₂ ≈ 50 %</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Typical quoted H₂ content ≈ <b>18 %</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 34 % is too high; that is closer to water gas.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 27 % is too high for air-blown producer gas.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 8 % is too low; H₂ is usually 10–20 %.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Water gas (steam over hot coke) is about 50% H<sub>2</sub> and 40% CO, with much higher heating value.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-7", year: 2021, no: 7, subject: "renew", topic: "Double-effect tidal schemes",
q: `In single basin, double effect scheme, power is generated`,
opts: ["during filling.", "during emptying.", "on ebb only.", "on both flood and ebb."], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) on both flood and ebb</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In a single-basin, double-effect (two-way) scheme, turbines generate while the basin fills on the rising (flood) tide and again while it empties on the falling (ebb) tide. Reversible bulb turbines are used, as at La Rance (France).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Generating on only one direction is the <b>single-effect</b> scheme (flood or ebb generation).</li><li><b>(b)</b> Generating on only one direction is the <b>single-effect</b> scheme (flood or ebb generation).</li><li><b>(c)</b> Ebb-only generation is single-effect.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Double-basin schemes allow nearly continuous generation by running between a high basin and a low basin.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-8", year: 2021, no: 8, subject: "renew", topic: "Alkaline fuel cell temperature",
q: `The operating temperature of alkaline fuel cell is`,
opts: ["39 °C", "90 °C", "127 °C", "192 °C"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 90 °C</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Low-temperature alkaline fuel cells (KOH electrolyte) operate at about 60–90 °C (high-pressure versions used in space ran up to about 250 °C). Of the options, 90 °C is the typical quoted value.</p><div class="formula">AFC operating range ≈ 60–90 °C</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Typical value <b>90 °C</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 39 °C is too low for practical AFC kinetics.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 127 °C is above the usual 60–90 °C range of low-temperature AFCs.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 192 °C is the PAFC range (≈ 200 °C).</li></ul>
<p>The alkaline electrolyte gives fast oxygen-reduction kinetics even at these low temperatures, so cheaper non-platinum catalysts (nickel, silver) can be used.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Operating temperatures: PEMFC 60–80 °C, AFC 60–90 °C, PAFC about 200 °C, MCFC about 650 °C, SOFC 800–1000 °C.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-9", year: 2021, no: 9, subject: "renew", topic: "Ideal emf of a hydrogen fuel cell",
q: `The ideal emf produced by polymer electrolyte membrane fuel cell at 25 °C is`,
opts: ["3·57 V", "2·94 V", "1·23 V", "0·73 V"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1·23 V</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The reversible cell voltage follows from the Gibbs free energy of the reaction H<sub>2</sub> + ½O<sub>2</sub> → H<sub>2</sub>O (liquid):</p>
<div class="formula">E = −ΔG/(nF) = 237,100/(2 × 96,485) = 1·23 V</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>ΔG = −237·1 kJ/mol at 25 °C; n = 2 electrons per H<sub>2</sub>; F = Faraday's constant</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 3.57 V exceeds the thermodynamic limit E° = −ΔG/(2F) = 1.23 V for H₂–O₂.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 2.94 V exceeds the thermodynamic limit of 1.23 V.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0.73 V is a typical operating voltage under load, not the ideal emf.</li></ul>
<p>The cell voltage falls slightly as temperature rises (about 1·18 V at 80 °C), because ΔG becomes less negative.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Ideal efficiency = ΔG/ΔH = 237·1/285·8 = 83% (HHV basis).</p>` },

{ id: "2021-10", year: 2021, no: 10, subject: "renew", topic: "Fuel cell efficiencies",
q: `Which one of the following fuel cells has highest efficiency?`,
opts: ["PAFC", "MCFC", "PEMFC", "AFC"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) AFC</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Alkaline fuel cells have the highest electrical efficiency of the common types, about 60–70% on pure hydrogen and oxygen, because oxygen reduction is fast in alkaline media. This is one reason they were used on Apollo and the Space Shuttle.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> PAFC: about 40–45% electrical.</li><li><b>(b)</b> MCFC: about 45–55% electrical (higher with heat recovery).</li><li><b>(c)</b> PEMFC: about 40–60%.</li></ul>
<p>The AFC's weakness is that CO<sub>2</sub> (even from air) reacts with KOH to form carbonate, so it needs pure gases, which limits it mainly to space and niche uses.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> In combined heat and power, high-temperature cells (MCFC, SOFC) can exceed 80% total efficiency.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-11", year: 2021, no: 11, subject: "renew", topic: "Auroville solar bowl", selfCorrected: true,
q: `How many kilograms of steam per day is produced by 15 m diameter community solar cooker developed by Centre for Scientific Research, Auroville (Puducherry)?`,
opts: ["100 kg", "300 kg", "600 kg", "1000 kg"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 600 kg</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The Auroville Solar Kitchen uses a fixed 15 m diameter spherical reflector (the "Solar Bowl", ferrocement lined with about 11,000 mirror facets) with a receiver that tracks the sun's focus. It produces about <b>600 kg of steam a day</b> on clear days, enough to cook around 2000 meals; a diesel boiler backs it up on cloudy days.</p><div class="formula">Steam output ≈ collected solar energy/latent heat</div>
<p>Our first attempt guessed (d) 1000 kg from memory; the Auroville project's own description gives 600 kg, so the key is right.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Auroville Solar Bowl (15 m): about <b>600 kg</b> of steam per clear day.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 100 is too small for the bowl's 2000-meal duty.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 300 kg is too small for the bowl's 2000-meal duty.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 1000 kg overstates the reported output.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Other large solar kitchens use Scheffler reflectors, as at Shirdi (Maharashtra) and Mount Abu.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-12", year: 2021, no: 12, subject: "renew", topic: "Trombe wall",
q: `In a solar passive space heating system, the south-facing thick wall is called`,
opts: ["Vent wall", "Trombe wall", "Damper wall", "Ventilation wall"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Trombe wall</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A Trombe wall is a thick, dark-coloured masonry wall facing the sun (south, in the northern hemisphere) behind a glazing with an air gap. It absorbs solar radiation by day, stores the heat in its mass and releases it into the room hours later; vents at top and bottom allow thermosiphon circulation of warm air.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 'Vent wall' describes the openings in a Trombe wall, not the wall's name.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 'Damper wall' describes the flow controls, not the wall's name.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 'Ventilation wall' is not the name of the thermal storage wall.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Named after Félix Trombe and Jacques Michel, who popularised it in France in the 1960s.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-13", year: 2021, no: 13, subject: "power", topic: "Advantages of superheated steam", selfCorrected: true,
q: `All power plants use superheated steam due to which of the following advantages?<ol><li>Superheating is mostly done from waste heat of boiler without additional cost of fuel.</li><li>The plant efficiency increases due to higher temperature of steam.</li><li>There is less corrosion and erosion of equipment due to absence of moisture in the steam.</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul>
<li><b>1 (correct, as intended):</b> Superheater tubes are placed in the hot flue-gas path after the furnace water walls, so they use heat in gases that would otherwise pass on toward the chimney. Engineering texts describe this as using the "waste heat" of the boiler.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> Higher steam temperature raises the mean temperature of heat addition and the cycle efficiency.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> Dry steam reduces moisture-related erosion of turbine blades and corrosion.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Our first attempt rejected statement 1, reasoning that superheat heat is still fuel heat. Checking engineering references, superheating is described as using heat in the flue gases leaving the evaporating surfaces, which supports the key's (d).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Large plants also use radiant superheaters in the furnace roof to keep steam temperature steady across loads.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-14", year: 2021, no: 14, subject: "power", topic: "Effects of regenerative feed heating",
q: `What are the effects of regenerative feedwater heating for the same turbine output?<ol><li>It significantly increases the cycle efficiency and reduces the heat rate.</li><li>It increases the steam flow rate.</li><li>It increases the steam flow to the condenser.</li><li>If there is no change of boiler output, the turbine output drops.</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 4 only", "1, 3 and 4 only", "2, 3 and 4 only"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1, 2 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Effects of regenerative feed heating.</p><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> Feedwater enters the boiler hotter, raising the mean temperature of heat addition: efficiency rises and heat rate (kJ/kWh) falls.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> Bled steam does less work per kg, so more total steam must be generated for the same output.</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong):</b> Part of the steam is bled before reaching the condenser, so the condenser flow <b>decreases</b>.</li>
<li><b>4 (correct):</b> With the boiler steam flow fixed, bleeding reduces the work done, so turbine output falls.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 4, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 4.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Compare 2017 Q45: regeneration raises boiler capacity per kW but reduces condenser size.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-15", year: 2021, no: 15, subject: "power", topic: "Pulverised coal firing",
q: `Which of the following are the advantages of pulverized coal firing?<ol><li>Higher boiler efficiency.</li><li>Fast response for no load changes.</li><li>Ability to use low preheated air reducing internal losses.</li><li>Ability to release large amounts of heat enabling it to generate about 2000 t/h of steam in one boiler.</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "1 and 4 only", "2, 3 and 4 only"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Pulverised coal firing.</p><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> Fine coal burns almost completely with little excess air, so boiler efficiency is high (about 85–90%).</li>
<li><b>2 (not an advantage as stated):</b> PF firing does respond quickly to <i>load changes</i>, but "fast response for no load changes" is muddled; the key does not count it.</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong):</b> PF firing can use <b>highly</b> preheated air (300–350 °C), which helps dry the coal and improves efficiency; "low preheated air" is the opposite.</li>
<li><b>4 (correct):</b> Large furnaces can release enough heat to make about 2000 t/h of steam in a single boiler (500–800 MW units).</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 4, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 4, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 4.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 2, 3, which are incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Disadvantages of PF: high cost of mills, fly-ash handling (ESPs needed), explosion risk in mills.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-16", year: 2021, no: 16, subject: "power", topic: "Higher calorific value from composition",
q: `A fuel consists of 92% carbon, 7% hydrogen and remaining residual matter by mass. Working from first principles, the higher calorific value of the fuel is`,
opts: ["40176 kJ/kg", "41176 kJ/kg", "40876 kJ/kg", "41678 kJ/kg"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 41176 kJ/kg</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Formula (Dulong, no oxygen or sulphur)</b></p><div class="formula">HCV = 33,800 C + 1,44,000 H kJ/kg</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>33,800 kJ/kg = heat of combustion of carbon to CO<sub>2</sub>; 1,44,000 kJ/kg = higher heat of combustion of hydrogen to liquid water; C = 0·92, H = 0·07 (mass fractions)</li></ul>
<p>HCV = 33,800 × 0·92 + 1,44,000 × 0·07 = 31,096 + 10,080 = <b>41,176 kJ/kg</b>.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>33 800 × 0.92 = 31 096 kJ/kg.</li><li>144 000 × 0.07 = 10 080 kJ/kg.</li><li>HCV = <b>41 176 kJ/kg</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 40,176 uses slightly different heating values for C or H.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 40,876 uses slightly different heating values for C or H.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 41,678 kJ/kg uses slightly different heating values for C or H.</li></ul>
<p>The residual matter (1%) is taken as non-combustible ash and contributes no heat.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> LCV = HCV − 9H × 2442 = 41,176 − 1538 = 39,638 kJ/kg.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-17", year: 2021, no: 17, subject: "power", topic: "Conditions for complete combustion",
q: `In order to burn a fuel completely, which of the following basic conditions must be fulfilled?<ol><li>Supply enough air for complete combustion of fuel.</li><li>Secure low turbulence for thorough mixing of fuel and air.</li><li>Maintain a furnace temperature high enough to ignite the incoming fuel air mixture.</li><li>Provide a furnace volume large enough to allow time for combustion to be completed.</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 4 only", "1, 3 and 4 only", "2, 3 and 4 only"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1, 3 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The "three Ts" of combustion (time, temperature, turbulence) plus enough air.</p>
<p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> Sufficient (usually some excess) air.</li><li><b>2 (wrong):</b> <b>High</b> turbulence is needed for thorough mixing of fuel and air; low turbulence gives incomplete combustion.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> A high enough temperature to ignite and sustain combustion.</li><li><b>4 (correct):</b> Enough furnace volume so the residence time completes the burn.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 4, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 3, 4.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Excess air: PF about 15–20%, stoker firing about 30–50%, gas firing 5–10%.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-18", year: 2021, no: 18, subject: "power", topic: "Raising Rankine efficiency",
q: `The efficiency of any cycle increases with`,
opts: ["the decrease of maximum pressure and the constant of exhaust pressure.", "the decrease of maximum pressure and the decrease of exhaust pressure.", "the increase of maximum pressure and the decrease of exhaust pressure.", "the increase of maximum pressure and the constant of exhaust pressure."], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) the increase of maximum pressure and the decrease of exhaust pressure</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Raising boiler pressure raises the mean temperature of heat addition; lowering exhaust (condenser) pressure lowers the temperature of heat rejection. Both widen the effective temperature range, so efficiency rises: η ≈ 1 − T<sub>out</sub>/T<sub>m,in</sub>.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Lowering maximum pressure reduces efficiency.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Lowering maximum pressure reduces efficiency.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Raising maximum pressure alone helps, but (c) gives both improvements and so the greatest increase.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Higher boiler pressure at fixed superheat temperature increases exhaust moisture, which is why reheat is added.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-19", year: 2021, no: 19, subject: "power", topic: "Efficiency of auxiliaries",
q: `In a power plant, the efficiencies of the electric generator, turbine (mechanical), boiler, cycle and the overall plant are 0·97, 0·95, 0·92, 0·42 and 0·33, respectively. What is the efficiency of auxiliaries?`,
opts: ["98·14%", "92·68%", "83·41%", "75·14%"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 92·68%</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Overall efficiency = product of all efficiencies, including an "auxiliary efficiency" that accounts for power used by auxiliaries:</p>
<div class="formula">η<sub>aux</sub> = η<sub>overall</sub>/(η<sub>gen</sub> × η<sub>turbine</sub> × η<sub>boiler</sub> × η<sub>cycle</sub>)</div>
<p>η<sub>aux</sub> = 0·33/(0·97 × 0·95 × 0·92 × 0·42) = 0·33/0·3561 = <b>92·68%</b>, so auxiliaries take 7·3% (compare 2019 Q58 and 2020 Q49).</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Product = 0.97 × 0.95 × 0.92 × 0.42 = 0.3561.</li><li>η_aux = 0.33/0.3561 = <b>92.68 %</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 98·14 omits or add one of the efficiencies.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 83·41 omits or add one of the efficiencies.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 75·14% omits or add one of the efficiencies.</li></ul>
<p>Each efficiency multiplies the energy passing through that stage, so the auxiliary efficiency is simply the factor needed to bring the gross product (35·61%) down to the overall 33%.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Station net output = gross output × η<sub>aux</sub>.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-20", year: 2021, no: 20, subject: "power", topic: "Steam power cycle facts",
q: `Consider the following statements for analysis of steam cycles :<ol><li>A steam power plant continuously converts the energy stored in fossil fuels or fissile fuels into shaft work.</li><li>Steam power plants work on Brayton cycle.</li><li>In supercritical steam cycle, steam is generated in a 'once-through' boiler at a pressure above the critical point of 27·5 bar.</li><li>Deaerator is used for the purpose of deaerating the feedwater.</li></ol>`,
opts: ["2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1 and 4 only", "2 and 4 only"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Steam power cycle facts.</p><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> Fossil or nuclear (fissile) heat raises steam, which drives turbines continuously.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> Steam plants work on the <b>Rankine</b> cycle; Brayton is the gas-turbine cycle.</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong):</b> Supercritical units do use once-through boilers, but the critical pressure of water is <b>220·6 bar</b> (374 °C), not 27·5 bar.</li>
<li><b>4 (correct):</b> The deaerator removes dissolved O<sub>2</sub> and CO<sub>2</sub> from feedwater.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 2, 3, which are incorrect; Omits 1, 4, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 4, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 4.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Ultra-supercritical plants run at about 250–300 bar and 600 °C, reaching about 45% efficiency.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-21", year: 2021, no: 21, subject: "power", topic: "Solid fuels",
q: `Consider the following statements for solid fuels :<ol><li>Peat is the first stage in the formation of coal from wood.</li><li>The average calorific value of bituminous coal is 1524 kJ/kg.</li><li>Anthracite is very hard coal and has a shining black lustre.</li><li>Wood charcoal is obtained by destructive distillation of wood.</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3 only", "1, 3 and 4 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 4 only"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1, 3 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Solid fuels.</p><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> Coalification order: wood → peat → lignite → sub-bituminous → bituminous → anthracite.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> Bituminous coal has a calorific value of about 31,000–33,000 kJ/kg, not 1524 kJ/kg.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> Anthracite is the hardest, highest-carbon coal (about 90%+ C), with a shiny black lustre, burning with little smoke.</li>
<li><b>4 (correct):</b> Charcoal is made by heating wood without air (destructive distillation, carbonisation).</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 4, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 3, 4.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, 4, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Indian coals are mostly high-ash bituminous/sub-bituminous, about 3500–4500 kcal/kg (15–19 MJ/kg) as fired.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-22", year: 2021, no: 22, subject: "power", topic: "Fluidised bed boilers",
q: `Consider the following statements for fluidized bed boilers :<ol><li>Fluidized bed boilers produce steam from fossil and waste fuels by using a technique called fluidized bed combustion.</li><li>Cyclone separators are gas cleaning devices that utilize the centrifugal force created by a spinning gas stream to separate particles from a gas.</li><li>In a pressurized fluidized bed boiler, the combustion process takes place in a pressurized environment resulting in a compact furnace and improved combustion efficiency.</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Fluidised bed boilers.</p><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> FBC boilers burn coal, lignite, biomass and wastes suspended in an air-fluidised bed of sand or ash with limestone.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> Cyclones separate particles by centrifugal force; in circulating FBC they return unburnt fuel and bed material to the furnace.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> Pressurised FBC (about 10–16 bar) gives a much smaller furnace and allows the hot pressurised flue gas to drive a gas turbine (combined cycle).</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> FBC advantages: fuel flexibility, in-bed sulphur capture, low NO<sub>x</sub> (bed at 800–900 °C).</p>` },

{ id: "2021-23", year: 2021, no: 23, subject: "turbo", topic: "Steam turbine definitions",
q: `Consider the following statements for steam turbines :<ol><li>The ratio of actual enthalpy drop to isentropic enthalpy drop is known as mechanical efficiency.</li><li>The ratio of enthalpy drop in moving blades to enthalpy drop in the stage is known as degree of reaction.</li><li>Rateau turbine is the example of reaction turbine.</li><li>Curtis turbine is the example of impulse turbine.</li></ol>`,
opts: ["2 and 4 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2, 3 and 4"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 2 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> Actual/isentropic enthalpy drop is the <b>isentropic (internal) efficiency</b>. Mechanical efficiency is shaft work/work done on the blades.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> Degree of reaction R = Δh<sub>rotor</sub>/Δh<sub>stage</sub>.</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong):</b> Rateau is a <b>pressure-compounded impulse</b> turbine.</li>
<li><b>4 (correct):</b> Curtis is a velocity-compounded impulse turbine.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Stage efficiency (or internal efficiency) uses enthalpy drops; mechanical efficiency compares power at the shaft with power developed on the blades (bearing and gland losses).</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 2, 4.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 1, 3, which are incorrect; Omits 2, 4, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 4, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 1, 3, which are incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Parsons is the classic reaction (50%) turbine; de Laval is the simple single-stage impulse turbine.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-24", year: 2021, no: 24, subject: "power", topic: "Cooling towers",
q: `Consider the following statements for cooling towers :<ol><li>Cooling tower is an artificial device used to cool the hot cooling water coming out of condenser more effectively.</li><li>The amount of water usually lost with induced draft cooling tower ranges from 5% to 6% by evaporation.</li><li>The amount of water usually lost with induced draft cooling tower ranges from 7% to 8% by drift losses.</li><li>The rate of evaporation of water and its cooling effect on the remaining water depends upon the relative humidity of air passing through the tower.</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1 and 4 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2, 3 and 4"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Cooling towers.</p><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> A cooling tower rejects the condenser's heat to the atmosphere, mainly by evaporation, so the water can be reused.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> Evaporation loss is about 1–2% of the circulating flow (roughly 0·15% per °C of cooling range), not 5–6%.</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong):</b> Drift (droplet carry-over) loss is very small, about 0·1–0·2% (0·005% with good drift eliminators), not 7–8%.</li>
<li><b>4 (correct):</b> Dry air (low RH, low wet-bulb) evaporates more water and cools better.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 4.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 4, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 2, 3, which are incorrect; Omits 1, 4, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 2, 3, which are incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Make-up water = evaporation + drift + blowdown (blowdown controls the build-up of dissolved salts).</p>` },

{ id: "2021-25", year: 2021, no: 25, subject: "turbo", topic: "Slip of a reciprocating pump",
q: `A single-acting reciprocating pump, running at 50 rpm delivers 0·00736 m³/s of water. The diameter of the piston is 200 mm and stroke length is 300 mm. What is the percentage slip of the pump?`,
opts: ["5·29%", "6·29%", "7·29%", "8·29%"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 6·29%</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Slip of a reciprocating pump.</p><div class="formula">Q<sub>th</sub> = A L N/60 (single-acting) &nbsp;&nbsp; slip = (Q<sub>th</sub> − Q<sub>act</sub>)/Q<sub>th</sub></div>
<ul class="sym"><li>A = π × 0·2²/4 = 0·03142 m²; L = 0·3 m; N = 50 rpm; Q<sub>act</sub> = 0·00736 m³/s</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Q<sub>th</sub> = 0·03142 × 0·3 × 50/60 = 0·007854 m³/s.</li><li>Slip = (0·007854 − 0·00736)/0·007854 = <b>6·29%</b>; C<sub>d</sub> = 0·937.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 5·29 comes from rounding the area or using the wrong number of strokes.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 7·29 comes from rounding the area or using the wrong number of strokes.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 8·29% comes from rounding the area or using the wrong number of strokes.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Negative slip (actual &gt; theoretical) can occur with long suction pipes, high speed and short delivery lines.</p>` }
);

