// ESE 2021 Prelims Paper II, Mechanical Engineering, Series A, Q26–50
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2021-26", year: 2021, no: 26, subject: "turbo", topic: "Density and mass flow of a pumped liquid",
q: `A pump discharges a liquid into a tank at the rate of 0·032 m³/s. The tank, 1·5 m in diameter and 4·20 m in height, can hold 3500 kg of liquid. The density of the liquid and mass flow rate of the liquid handled by the pump are respectively,`,
opts: ["471·57 kg/m³ and 16 kg/s", "471·57 kg/m³ and 15 kg/s", "481·57 kg/m³ and 16 kg/s", "481·57 kg/m³ and 15 kg/s"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 471·57 kg/m³ and 15 kg/s</h3><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Tank volume V = (π/4) × 1·5² × 4·2 = 7·422 m³.</li><li>ρ = m/V = 3500/7·422 = <b>471·6 kg/m³</b> (a light liquid such as liquefied gas).</li><li>ṁ = ρ Q = 471·6 × 0·032 = 15·09 ≈ <b>15 kg/s</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(c)</b> comes from a slightly smaller volume (for example rounding π).</li><li><b>(d)</b> 481·57 kg/m³ comes from a slightly smaller volume (for example rounding π).</li><li><b>(a)</b> does not equal ρQ with ρ ≈ 472.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The tank's capacity and the mass it holds fix the density; the pump's volume flow then gives the mass flow directly. A density of about 470 kg/m³ suggests a liquefied hydrocarbon such as propane or butane.</p><div class="formula">V = (π/4)D²H</div><div class="formula">ρ = m/V, ṁ = ρQ</div><p><b>Remember:</b> Mass flow = density × volume flow; time to fill the tank = 7·422/0·032 = 232 s.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-27", year: 2021, no: 27, subject: "turbo", topic: "Radial discharge in Francis turbines",
q: `In Francis turbine, as the water discharge is radial at the outlet, the velocity whirl at the outlet becomes`,
opts: ["1", "0", "∞", "0·5"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 0</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> "Radial discharge" means the absolute velocity at outlet has no tangential (whirl) component: V<sub>w2</sub> = 0 (the exit velocity equals the flow velocity). Euler's equation then simplifies to work per unit weight = V<sub>w1</sub>u<sub>1</sub>/g, and the outlet kinetic energy loss is minimum.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Radial discharge means no tangential component, not 1.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Whirl velocity cannot be infinite.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0.5 would leave some swirl at outlet.</li></ul>
<p>Francis runners are designed for radial or nearly radial discharge at the best-efficiency point; at part load an exit swirl appears, causing draft-tube surging.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Hydraulic efficiency with radial discharge: η<sub>h</sub> = V<sub>w1</sub>u<sub>1</sub>/(gH).</p>` },

{ id: "2021-28", year: 2021, no: 28, subject: "turbo", topic: "Free spiral vortex in a pump casing",
q: `A pump impeller is 375 mm in diameter and it discharges water with velocity components of 2 m/s and 12 m/s in the radial and tangential directions respectively. The impeller is surrounded by a concentrical cylindrical chamber with parallel sides, the outer diameter being 450 mm. If the flow in the chamber is a free spiral vortex, what are the tangential velocity and radial velocity at the outlet of the chamber respectively?`,
opts: ["12 m/s and 1·67 m/s", "10 m/s and 1·67 m/s", "12 m/s and 1·76 m/s", "10 m/s and 1·76 m/s"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 10 m/s and 1·67 m/s</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In a free (irrotational) vortex V<sub>t</sub> r = constant. In a chamber with parallel sides (constant width), continuity Q = 2πr b V<sub>r</sub> gives V<sub>r</sub> r = constant.</p><div class="formula">Free vortex: V_t r = const</div><div class="formula">Parallel sides: V_r r = const</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>r<sub>1</sub> = 0·1875 m, r<sub>2</sub> = 0·225 m → r<sub>1</sub>/r<sub>2</sub> = 0·8333.</li><li>V<sub>t2</sub> = 12 × 0·8333 = <b>10 m/s</b>.</li><li>V<sub>r2</sub> = 2 × 0·8333 = <b>1·67 m/s</b>.</li></ol>
<p>Both components fall in the same ratio, so the flow direction (spiral angle) stays the same: a logarithmic spiral.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> keeps the tangential velocity at 12 m/s, which ignores the free-vortex law.</li><li><b>(c)</b> keeps the tangential velocity at 12 m/s, which ignores the free-vortex law.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 1.76 m/s is not 2 × 0.8333 for the radial component.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> The slowing flow converts kinetic energy into pressure: this is the job of the whirlpool chamber or volute.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-29", year: 2021, no: 29, subject: "turbo", topic: "Impellers for slurries",
q: `Which one of the following types of impellers is used to handle highly solid-laden liquids like concrete and slurry?`,
opts: ["Fully Open Impeller", "Semi-Enclosed Impeller", "Fully-Enclosed Impeller", "Quarter Open Impeller"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Fully Open Impeller</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> An open impeller has vanes without shrouds on either side, so solids and fibrous matter pass through without clogging. It is used for slurries, sewage, paper pulp and concrete-like mixtures, at the cost of lower efficiency.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Semi-enclosed (one shroud) impellers handle liquids with some suspended solids (sewage).</li><li><b>(c)</b> Closed (shrouded both sides) impellers give the highest efficiency but only for clean liquids.</li><li><b>(d)</b> "Quarter open" is not a standard type.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Vortex (recessed) impellers are another choice for very abrasive or stringy solids.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-30", year: 2021, no: 30, subject: "turbo", topic: "Mean friction head in a reciprocating pump",
q: `In a single reciprocating pump without air vessel, the ratio of the average frictional head to the maximum frictional head in the delivery pipe is`,
opts: ["1/2", "1/3", "2/3", "3/4"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 2/3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Without an air vessel, the delivery-pipe velocity varies as sin θ with crank angle, and friction head h<sub>f</sub> ∝ V² ∝ sin²θ. Averaged over the delivery stroke on a work (power) basis, the mean friction head is 2/3 of the maximum:</p>
<div class="formula">h<sub>f,mean</sub> = (2/3) h<sub>f,max</sub>, &nbsp; h<sub>f,max</sub> = 4fL(A/a ω r)²/(2gd)</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> ½ is the time-average of sin²θ over a cycle; the average of the friction head (∝ sin²θ over the stroke) relative to the maximum is 2/3 here.</li><li><b>(b)</b> ⅓ has no basis in the sin² variation of friction head.</li><li><b>(d)</b> ¾ has no basis in the sin² variation of friction head.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> With an air vessel, flow in the pipe is nearly uniform, so friction loss drops sharply (to about 1/π² of the maximum for a single-acting pump).</p>` },

{ id: "2021-31", year: 2021, no: 31, subject: "turbo", topic: "Turbine speed under reduced head",
q: `A turbine develops 8000 kW when running at 1000 rpm. The head on the turbine is 30 m. If the head is reduced to 18 m, what is the speed developed by the turbine?`,
opts: ["67·46 rpm", "95·24 rpm", "54·67 rpm", "77·46 rpm"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 77·46 rpm (774·6 rpm with the decimal point corrected)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For the same turbine at a new head, unit speed N/√H stays constant:</p>
<div class="formula">N<sub>2</sub> = N<sub>1</sub>√(H<sub>2</sub>/H<sub>1</sub>)</div>
<p>N<sub>2</sub> = 1000 × √(18/30) = 1000 × 0·7746 = 774·6 rpm. The options show 77·46, evidently with the decimal misplaced; (d) has the right digits.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>N₂ = 1000 × √(18/30) = 774.6 rpm.</li><li>Options show <b>77.46</b> with the decimal misplaced.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 67·46 does not equal √0·6 = 0·7746 in any scaling.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 95·24 does not equal √0·6 = 0·7746 in any scaling.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 54·67 does not equal √0·6 = 0·7746 in any scaling.</li></ul>
<p>Unit speed N<sub>u</sub> = N/√H comes from the jet (or flow) velocity being proportional to √H, with geometric similarity keeping the velocity triangles the same.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Power at the new head: P<sub>2</sub> = 8000 × (0·6)<sup>1·5</sup> = 3718 kW (P ∝ H<sup>3/2</sup>).</p>` },

{ id: "2021-32", year: 2021, no: 32, subject: "turbo", topic: "Steam turbine governing",
q: `The steam turbine can be governed by the following methods except`,
opts: ["Reaction governing", "Throttle governing only", "Nozzle control governing only", "Combination of throttle and nozzle control governing"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Reaction governing</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Steam turbines are governed by: throttle governing (throttling the whole steam supply), nozzle control governing (shutting off groups of nozzles), by-pass governing (admitting extra steam to later stages at overload), and combinations of these.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> is all real methods.</li><li><b>(c)</b> is all real methods.</li><li><b>(d)</b> is all real methods.</li><li>"Reaction governing" is not a method; reaction refers to a blading type.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Throttle governing is simple but wasteful at part load; nozzle control keeps better part-load efficiency.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-33", year: 2021, no: 33, subject: "power", topic: "Gas turbine plant efficiency",
q: `In a gas turbine plant, heat supplied is 667·2 kJ/kg, and heat rejected is 391·43 kJ/kg. What is the thermal efficiency of the plant?`,
opts: ["57·29%", "72·51%", "41·33%", "32·83%"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 41·33%</h3><div class="formula">η = 1 − Q<sub>out</sub>/Q<sub>in</sub> = (Q<sub>in</sub> − Q<sub>out</sub>)/Q<sub>in</sub></div>
<p><b>Concept:</b> η = 1 − 391·43/667·2 = 1 − 0·5867 = <b>41·33%</b>; net work = 275·8 kJ/kg.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>η = 1 − 391.43/667.2.</li><li>= 1 − 0.5867 = <b>41.33 %</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 57·29% is close to the ratio Q<sub>out</sub>/Q<sub>in</sub> = 58·7%.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 72.51 % would need Q_out ≈ 183 kJ/kg.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 32.83 % would need Q_out ≈ 448 kJ/kg.</li></ul>
<p>Thermal efficiency is the net work as a fraction of heat supplied; the 58·7% rejected goes out with the exhaust, which is why combined cycles recover it in a steam bottoming plant.</p><p>Net work per kg = 667·2 − 391·43 = 275·77 kJ/kg; a plant with 41% efficiency needs about 2·42 kJ of fuel heat per kJ of work.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> This matches an ideal Brayton cycle with r<sub>p</sub> ≈ 6·5.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-34", year: 2021, no: 34, subject: "power", topic: "Constant-pressure gas turbine cycle",
q: `The constant pressure gas turbine works on`,
opts: ["Stirling Cycle", "Atkinson Cycle", "Rankine Cycle", "Brayton Cycle"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Brayton Cycle</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In a constant-pressure (continuous combustion) gas turbine, heat is added at constant pressure in the combustor and rejected at constant pressure: the Brayton (Joule) cycle.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Stirling: isothermal compression and expansion with constant-volume regeneration.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Atkinson: constant-volume heat addition with extended expansion; it applies to the old <b>constant-volume (explosion) gas turbine</b> (Holzwarth) and to some engines.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Rankine is the vapour (steam) cycle.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> "Constant-volume gas turbine" (intermittent explosion) works on the Atkinson cycle.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-35", year: 2021, no: 35, subject: "turbo", topic: "Impulse turbines",
q: `In hydraulic turbines, if the energy available at inlet is only kinetic energy, then that type of turbine is`,
opts: ["Reaction turbine", "Impulse turbine", "Francis turbine", "Kaplan turbine"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Impulse turbine</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In an impulse turbine (Pelton wheel) the whole available head is converted to kinetic energy in the nozzle before the water reaches the runner; the jet strikes the buckets at atmospheric pressure.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> In reaction turbines (including Francis and Kaplan) the water at runner inlet has both pressure and kinetic energy; pressure falls through the runner.</li><li><b>(c)</b> In reaction turbines (including Francis and Kaplan) the water at runner inlet has both pressure and kinetic energy; pressure falls through the runner.</li><li><b>(d)</b> In reaction turbines (including Francis and Kaplan) the water at runner inlet has both pressure and kinetic energy; pressure falls through the runner.</li></ul>
<p>Pelton wheels have only one stage of energy conversion in the nozzle; the runner simply changes the direction of the jet, and no casing pressure acts on it.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Impulse turbines suit high heads (above about 250 m) and low flows.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-36", year: 2021, no: 36, subject: "turbo", topic: "Manometric head of a centrifugal pump",
q: `A centrifugal pump has an impeller of 30 cm outer diameter. The vane tips are radial at the outlet. For a rotative speed of 1450 rpm, what is the manometric head developed? (Assume a manometric efficiency of 82% and take g = 9·81 m/s²)`,
opts: ["37·24 m", "43·38 m", "29·46 m", "32·88 m"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 43·38 m</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> With radial vane tips at outlet (β<sub>2</sub> = 90°), the whirl velocity at exit equals the blade speed: V<sub>w2</sub> = u<sub>2</sub>.</p>
<div class="formula">u<sub>2</sub> = π D N/60 &nbsp;&nbsp; H<sub>m</sub> = η<sub>mano</sub> × V<sub>w2</sub>u<sub>2</sub>/g = η<sub>mano</sub> u<sub>2</sub>²/g</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>u<sub>2</sub> = π × 0·3 × 1450/60 = 22·78 m/s.</li><li>u<sub>2</sub>²/g = 518·8/9·81 = 52·9 m (Euler head).</li><li>H<sub>m</sub> = 0·82 × 52·9 = <b>43·4 m</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 37·24 assumes backward-curved vanes or other diameters.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 29·46 assumes backward-curved vanes or other diameters.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 32·88 m assumes backward-curved vanes or other diameters.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Radial-tipped impellers have a flat head–flow curve in the ideal case (H = u<sub>2</sub>²/g, independent of Q).</p>` },

{ id: "2021-37", year: 2021, no: 37, subject: "turbo", topic: "Lenoir cycle",
q: `Lenoir cycle is used for`,
opts: ["Gas turbines", "Pulse jet engines", "S.I. engines", "C.I. engines"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Pulse jet engines</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The Lenoir cycle has constant-volume heat addition, isentropic expansion and constant-pressure heat rejection, with no compression stroke. It describes Lenoir's 1860 gas engine and, approximately, the pulse jet, where combustion occurs in a closed chamber (valves shut) before the gases expand out.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Gas turbines use the Brayton cycle.</li><li><b>(c)</b> SI engines use the Otto cycle.</li><li><b>(d)</b> CI engines use the Diesel or dual cycle.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Lenoir cycle efficiency is low (no pre-compression): η = 1 − γ(r − 1)/(r<sup>γ</sup> − 1), with r the expansion ratio.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-38", year: 2021, no: 38, subject: "icrac", topic: "Cut-off ratio",
q: `A diesel engine has a compression ratio of 20 and cut-off takes place at 5% of the stroke. What is the cut-off ratio?`,
opts: ["1·21", "1·47", "1·73", "1·95"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1·95</h3><div class="formula">ρ = V<sub>3</sub>/V<sub>2</sub> = 1 + k(r − 1)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>k = 0·05 (cut-off as a fraction of the stroke); r = 20; V<sub>s</sub> = (r − 1)V<sub>c</sub> = 19 V<sub>c</sub></li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> ρ = 1 + 0·05 × 19 = <b>1·95</b>.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 1·21 corresponds to cut-offs of about 1·1%, 2·5% and 3·8% of stroke.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 1·47 corresponds to cut-offs of about 1·1%, 2·5% and 3·8% of stroke.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 1·73 corresponds to cut-offs of about 1·1%, 2·5% and 3·8% of stroke.</li></ul>
<p>Cut-off ratio is the volume at the end of heat addition divided by the clearance volume; it is related to how long fuel is injected and burned at constant pressure.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Compare 2019 Q16, where the same relation was used with r = 14 and 6% cut-off.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-39", year: 2021, no: 39, subject: "icrac", topic: "Compression ratio",
q: `The cubic capacity of a four-stroke over-square spark-ignition engine is 275 cc. The clearance volume is 25 cc. What is the compression ratio of the engine?`,
opts: ["8", "10", "12", "14"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 12</h3><div class="formula">r = (V<sub>s</sub> + V<sub>c</sub>)/V<sub>c</sub></div>
<p><b>Concept:</b> r = (275 + 25)/25 = <b>12</b>. "Over-square" (bore larger than stroke) and "four-stroke" are descriptive only.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 8 would need larger clearance volumes (39·3 and 30·6 cc).</li><li><b>(b)</b> 10 would need larger clearance volumes (39·3 and 30·6 cc).</li><li><b>(d)</b> 14 would need 21·2 cc.</li><li>275/25 = 11 (forgetting to add V<sub>c</sub>) is a common slip.</li></ul>
<p>The swept (stroke) volume is the engine's cubic capacity; the total cylinder volume at BDC is swept plus clearance.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> r = 12 is high for an SI engine; it needs high-octane fuel or direct injection to avoid knock.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-40", year: 2021, no: 40, subject: "icrac", topic: "Indicated power from mechanical efficiency",
q: `The mechanical efficiency of a single-cylinder four-stroke engine is 60%. The frictional power is estimated to be 30 kW. What is the indicated power?`,
opts: ["120 kW", "75 kW", "150 kW", "130 kW"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 75 kW</h3><div class="formula">η<sub>m</sub> = BP/IP &nbsp;&nbsp; FP = IP − BP = IP(1 − η<sub>m</sub>)</div>
<p><b>Concept:</b> IP = 30/(1 − 0·6) = <b>75 kW</b>; BP = 45 kW.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>FP = IP(1 − 0.6) = 30 kW.</li><li>IP = 30/0.4 = <b>75 kW</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 120 kW would need η_m = 0.75.</li><li>30/0·6 = 50 kW (confusing FP with BP) is another common slip.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 150 kW would need η_m = 0.8.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 130 kW would need η_m ≈ 0.77.</li></ul>
<p>Mechanical efficiency connects the three powers: IP = BP + FP and η<sub>m</sub> = BP/IP; any two give the third.</p><p>In a single-cylinder engine friction is a large fraction of output, so mechanical efficiencies of 60–80% are typical at part load.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Friction power includes piston and bearing friction, pumping losses and accessory drives.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-41", year: 2021, no: 41, subject: "icrac", topic: "Mechanical efficiency at part load",
q: `A four-stroke petrol engine at full load delivers 100 kW. It requires 10 kW to rotate it without load at same speed. What is the mechanical efficiency at half load?`,
opts: ["67·82%", "70·24%", "77·32%", "83·33%"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 83·33%</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The power needed to motor the engine at the same speed with no load is its friction power, which stays about the same at a fixed speed.</p><div class="formula">IP = BP + FP</div><div class="formula">η_m = BP/IP</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>FP = 10 kW (constant at this speed).</li><li>Half load: BP = 50 kW → IP = 60 kW.</li><li>η<sub>m</sub> = 50/60 = <b>83·3%</b>. (At full load: 100/110 = 90·9%.)</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 67·82 assumes friction changes with load or use the full-load BP.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 70·24 assumes friction changes with load or use the full-load BP.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 77·32% assumes friction changes with load or use the full-load BP.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Mechanical efficiency falls at part load because friction is nearly constant while output drops; at no load η<sub>m</sub> = 0.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-42", year: 2021, no: 42, subject: "icrac", topic: "Volumetric efficiency of a refrigerant compressor",
q: `Freon-12 is used in a simple saturation cycle, with suction saturation temperature of −10 °C and condensing saturation temperature of 30 °C. If the clearance volume is 6% of the stroke volume, what is the volumetric efficiency? (Consider specific volume at suction and discharge to be 0·07815 m³/kg and 0·025 m³/kg respectively)`,
opts: ["87·24%", "71·31%", "64·85%", "55·43%"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 87·24%</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The clearance vapour re-expands from discharge to suction conditions; its volume grows in the ratio of specific volumes.</p>
<div class="formula">η<sub>v</sub> = 1 + C − C (v<sub>suction</sub>/v<sub>discharge</sub>)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>C = 0·06; v<sub>s</sub> = 0·07815 m³/kg; v<sub>d</sub> = 0·025 m³/kg</li></ul>
<p>η<sub>v</sub> = 1 + 0·06 − 0·06 × 3·126 = 1·06 − 0·1876 = <b>87·24%</b>.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>v_s/v_d = 0.07815/0.025 = 3.126.</li><li>η_v = 1.06 − 0.06 × 3.126 = <b>87.24 %</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 71·31 uses a larger re-expansion ratio (for example the pressure ratio instead of the volume ratio) or a larger clearance.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 64·85 uses a larger re-expansion ratio (for example the pressure ratio instead of the volume ratio) or a larger clearance.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 55·43% uses a larger re-expansion ratio (for example the pressure ratio instead of the volume ratio) or a larger clearance.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> v<sub>s</sub>/v<sub>d</sub> = (p<sub>d</sub>/p<sub>s</sub>)<sup>1/n</sup> for polytropic re-expansion; using specific volumes avoids needing n.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-43", year: 2021, no: 43, subject: "icrac", topic: "Ozone depletion potential of R-12",
q: `Relative ozone destruction efficiency of R-12 is`,
opts: ["0·29", "0·86", "0·05", "0·57"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 0·86</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Ozone depletion potential (ODP) is measured relative to R-11 (ODP = 1). Older refrigeration texts (for example Arora) quote R-12 at about 0·86 relative ozone destruction efficiency; modern references often round it to 1·0 (as defined under the Montreal Protocol).</p><div class="formula">ODP relative to R-11 = 1</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>R-12 relative ozone destruction efficiency ≈ <b>0.86</b> (older texts).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0.29 is far below R-12's high ODP.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0.05 is typical of HCFCs like R-22.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0.57 is below the textbook 0.86 for R-12.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> R-134a, R-410A and other HFCs have ODP = 0 but high global warming potential; HFOs (R-1234yf) have both low ODP and low GWP.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-44", year: 2021, no: 44, subject: "icrac", topic: "Air-cooled condenser temperature",
q: `An air cooled condenser has 6 m² of surface with a removal of 50 kJ.hr<sup>−1</sup>.m<sup>−2</sup>.°C<sup>−1</sup>. What is the refrigerant temperature to dissipate 5235 kJ/hr, if the room temperature is 25 °C?`,
opts: ["24·31 °C", "35·82 °C", "42·45 °C", "56·94 °C"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 42·45 °C</h3><div class="formula">Q = U A ΔT → ΔT = Q/(U A)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>Q = 5235 kJ/h; U = 50 kJ/h m² °C; A = 6 m²</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> ΔT = 5235/300 = 17·45 °C → T<sub>refrigerant</sub> = 25 + 17·45 = <b>42·45 °C</b>.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>UA = 50 × 6 = 300 kJ/h°C.</li><li>ΔT = 5235/300 = 17.45 °C.</li><li>T = 25 + 17.45 = <b>42.45 °C</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 24·31 °C is below room temperature, so no heat could be rejected.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 35·82 corresponds to other areas or coefficients.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 56·94 °C corresponds to other areas or coefficients.</li></ul>
<p>The coefficient of 50 kJ/h m² °C (13·9 W/m² K) is typical of air-side natural or low-speed forced convection over a finned condenser.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Condensing temperatures of air-cooled units are usually 10–15 °C above ambient air.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-45", year: 2021, no: 45, subject: "icrac", topic: "Relative COP",
q: `The actual and theoretical COP of rolling piston compressor are 3·6 and 4·7 respectively. What is the relative COP?`,
opts: ["8·3", "16·92", "1·3", "0·76"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 0·76</h3><div class="formula">Relative COP = actual COP/theoretical COP</div>
<p><b>Concept:</b> 3·6/4·7 = <b>0·766</b>.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 8·3 = 3·6 + 4·7; = 4·7/3·6 (inverted).</li><li><b>(b)</b> 16·92 = 3·6 × 4·7; = 4·7/3·6 (inverted).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 1·3 = 4·7/3·6 (inverted).</li></ul>
<p>The gap between actual and theoretical COP reflects compressor inefficiency, pressure drops, heat transfer and superheating losses.</p><p>Rolling-piston (rotary) compressors are used in room air conditioners and small refrigerators because of their compactness and low noise.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Relative COP must be below 1, since a real machine cannot beat its ideal cycle.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-46", year: 2021, no: 46, subject: "icrac", topic: "Maximum COP of an absorption system",
q: `In an absorption type refrigeration system, heating in generator, refrigeration in evaporator and cooling by cooling water in condenser, take place at 95 °C, −5 °C and 30 °C respectively. What is the maximum COP of the system?`,
opts: ["1·17", "1·35", "1·52", "1·78"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1·35</h3><div class="formula">COP<sub>max</sub> = [(T<sub>g</sub> − T<sub>0</sub>)/T<sub>g</sub>] × [T<sub>e</sub>/(T<sub>0</sub> − T<sub>e</sub>)]</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>T<sub>g</sub> = 368 K (generator); T<sub>0</sub> = 303 K (condenser, heat rejection); T<sub>e</sub> = 268 K (evaporator)</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Engine part: 65/368 = 0·1766.</li><li>Refrigerator part: 268/35 = 7·657.</li><li>COP<sub>max</sub> = 0·1766 × 7·657 = <b>1·35</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 1·17 uses Celsius values or omit one factor.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 1·52 uses Celsius values or omit one factor.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 1·78 uses Celsius values or omit one factor.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The ideal COP improves with a hotter generator, colder condenser and warmer evaporator, which is why absorption chillers are used for air conditioning rather than freezing.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Real single-effect absorption chillers reach about half this ideal value.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-47", year: 2021, no: 47, subject: "icrac", topic: "Negative sensible heat factor",
q: `Consider the following statements for sensible heat factor :<ol><li>Sensible heat factor will be negative if sensible heat and latent heat are both negative.</li><li>Sensible heat factor will be negative if sensible heat is negative and latent heat is positive.</li><li>Sensible heat factor will be negative if sensible heat is positive and latent heat is negative.</li><li>Sensible heat factor will be negative if sensible heat and latent heat are both positive.</li></ol>`,
opts: ["2 and 3 only", "1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 4 only"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 2 and 3 only</h3><div class="formula">SHF = SH/(SH + LH)</div>
<p><b>Concept:</b> SHF is negative only when numerator and denominator have opposite signs, which happens when SH and LH have opposite signs (and the latent part dominates in magnitude).</p>
<p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (wrong):</b> Both negative: the ratio is positive.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> Sensible cooling with humidification (evaporative cooling region) can give a negative SHF.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> Sensible heating with dehumidification (for example chemical dehumidification) can too.</li><li><b>4 (wrong):</b> Both positive: SHF between 0 and 1.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 2, 3.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 4, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> On the psychrometric protractor, negative SHF lines slope up to the left (cooling with humidification) or down to the right.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-48", year: 2021, no: 48, subject: "icrac", topic: "Air washer with recirculated water",
q: `If the air is initially at dry bulb temperature 35 °C and wet bulb temperature 26·1 °C as it enters an air washer which has a humidifying efficiency of 85%, then what is the final dry bulb temperature of air washed with recirculated spray water?`,
opts: ["26·81 °C", "27·43 °C", "32·83 °C", "30·49 °C"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 27·43 °C</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> With recirculated spray water, the water settles at the air's wet-bulb temperature and the process is adiabatic saturation along the constant-WBT line. Humidifying efficiency compares the actual drop in DBT with the maximum possible (down to the WBT):</p>
<div class="formula">η<sub>H</sub> = (t<sub>1</sub> − t<sub>2</sub>)/(t<sub>1</sub> − t<sub>wb</sub>) → t<sub>2</sub> = t<sub>1</sub> − η<sub>H</sub>(t<sub>1</sub> − t<sub>wb</sub>)</div>
<p>t<sub>2</sub> = 35 − 0·85 × 8·9 = 35 − 7·57 = <b>27·43 °C</b>.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>t₁ − t_wb = 35 − 26.1 = 8.9 °C.</li><li>t₂ = 35 − 0.85 × 8.9 = <b>27.43 °C</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 26·81 °C would need about 92% efficiency.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 32·83 uses the efficiency in the wrong way (for example 15% of the drop).</li><li><b>(d)</b> 30·49 °C uses the efficiency in the wrong way (for example 15% of the drop).</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> The WBT stays 26·1 °C throughout; the air leaves cooler and more humid.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-49", year: 2021, no: 49, subject: "thermo", topic: "Nucleate boiling", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Consider the following statements for Nucleate boiling :<ol><li>For water, the critical heat flux does not exceed 1 MW/m².</li><li>Nucleate boiling is the most desirable boiling regime in practice because of high heat transfer rates.</li><li>Heat flux increases at a higher rate with increase in temperature.</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> For water at 1 atm the critical heat flux is about 1·1–1·3 MW/m² (Cengel), so it does exceed 1 MW/m².</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> Nucleate boiling gives very high heat transfer at small temperature differences, so equipment is designed to work in it (below CHF).</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong as worded):</b> Cengel describes the upper part of the nucleate regime (region B–C) as one where the heat flux increases at a <b>lower</b> rate with increasing excess temperature, as vapour begins to cover more of the surface before the peak.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Our first attempt accepted statement 3 (heat flux does rise steeply in early nucleate boiling). On checking Cengel's description, the statement's "higher rate" does not fit the regime as a whole, so the key's (b) is accepted.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 2.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 1, 3, which are incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Rohsenow's correlation predicts nucleate boiling flux: q ∝ ΔT<sub>e</sub>³.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-50", year: 2021, no: 50, subject: "thermo", topic: "Dropwise vs film condensation",
q: `In drop-wise condensation, the heat transfer rate is`,
opts: ["5 times less than that in film-wise condensation.", "15 times less than that in film-wise condensation.", "25 times more than that in film-wise condensation.", "10 times more than that in film-wise condensation."], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 10 times more than that in film-wise condensation</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In dropwise condensation, much of the surface is bare and in direct contact with vapour, so there is no continuous liquid film resisting heat flow. Heat transfer coefficients are typically 5–10 times (often quoted as about 10 times) those of film condensation.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> says dropwise is <i>worse</i>, which is the opposite of the truth.</li><li><b>(b)</b> says dropwise is <i>worse</i>, which is the opposite of the truth.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 25 times is an overstatement for the usual textbook comparison.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Compare 2020 Q24: dropwise condensation is promoted by non-wetting coatings.</p>` }
);

// ---- q2021c.js ----
// ESE 2021 Prelims Paper II, Mechanical Engineering, Series A, Q51–75
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2021-51", year: 2021, no: 51, subject: "thermo", topic: "Temperature rise of a falling body of water",
q: `1 kg of water falls from an altitude of 1000 m above ground level. What is the change in the temperature of water at the foot of the fall, if there are no losses during the fall? (Take specific heat of water as 1 kcal.kg<sup>−1</sup>.K<sup>−1</sup> and g = 9·81 m/s²)`,
opts: ["3·35 °C", "2·35 °C", "3·32 °C", "4·12 °C"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2·35 °C</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> All the potential energy lost is converted (on impact, with no losses) into internal energy of the water.</p>
<div class="formula">m g h = m c ΔT → ΔT = g h/c</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>g h = 9·81 × 1000 = 9810 J/kg; c = 1 kcal/kg K = 4186·8 J/kg K</li></ul>
<p>ΔT = 9810/4186·8 = <b>2·34 °C</b> ≈ 2·35 °C.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 3·35 uses wrong conversions between kcal and J.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 3·32 uses wrong conversions between kcal and J.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 4·12 °C uses wrong conversions between kcal and J.</li></ul>
<p>Energy check: 9810 J/kg of potential energy is about 2·34 kcal/kg, so a 1000 m fall warms water by only about 2·3 °C; in real waterfalls much of this energy goes into spray and evaporation, so the measured rise is even smaller.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Joule measured this kind of temperature rise to establish the mechanical equivalent of heat (4·186 J/cal).</p>` },

{ id: "2021-52", year: 2021, no: 52, subject: "thermo", topic: "First law at constant pressure",
q: `A stationary mass of gas is compressed without friction from an initial state of 0·3 m³ and 0·105 MPa to a final state of 0·15 m³ and 0·105 MPa, the pressure remaining constant during the process. There is a transfer of 40 kJ of heat from the gas during the process. How much does the internal energy of the gas change?`,
opts: ["−24·25 kJ", "−19·62 kJ", "−15·91 kJ", "−12·72 kJ"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) −24·25 kJ</h3><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Work done by the gas: W = p(V<sub>2</sub> − V<sub>1</sub>) = 105 × (0·15 − 0·3) = −15·75 kJ (work is done <i>on</i> the gas).</li><li>Heat: Q = −40 kJ (lost by the gas).</li><li>First law: ΔU = Q − W = −40 − (−15·75) = <b>−24·25 kJ</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> −19·62 comes from sign errors or using kPa as MPa.</li><li><b>(c)</b> −15·91 comes from sign errors or using kPa as MPa.</li><li><b>(d)</b> −12·72 kJ comes from sign errors or using kPa as MPa.</li><li>ΔU = −40 − 15·75 = −55·75 kJ is the result of a sign slip on W.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Physical picture: the gas is compressed (work done on it, 15·75 kJ) but loses even more heat (40 kJ), so its internal energy, and hence its temperature, falls by 24·25 kJ worth. Enthalpy change at constant pressure is ΔH = Q = −40 kJ, a useful cross-check: ΔH = ΔU + pΔV = −24·25 − 15·75 = −40 kJ.</p><div class="formula">W = p(V₂ − V₁)</div><div class="formula">ΔU = Q − W</div><p><b>Remember:</b> Sign convention used: Q into the system positive, W by the system positive; ΔU = Q − W.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-53", year: 2021, no: 53, subject: "thermo", topic: "State postulate",
q: `The state of a simple compressible pure substance can be fixed by specifying`,
opts: ["one independent property.", "two independent properties.", "three independent properties.", "four independent properties."], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) two independent properties</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> State postulate: the state of a simple compressible system (only one reversible work mode, p dV) is fixed by two independent intensive properties. All other properties then follow (from tables or equations of state).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> One property is not enough (except in special cases).</li><li><b>(c)</b> More properties are needed only when additional work modes (magnetic, electrical, surface tension) are present.</li><li><b>(d)</b> More properties are needed only when additional work modes (magnetic, electrical, surface tension) are present.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> In the wet (two-phase) region, p and T are <i>not</i> independent; use p (or T) with quality x.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-54", year: 2021, no: 54, subject: "thermo", topic: "Thermocouple calibration: non-linear emf",
q: `In a thermoelectric thermometer for t °C temperature, the emf is given as : E = 0·003t − 5 × 10<sup>−7</sup> t² + 0·5 × 10<sup>−3</sup> volts. Thermometer is having reference junction at ice point and is calibrated at ice point and steam point. What is the temperature shown by the thermometer for a substance at 30 °C?`,
opts: ["33·23 °C", "36·28 °C", "41·23 °C", "46·28 °C"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 33·23 °C</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A thermometer calibrated at the ice and steam points reads linearly between them: t<sub>reading</sub> = 100 × (E − E<sub>ice</sub>)/(E<sub>steam</sub> − E<sub>ice</sub>).</p><div class="formula">t_reading = 100(E − E₀)/(E₁₀₀ − E₀)</div>
<p><b>Working with the printed equation</b></p><ol class="work"><li>E(0) = 0·0005 V; E(100) = 0·3 − 0·005 + 0·0005 = 0·2955 V.</li><li>E(30) = 0·09 − 0·00045 + 0·0005 = 0·09005 V.</li><li>Reading = 100 × (0·09005 − 0·0005)/(0·2955 − 0·0005) = 100 × 0·08955/0·295 = 30·36 °C.</li></ol>
<p>The printed constants do not reproduce any option exactly; the key gives (a) 33·23 °C, and of the options it is the one nearest the non-linear thermometer's deviation from the true temperature. The intended principle is that a non-linear sensor calibrated at two points reads differently from the true temperature between them.</p>
<p><b>Other options</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 36·28 overstates the deviation.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 41·23 overstates the deviation.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 46·28 °C overstates the deviation.</li></ul>
<div class="note">Data note: with the coefficients exactly as printed, the two-point calibrated reading at 30 °C works out to about 30·4 °C, which matches none of the options; the coefficients appear to be misprinted. Our answer (a) agrees with the official key.</div>
<p><b>Remember:</b> This is why thermocouples use reference tables or polynomial fits rather than a single linear scale.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-55", year: 2021, no: 55, subject: "thermo", topic: "Heat and work",
q: `Consider the following statements for comparison of heat and work :<ol><li>Both heat and work are transient phenomena.</li><li>Both heat and work are boundary phenomena.</li><li>Both heat and work are path functions and inexact differentials.</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1, 2 and 3", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> Heat and work exist only while energy is crossing the boundary during a process; a system does not "contain" heat or work.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> Both are identified at the system boundary as energy crossing it.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> Both depend on the path: δQ and δW are inexact differentials, ∮δQ and ∮δW are not zero in general.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Contrast with properties: internal energy, enthalpy and entropy are point functions (exact differentials) whose change depends only on the end states. Heat and work are energy in transit and are written as δQ and δW to remind us that their integrals depend on how the process is carried out.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Their difference, δQ − δW = dU, is an exact differential: the first law in differential form.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-56", year: 2021, no: 56, subject: "thermo", topic: "Stirred tank: first law",
q: `A tank containing a fluid is stirred by a paddle wheel. The work input to the paddle wheel is 5090 kJ. The heat transfer from the tank is 1500 kJ. What is the change in internal energy of this control mass? (Consider the tank and the fluid inside a control surface)`,
opts: ["−3590 kJ", "+3590 kJ", "+4590 kJ", "−4590 kJ"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) +3590 kJ</h3><div class="formula">ΔU = Q − W</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>Q = −1500 kJ (heat leaves); W = −5090 kJ (work done <i>on</i> the system)</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> ΔU = −1500 − (−5090) = <b>+3590 kJ</b>.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Q = −1500 kJ, W = −5090 kJ.</li><li>ΔU = −1500 + 5090 = <b>+3590 kJ</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> −3590 kJ has the sign reversed.</li><li><b>(c)</b> ±4590 kJ come from arithmetic slips.</li><li><b>(d)</b> ±4590 kJ come from arithmetic slips.</li></ul>
<p>Interpretation: of the 5090 kJ supplied by the paddle wheel, 1500 kJ escapes as heat and the remaining 3590 kJ is stored in the fluid as internal energy, raising its temperature. The fluid and tank form a closed system (control mass), so no flow terms appear.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Paddle-wheel (stirring) work is always work input; it cannot be extracted, a classic irreversible process.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-57", year: 2021, no: 57, subject: "thermo", topic: "Battery charging: internal energy rate",
q: `During the charging of a storage battery, the current is 20 A and the voltage is 12·8 V. The rate of heat transfer from the battery is 10 W. At what rate is the internal energy increasing?`,
opts: ["−256 J/s", "+246 J/s", "+256 J/s", "−246 J/s"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) +246 J/s</h3><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Electrical work input = V I = 12·8 × 20 = 256 W (work done on the battery: Ẇ = −256 W).</li><li>Heat lost: Q̇ = −10 W.</li><li>dU/dt = Q̇ − Ẇ = −10 + 256 = <b>+246 W</b> (J/s).</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(c)</b> +256 J/s ignores the heat loss.</li><li><b>(a)</b> has the wrong sign: a charging battery stores energy.</li><li><b>(d)</b> has the wrong sign: a charging battery stores energy.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Interpretation: of the 256 W supplied electrically, 10 W is lost as heat (internal resistance) and 246 W is stored. The battery is a closed system with electrical work as the only work mode, so the first law in rate form applies directly.</p><div class="formula">Ẇ = −VI (work in)</div><div class="formula">dU/dt = Q̇ − Ẇ</div><p><b>Remember:</b> The stored energy is chemical (part of internal energy); the 10 W is lost as Joule heating.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-58", year: 2021, no: 58, subject: "icrac", topic: "Carnot refrigerator power",
q: `A refrigerator operates on Reversed Carnot cycle. What is the power required to drive the refrigerator between temperatures of 42 °C and 4 °C, if heat at the rate of 2 kJ/s is extracted from the low temperature region?`,
opts: ["0·174 kW", "0·374 kW", "0·274 kW", "0·474 kW"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 0·274 kW</h3><div class="formula">COP = T<sub>L</sub>/(T<sub>H</sub> − T<sub>L</sub>) &nbsp;&nbsp; W = Q<sub>L</sub>/COP</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>T<sub>L</sub> = 277 K; T<sub>H</sub> = 315 K; Q<sub>L</sub> = 2 kW</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> COP = 277/38 = 7·29 → W = 2/7·29 = <b>0·274 kW</b>.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>COP = 277/38 = 7.29.</li><li>W = 2/7.29 = <b>0.274 kW</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0·174 kW would beat the Carnot limit.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 0·374 uses wrong temperatures (for example 4 °C as 273 K).</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0·474 kW uses wrong temperatures (for example 4 °C as 273 K).</li></ul>
<p>Note that the COP depends only on the two absolute temperatures. A real vapour-compression refrigerator between the same limits would need perhaps two to three times this power, because of throttling, superheat and compressor losses.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Heat rejected = 2 + 0·274 = 2·274 kW.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-59", year: 2021, no: 59, subject: "thermo", topic: "Entropy generation criterion",
q: `Entropy generated (S<sub>gen</sub>) can be taken as a criterion to indicate feasibility of process. Which of the following conditions are correct?<ol><li>If S<sub>gen</sub> = 0, then the process is a reversible process.</li><li>If S<sub>gen</sub> &gt; 0, then the process is an irreversible process.</li><li>If S<sub>gen</sub> &lt; 0, then the process is impossible.</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The entropy balance ΔS = ∫δQ/T + S<sub>gen</sub>, with the second law requiring S<sub>gen</sub> ≥ 0.</p>
<p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> S<sub>gen</sub> = 0 means no irreversibility: a reversible process.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> S<sub>gen</sub> &gt; 0 means an irreversible (real) process.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> S<sub>gen</sub> &lt; 0 would violate the second law: impossible.</li></ul>
<p>The entropy of a system may decrease (for example by losing heat), so ΔS<sub>system</sub> &lt; 0 alone does not make a process impossible; it is S<sub>gen</sub>, the entropy produced by irreversibilities, that can never be negative. For an isolated system ΔS = S<sub>gen</sub> ≥ 0, the increase-of-entropy principle.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Lost work (exergy destruction) = T<sub>0</sub> S<sub>gen</sub>.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-60", year: 2021, no: 60, subject: "thermo", topic: "Critical radius of insulation",
q: `What is the critical radius of insulation for asbestos (thermal conductivity = 0·17 W.m<sup>−1</sup>.°C<sup>−1</sup>) surrounding a circular pipe and exposed to room air at 20 °C with heat transfer coefficient 3 W.m<sup>−2</sup>.°C<sup>−1</sup>?`,
opts: ["7·21 cm", "6·37 cm", "5·67 cm", "6·93 cm"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 5·67 cm</h3><div class="formula">Cylinder: r<sub>c</sub> = k/h</div>
<p><b>Concept:</b> r<sub>c</sub> = 0·17/3 = 0·0567 m = <b>5·67 cm</b>. Room temperature does not enter.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>r_c = 0.17/3.</li><li>= 0.0567 m = <b>5.67 cm</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 7·21 corresponds to other k/h ratios; the sphere formula 2k/h would give 11·3 cm.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 6·37 corresponds to other k/h ratios; the sphere formula 2k/h would give 11·3 cm.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 6·93 cm corresponds to other k/h ratios; the sphere formula 2k/h would give 11·3 cm.</li></ul>
<p>Physical meaning: below r<sub>c</sub>, adding insulation increases the outer surface area faster than it adds conduction resistance, so heat loss actually rises; maximum heat loss occurs at r = r<sub>c</sub>. This is used deliberately for electric cables, which need to shed heat.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> For pipes larger than r<sub>c</sub> (about 11 cm diameter here), adding asbestos always reduces heat loss.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-61", year: 2021, no: 61, subject: "fm", topic: "Head loss with a Re-dependent friction factor",
q: `Water is flowing through a pipe of diameter 200 mm with a velocity of 3 m/s. What is the head loss due to friction for a length of 5 m if the coefficient of friction is given by f = 0·02 + 0·09/Re<sup>0·3</sup>, where Re is Reynolds number? (Take the kinematic viscosity of water as 0·01 stoke, g = 9·81 m/s² and (6 × 10⁵)<sup>0·3</sup> = 54·13)`,
opts: ["0·993 m of water", "0·783 m of water", "0·685 m of water", "0·552 m of water"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 0·993 m of water</h3><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>ν = 0·01 stoke = 10<sup>−6</sup> m²/s → Re = VD/ν = 3 × 0·2/10<sup>−6</sup> = 6 × 10⁵.</li><li>f = 0·02 + 0·09/54·13 = 0·02166 (the "coefficient of friction", Fanning-type).</li><li>h<sub>f</sub> = 4 f L V²/(2 g D) = 4 × 0·02166 × 5 × 9/(2 × 9·81 × 0·2) = 3·899/3·924 = <b>0·994 m</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> This uses the older Indian form of Darcy's equation with 4f (Bansal), consistent with "coefficient of friction".</p><div class="formula">Re = VD/ν</div><div class="formula">h_f = 4fLV²/(2gD)</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(d)</b> 0·552 does not match either convention; using f directly in fLV²/2gD would give only 0·248 m.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0·685 does not match either convention; using f directly in fLV²/2gD would give only 0·248 m.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 0·783 m does not match either convention; using f directly in fLV²/2gD would give only 0·248 m.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> The given expression is a classic empirical law for turbulent flow in smooth pipes.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-62", year: 2021, no: 62, subject: "fm", topic: "Loss due to an obstruction in a pipe",
q: `Water is flowing through a horizontal pipe of diameter 200 mm at a velocity of 3 m/s. A circular solid plate of diameter 150 mm is placed in the pipe to obstruct the flow. What is the loss of head due to obstruction in the pipe if C<sub>c</sub> = 0·62? (Take g = 9·81 m/s²)`,
opts: ["3·311 m", "4·211 m", "5·211 m", "6·211 m"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 3·311 m</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Flow squeezes through the annulus around the plate and contracts to a vena contracta of area C<sub>c</sub>(A − a), then expands suddenly back to the full pipe. The loss is that of a sudden expansion:</p>
<div class="formula">h<sub>L</sub> = (V²/2g) [A/(C<sub>c</sub>(A − a)) − 1]²</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>A = π × 0·2²/4 = 0·03142 m² (pipe); a = π × 0·15²/4 = 0·01767 m² (plate); A − a = 0·01374 m²</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>A/(C<sub>c</sub>(A − a)) = 0·03142/(0·62 × 0·01374) = 3·688.</li><li>(3·688 − 1)² = 7·226.</li><li>h<sub>L</sub> = (9/19·62) × 7·226 = <b>3·31 m</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> is each about 1, 2 and 3 m larger, from errors such as omitting C<sub>c</sub>-related terms or using diameters instead of areas.</li><li><b>(c)</b> is each about 1, 2 and 3 m larger, from errors such as omitting C<sub>c</sub>-related terms or using diameters instead of areas.</li><li><b>(d)</b> is each about 1, 2 and 3 m larger, from errors such as omitting C<sub>c</sub>-related terms or using diameters instead of areas.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Orifice plates in pipes work on the same principle; their permanent loss is what this formula estimates.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-63", year: 2021, no: 63, subject: "fm", topic: "Equivalent pipe for pipes in series",
q: `Three pipes of length 800 m, 500 m and 400 m and of diameters 500 mm, 400 mm and 300 mm respectively are connected in series. These pipes are to be replaced by a single pipe of length 1700 m. What is the diameter of the single pipe?`,
opts: ["(0·007118)<sup>0·2</sup> m", "(0·003609)<sup>0·3</sup> m", "(0·003609)<sup>0·2</sup> m", "(0·007118)<sup>0·3</sup> m"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) (0·007118)<sup>0·2</sup> m</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For the same flow and total head loss (same friction factor), Dupuit's equation gives:</p>
<div class="formula">L/D⁵ = L<sub>1</sub>/D<sub>1</sub>⁵ + L<sub>2</sub>/D<sub>2</sub>⁵ + L<sub>3</sub>/D<sub>3</sub>⁵</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>800/0·5⁵ = 25,600; 500/0·4⁵ = 48,828; 400/0·3⁵ = 1,64,609 → sum = 2,39,037.</li><li>D⁵ = 1700/2,39,037 = 0·00711 → D = (0·00711)<sup>1/5</sup> = 0·372 m.</li></ol>
<p>This matches (a), with D⁵ written as 0·007118.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> uses the exponent 0·3, which is not 1/5.</li><li><b>(d)</b> uses the exponent 0·3, which is not 1/5.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0·003609 is about half the correct D⁵.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Minor losses are neglected; the equivalent pipe is then about 372 mm in diameter.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-64", year: 2021, no: 64, subject: "fm", topic: "Nozzle velocity at the end of a long pipe",
q: `The head of water at the inlet of a pipe 2000 m long and 500 mm diameter is 60 m. A nozzle of diameter 100 mm at its outlet is fitted to the pipe. What is the velocity of water at the outlet of the nozzle if f = 0·01 for the pipe? (Take g = 9·81 m/s²)`,
opts: ["30·61 m/s", "34·81 m/s", "36·52 m/s", "38·36 m/s"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 30·61 m/s</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Head at inlet = pipe friction loss + velocity head of the jet (minor losses neglected); continuity links the pipe velocity V to the jet velocity v.</p>
<div class="formula">H = v²/2g + 4fLV²/(2gD), &nbsp; V = v (d/D)²</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>H = 60 m; L = 2000 m; D = 0·5 m; d = 0·1 m; f = 0·01 (Fanning-type coefficient)</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>(d/D)⁴ = (0·2)⁴ = 0·0016.</li><li>4fL/D × 0·0016 = 4 × 0·01 × 2000/0·5 × 0·0016 = 0·256.</li><li>v² = 2gH/(1 + 0·256) = 1177·2/1·256 = 937·2 → v = <b>30·61 m/s</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 34·81 uses f without the 4 (giving 33·3 m/s) or neglect friction (34·3 m/s with no loss).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 36·52 uses f without the 4 (giving 33·3 m/s) or neglect friction (34·3 m/s with no loss).</li><li><b>(d)</b> 38·36 m/s uses f without the 4 (giving 33·3 m/s) or neglect friction (34·3 m/s with no loss).</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Power in the jet = ½ρav³ = ½ × 1000 × 0·007854 × 30·61³ = 112·6 kW.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-65", year: 2021, no: 65, subject: "fm", topic: "Water hammer: gradual valve closure",
q: `Water is flowing with a velocity of 1·5 m/s in a pipe of length 2500 m and of diameter 500 mm. At the end of the pipe, a valve is provided. What is the rise in pressure if the valve is closed in 25 seconds? (Take the value of C as 1460 m/s)`,
opts: ["12 N/cm²", "15 N/cm²", "16 N/cm²", "18 N/cm²"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 15 N/cm²</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> First decide whether closure is gradual or sudden: compare the closure time with the pressure-wave round-trip time 2L/C.</p><div class="formula">Gradual if T &gt; 2L/C</div><div class="formula">Δp = ρLV/T (gradual closure)</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>2L/C = 2 × 2500/1460 = 3·42 s &lt; 25 s → gradual closure.</li><li>For gradual closure: Δp = ρ L V/T = 1000 × 2500 × 1·5/25 = 1·5 × 10⁵ N/m².</li><li>= <b>15 N/cm²</b> (1·5 bar).</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 12 comes from wrong closure times or diameters.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 16 comes from wrong closure times or diameters.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 18 N/cm² comes from wrong closure times or diameters.</li><li>Sudden closure (ρCV = 21·9 × 10⁵ N/m² = 219 N/cm²) would apply only if T &lt; 3·42 s.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Surge tanks and slow-closing valves are used to keep water hammer pressures down in penstocks.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-66", year: 2021, no: 66, subject: "fm", topic: "Stability of submerged bodies",
q: `If a submerged body is in unstable equilibrium, then`,
opts: ["the centre of buoyancy is below the centre of gravity.", "the centre of buoyancy is above the centre of gravity.", "meta-centre is below the centre of buoyancy.", "meta-centre is above the centre of buoyancy."], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) the centre of buoyancy is below the centre of gravity</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For a fully submerged body, B does not move when the body tilts (the displaced volume shape is fixed). If G is above B, any tilt creates a couple that turns the body further: unstable. If G is below B, the couple restores it: stable. G at B: neutral.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> B above G is the <i>stable</i> condition.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The metacentre concept applies to floating bodies; for a submerged body the metacentre coincides with B.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The metacentre concept applies to floating bodies; for a submerged body the metacentre coincides with B.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Submarines and balloons carry heavy ballast low down to keep G well below B.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-67", year: 2021, no: 67, subject: "fm", topic: "Concrete needed to sink a beam",
q: `How much of concrete with γ = 25 kN/m³ must be attached to a beam having a volume of 0·1 m³ and specific gravity 0·6 to cause both to sink in water? (Take g = 9·81 m/s²)`,
opts: ["0·825 kN", "0·745 kN", "0·525 kN", "0·645 kN"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 0·645 kN</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For both to (just) sink, the total weight must equal the total buoyancy of beam + concrete when fully submerged.</p><div class="formula">Sinking: total weight = total buoyancy</div><div class="formula">Net downward force of concrete = (γ_c − γ_w)V</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Beam weight = 0·6 × 9·81 × 0·1 = 0·589 kN; beam buoyancy = 9·81 × 0·1 = 0·981 kN; surplus buoyancy = 0·392 kN.</li><li>Concrete of volume V gives net downward force (25 − 9·81)V = 15·19V kN.</li><li>15·19V = 0·392 → V = 0·0258 m³ → weight = 25 × 0·0258 = <b>0·646 kN</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(c)</b> 0·525 kN ignores the buoyancy of the concrete itself.</li><li><b>(a)</b> 0·825 overestimates.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 0·745 kN overestimates.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Concrete underwater "weighs" only 15·19 kN/m³, which is why more is needed than the beam's surplus buoyancy (0·392 kN) suggests.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-68", year: 2021, no: 68, subject: "fm", topic: "Dynamic viscosity from kinematic viscosity",
q: `A liquid has a specific gravity of 1·9 and a kinematic viscosity of 6 stokes. What is the dynamic viscosity?`,
opts: ["1·14 N/m²", "2·44 N/m²", "3·40 N/m²", "11·40 N/m²"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1·14 N s/m²</h3><div class="formula">μ = ρ ν</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>ρ = 1·9 × 1000 = 1900 kg/m³; ν = 6 stokes = 6 cm²/s = 6 × 10<sup>−4</sup> m²/s</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> μ = 1900 × 6 × 10<sup>−4</sup> = <b>1·14 N s/m²</b> (Pa s). The options' unit "N/m²" should read N s/m².</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(d)</b> 11·40 uses ν = 6 × 10<sup>−3</sup> (wrong stoke conversion).</li><li><b>(b)</b> 2·44 has no basis.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 3·40 has no basis.</li></ul>
<p>Unit check: kinematic viscosity ν has units m²/s (stokes in CGS); dynamic viscosity μ has units N s/m² = Pa s = kg/(m s). Here μ = 1·14 Pa s = 11·4 poise, which is the viscosity of a thick oil or glycerine-like liquid.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> 1 stoke = 10<sup>−4</sup> m²/s; 1 poise = 0·1 Pa s.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-69", year: 2021, no: 69, subject: "fm", topic: "Discharge from total head",
q: `Oil of specific gravity 0·8 flows through a 0·2 m diameter pipe under a pressure of 100 kN/m². If the datum is 5 m below the centerline of the pipe and the total energy with respect to the datum is 35 Nm/N, the discharge is (Take g = 9·81 m/s²)`,
opts: ["0·58 m³/sec", "0·47 m³/sec", "0·31 m³/sec", "0·22 m³/sec"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 0·58 m³/s</h3><div class="formula">H = p/(ρg) + V²/2g + z</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Pressure head = 100,000/(800 × 9·81) = 12·74 m of oil.</li><li>z = 5 m.</li><li>V²/2g = 35 − 12·74 − 5 = 17·26 m → V = √(2 × 9·81 × 17·26) = 18·4 m/s.</li><li>Q = (π × 0·2²/4) × 18·4 = 0·03142 × 18·4 = <b>0·578 m³/s</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 0·47 uses the water density for the pressure head or forget the datum height.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0·31 uses the water density for the pressure head or forget the datum height.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0·22 m³/s uses the water density for the pressure head or forget the datum height.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The total energy of 35 m is split into 12·74 m pressure head, 5 m datum head and 17·26 m velocity head. A velocity of 18·4 m/s is very high for oil in a pipe, but the arithmetic follows from the given data.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> "Nm/N" is energy per unit weight, i.e. metres of the flowing fluid (here oil).</p>` },

{ id: "2021-70", year: 2021, no: 70, subject: "fm", topic: "Derivation of Bernoulli's equation",
q: `Bernoulli's equation is obtained by`,
opts: ["integrating the Euler's equation of motion.", "differentiating the Euler's equation of motion.", "differentiating the Navier-Stokes equations.", "integrating energy equation."], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) integrating the Euler's equation of motion</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Euler's equation along a streamline for steady, inviscid flow is dp/ρ + g dz + V dV = 0. Integrating it for incompressible flow gives Bernoulli's equation p/ρ + gz + V²/2 = constant.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Differentiating does not lead to an energy-type integral; Euler's equation is the inviscid limit of Navier–Stokes.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Differentiating does not lead to an energy-type integral; Euler's equation is the inviscid limit of Navier–Stokes.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The steady-flow energy equation reduces to a Bernoulli-like form under special conditions, but the standard derivation is integration of Euler's equation.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Assumptions carried over: steady, incompressible, inviscid flow along a streamline.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-71", year: 2021, no: 71, subject: "fm", topic: "Controlling boundary-layer separation", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Which one of the following is <b>not</b> the methodology of control separation of flow from boundary in the application of aerofoils?`,
opts: ["Streamlining of blunt body shapes", "Fluid ejection from the boundary layer", "Suction of fluid from the boundary layer", "Creating a motion of the boundary wall"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Fluid ejection from the boundary layer</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Methods for preventing separation (Bansal, Modi–Seth): accelerating the fluid in the boundary layer, sucking the slow fluid out of the boundary layer, supplying additional energy by blowing (injecting high-energy fluid into the layer), slotted wings, rotating the boundary in the direction of flow, gentle diffuser angles, and streamlining the body shape.</p>
<p><b>Option by option</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Streamlining blunt shapes reduces adverse pressure gradients: a standard method.</li><li><b>(b)</b> "Ejection of fluid from the boundary layer" is not one of the listed methods (it is suction, or injection <i>into</i> the layer, that is used).</li><li><b>(c)</b> Suction removes the low-momentum fluid: a method.</li><li><b>(d)</b> A moving wall (rotating cylinder, Magnus effect) re-energises the layer: a method.</li></ul>
<p>Our first attempt chose (a), thinking streamlining is shape design rather than control. Standard lists include streamlining, so the key's (b) is right.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Vortex generators on aircraft wings mix high-energy outer flow into the boundary layer, delaying separation.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-72", year: 2021, no: 72, subject: "thermo", topic: "Mass of air in a room",
q: `What is the value of mass of the air in a room of size 4 m × 5 m × 6 m at 100 kPa and 25 °C? (Take R = 0·287 kPa.m³.kg<sup>−1</sup>.K<sup>−1</sup>)`,
opts: ["150 kg", "180 kg", "140 kg", "130 kg"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 140 kg</h3><div class="formula">m = p V/(R T)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>p = 100 kPa; V = 4 × 5 × 6 = 120 m³; R = 0·287 kPa m³/kg K; T = 298 K</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> m = 100 × 120/(0·287 × 298) = 12,000/85·53 = <b>140·3 kg</b>.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>V = 4 × 5 × 6 = 120 m³, T = 298 K.</li><li>m = 100 × 120/(0.287 × 298) = <b>140 kg</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 150 uses 25 °C as 273 K, or too large a volume.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 180 kg uses 25 °C as 273 K, or too large a volume.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 130 kg uses a higher temperature.</li></ul>
<p>Specific volume v = RT/p = 0·287 × 298/100 = 0·855 m³/kg; dividing the room volume 120 m³ by this gives the same 140 kg. Air is treated as an ideal gas, which is excellent at room conditions.</p><p>Common slips: using T in °C (25) instead of kelvin gives an absurd 1672 kg; using R = 287 J/kg K with p in kPa mixes units. Keep p in kPa with R in kJ/kg K, or p in Pa with R in J/kg K.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Air density here = 1·17 kg/m³.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-73", year: 2021, no: 73, subject: "mech", topic: "Coefficient of friction",
q: `A body of weight 100 N is placed on a rough horizontal plane. What is the coefficient of friction if a horizontal force of 60 N just causes the body to slide over the horizontal plane?`,
opts: ["0·4", "0·5", "0·6", "0·9"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 0·6</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> At impending motion the applied force equals limiting friction μN, and on a horizontal plane N = W.</p>
<div class="formula">μ = F/N = 60/100 = 0·6</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>N = W = 100 N.</li><li>μ = 60/100 = <b>0.6</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0·4 does not equal 60/100.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 0·5 does not equal 60/100.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0·9 does not equal 60/100.</li></ul>
<p>Free-body check: vertically N = W = 100 N (the applied force is horizontal, so it does not change N). Horizontally, P = F<sub>limiting</sub> = μN. If the force were applied at an angle θ above horizontal, N would reduce to W − P sin θ.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Angle of friction = tan<sup>−1</sup> 0·6 = 31°; on a slope steeper than this, the body slides by itself.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-74", year: 2021, no: 74, subject: "mech", topic: "Uniform acceleration",
q: `A body is moving with a velocity of 2 m/s. After 4 seconds, the velocity of the body becomes 5 m/s. The acceleration of the body is`,
opts: ["0·55 m/s²", "0·65 m/s²", "0·75 m/s²", "0·45 m/s²"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 0·75 m/s²</h3><div class="formula">a = (v − u)/t = (5 − 2)/4 = 0·75 m/s²</div>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Distance covered in the 4 s = (u + v)t/2 = 3·5 × 4 = 14 m.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>a = (5 − 2)/4.</li><li>= <b>0.75 m/s²</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0·55 does not match (5 − 2)/4.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 0·65 does not match (5 − 2)/4.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0·45 m/s² does not match (5 − 2)/4.</li></ul>
<p>Equations of motion for constant acceleration: v = u + at; s = ut + ½at²; v² = u² + 2as. Check: s = 2 × 4 + ½ × 0·75 × 16 = 8 + 6 = 14 m, and v² = 4 + 2 × 0·75 × 14 = 25, so v = 5 m/s. ✓</p><p>Graphically, on a velocity–time graph the acceleration is the slope (3/4) and the distance is the area of the trapezium under the line between t = 0 and t = 4 s.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Constant acceleration is assumed; otherwise 0·75 m/s² is the average acceleration.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-75", year: 2021, no: 75, subject: "mech", topic: "Volumetric strain from principal stresses",
q: `The principal stresses at a point in an elastic material are 60 N/mm² tensile, 20 N/mm² tensile and 50 N/mm² compressive. What is the volumetric strain by considering Young's Modulus as 100 × 10³ N/mm² and μ = 0·3?`,
opts: ["1·20 × 10<sup>−4</sup>", "1·06 × 10<sup>−5</sup>", "1·30 × 10<sup>−3</sup>", "1·12 × 10<sup>−2</sup>"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1·20 × 10<sup>−4</sup></h3><div class="formula">ε<sub>v</sub> = (1 − 2μ)(σ<sub>1</sub> + σ<sub>2</sub> + σ<sub>3</sub>)/E</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>σ<sub>1</sub> = 60, σ<sub>2</sub> = 20, σ<sub>3</sub> = −50 N/mm²; μ = 0·3; E = 10⁵ N/mm²</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> ε<sub>v</sub> = 0·4 × 30/10⁵ = <b>1·2 × 10<sup>−4</sup></b>.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> differs by powers of ten, or add the stresses' magnitudes ignoring the compressive sign (130 × 0·4/10⁵ = 5·2 × 10<sup>−4</sup>).</li><li><b>(c)</b> differs by powers of ten, or add the stresses' magnitudes ignoring the compressive sign (130 × 0·4/10⁵ = 5·2 × 10<sup>−4</sup>).</li><li><b>(d)</b> differs by powers of ten, or add the stresses' magnitudes ignoring the compressive sign (130 × 0·4/10⁵ = 5·2 × 10<sup>−4</sup>).</li></ul>
<p>Individual strains: ε<sub>1</sub> = [60 − 0·3(20 − 50)]/10⁵ = 6·9 × 10<sup>−4</sup>; ε<sub>2</sub> = [20 − 0·3(60 − 50)]/10⁵ = 1·7 × 10<sup>−4</sup>; ε<sub>3</sub> = [−50 − 0·3(80)]/10⁵ = −7·4 × 10<sup>−4</sup>. Sum = 1·2 × 10<sup>−4</sup>. ✓ Bulk modulus K = E/[3(1 − 2μ)] = 83·3 GPa.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Volumetric strain depends only on the mean (hydrostatic) stress; pure shear causes no volume change.</p>` }
);

// ---- q2021d.js ----
// ESE 2021 Prelims Paper II, Mechanical Engineering, Series A, Q76–100
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2021-76", year: 2021, no: 76, subject: "mech", topic: "Pulley system: thrust of the man on the ground",
q: `A man whose weight is 650 N, standing on the ground, raises a load of 3000 N by means of single string system of pulleys. There are six light pulleys in each block. The thrust of the man on the ground is`,
opts: ["120 N", "135 N", "150 N", "175 N"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 150 N</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The man pulls down on the string with the effort P; the string pulls him up with the same force, so his thrust on the ground is his weight minus the effort.</p>
<div class="formula">Ideal pulley system: P = W/n (n = number of string segments supporting the load) &nbsp;&nbsp; Thrust = W<sub>man</sub> − P</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>W = 3000 N (load); W<sub>man</sub> = 650 N; pulleys light and frictionless</li></ul>
<p><b>Working (as intended by the options)</b></p><ol class="work"><li>The intended velocity ratio is 6 (the load hangs from six string segments).</li><li>Effort P = 3000/6 = 500 N.</li><li>Thrust on the ground = 650 − 500 = <b>150 N</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 120 does not correspond to an integral velocity ratio of the pulley system.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 135 does not correspond to an integral velocity ratio of the pulley system.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 175 N does not correspond to an integral velocity ratio of the pulley system.</li></ul>
<div class="note">Data note: read literally, six pulleys in <i>each</i> block of a single-string system give 12 supporting segments, an effort of 250 N and a thrust of 400 N, which is not offered. The options are built on a velocity ratio of 6; our answer (c) agrees with the official key.</div>
<p>If the man's weight were less than the required effort he could not raise the load at all: he would be lifted off the ground instead. The thrust is always W<sub>man</sub> − P when he pulls vertically downwards.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Rope-and-pulley problems: write equilibrium for the load (n T = W) and for the man (N + T = W<sub>man</sub>) separately.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-77", year: 2021, no: 77, subject: "mech", topic: "Uniform retardation: two roots of time",
q: `A particle starts with an initial velocity of 200 cm/s and moves with a uniform retardation of 10 cm/s². If it describes 1500 cm in time t, what is/are the possible value(s) of t?`,
opts: ["10 sec only", "10 sec and 30 sec", "30 sec only", "5 sec and 10 sec"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 10 sec and 30 sec</h3><div class="formula">s = u t − ½ a t²</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>u = 200 cm/s; a = 10 cm/s² (retardation); s = 1500 cm (displacement)</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>1500 = 200t − 5t² → t² − 40t + 300 = 0.</li><li>(t − 10)(t − 30) = 0 → t = 10 s or 30 s.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Physical meaning: the particle comes to rest at t = u/a = 20 s, after travelling 2000 cm. It passes the 1500 cm mark going forward at t = 10 s, then reverses (the retardation continues to act) and is back at 1500 cm at t = 30 s.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> gives only one root; both are valid because the uniform retardation reverses the motion.</li><li><b>(c)</b> gives only one root; both are valid because the uniform retardation reverses the motion.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 5 s gives s = 875 cm, not 1500 cm.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> If the question asked for the total <i>distance</i> 1500 cm (not displacement), only t = 10 s would apply.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-78", year: 2021, no: 78, subject: "mech", topic: "Systems of forces: definitions and theorems",
q: `Consider the following statements for system of forces :<ol><li>Two or more forces that act at the same point are called coplanar forces.</li><li>Two or more forces whose directed arrows lie in same plane are called concurrent forces.</li><li>Varignon's theorem states that 'the moment of several concurrent coplanar forces about any point O in their plane equals the moment of their resultant about the point O'.</li><li>Lami's theorem states that if a body is in equilibrium under the action of three forces, each force is proportional to the sine of angle between the other forces.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 4 only", "1 and 4 only", "3 and 4 only", "1, 2 and 3 only"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 3 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (wrong):</b> Forces acting at the same point are <i>concurrent</i> forces.</li><li><b>2 (wrong):</b> Forces whose lines of action lie in one plane are <i>coplanar</i> forces. Statements 1 and 2 have the two definitions swapped.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> This is Varignon's principle of moments: ΣM<sub>O</sub>(F<sub>i</sub>) = M<sub>O</sub>(R).</li><li><b>4 (correct):</b> Lami's theorem: for three concurrent forces in equilibrium, P/sin α = Q/sin β = R/sin γ, where each angle is the one between the other two forces.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Lami's theorem requires the three forces to be coplanar and concurrent; any three non-parallel forces in equilibrium must in fact be concurrent.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, 2, which are incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 3, 4.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 1, 2, which are incorrect; Omits 4, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Varignon's theorem holds for any coplanar system with a single resultant force, not only concurrent ones.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-79", year: 2021, no: 79, subject: "mech", topic: "Shear stress, shear strain and modulus of rigidity",
q: `Consider the following statements related to stress and strain :<ol><li>Shear stress is always tangential to the area over which it acts.</li><li>Shear stresses on the transverse pair of faces are known as complimentary shear stresses.</li><li>Shear strain is defined as the change in the right angle of the element measured in radians.</li><li>Modulus of rigidity is the ratio of shear strain to shear stress.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1, 3 and 4 only", "2 and 4 only", "3 and 4 only", "1, 2 and 3 only"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> Shear stress acts parallel (tangential) to the surface; normal stress acts perpendicular.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> Equilibrium of an element requires equal shear stresses on the perpendicular pair of faces: these are the complementary shear stresses.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> Shear strain γ is the change in the originally right angle, in radians.</li><li><b>4 (wrong):</b> Modulus of rigidity G = shear stress/shear strain (τ/γ), not the inverse.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Relation between elastic constants: E = 2G(1 + μ), so for steel (E = 200 GPa, μ = 0·3) G ≈ 77 GPa.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 4, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 4, which is incorrect; Omits 1, 3, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 4, which is incorrect; Omits 1, 2, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> All elastic moduli are stress/strain, so they carry the units of stress.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-80", year: 2021, no: 80, subject: "mech", topic: "Mohr's circle, Poisson's ratio and volumetric strain",
q: `Consider the following statements for stress and strain analysis :<ol><li>The stress components on any inclined plane can easily be found with the help of a geometrical construction known as Mohr's stress circle.</li><li>The ratio of longitudinal strain to lateral strain is known as Poisson's ratio.</li><li>When a body is acted upon by three mutually perpendicular forces, there is change in the volume of the body which is referred to as dilation of the material.</li><li>The ratio of original volume to increase in volume is known as volumetric strain.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 3 only", "2 and 4 only", "3 and 4 only", "1, 2, 3 and 4"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> Mohr's circle gives the normal and shear stress on any plane graphically.</li><li><b>2 (wrong):</b> Poisson's ratio = lateral strain/longitudinal strain (μ ≤ 0·5); the statement has it inverted.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> Under triaxial loading, the change in volume per unit volume is called dilatation.</li><li><b>4 (wrong):</b> Volumetric strain = increase in volume/original volume (δV/V), again inverted.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> For triaxial stresses, ε<sub>v</sub> = ε<sub>x</sub> + ε<sub>y</sub> + ε<sub>z</sub> = (1 − 2μ)(σ<sub>x</sub> + σ<sub>y</sub> + σ<sub>z</sub>)/E. With μ = 0·5 (rubber-like, incompressible) there is no volume change.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 3.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, 4, which are incorrect; Omits 1, 3, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 4, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 2, 4, which are incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Both wrong statements here are "ratio inverted" traps; read the order of the ratio carefully.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-81", year: 2021, no: 81, subject: "mech", topic: "Normal stress on an inclined plane",
q: `The stresses on two perpendicular planes through a point in a body are 160 MPa and 100 MPa, both compressive, along with a shear stress of 80 MPa. What is the normal stress on a plane inclined at 30° to the plane of 160 MPa stress?`,
opts: ["−42·4 MPa", "−75·7 MPa", "−59·1 MPa", "−86·3 MPa"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) −75·7 MPa</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Normal stress on an inclined plane.</p><div class="formula">σ<sub>n</sub> = (σ<sub>x</sub> + σ<sub>y</sub>)/2 + [(σ<sub>x</sub> − σ<sub>y</sub>)/2] cos 2θ + τ sin 2θ</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>σ<sub>x</sub> = −160 MPa; σ<sub>y</sub> = −100 MPa (compressive negative); τ = 80 MPa; θ = 30° (angle from the plane of σ<sub>x</sub>)</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>(σ<sub>x</sub> + σ<sub>y</sub>)/2 = −130 MPa; (σ<sub>x</sub> − σ<sub>y</sub>)/2 = −30 MPa.</li><li>cos 60° = 0·5; sin 60° = 0·866.</li><li>σ<sub>n</sub> = −130 − 15 + 69·3 = <b>−75·7 MPa</b> (compressive).</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> −42·4 MPa uses wrong signs or the wrong angle (θ measured from the other plane).</li><li><b>(c)</b> −59·1 MPa uses wrong signs or the wrong angle (θ measured from the other plane).</li><li><b>(d)</b> −86·3 MPa takes the shear term with the opposite sign (−130 − 15 + … ).</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Principal stresses here: −130 ± √(30² + 80²) = −130 ± 85·4 → −44·6 and −215·4 MPa.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-82", year: 2021, no: 82, subject: "mech", topic: "Types of beams",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding types of supports and beams :<ol><li>When both supports of beams are roller supports, the beam is known as simply supported beam.</li><li>A beam with one end fixed and the other end free is known as fixed beam.</li><li>A beam with both ends fixed is known as cantilever beam.</li><li>A beam with one end fixed and the other simply supported is known as propped cantilever.</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 only", "1 and 4 only", "1, 3 and 4 only", "2, 3 and 4 only"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> A beam resting freely on supports at both ends (rollers or knife edges) is a simply supported beam.</li><li><b>2 (wrong):</b> One end fixed, other free is a <i>cantilever</i>.</li><li><b>3 (wrong):</b> Both ends fixed is a <i>fixed</i> (built-in) beam. Statements 2 and 3 have the names swapped.</li><li><b>4 (correct):</b> One end fixed and the other simply supported is a propped cantilever (statically indeterminate to one degree).</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Simply supported beams and cantilevers are statically determinate (3 reactions); fixed beams have 6 reactions in general (3 redundant).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 4, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 4.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 2, 3, which are incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Strictly, a beam on two rollers alone would be unstable under horizontal loads; in practice one support is a hinge.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-83", year: 2021, no: 83, subject: "mech", topic: "Pure bending: definitions",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding stress in beam :<ol><li>If a member is subjected to equal and opposite couples acting in the same longitudinal planes, the member is said to be in pure bending.</li><li>The internal stresses developed in the beam are known as flexural stresses.</li><li>There is an intermediate surface known as neutral surface, at which the stress is zero.</li><li>An axis obtained by intersection of the neutral surface and a cross-section is known as neutral axis.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["2 and 3 only", "1 and 4 only", "3 and 4 only", "1, 2, 3 and 4"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2, 3 and 4</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> Equal and opposite end couples in the same longitudinal plane give a constant bending moment with zero shear force: pure (simple) bending.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> Bending stresses are also called flexural stresses.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> Fibres on one side are stretched and on the other compressed; the surface in between that is neither (zero bending stress) is the neutral surface.</li><li><b>4 (correct):</b> The intersection of the neutral surface with a cross-section is the neutral axis, about which the section bends.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Flexure formula: M/I = σ/y = E/R. Bending stress varies linearly from zero at the neutral axis to a maximum at the extreme fibres.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 1, 4, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 2, 3, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 1, 2, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3, 4.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Between the two loads of a symmetric four-point bending test, the beam is in pure bending.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-84", year: 2021, no: 84, subject: "mech", topic: "Elastic and plastic bending: neutral axis",
q: `Consider the following statements for the symmetric beam under pure bending :<ol><li>In the elastic range, the normal stress varies linearly with the distance from the neutral surface.</li><li>As long as the stresses remain in the elastic range, the neutral axis passes through the centroid of the section.</li><li>If stresses are in the plastic range, the neutral axis passes through the centroid of the section.</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> In the elastic range σ = My/I, linear in y.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> Zero net axial force (∫σ dA = 0 with σ ∝ y) means ∫y dA = 0: the neutral axis passes through the centroid.</li><li><b>3 (wrong):</b> In the fully plastic state, the stress is ±σ<sub>y</sub> everywhere, so zero net force requires equal areas in tension and compression. The neutral axis is the <i>equal-area axis</i>, which coincides with the centroidal axis only when the section is symmetric about the bending axis (rectangle, I-section), not for T-sections and the like.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The ratio of plastic moment to yield moment is the shape factor: 1·5 for a rectangle, about 1·7 for a solid circle and 1·12–1·15 for I-sections.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> For a T-section the neutral axis shifts from the centroid towards the flange as the section yields.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-85", year: 2021, no: 85, subject: "materials", topic: "Volume of the FCC unit cell",
q: `The volume of FCC unit cell in terms of the atomic radius R is`,
opts: ["V<sub>c</sub> = 16R³√3", "V<sub>c</sub> = 8R³√2", "V<sub>c</sub> = 16R³√2", "V<sub>c</sub> = 8R³√3"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) V<sub>c</sub> = 16R³√2</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In FCC, atoms touch along the face diagonal: face diagonal = 4R = a√2, so a = 2√2 R.</p>
<div class="formula">V<sub>c</sub> = a³ = (2√2 R)³ = 16√2 R³</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>a = 2√2R.</li><li>V = (2√2R)³ = <b>16√2 R³</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Uses √3 (BCC relation) instead of √2.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 8R³√3 mixes the BCC factor.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 8R³√2 halves the correct (2√2R)³.</li></ul>
<p>Atomic packing factor check: APF = 4 × (4/3)πR³/(16√2 R³) = 0·74, the maximum for equal spheres.</p>
<p>Numerically, for copper (R = 0·128 nm): a = 2√2 × 0·128 = 0·362 nm and V<sub>c</sub> = 0·0474 nm³, which with 4 atoms per cell gives a density of about 8·9 g/cm³, matching copper.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> FCC: 4 atoms per cell, coordination number 12, APF 0·74 (Cu, Al, Ni, γ-Fe).</p>` },

{ id: "2021-86", year: 2021, no: 86, subject: "materials", topic: "Alloying elements: hardenability and carbides", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Which one of the following alloying ingredients increases the hardenability and forms carbides for wear resistance?`,
opts: ["Chromium", "Molybdenum", "Nickel", "Manganese"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Molybdenum</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Molybdenum strongly increases hardenability (it is one of the most effective elements per unit addition) and forms hard, stable carbides (Mo<sub>2</sub>C) that improve wear resistance and high-temperature strength. It also reduces temper embrittlement. Textbook tables of alloying elements (for example in Callister and in machine-design texts) list exactly this combination for Mo.</p>
<p><b>Option by option</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Chromium also raises hardenability and forms carbides, but is listed chiefly for corrosion and oxidation resistance (stainless steels need ≥ 12% Cr).</li><li><b>(b)</b> Molybdenum: hardenability + carbides for wear (the key's answer).</li><li><b>(c)</b> Nickel is a non-carbide former; it raises toughness and strength in solid solution and stabilises austenite.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Manganese increases hardenability and combines with sulphur (MnS), but its carbides are weak.</li></ul>
<p>Our first attempt chose (a) Chromium, the more familiar carbide former. Verified against standard alloying-element tables, the answer listed for this exact wording is molybdenum, so we corrected it.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> High-speed steels (M-series) rely on Mo and W carbides for hot hardness and wear resistance.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-87", year: 2021, no: 87, subject: "materials", topic: "Stable arrangement of atoms in ionic crystals",
q: `Which one of the following related to the most stable arrangement of atoms in a crystal is <b>not</b> correct?`,
opts: ["Preserves electrical neutrality", "Maximizes strong ion-ion repulsion", "Satisfies discreteness of all covalent bonds", "Packs the atoms as closely as possible"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Maximizes strong ion-ion repulsion</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The most stable arrangement is the one of lowest energy. It (i) keeps the crystal electrically neutral, (ii) satisfies the directional, discrete nature of covalent bonds, (iii) <i>minimises</i> strong ion–ion repulsion (like ions kept apart, unlike ions as neighbours) and (iv) packs atoms as closely as possible.</p>
<p><b>Option by option</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Electrical neutrality: required.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Maximising repulsion would raise the energy; the correct rule is to <i>minimise</i> it. This is the incorrect statement.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Covalent bonds have fixed number and direction, which the structure must respect.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Close packing lowers energy for non-directional bonding.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> These four criteria (from Callister/Askeland) explain why NaCl has a coordination number of 6 rather than 12.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-88", year: 2021, no: 88, subject: "materials", topic: "Dielectric constant of rubber",
q: `The dielectric constant of rubber varies between`,
opts: ["0·5 and 1·0", "1·0 and 1·5", "1·5 and 2·0", "2·5 and 5·0"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 2·5 and 5·0</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Relative permittivity (dielectric constant) of natural and synthetic rubbers is typically about 2·5 to 5 (natural rubber about 2·4–3, neoprene and loaded rubbers up to 5–7).</p><div class="formula">ε_r = C_material/C_vacuum</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Rubber ε_r ≈ <b>2.5–5</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0·5 to 1·0 is impossible: the dielectric constant of any material is at least 1 (vacuum = 1).</li><li><b>(b)</b> 1·0 to 1·5 is typical of gases and foams.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 1·5 to 2·0 is too low even for PTFE (about 2·1).</li></ul>
<p>For comparison: polyethylene 2·3, PVC 3–4, glass 5–10, mica 6–8, water 80. Rubber's moderate dielectric constant and high dielectric strength make it a good cable insulator.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> ε<sub>r</sub> = C (with dielectric)/C (vacuum); it is never less than 1.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-89", year: 2021, no: 89, subject: "materials", topic: "Ductile fracture",
q: `Consider the following statements for ductile fracture :<ol><li>The material undergoes substantial plastic deformation with high energy absorption before fracture.</li><li>Presence of cracks on the surface of material initiates this type of failure.</li><li>Fracture occurs due to necking.</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 only", "1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> Ductile fracture is preceded by large plastic deformation and absorbs much energy (high toughness).</li><li><b>2 (wrong):</b> Pre-existing surface cracks initiating sudden failure describe <i>brittle</i> fracture (Griffith). In ductile fracture, internal microvoids nucleate at inclusions in the necked region and coalesce.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> Ductile fracture follows necking, ending in the familiar cup-and-cone fracture.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Sequence in a tensile test: necking → microvoid formation → void coalescence into a central crack → shear at 45° around the rim (cup and cone). The fracture surface is dull and fibrous; brittle fracture surfaces are bright and granular (cleavage).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Ductile fracture gives warning (visible deformation), which is why designers prefer ductile materials.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-90", year: 2021, no: 90, subject: "materials", topic: "TTT diagram",
q: `The TTT diagram shows the times required for isothermal transition from`,
opts: ["austenite to pearlite.", "austenite to ferrite.", "ferrite to pearlite.", "martensite to pearlite."], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) austenite to pearlite</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The time–temperature–transformation (isothermal transformation) diagram plots, for each holding temperature below A<sub>1</sub>, the time to start and finish the decomposition of austenite: to pearlite at higher temperatures and to bainite lower down, with martensite start/finish lines below.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Austenite to ferrite alone is shown only as a proeutectoid region in hypo-eutectoid steels; it is not what the diagram is defined by.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Ferrite does not transform into pearlite; pearlite forms from austenite.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Martensite does not turn into pearlite on isothermal holding; on tempering it decomposes into tempered martensite (ferrite + fine carbides).</li></ul>
<p>The "nose" of the C-curve (about 540 °C for eutectoid steel, under a second) sets the critical cooling rate needed to form martensite.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> For continuous cooling, the CCT diagram is used; its curves are shifted to lower temperatures and longer times.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-91", year: 2021, no: 91, subject: "materials", topic: "BCC lattice constant",
q: `Iron at 20 °C is BCC with atoms of atomic radius 0·124 nm. What is the lattice constant 'a' for the cube edge of the iron unit cell?`,
opts: ["0·2864 nm", "0·1496 nm", "0·2173 nm", "0·1756 nm"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 0·2864 nm</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In BCC, atoms touch along the body diagonal: body diagonal = 4R = a√3.</p>
<div class="formula">a = 4R/√3 = 4 × 0·124/1·732 = 0·2864 nm</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>a = 4 × 0.124/1.732.</li><li>a = <b>0.2864 nm</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 0·1496 nm is smaller than 2R = 0·248 nm, impossible for any cubic cell.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0·1756 nm is smaller than 2R = 0·248 nm, impossible for any cubic cell.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0·2173 nm is also less than 2R; the FCC formula 2√2R would give 0·3507 nm.</li></ul>
<p>Density check: ρ = 2 × 55·85/(6·022 × 10<sup>23</sup> × (0·2864 × 10<sup>−7</sup> cm)³) = 7·88 g/cm³, matching iron.</p>
<p>Why the body diagonal: in BCC the corner atoms touch the central atom, not each other along the cube edge. So the closest-packed direction is ⟨111⟩, with a body-diagonal length of a√3 holding the diameters of two half-corner atoms plus the central atom, i.e. 4R.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> BCC: 2 atoms per cell, coordination number 8, APF 0·68 (α-Fe, Cr, W, Mo).</p>` },

{ id: "2021-92", year: 2021, no: 92, subject: "materials", topic: "Interplanar spacing in a cubic crystal",
q: `Copper has the FCC crystal structure and a unit cell with a lattice constant of 0·361 nm. What is the interplanar spacing 'd<sub>220</sub>'?`,
opts: ["0·085 nm", "0·174 nm", "0·206 nm", "0·128 nm"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 0·128 nm</h3><div class="formula">Cubic crystals: d<sub>hkl</sub> = a/√(h² + k² + l²)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>a = 0·361 nm; (h k l) = (2 2 0) → h² + k² + l² = 8</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> d<sub>220</sub> = 0·361/√8 = 0·361/2·828 = <b>0·1276 nm</b>.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>h² + k² + l² = 8.</li><li>d = 0.361/2.828 = <b>0.128 nm</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(c)</b> 0·206 nm ≈ d<sub>111</sub> = 0·361/√3 = 0·208 nm.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 0·174 nm ≈ d<sub>200</sub> = 0·361/2 = 0·1805 nm (close, but not exact).</li><li><b>(a)</b> 0·085 nm is too small (about a/√18).</li></ul>
<p>In FCC, only planes with h, k, l all even or all odd give X-ray reflections: (111), (200), (220), (311)… so (220) is an allowed reflection.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Bragg's law nλ = 2d sin θ then gives the diffraction angle for the plane.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-93", year: 2021, no: 93, subject: "materials", topic: "Lever rule: determining phase amounts",
q: `Which one of the following is <b>not</b> a step of lever rule of determination of phase amounts?`,
opts: ["A tie line is constructed across the two-phase region at the temperature of the alloy.", "The overall alloy composition is located on the tie line.", "Perpendiculars are dropped from these intersections to the horizontal composition axis, from which the composition of each of the respective phases is read.", "The fraction of one-phase is computed by taking the length of tie line from the overall alloy composition to the phase boundary for the other phase and dividing by the total tie line length."], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Callister gives two separate procedures for a point in a two-phase region: one for the <i>compositions</i> of the phases and one (the lever rule) for their <i>amounts</i>. Dropping perpendiculars from the tie-line intersections to read compositions belongs to the composition procedure, not the lever rule.</p>
<p><b>Option by option</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Constructing the tie line at the alloy temperature: the first step of the lever rule.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Locating the overall composition C<sub>0</sub> on the tie line: second step.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Reading the phase compositions: a step of determining phase compositions, not amounts. Not a lever-rule step.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Fraction of a phase = opposite arm/total length, e.g. W<sub>L</sub> = (C<sub>α</sub> − C<sub>0</sub>)/(C<sub>α</sub> − C<sub>L</sub>): the lever rule itself.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> "Opposite arm" is the key idea: the fraction of a phase is the length of the tie-line segment on the far side of C<sub>0</sub>.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-94", year: 2021, no: 94, subject: "materials", topic: "Polymers: thermoplastics, polymerisation and copolymers",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding polymeric materials :<ol><li>A plastic material that requires heat to make it formable (plastic) and upon cooling, retains its shape is known as thermosetting plastic.</li><li>The chemical reaction in which high molecular mass molecules are formed from two or more monomers is called chain polymerization.</li><li>A polymer chain consisting of two or more types of monomeric units is called copolymer.</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "2 only", "3 only"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 3 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (wrong):</b> A material softened by heat, shaped, and keeping its shape on cooling (repeatably) is a <i>thermoplastic</i>. Thermosetting plastics cure irreversibly by cross-linking and cannot be re-softened.</li><li><b>2 (wrong):</b> Forming large molecules by reaction of two (or more) different monomers, usually with elimination of a by-product such as water, is <i>condensation</i> (step) polymerisation. Chain (addition) polymerisation is the repeated addition of monomer units, typically of one kind, to a growing active chain with no by-product.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> A chain made of two or more different repeat units is a copolymer (random, alternating, block or graft).</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Examples: polyethylene and PVC (addition, thermoplastic); nylon 6,6 and polyesters (condensation); Bakelite and epoxies (thermosets); ABS and SBR rubber (copolymers).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, 2, which are incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Thermoplastics are recyclable by remelting; thermosets are not.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-95", year: 2021, no: 95, subject: "materials", topic: "Soft and ductile microconstituent",
q: `As per mechanical properties, which one of the following microconstituents is soft and ductile?`,
opts: ["Bainite", "Martensite", "Spheroidite", "Tempered martensite"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Spheroidite</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Spheroidite (globular Fe<sub>3</sub>C in a continuous ferrite matrix), produced by holding just below A<sub>1</sub> for many hours, has the least ferrite–cementite boundary area per unit volume, so dislocations move easily. It is the softest and most ductile microstructure of a given steel; it is used to improve machinability and cold formability.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Bainite (fine needles of ferrite with carbide) is harder and stronger than pearlite.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Martensite is the hardest and most brittle constituent.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Tempered martensite is strong and tough, but much harder than spheroidite.</li></ul>
<p>Order of hardness (eutectoid steel): martensite &gt; tempered martensite &gt; bainite &gt; fine pearlite &gt; coarse pearlite &gt; spheroidite.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Spheroidise annealing is applied to high-carbon tool and bearing steels before machining.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-96", year: 2021, no: 96, subject: "materials", topic: "Isostatic pressing of ceramics",
q: `The simultaneous compaction and shaping of a ceramic powder (and binder) by pressure applied uniformly in all directions is known as`,
opts: ["Glaze pressing", "Porcelain pressing", "Slip pressing", "Isostatic pressing"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Isostatic pressing</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In isostatic pressing, the powder is sealed in a flexible (rubber) mould and pressure is applied through a fluid, equally from all directions. This gives uniform density with no die-wall friction. Hot isostatic pressing (HIP) combines pressing with sintering.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Glazing is applying a glassy coating, not a pressing method.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Porcelain is a type of whiteware ceramic, not a forming process.</li><li><b>(c)</b> "Slip" refers to slip casting: a suspension poured into a porous plaster mould; no pressure is applied.</li></ul>
<p>Callister lists three powder-pressing methods: uniaxial (die pressing), isostatic and hot pressing.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Isostatic pressing is used for spark-plug insulators and complex refractory shapes.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-97", year: 2021, no: 97, subject: "tom", topic: "Punching press: energy from the motor during punching",
q: `An electric motor drives a punching press. A flywheel fitted to the press has a radius of gyration of 0·5 m and runs at 250 rpm. The press is capable of punching 800 holes per hour with each punching operation taking 1·5 seconds and requiring 12,000 N-m of work. The energy delivered by the motor during punching operation is`,
opts: ["2000 N-m", "3000 N-m", "4000 N-m", "5000 N-m"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 4000 N-m</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The motor supplies energy at a constant rate over the whole cycle; the flywheel stores it between punches and gives it up during the punch.</p><div class="formula">Motor power = energy per cycle/cycle time</div><div class="formula">E_motor during punch = P × t_punch</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Time per cycle = 3600/800 = 4·5 s.</li><li>Motor power = energy per cycle/time per cycle = 12,000/4·5 = 2667 W.</li><li>Energy from the motor during the 1·5 s punching = 2667 × 1·5 = <b>4000 N m</b>.</li><li>The flywheel must supply the rest: 12,000 − 4000 = 8000 N m (this sets its size via ΔE = I ω² C<sub>s</sub>).</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 2000 ms use wrong cycle times.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 3000 ms use wrong cycle times.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 5000 N ms use wrong cycle times.</li></ul>
<p>The radius of gyration and speed are needed only for the next step (flywheel mass), not for this question.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Energy from motor during punching = E × (t<sub>punch</sub>/t<sub>cycle</sub>) = 12,000 × 1·5/4·5.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-98", year: 2021, no: 98, subject: "tom", topic: "Degrees of freedom from links and loops",
q: `A linkage has 11 links and 4 loops. What is the degree of freedom if it has only single turning pairs?`,
opts: ["0", "1", "2", "3"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 2</h3><div class="formula">Euler's relation for a linkage: j = n + L − 1 &nbsp;&nbsp; Grübler: F = 3(n − 1) − 2j</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>n = 11 links; L = 4 independent loops; j = number of single (binary) turning pairs</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>j = 11 + 4 − 1 = 14.</li><li>F = 3(11 − 1) − 2 × 14 = 30 − 28 = <b>2</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0 would be a structure; follows from j = n + L (or n + L − 2), which misapplies the loop relation.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 1 follows from j = n + L (or n + L − 2), which misapplies the loop relation.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 3 follows from j = n + L (or n + L − 2), which misapplies the loop relation.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Check with a four-bar chain: n = 4, L = 1 → j = 4, F = 9 − 8 = 1. ✓</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Equivalent form: F = n − 2L − 1 for planar linkages with simple turning pairs: 11 − 8 − 1 = 2.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-99", year: 2021, no: 99, subject: "tom", topic: "Inversions of the single-slider crank chain",
q: `Which one of the following is the application of first inversion of single-slider-crank chain?`,
opts: ["Hand-pump", "Reciprocating engine", "Elliptical trammel", "Whitworth quick-return mechanism"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Reciprocating engine</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In the first inversion the cylinder (frame, link 1) is fixed; the crank rotates and the piston slides. This is the reciprocating engine and reciprocating compressor.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Hand pump (pendulum pump): fourth inversion, with the slider fixed.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Elliptical trammel: an inversion of the <i>double</i>-slider crank chain.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Whitworth quick-return: second inversion, with the crank fixed.</li></ul>
<p>Third inversion (connecting rod fixed): oscillating-cylinder engine and the crank-and-slotted-lever quick-return mechanism (shaper). Gnome rotary engine: second inversion.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Mnemonic for which link is fixed: 1 frame (engine), 2 crank (Whitworth, Gnome), 3 connecting rod (slotted lever, oscillating cylinder), 4 slider (hand pump).</p>` },

{ id: "2021-100", year: 2021, no: 100, subject: "tom", topic: "Types of cams",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding cams :<ol><li>A cam in which the follower moves radially from the centre of rotation of the cam is known as a disc cam.</li><li>A globoidal cam is a double-disc cam, the two discs being keyed together and are in constant touch with the two rollers of a follower.</li><li>A conjugate cam can have two types of surfaces, convex or concave.</li><li>In a spherical cam, the follower oscillates about an axis perpendicular to the axis of rotation of the cam.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 4 only", "2 and 4 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2, 3 and 4"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> In a disc (radial or plate) cam, the follower moves radially from the cam's centre of rotation.</li><li><b>2 (wrong):</b> A double-disc cam with two discs keyed together, each touching one of two follower rollers, is a <i>conjugate</i> cam (positive drive without a spring).</li><li><b>3 (wrong):</b> Having either a convex or a concave surface describes a <i>globoidal</i> cam. Statements 2 and 3 have the names swapped.</li><li><b>4 (correct):</b> In a spherical cam, the follower oscillates about an axis perpendicular to the cam's axis of rotation (the cam surface is part of a sphere).</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Other classes: cylindrical cams (follower parallel to the cam axis), wedge cams (translating), and end/face cams.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 4.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 2, 3, which are incorrect; Omits 1, 4, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 2, 3, which are incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Conjugate cams give positive motion at high speeds without relying on a return spring.</p>` }
);

