// ESE 2021 Prelims Paper II, Mechanical Engineering, Series A, Q101–125
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2021-101", year: 2021, no: 101, subject: "tom", topic: "Conditions for the Coriolis component",
q: `Under which of the following conditions is Coriolis component encountered in the relative acceleration of two points?<ol><li>The two points are coincident, but on different links.</li><li>The point on one link traces a path on the other link.</li><li>The link that contains the path rotates.</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The Coriolis component 2ωv appears when a point slides along a link (relative velocity v) while that link rotates (angular velocity ω). All three conditions are needed together:</p>
<ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> The two points coincide at the instant but belong to different links (for example the slider block and the slotted lever).</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> One point moves along a path on the other link (relative sliding).</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> The link carrying the path rotates; if it only translated, there would be no Coriolis term.</li></ul>
<div class="formula">a<sub>c</sub> = 2 ω v (perpendicular to the sliding velocity, turned 90° in the sense of ω)</div>
<p>Typical case: the crank-and-slotted-lever quick-return mechanism of a shaper, where the slider moves along the rotating slotted lever.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> If ω = 0 (the guide translates) or v = 0 (no sliding), the Coriolis component vanishes.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-102", year: 2021, no: 102, subject: "tom", topic: "Spring–mass period with added mass",
q: `A mass 'm' attached to a light spring oscillates with a period of 2 seconds. If the mass is increased by 2 kg, the period increases by 1 second. What is the value of the mass?`,
opts: ["0·9 kg", "1·6 kg", "2·1 kg", "2·7 kg"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1·6 kg</h3><div class="formula">T = 2π √(m/k) → T ∝ √m</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>T<sub>1</sub> = 2 s with mass m; T<sub>2</sub> = 3 s with mass m + 2 kg; k unchanged</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>(T<sub>2</sub>/T<sub>1</sub>)² = (m + 2)/m → (3/2)² = 2·25.</li><li>2·25 m = m + 2 → 1·25 m = 2 → m = <b>1·6 kg</b>.</li><li>Stiffness: k = 4π²m/T² = 4π² × 1·6/4 = 15·8 N/m.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0·9 does not satisfy (m + 2)/m = 2·25; for example m = 2·1 gives a period ratio of only 1·40.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 2·1 does not satisfy (m + 2)/m = 2·25; for example m = 2·1 gives a period ratio of only 1·40.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 2·7 kg does not satisfy (m + 2)/m = 2·25; for example m = 2·1 gives a period ratio of only 1·40.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Check: with m = 1·6 kg, T<sub>2</sub>/T<sub>1</sub> = √(3·6/1·6) = √2·25 = 1·5, so T<sub>2</sub> = 3 s. ✓ The spring is assumed massless; a spring of mass m<sub>s</sub> would add m<sub>s</sub>/3 to the effective mass.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> The stiffness never needs to be known: ratios of periods give ratios of masses.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-103", year: 2021, no: 103, subject: "tom", topic: "Critical speed of a shaft",
q: `What is the critical speed of the shaft if its natural frequency of transverse vibration is 2·85 Hz?`,
opts: ["171 rpm", "285 rpm", "570 rpm", "142·5 rpm"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 171 rpm</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The critical (whirling) speed of a shaft equals its natural frequency of transverse vibration expressed as a rotational speed.</p>
<div class="formula">N<sub>c</sub> (rpm) = f<sub>n</sub> (Hz) × 60 = 2·85 × 60 = 171 rpm</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>N_c = 2.85 × 60 = <b>171 rpm</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 285 rpm multiplies by 100 instead of 60.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 570 rpm multiplies by 200.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 142·5 rpm is 2·85 × 50.</li></ul>
<p>At the critical speed, the shaft's rotation frequency matches its bending natural frequency and the deflection grows very large (limited only by damping). Shafts run either well below it or are run quickly through it.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> With static deflection δ (m): f<sub>n</sub> = 0·4985/√δ Hz, so N<sub>c</sub> = 29·9/√δ rpm.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-104", year: 2021, no: 104, subject: "tom", topic: "Gear terminology",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding gears :<ol><li>The ratio of number of teeth on the gear to that on the pinion is known as gear ratio.</li><li>The circle passing through the tips of teeth is called dedendum circle.</li><li>The circle passing through the roots of teeth is called addendum circle.</li><li>Backlash is the difference between the space width and the tooth thickness along the pitch circle.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3 only", "1 and 4 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2, 3 and 4"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> Gear ratio G = T<sub>gear</sub>/T<sub>pinion</sub> (≥ 1).</li><li><b>2 (wrong):</b> The circle through the tooth tips is the <i>addendum</i> circle.</li><li><b>3 (wrong):</b> The circle through the roots is the <i>dedendum</i> (root) circle. Statements 2 and 3 have the names swapped.</li><li><b>4 (correct):</b> Backlash = space width − tooth thickness, measured on the pitch circle; it gives clearance for lubrication and thermal expansion.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Standard 20° full-depth involute teeth: addendum = 1 m, dedendum = 1·25 m, clearance = 0·25 m, where m is the module.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 2, 3, which are incorrect; Omits 4, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 4.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 2, 3, which are incorrect; Omits 1, 4, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 2, 3, which are incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Addendum circle diameter = m(T + 2); root circle diameter = m(T − 2·5).</p>` },

{ id: "2021-105", year: 2021, no: 105, subject: "tom", topic: "Pitch-line velocity of a spur gear",
q: `The number of teeth of a spur gear is 30 and it rotates at 200 rpm. What is the pitch line velocity if it has a module of 2 mm?`,
opts: ["341·7 mm/s", "497·2 mm/s", "628·3 mm/s", "758·5 mm/s"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 628·3 mm/s</h3><div class="formula">d = m T &nbsp;&nbsp; v = π d N/60</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>m = 2 mm (module); T = 30 teeth; N = 200 rpm</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Pitch circle diameter d = 2 × 30 = 60 mm.</li><li>v = π × 60 × 200/60 = 200π = <b>628·3 mm/s</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 341·7 uses wrong diameters or radius in place of diameter.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 497·2 uses wrong diameters or radius in place of diameter.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 758·5 mm/s uses wrong diameters or radius in place of diameter.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Pitch-line velocity sets the dynamic (velocity) factor in gear design: for example the Barth factor C<sub>v</sub> = 3/(3 + v) for ordinary cut gears with v in m/s.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Module m = d/T (mm); circular pitch p = π m = 6·28 mm here.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-106", year: 2021, no: 106, subject: "tom", topic: "Compound gear train",
q: `The compound gear train shown in the figure below consists of compound gear B-C and D-E. All gears are mounted on parallel shafts. The motor shaft rotating at 800 rpm is connected to the gear A and the output shaft to the gear F. The number of teeth on gears A, B, C, D, E and F are 24, 56, 30, 80, 32 and 72 respectively. What is the speed of the gear F?<br><img class="fig" src="img/me/2021-106.png" alt="Compound gear train: A (24) meshes with B (56); C (30) on the same shaft as B meshes with D (80); E (32) on the same shaft as D meshes with F (72)">`,
opts: ["57·14 rpm", "32·51 rpm", "74·63 rpm", "69·72 rpm"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 57·14 rpm</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In a compound train, gears on the same shaft (B–C, D–E) turn at the same speed. The speed ratio is the product of drivers' teeth over the product of driven teeth.</p>
<div class="formula">N<sub>F</sub>/N<sub>A</sub> = (T<sub>A</sub> × T<sub>C</sub> × T<sub>E</sub>)/(T<sub>B</sub> × T<sub>D</sub> × T<sub>F</sub>)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>Drivers: A = 24, C = 30, E = 32; driven: B = 56, D = 80, F = 72; N<sub>A</sub> = 800 rpm</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>N<sub>B</sub> = N<sub>C</sub> = 800 × 24/56 = 342·86 rpm.</li><li>N<sub>D</sub> = N<sub>E</sub> = 342·86 × 30/80 = 128·57 rpm.</li><li>N<sub>F</sub> = 128·57 × 32/72 = <b>57·14 rpm</b>.</li></ol>
<p>Overall train value = 23,040/3,22,560 = 1/14. With three external meshes, F turns opposite to A.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 32·51 results from using a wrong pair as driver/driven.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 74·63 results from using a wrong pair as driver/driven.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 69·72 rpm results from using a wrong pair as driver/driven.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Each external mesh reverses the direction; idlers change direction but not the ratio.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-107", year: 2021, no: 107, subject: "tom", topic: "Quick-return mechanism: drive speed",
q: `A quick-return mechanism is to be designed, where the outward stroke must consume 1·2 s and the return stroke 0·8 s. If the cycle time is 2·0 s/rev, what is the speed at which the mechanism should be driven?`,
opts: ["10 rev/s", "30 rev/s", "10 rev/min", "30 rev/min"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 30 rev/min</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> One full revolution of the driving crank equals one complete cycle (outward + return stroke).</p>
<div class="formula">N = 1/(cycle time) = 1/2·0 rev/s = 0·5 rev/s = 30 rev/min</div>
<p>Time ratio Q = 1·2/0·8 = 1·5, which corresponds to crank angles of 216° for the forward stroke and 144° for the return (Q = α/β, α + β = 360°).</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>N = 1/2 rev/s = <b>30 rev/min</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 10 rev/s is far too fast (cycle times of 0·1 and 0·033 s).</li><li><b>(b)</b> 30 rev/s is far too fast (cycle times of 0·1 and 0·033 s).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 10 rev/min would give a 6 s cycle.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> In a crank-and-slotted-lever mechanism, the time ratio is fixed by the geometry: cos(β/2) = crank radius/distance between fixed centres.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-108", year: 2021, no: 108, subject: "tom", topic: "Balancing a single-cylinder engine",
q: `The following data relate to a single-cylinder reciprocating engine : mass of reciprocating parts = 40 kg, mass of revolving parts = 30 kg at crank radius, speed = 150 rpm, stroke = 350 mm. If 60% of the reciprocating parts and all the revolving parts are to be balanced, what is the balance mass required at a radius of 320 mm?`,
opts: ["15·27 kg", "21·43 kg", "24·96 kg", "29·53 kg"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 29·53 kg</h3><div class="formula">B b = (m + c M) r</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>m = 30 kg (revolving); M = 40 kg (reciprocating); c = 0·6 (fraction balanced); r = stroke/2 = 175 mm (crank radius); b = 320 mm (radius of balance mass B)</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Mass to be balanced at crank radius = 30 + 0·6 × 40 = 54 kg.</li><li>B = 54 × 175/320 = <b>29·53 kg</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The speed is not needed for the balance mass; it would be needed to find the unbalanced force left over, e.g. the residual primary force (1 − c) M ω² r cos θ along the line of stroke, plus c M ω² r sin θ introduced perpendicular to it.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 15·27 kg balances only the reciprocating part.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 21·43 kg uses r = 350 mm or omits part of the revolving mass.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 24·96 kg uses a different fraction.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Reciprocating masses can only be partially balanced (typically 2/3 to 3/4): balancing them fully along the stroke introduces an equal unbalance perpendicular to it.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-109", year: 2021, no: 109, subject: "design", topic: "Leaf spring length from bending stress",
q: `A leaf spring consists of seven steel plates, each 60 mm wide and 6 mm thick. What is the length of the spring if it is to carry a central load of 3 kN, without the stress exceeding 150 MPa?`,
opts: ["547 mm", "498 mm", "494 mm", "504 mm"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 504 mm</h3><div class="formula">Semi-elliptic leaf spring (central load W, span L): σ = 3 W L/(2 n b t²)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>W = 3000 N; n = 7 leaves; b = 60 mm; t = 6 mm; σ = 150 N/mm²</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>L = 2 n b t² σ/(3W) = 2 × 7 × 60 × 36 × 150/(3 × 3000).</li><li>Numerator = 45,36,000; denominator = 9000.</li><li>L = <b>504 mm</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Derivation: the spring acts as a simply supported beam with central load W; maximum moment WL/4 is shared by n leaves, each with section modulus bt²/6, giving σ = (WL/4)/(n bt²/6) = 3WL/(2nbt²).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 547 comes from slips such as using 6 leaves or σ = 140 MPa.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 498 comes from slips such as using 6 leaves or σ = 140 MPa.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 494 mm comes from slips such as using 6 leaves or σ = 140 MPa.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Deflection of the same spring: δ = 3WL³/(8Enbt³).</p>` },

{ id: "2021-110", year: 2021, no: 110, subject: "design", topic: "Maximum shear stress theory: shear yield strength",
q: `According to maximum shear stress theory, with comparison to yield strength in tension, the yield strength in shear is`,
opts: ["half the yield strength in tension.", "same as that of yield strength in tension.", "double the yield strength in tension.", "1·33 times that of the yield strength in tension."], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) half the yield strength in tension</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Tresca's theory: yielding occurs when the maximum shear stress reaches the maximum shear stress at yield in a simple tension test, which is S<sub>yt</sub>/2. In pure shear τ<sub>max</sub> = τ, so S<sub>sy</sub> = 0·5 S<sub>yt</sub>.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> S<sub>sy</sub> = S<sub>yt</sub> follows from the maximum principal stress theory.</li><li><b>(c)</b> No failure theory predicts a shear strength greater than the tensile strength; the distortion-energy (von Mises) theory gives S<sub>sy</sub> = 0·577 S<sub>yt</sub>.</li><li><b>(d)</b> No failure theory predicts a shear strength greater than the tensile strength; the distortion-energy (von Mises) theory gives S<sub>sy</sub> = 0·577 S<sub>yt</sub>.</li></ul>
<p>Summary: max principal stress → 1·0; max shear stress → 0·5; distortion energy → 0·577; max principal strain (μ = 0·3) → 1/(1 + μ) = 0·77.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Tresca is slightly conservative (safer) than von Mises for ductile materials.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-111", year: 2021, no: 111, subject: "design", topic: "Stress distribution in curved beams",
q: `In curved beams, normally the nature of stress distribution is`,
opts: ["linear", "circular", "parabolic", "hyperbolic"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) hyperbolic</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In a curved beam (for example a crane hook or C-frame), fibres on the inside are shorter than those outside. Equal rotation of the cross-section produces strains inversely related to the radius, so the bending stress varies hyperbolically across the depth.</p>
<div class="formula">σ = M y/[A e (R<sub>n</sub> − y)]</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>e = R − R<sub>n</sub>: shift of the neutral axis towards the centre of curvature; y measured from the neutral axis</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Linear distribution holds for straight beams (σ = My/I).</li><li><b>(b)</b> Circular has no basis.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Parabolic is the distribution of <i>shear</i> stress across a rectangular section.</li></ul>
<p>As a result, the maximum stress occurs at the inner fibre and the neutral axis lies closer to the centre of curvature than the centroidal axis.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> This is why crane hooks have a trapezoidal section with the wider side on the inside.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-112", year: 2021, no: 112, subject: "design", topic: "Where cracks start: stress raisers",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding crack :<ol><li>Crack is more likely to occur in the regions of discontinuity such as oil holes.</li><li>Crack is more likely to occur in the regions of irregularities in machining operations such as stamp mark.</li><li>Crack is more likely to occur in the internal cracks due to defects in materials like blow holes.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Fatigue cracks start at stress concentrations. Bhandari lists three sources:</p>
<ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> Variations in geometry: oil holes, keyways, splines, shoulders and threads.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> Surface irregularities from machining: scratches, tool marks, stamp and inspection marks.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> Internal defects in the material: blow holes, slag inclusions and porosity in castings.</li></ul>
<p>Remedies include generous fillet radii, relief grooves, fine surface finish, shot peening (compressive residual stress) and inspection to reject defective castings.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Fatigue stress concentration factor K<sub>f</sub> = 1 + q(K<sub>t</sub> − 1), where q is notch sensitivity.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-113", year: 2021, no: 113, subject: "design", topic: "Maximum principal stress theory: allowable stress",
q: `The yield strength of bolt material is 300 MPa and factor of safety is 2·5. What is the maximum principal stress using maximum principal stress theory?`,
opts: ["750 MPa", "120 MPa", "27·38 MPa", "10·95 MPa"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 120 MPa</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Rankine's theory: failure occurs when the maximum principal stress reaches the yield strength in simple tension. For safe design:</p>
<div class="formula">σ<sub>1</sub> ≤ S<sub>yt</sub>/N = 300/2·5 = 120 MPa</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>σ₁ = 300/2.5 = <b>120 MPa</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 750 MPa multiplies by the factor of safety instead of dividing.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 27.38 MPa does not equal S_yt/N.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 10.95 MPa is far below S_yt/N = 120 MPa.</li></ul>
<p>Under the maximum shear stress theory, the corresponding limit would be τ<sub>max</sub> ≤ S<sub>yt</sub>/(2N) = 60 MPa.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> The maximum principal stress theory is best suited to brittle materials; for a ductile bolt, Tresca or von Mises would usually be used.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-114", year: 2021, no: 114, subject: "design", topic: "Failure theory for brittle materials",
q: `Which one of the following theories gives satisfactory results for brittle materials?`,
opts: ["Maximum principal stress theory", "Maximum shear stress theory", "Distortion energy theory", "Shear stress energy theory"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Maximum principal stress theory</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Brittle materials (cast iron, ceramics) fail by separation (fracture) under tensile stress, with little plastic flow. Rankine's maximum principal stress theory, which takes the largest normal stress as the cause of failure, therefore fits them well.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Maximum shear stress (Tresca) theory suits ductile materials.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Distortion energy (von Mises) theory suits ductile materials.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 'Shear stress energy theory' is not a standard failure theory.</li></ul>
<p>Refinements for brittle materials with unequal tensile and compressive strengths are the Coulomb–Mohr and modified Mohr theories.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Ductile → von Mises (most accurate) or Tresca (conservative); brittle → maximum normal stress or modified Mohr.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-115", year: 2021, no: 115, subject: "design", topic: "Equivalent diameter of a rectangular section",
q: `A cast steel bar having an ultimate strength of 120 MPa is subjected to a reversed, repeated, bending load. The bar will be machined to a rectangular cross-section, 150 mm wide × 200 mm high. What is the equivalent diameter?`,
opts: ["14 mm", "30 mm", "140 mm", "300 mm"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 140 mm</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For the size factor in fatigue, a non-rotating non-circular section is replaced by an equivalent round bar having the same area stressed above 95% of the maximum stress (Shigley).</p>
<div class="formula">Rectangle h × b in non-rotating bending: d<sub>e</sub> = 0·808 √(h b)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>h = 200 mm; b = 150 mm</li></ul>
<p>d<sub>e</sub> = 0·808 × √(30,000) = 0·808 × 173·2 = <b>140 mm</b>.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>hb = 200 × 150 = 30 000.</li><li>d_e = 0.808 × 173.2 = <b>140 mm</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 14 mm is far smaller than the section itself.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 30 mm is far smaller than the section itself.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 300 mm is larger than either side of the section.</li></ul>
<p>The ultimate strength (120 MPa) is not needed for d<sub>e</sub>; it enters later when estimating the endurance limit.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> For a solid round bar in non-rotating bending, d<sub>e</sub> = 0·37 d.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-116", year: 2021, no: 116, subject: "design", topic: "Where bolts fail",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding typical analysis of bolt failure :<ol><li>15% failure of bolt occur at the fillet under the head.</li><li>50% failure of bolt occur at the end of threads on the shank.</li><li>80% failure of bolt occur in the threads that are in contact with the nut.</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 only", "3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Bhandari's typical analysis of bolt failures: about 15% at the fillet under the head, 20% at the end of the threads on the shank (thread run-out), and 65% in the threads in contact with the nut (the first engaged thread carries most of the load).</p>
<p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> 15% at the fillet under the head.</li><li><b>2 (wrong):</b> It is about 20%, not 50%.</li><li><b>3 (wrong):</b> It is about 65%, not 80%.</li></ul>
<p>Design measures: a generous fillet radius under the head, rolled (not cut) threads, a reduced shank diameter (waisted bolt) to make the bolt more flexible, and tension-type nuts that spread the load over more threads.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 2, 3, which are incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 2, 3, which are incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> The three percentages (15 + 20 + 65) add up to 100%.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-117", year: 2021, no: 117, subject: "design", topic: "Functions of lubrication in bearings", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Which of the following are the functions of lubrication in a bearing unit?<ol><li>To protect the bearing components from corrosion.</li><li>To absorb heat from the bearing unit.</li><li>To carry heat away from the bearing unit.</li></ol>Select the correct answer using the code given below :`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Lubricant functions in a bearing: reduce friction and wear, carry the load (fluid film), protect against corrosion, keep dirt out (sealing), and dissipate heat. Heat dissipation has two parts: the oil absorbs the frictional heat generated in the bearing and then carries it away (to the sump or cooler).</p>
<p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> The oil film coats the surfaces and protects them from rust and corrosion.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> The lubricant absorbs the heat generated by friction.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> Circulating oil carries the heat away from the bearing.</li></ul>
<p>Our first attempt chose (b), treating "absorb" and "carry away" as one function. Bearing manufacturers' literature (for example SKF and NTN) lists both: lubricant absorbs heat and carries it away. We corrected to (d), as in the key.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> In hydrodynamic bearings, the heat balance (heat generated = heat carried away by oil) fixes the oil flow rate and temperature rise.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-118", year: 2021, no: 118, subject: "design", topic: "Dry and wet clutches",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding clutches :<ol><li>Dry clutch has higher coefficient of friction compared to wet clutch.</li><li>The torque capacity of wet clutch is high compared to dry clutch.</li><li>The engagement in a dry clutch is smoother than in case of wet clutch.</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> Without an oil film, dry friction linings have a higher μ (about 0·3–0·4) than wet clutches (about 0·06–0·1).</li><li><b>2 (wrong):</b> With higher μ, a dry clutch of the same size transmits more torque. Wet clutches need more plates to make up for low μ.</li><li><b>3 (wrong):</b> Wet clutches engage more smoothly (oil damps the engagement and carries away heat); dry clutches tend to grab or judder.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Wet clutches are used in automatic transmissions and motorcycles because of smooth engagement, cooling and long life; dry clutches suit manual car transmissions.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 2, 3, which are incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Torque of a multi-plate clutch: T = n μ W R<sub>m</sub>, with n the number of pairs of contact surfaces.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-119", year: 2021, no: 119, subject: "design", topic: "Causes of tooth misalignment in gears",
q: `Which of the following factors can cause misalignment of the teeth on the pinion relative to those on the gear?<ol><li>Inaccurate gear teeth</li><li>Misalignment of the axes of shafts carrying gears</li><li>Thermal distortions during operation</li></ol>Select the correct answer using the code given below :`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Bhandari lists the causes of misalignment of the teeth, which leads to uneven load distribution across the face width:</p>
<ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> Inaccuracies in tooth profile and lead from manufacturing.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> Misalignment of the shaft axes (bearing and housing errors, shaft deflection).</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> Elastic and thermal distortions of gears, shafts and housing during operation.</li></ul>
<p>Misalignment is taken into account with the load-distribution factor (K<sub>m</sub>) and reduced in practice by rigid mountings, crowning of teeth and limited face width (b ≈ 10 m).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Wider face width makes gears more sensitive to misalignment, which is why it is limited.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-120", year: 2021, no: 120, subject: "design", topic: "Welded versus riveted joints",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding welded and riveted joints :<ol><li>Welded assemblies are tight and leak proof as compared with riveted assemblies.</li><li>Single-welded V-joint is less reliable than square butt joint.</li><li>Welding results in a thermal distortion of the parts, thereby inducing residual stresses.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> Welded joints are continuous and leak-proof; riveted joints need caulking and fullering to be tight.</li><li><b>2 (wrong):</b> A square butt joint gives full penetration only in thin plates (up to about 5 mm). For thicker plates, the V-preparation lets the weld penetrate the full thickness, so the V-joint is the more reliable one.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> Local heating and cooling cause thermal distortion and residual stresses, a key disadvantage of welding; stress-relief annealing is used to remove them.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Other advantages of welding: lighter structures (no cover plates or rivet heads), no holes weakening the plates, and easier changes to design. Disadvantages: need for skilled labour and inspection, and poor vibration damping.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 3.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, 3, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Riveted joints are still preferred where joints must be inspected easily or where welding would harm the material.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-121", year: 2021, no: 121, subject: "mfg", topic: "Lubricant for hot extrusion of steel",
q: `Which one of the following lubricants is used in forward hot extrusion of steel?`,
opts: ["Molten glass", "Soap solution", "Copper sulphate", "Vegetable oil"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Molten glass</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In the Ugine–Séjournet process, a glass pad or glass powder placed in front of the billet melts at the extrusion temperature (about 1100–1250 °C). It acts as a lubricant and as a thermal insulator between the hot billet and the die.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Soap solution is used in cold drawing and cold working of light metals.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Copper sulphate is a coating used in wire drawing of steel (copper coating).</li><li><b>(d)</b> Vegetable oils are used at moderate temperatures (for example in cold working); they would burn at steel hot-extrusion temperatures.</li></ul>
<p>Graphite is the usual lubricant for hot working of non-ferrous metals and in hot forging.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Cold extrusion of steel uses a zinc phosphate conversion coating with soap.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-122", year: 2021, no: 122, subject: "mfg", topic: "OC curve of a sampling plan",
q: `Which one of the following statements is <b>not</b> the correct statement regarding operating characteristics (OC) curve for sampling plan?`,
opts: ["It shows ability to distinguish between good and bad lots.", "No sampling plans can give complete protection against acceptance of defectives.", "Larger the sample size, steeper is the slope of the curve.", "Acceptance number is zero for ideal sampling plan."], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Acceptance number is zero for ideal sampling plan</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The OC curve plots the probability of accepting a lot against its fraction defective. The ideal OC curve is a vertical step at the acceptable quality level: every lot better than it is accepted, and every worse lot rejected. This can be achieved only by 100% (error-free) inspection, not by a plan with acceptance number c = 0.</p>
<p><b>Option by option</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> True: the steeper the curve, the better it discriminates between good and bad lots.</li><li><b>(b)</b> True: with sampling there is always some chance of accepting a bad lot (consumer's risk β).</li><li><b>(c)</b> True: for a given ratio c/n, a larger sample makes the curve steeper.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Not correct: c = 0 plans give a curve that falls steeply at first but is concave throughout, and they reject many good lots. Zero acceptance number does not make a plan ideal.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Producer's risk α: chance of rejecting a good lot (at AQL). Consumer's risk β: chance of accepting a bad lot (at LTPD).</p>` },

{ id: "2021-123", year: 2021, no: 123, subject: "mfg", topic: "Direct (forward) extrusion",
q: `Which one of the following statements is <b>not</b> correct for forward or direct extrusion process?`,
opts: ["High friction forces must be overcome.", "High extrusion forces are required but mechanically simple and uncomplicated.", "Low scrap or material waste only 5 – 6% of billet weight.", "Simple, but the material must slide along the chamber wall."], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Low scrap or material waste only 5 – 6% of billet weight</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In direct extrusion the ram pushes the billet through a die at the far end, so the whole billet slides along the container wall.</p>
<p><b>Option by option</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> True: friction between billet and container must be overcome, so the ram force is high, especially at the start.</li><li><b>(b)</b> True: the forces are high, but the equipment is mechanically simple.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Not correct: direct extrusion leaves a large butt end (often 10–20% of billet weight) that cannot be pushed through. Low scrap (about 5–6%) is a feature of <i>indirect</i> (backward) extrusion.</li><li><b>(d)</b> True: the process is simple, but the material slides along the chamber wall.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Indirect extrusion: the die moves into the stationary billet, so there is no billet–container friction; the hollow ram limits the force that can be applied.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-124", year: 2021, no: 124, subject: "mfg", topic: "Forging defects",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding defects in forgings :<ol><li>Flakes are internal breaks or ruptures occurring in some grades of alloy steel.</li><li>Die shift is caused by misalignment between the top and bottom forging dies.</li><li>Fins and rags are small projections or loose metal driven into the surface of the forging.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> Flakes are internal hairline ruptures in some alloy steels, caused by hydrogen and too-rapid cooling of large forgings; slow cooling prevents them.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> Die shift (mismatch) is the displacement of the upper part of the forging relative to the lower, from misaligned dies.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> Fins and rags are small projections or loose metal driven into the surface of the forging during working.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Other forging defects: laps and cold shuts (metal folds over without welding), unfilled sections, scale pits (scale pressed into the surface), cracks, and improper grain flow.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Cold shuts often come from sharp die corners; generous fillet radii help prevent them.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-125", year: 2021, no: 125, subject: "mfg", topic: "Desirable properties of cutting fluids",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding desirable properties of cutting fluid :<ol><li>It should get oxidised when left in air.</li><li>It should react with the materials of machine tool parts.</li><li>It should wet the surface of cutting tool and workpiece.</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["2 only", "3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 3 only</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (wrong):</b> A cutting fluid should be chemically stable and <i>not</i> oxidise in air (oxidation forms gums and sludge).</li><li><b>2 (wrong):</b> It should be non-corrosive and <i>not</i> react with machine-tool parts, seals or paint.</li><li><b>3 (correct):</b> It should wet the tool and workpiece well so that it reaches the cutting zone, cools and lubricates.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Other desirable properties: high specific heat and thermal conductivity, good lubricity, low viscosity for easy flow, no foaming, non-toxic, no unpleasant smell, resistance to bacterial growth, and low cost.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 3.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 1, 2, which are incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Water-based emulsions are best for cooling (high speed); straight oils are best for lubrication (low-speed, heavy cuts such as broaching and gear cutting).</p>` }
);

