// ESE 2017 Prelims Paper II, Mechanical Engineering, Series A, Q1–25
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2017-1", year: 2017, no: 1, subject: "fm", topic: "Hydrostatic force on a dam",
q: `A section of a dam made of concrete, ρ = 2·6, total height = 35 m, with top walkway width of 6 m, is shown. The upstream bottommost point is called the Heel of the dam. The sloped part on downstream side is 3 vertical on 2 horizontal. Water stands till 2 m short of the top of the dam section. The net resultant force acting on the base level of the dam is nearly<img class="fig" src="img/me/2017-1.png" alt="Dam section: vertical upstream face, top width 6 m, downstream face vertical for the top 5 m then sloping 3 vertical : 2 horizontal over the lower 30 m; water 2 m below the top">`,
opts: ["1370 k kgf", "1385 k kgf", "1400 k kgf", "1433 k kgf"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1433 k kgf</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Per metre length of the dam, two forces act on the base: the <b>weight</b> of the concrete (vertical) and the <b>water thrust</b> on the vertical upstream face (horizontal). The resultant is their vector sum. (Uplift is not mentioned, so it is ignored.)</p>
<p><b>Given</b></p><ul><li>Specific gravity of concrete = 2·6 → density 2·6 t/m³ (2600 kgf/m³)</li><li>Height H = 35 m, top width 6 m; downstream face vertical for the top 5 m, then slopes 3 V : 2 H over the lower 30 m</li><li>Water depth h = 35 − 2 = 33 m (water weighs 1 t/m³)</li></ul>
<div class="formula">W = ρ<sub>c</sub> × (area of section) × 1 m &nbsp;&nbsp; P = ½ ρ<sub>w</sub> g h² &nbsp;&nbsp; R = √(W² + P²)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>W = weight of the dam per metre run; ρ<sub>c</sub> = density of concrete</li><li>P = horizontal hydrostatic thrust per metre run; h = depth of water on the vertical face</li><li>R = resultant on the base</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Horizontal width of the slope = 30 × 2/3 = 20 m, so the base width = 6 + 20 = 26 m.</li>
<li>Area = rectangle 6 × 35 + triangle ½ × 20 × 30 = 210 + 300 = 510 m².</li>
<li>W = 2·6 × 510 = 1326 t = 1326 k kgf (vertical).</li>
<li>P = ½ × 1 × 33² = 544·5 t = 544·5 k kgf (horizontal, acting at h/3 = 11 m above the base).</li>
<li>R = √(1326² + 544·5²) = √(17,58,276 + 2,96,480) = √20,54,756 ≈ <b>1433 k kgf</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 1370 comes from slips such as taking the water depth as 35 m only on part of the face, using the wrong base width, or adding forces partly as scalars. Only the vector sum with the correct section area gives 1433.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 1385 comes from slips such as taking the water depth as 35 m only on part of the face, using the wrong base width, or adding forces partly as scalars. Only the vector sum with the correct section area gives 1433.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 1400 comes from slips such as taking the water depth as 35 m only on part of the face, using the wrong base width, or adding forces partly as scalars. Only the vector sum with the correct section area gives 1433.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Gravity dam checks: overturning (moments about the toe), sliding (μW/P ≥ about 1·5) and no tension at the heel (resultant within the middle third of the base).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-2", year: 2017, no: 2, subject: "fm", topic: "Surface tension energy",
q: `A spherical waterdrop of 1 mm in diameter splits up in air into 64 smaller drops of equal size. The surface tension coefficient of water in air is 0·073 N/m. The work required in splitting up the drop is`,
opts: ["0·96 × 10<sup>−6</sup> J", "0·69 × 10<sup>−6</sup> J", "0·32 × 10<sup>−6</sup> J", "0·23 × 10<sup>−6</sup> J"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 0·69 × 10<sup>−6</sup> J</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Splitting creates new free surface. The work needed equals surface tension × increase in surface area (σ is surface energy per unit area).</p>
<p><b>Given</b></p><ul><li>D = 1 mm = 1 × 10<sup>−3</sup> m, n = 64 drops, σ = 0·073 N/m</li></ul>
<div class="formula">Volume conserved: π D³/6 = n × π d³/6 → d = D / n<sup>1/3</sup> &nbsp;&nbsp; W = σ ΔA = σ (n π d² − π D²)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>D, d = diameters of the big drop and of each small drop; n = number of small drops</li><li>σ = surface tension (J/m² = N/m); ΔA = increase in surface area</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>d = 1/64<sup>1/3</sup> = 1/4 = 0·25 mm.</li>
<li>Initial area = π D² = π × 10<sup>−6</sup> m². Final area = 64 × π × (0·25 × 10<sup>−3</sup>)² = 4π × 10<sup>−6</sup> m².</li>
<li>ΔA = 3π × 10<sup>−6</sup> = 9·425 × 10<sup>−6</sup> m².</li>
<li>W = 0·073 × 9·425 × 10<sup>−6</sup> = <b>0·688 × 10<sup>−6</sup> J</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0·96 × 10<sup>−6</sup> J is σ × final area (forgetting to subtract the original surface).</li><li><b>(c)</b> is too small: it come from using radius instead of diameter (area divided by 4) or from n<sup>1/2</sup> instead of n<sup>1/3</sup>.</li><li><b>(d)</b> is too small: it come from using radius instead of diameter (area divided by 4) or from n<sup>1/2</sup> instead of n<sup>1/3</sup>.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Short cut: W = σ π D² (n<sup>1/3</sup> − 1) = 0·073 × π × 10<sup>−6</sup> × 3.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-3", year: 2017, no: 3, subject: "fm", topic: "Stability of floating bodies", doubt: true,
q: `Consider the following statements pertaining to stability of floating bodies :<ol><li>A floating body will be stable when the centre of gravity is above the centre of buoyancy.</li><li>The positions of metacentres corresponding to different axes of rotation are generally different for the same floating object.</li><li>For cargo ships, the metacentric height varies with loading.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3", "1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1, 2 and 3 (official key)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A floating body is stable when its <b>metacentre M lies above its centre of gravity G</b> (metacentric height GM &gt; 0). GM = BM − BG, with BM = I/V, where I is the second moment of the waterline area about the tilt axis and V the displaced volume.</p>
<p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul>
<li><b>1:</b> Unlike a submerged body (which needs G below B), a floating body <i>can</i> be stable even with G above B, because the centre of buoyancy shifts when it tilts. UPSC's key treats this as correct in that sense. Strictly, G above B is not by itself a condition for stability; what matters is M above G.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> BM = I/V depends on the axis: for a ship, I about the long (roll) axis is much smaller than about the transverse (pitch) axis, so the transverse and longitudinal metacentres are at very different heights.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> Loading changes V, the waterline area (hence I) and the position of G, so GM changes with cargo.</li></ul>
<div class="note">In doubt (key followed). Our own answer was (d) 2 and 3 only: statement 1 reads as a condition ("will be stable when G is above B"), which is not true. A floating body with G above B is stable only if M is still above G. The key's (a) accepts statement 1 in the looser sense that floating bodies can be stable with G above B.</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Submerged body: stable if G below B. Floating body: stable if M above G; neutral if M = G; unstable if M below G.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-4", year: 2017, no: 4, subject: "fm", topic: "Free jet: Bernoulli and continuity",
q: `Water is coming out from a tap and falls vertically downwards. At the tap opening, the stream diameter is 20 mm with uniform velocity of 2 m/s. Assuming steady inviscid flow, constant pressure atmosphere everywhere, and neglecting curvature and surface tension effects, the diameter of the stream 0·5 m below the tap opening is nearly`,
opts: ["11·7 mm", "14·6 mm", "17·5 mm", "20·4 mm"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 14·6 mm</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The stream is a free jet at atmospheric pressure, so Bernoulli gives the velocity gained by falling, and continuity gives the narrower diameter.</p>
<p><b>Given</b></p><ul><li>d<sub>1</sub> = 20 mm, V<sub>1</sub> = 2 m/s, fall z = 0·5 m, g = 9·81 m/s²</li></ul>
<div class="formula">V<sub>2</sub>² = V<sub>1</sub>² + 2 g z &nbsp;&nbsp; A<sub>1</sub>V<sub>1</sub> = A<sub>2</sub>V<sub>2</sub> → d<sub>2</sub> = d<sub>1</sub> √(V<sub>1</sub>/V<sub>2</sub>)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>V<sub>1</sub>, V<sub>2</sub> = stream velocities at the tap and 0·5 m below; d<sub>1</sub>, d<sub>2</sub> = stream diameters; z = height of fall</li><li>Pressure terms cancel (both atmospheric)</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>V<sub>2</sub> = √(4 + 2 × 9·81 × 0·5) = √13·81 = 3·716 m/s.</li>
<li>d<sub>2</sub> = 20 × √(2/3·716) = 20 × 0·7336 = <b>14·67 mm</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 11·7 mm would need V<sub>2</sub> ≈ 5·8 m/s (fall of about 1·5 m).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 17·5 mm uses a near-linear ratio or forgets the square root wrongly.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 20·4 mm is larger than the tap: a falling stream speeds up, so it must get <i>thinner</i>, not thicker.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> This necking of a falling stream is why tap water narrows; with surface tension it later breaks into drops (Plateau–Rayleigh instability).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-5", year: 2017, no: 5, subject: "fm", topic: "Bernoulli's equation",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding Bernoulli's equation :<ol><li>It is assumed that no energy has been supplied.</li><li>The velocity of a steady stream of fluid flow will depend on the cross-sectional area of the stream.</li><li>Consider two sections 1 and 2 along a flow stream. In this reach, if q is work done by a pump, w is work absorbed by turbine, ρ is density of water and g is acceleration of gravity, with p, v and z carrying standard meanings, Bernoulli's equation will read<br>p<sub>1</sub>/ρ + v<sub>1</sub>²/2g + z<sub>1</sub> = p<sub>2</sub>/ρ + v<sub>2</sub>²/2g + z<sub>2</sub> + w + g</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Bernoulli's equation is the energy equation along a streamline for steady, incompressible, frictionless flow with <b>no shaft work</b> (no pump or turbine) and no heat transfer.</p>
<p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> No energy is added or taken out between the two sections; total head stays constant.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> For steady flow, continuity A<sub>1</sub>V<sub>1</sub> = A<sub>2</sub>V<sub>2</sub> makes velocity depend on cross-section: a smaller area means a higher velocity.</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong):</b> The equation is dimensionally inconsistent (p/ρ is energy per unit mass while v²/2g and z are heads), and it ends in "+ w + g", which has no meaning. With a pump and a turbine the correct form (per unit mass) is<div class="formula">p<sub>1</sub>/ρ + v<sub>1</sub>²/2 + g z<sub>1</sub> + q = p<sub>2</sub>/ρ + v<sub>2</sub>²/2 + g z<sub>2</sub> + w</div>or, in heads, p/ρg + v²/2g + z + H<sub>pump</sub> = … + H<sub>turbine</sub> + h<sub>L</sub>.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Bernoulli assumptions: steady, incompressible, inviscid, along a streamline (or irrotational flow between any two points), no shaft work, no heat transfer.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-6", year: 2017, no: 6, subject: "fm", topic: "Laminar pipe flow head loss",
q: `An oil flows through a pipe at a velocity of 1·0 m/s. The pipe is 45 m long and has 150 mm diameter. What is the head loss due to friction, if ρ = 869 kg/m³ and μ = 0·0814 kg/m s?`,
opts: ["0·61 m", "0·51 m", "0·41 m", "0·31 m"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 0·61 m</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> First check the flow regime with the Reynolds number; it turns out laminar, so the friction factor is 64/Re and Darcy–Weisbach gives the head loss.</p>
<p><b>Given</b></p><ul><li>V = 1·0 m/s, L = 45 m, D = 0·15 m, ρ = 869 kg/m³, μ = 0·0814 Pa·s</li></ul>
<div class="formula">Re = ρVD/μ &nbsp;&nbsp; f = 64/Re (laminar) &nbsp;&nbsp; h<sub>f</sub> = f L V² / (2 g D)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>Re = Reynolds number; f = Darcy friction factor; h<sub>f</sub> = friction head loss (m of oil)</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Re = 869 × 1 × 0·15 / 0·0814 = 1601 &lt; 2000 → laminar.</li>
<li>f = 64/1601 = 0·0400.</li>
<li>h<sub>f</sub> = 0·04 × 45 × 1² / (2 × 9·81 × 0·15) = 1·80/2·943 = <b>0·61 m</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 0·51 follows from using a turbulent friction factor, the Fanning factor (f/4), or dropping g wrongly. Hagen–Poiseuille confirms (a): h<sub>f</sub> = 32 μ L V/(ρ g D²) = 32 × 0·0814 × 45 × 1/(869 × 9·81 × 0·0225) = 0·61 m.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0·41 follows from using a turbulent friction factor, the Fanning factor (f/4), or dropping g wrongly. Hagen–Poiseuille confirms (a): h<sub>f</sub> = 32 μ L V/(ρ g D²) = 32 × 0·0814 × 45 × 1/(869 × 9·81 × 0·0225) = 0·61 m.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0·31 m follows from using a turbulent friction factor, the Fanning factor (f/4), or dropping g wrongly. Hagen–Poiseuille confirms (a): h<sub>f</sub> = 32 μ L V/(ρ g D²) = 32 × 0·0814 × 45 × 1/(869 × 9·81 × 0·0225) = 0·61 m.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> In laminar flow h<sub>f</sub> ∝ V (not V²); in fully rough turbulent flow h<sub>f</sub> ∝ V².</p>` },

{ id: "2017-7", year: 2017, no: 7, subject: "fm", topic: "Reynolds number and transition",
q: `Consider the following statements :<ol><li>At low Reynolds numbers of any flow, viscous forces dominate over inertial forces.</li><li>Transition from laminar to turbulent flow occurs over a range of Reynolds numbers depending on the surface presented to the flow.</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Both 1 and 2</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The Reynolds number Re = ρVL/μ is the ratio of inertial to viscous forces.</p>
<p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> Small Re means viscous forces are large compared with inertia; disturbances are damped and the flow is laminar (creeping flow at Re ≪ 1).</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> Transition is not at one fixed number. It spreads over a range that depends on surface roughness, entrance shape, upstream disturbances and pressure gradient. In pipes it usually lies between about 2000 and 4000; with great care laminar flow has been kept to Re above 40,000. For a flat plate, transition is around Re<sub>x</sub> ≈ 5 × 10<sup>5</sup>.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Omits 1, 2, which are also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Typical critical values: pipe 2000–4000; flat plate 3 × 10<sup>5</sup> to 5 × 10<sup>5</sup>; open channel about 500 (based on hydraulic radius); flow past a sphere about 1 (Stokes' law limit).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-8", year: 2017, no: 8, subject: "fm", topic: "Hagen–Poiseuille flow",
q: `In a steady laminar flow of a given discharge through a circular pipe of diameter D, the head loss is proportional to`,
opts: ["D<sup>−1</sup>", "D<sup>−2</sup>", "D<sup>−3</sup>", "D<sup>−4</sup>"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) D<sup>−4</sup></h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For laminar flow the Hagen–Poiseuille equation gives the head loss.</p>
<div class="formula">h<sub>f</sub> = 32 μ L V / (ρ g D²) and V = 4Q/(πD²) → h<sub>f</sub> = 128 μ L Q / (π ρ g D⁴)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>μ = dynamic viscosity; L = pipe length; V = mean velocity; Q = discharge; D = diameter</li></ul>
<p>With Q fixed, h<sub>f</sub> ∝ 1/D⁴. Halving the diameter raises the head loss 16 times.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>h_f = 32μLV/(ρgD²) with V = 4Q/(πD²).</li><li>h_f = 128μLQ/(πρgD⁴).</li><li>Q fixed ⇒ h_f ∝ <b>D⁻⁴</b> (halving D raises h_f 16 times).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> D⁻¹ would hold for neither laminar nor turbulent flow at fixed Q.</li><li><b>(b)</b> D<sup>−2</sup> holds at fixed <i>velocity</i>, not fixed discharge.</li><li><b>(c)</b> D<sup>−3</sup> mixes the two.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Turbulent flow at fixed Q (Darcy, constant f): h<sub>f</sub> = 8 f L Q²/(π² g D⁵), so h<sub>f</sub> ∝ D<sup>−5</sup>.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-9", year: 2017, no: 9, subject: "fm", topic: "Streamline equation",
q: `A two-dimensional flow field is defined as V = i x − j y. The equation of the stream-line passing through the point (1, 2) is`,
opts: ["xy + 2 = 0", "x²y + 2 = 0", "xy − 2 = 0", "x²y − 2 = 0"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) xy − 2 = 0</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A streamline is everywhere tangent to the velocity vector.</p>
<div class="formula">dx/u = dy/v, with u = x and v = −y</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>dx/x = dy/(−y) → ln x = −ln y + ln C.</li>
<li>ln(xy) = ln C → xy = C.</li>
<li>Through (1, 2): C = 1 × 2 = 2, so the streamline is <b>xy = 2</b>, i.e. xy − 2 = 0 (a rectangular hyperbola: flow near a stagnation point / corner).</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> xy + 2 = 0 gives xy = −2; at (1, 2) it gives 4 ≠ 0.</li><li><b>(b)</b> xes²y = constant would need dx/x = dy/(−2y), i.e. v = −2y.</li><li><b>(d)</b> xes²y = constant would need dx/x = dy/(−2y), i.e. v = −2y.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Check continuity: ∂u/∂x + ∂v/∂y = 1 − 1 = 0, so the flow is incompressible. The stream function is ψ = xy (u = ∂ψ/∂y, v = −∂ψ/∂x).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-10", year: 2017, no: 10, subject: "fm", topic: "Laminar velocity profile",
q: `The centre-line velocity in a pipe flow is 2 m/s. What is the average flow velocity in the pipe if the Reynolds number of the flow is 800?`,
opts: ["2 m/s", "1·5 m/s", "1 m/s", "0·5 m/s"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 m/s</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Re = 800 is below 2000, so the flow is laminar with a parabolic (Hagen–Poiseuille) profile.</p>
<div class="formula">u(r) = u<sub>max</sub> (1 − r²/R²) → V<sub>avg</sub> = u<sub>max</sub>/2</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>u<sub>max</sub> = centre-line velocity; R = pipe radius</li></ul>
<p>V<sub>avg</sub> = 2/2 = <b>1 m/s</b>.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 2 m/s would mean a flat (uniform) profile.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 1·5 m/s is too high even for turbulent flow at this Re; turbulent flow gives V/u<sub>max</sub> ≈ 0·8–0·87.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0·5 m/s is a quarter of u<sub>max</sub>, which matches no standard profile.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Between parallel plates (laminar) V<sub>avg</sub> = (2/3) u<sub>max</sub>; in a pipe, ½ u<sub>max</sub>.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-11", year: 2017, no: 11, subject: "thermo", topic: "Constant-pressure process",
q: `During a constant pressure expansion of a gas, 33·3% heat is converted into work while the temperature rises by 20 K. The specific heat of the gas at constant pressure as a proportion of work, W is`,
opts: ["8%", "10%", "12%", "15%"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 15%</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> At constant pressure, heat added Q = m c<sub>p</sub> ΔT and work W = p ΔV = m R ΔT. One third of the heat becomes work.</p><div class="formula">Q = mc_pΔT</div><div class="formula">W = pΔV = mRΔT</div><div class="formula">W/Q = R/c_p</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>W = Q/3 → Q = 3W.</li>
<li>Q = m c<sub>p</sub> ΔT → c<sub>p</sub> = Q/(m ΔT) = 3W/(m × 20).</li>
<li>Per unit mass: c<sub>p</sub> = 0·15 W, i.e. c<sub>p</sub> is <b>15% of W</b> (numerically, per kelvin).</li></ol>
<p>Check: W/Q = R/c<sub>p</sub> = (γ − 1)/γ = 1/3 → γ = 1·5, a consistent gas.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 8% would need a temperature rise of 37·5, 30 or 25 K respectively (c<sub>p</sub> = 3W/ΔT).</li><li><b>(b)</b> 10% would need a temperature rise of 37·5, 30 or 25 K respectively (c<sub>p</sub> = 3W/ΔT).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 12% would need a temperature rise of 37·5, 30 or 25 K respectively (c<sub>p</sub> = 3W/ΔT).</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> At constant pressure, the fraction of heat converted into work is R/c<sub>p</sub> = 1 − 1/γ: 0·286 for air.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-12", year: 2017, no: 12, subject: "thermo", topic: "Isothermal compression work",
q: `A cylinder contains 10 m³ of an ideal gas at a pressure of 2 bar. This gas is compressed in a reversible isothermal process till its pressure increases to 15 bar. What quantum of work will be required for this process? (You can use the table given herewith.)<table><tr><th>Number</th><th>2</th><th>2·5</th><th>3</th><th>5</th><th>7</th></tr><tr><td>log<sub>10</sub></td><td>0·301</td><td>0·397</td><td>0·475</td><td>0·698</td><td>0·845</td></tr></table>`,
opts: ["4500 kJ", "4030 kJ", "450 kJ", "403 kJ"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 4030 kJ</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Isothermal compression work.</p><div class="formula">W = p<sub>1</sub>V<sub>1</sub> ln(p<sub>2</sub>/p<sub>1</sub>) = 2·303 p<sub>1</sub>V<sub>1</sub> log<sub>10</sub>(p<sub>2</sub>/p<sub>1</sub>)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>p<sub>1</sub> = 2 bar = 200 kPa; V<sub>1</sub> = 10 m³; p<sub>2</sub>/p<sub>1</sub> = 15/2 = 7·5</li><li>For an isothermal process pV = constant, so p<sub>1</sub>V<sub>1</sub> = mRT</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>log 7·5 = log(3 × 5/2) = 0·475 + 0·698 − 0·301 = 0·872.</li>
<li>ln 7·5 = 2·303 × 0·872 = 2·008.</li>
<li>W = 200 × 10 × 2·008 ≈ 4016 kJ ≈ <b>4030 kJ</b> (ln 7·5 = 2·015 exactly gives 4030 kJ), done <i>on</i> the gas.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 4500 kJ would need ln(p<sub>2</sub>/p<sub>1</sub>) = 2·25 (pressure ratio about 9·5), not 7·5.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 450 kJ is a factor of 10 too small (taking 2 bar as 20 kPa).</li><li><b>(d)</b> 403 kJ is a factor of 10 too small (taking 2 bar as 20 kPa).</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Of all reversible compression paths between the same pressures, isothermal needs the least work; adiabatic needs the most.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-13", year: 2017, no: 13, subject: "thermo", topic: "Entropy principle",
q: `A system of 100 kg mass undergoes a process in which its specific entropy increases from 0·3 kJ/kg K to 0·4 kJ/kg K. At the same time, the entropy of the surroundings decreases from 80 kJ/kg K to 75 kJ/kg K. The process is`,
opts: ["reversible and isothermal", "irreversible", "reversible only", "isothermal only"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) irreversible</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Apply the increase-of-entropy principle: ΔS<sub>universe</sub> = ΔS<sub>system</sub> + ΔS<sub>surroundings</sub>. It is &gt; 0 for an irreversible process, = 0 for a reversible one, and &lt; 0 is impossible.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>ΔS<sub>system</sub> = m (s<sub>2</sub> − s<sub>1</sub>) = 100 × (0·4 − 0·3) = +10 kJ/K.</li>
<li>ΔS<sub>surroundings</sub> = 75 − 80 = −5 kJ/K. The question prints "kJ/kg K", but the surroundings' total entropy is meant.</li>
<li>ΔS<sub>universe</sub> = 10 − 5 = +5 kJ/K &gt; 0 → <b>irreversible</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A reversible process would need ΔS<sub>universe</sub> = 0.</li><li><b>(c)</b> A reversible process would need ΔS<sub>universe</sub> = 0.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Nothing about temperature is given, so "isothermal" cannot be concluded; isothermal or not, the process is irreversible.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Entropy generation S<sub>gen</sub> = ΔS<sub>universe</sub>; lost work (irreversibility) I = T<sub>0</sub> S<sub>gen</sub> (Gouy–Stodola).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-14", year: 2017, no: 14, subject: "thermo", topic: "Reversed heat engine COP",
q: `A reversible heat engine rejects 80% of the heat supplied during a cycle of operation. If the engine is reversed and operates as a refrigerator, then its coefficient of performance shall be`,
opts: ["6", "5", "4", "3"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 4</h3><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Heat supplied Q<sub>1</sub> = 1, rejected Q<sub>2</sub> = 0·8, so W = 0·2 and η = 0·2.</li>
<li>Reversed, the same quantities flow the other way: the refrigerator takes Q<sub>2</sub> = 0·8 from the cold body using W = 0·2.</li></ol>
<div class="formula">COP<sub>R</sub> = Q<sub>2</sub>/W = 0·8/0·2 = 4 &nbsp;&nbsp; (also COP<sub>R</sub> = 1/η − 1 = 5 − 1)</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 5 = 1/0.2 is the heat-pump COP (Q₁/W), not the refrigerator COP.</li><li><b>(a)</b> 6 would need 1/(1 − 0.8) + 1 – adds an extra 1 to the heat-pump COP.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 3 does not equal Q₂/W = 0.8/0.2.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The reversed machine works between the same two reservoirs, so the temperature ratio and hence the heat ratio Q<sub>2</sub>/Q<sub>1</sub> = 0·8 stay the same; only the directions of heat and work flow change.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> For reversible machines between the same reservoirs: COP<sub>HP</sub> = 1/η<sub>engine</sub>, COP<sub>R</sub> = COP<sub>HP</sub> − 1. Here T<sub>2</sub>/T<sub>1</sub> = 0·8, so COP<sub>R</sub> = T<sub>2</sub>/(T<sub>1</sub> − T<sub>2</sub>) = 0·8/0·2 = 4 as well.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-15", year: 2017, no: 15, subject: "thermo", topic: "Brayton cycle work and efficiency",
q: `For the same efficiency of the Brayton cycle and the Carnot cycle working between temperature limits of T<sub>max</sub> and T<sub>min</sub>, the power contribution of the Brayton cycle will be`,
opts: ["zero", "maximum", "minimum", "50% of the Carnot cycle"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) zero</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Ideal Brayton efficiency is η = 1 − T<sub>1</sub>/T<sub>2</sub> = 1 − 1/r<sub>p</sub><sup>(γ−1)/γ</sup>, where T<sub>2</sub> is the temperature after isentropic compression. It equals the Carnot value 1 − T<sub>min</sub>/T<sub>max</sub> only when T<sub>2</sub> = T<sub>max</sub>, i.e. compression alone reaches the maximum temperature.</p>
<p>Then no heat can be added in the combustor (T<sub>3</sub> = T<sub>2</sub>), the cycle collapses to a line, and the net work is <b>zero</b>.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Maximum net work occurs at the optimum pressure ratio r<sub>p</sub> = (T<sub>max</sub>/T<sub>min</sub>)<sup>γ/(2(γ−1))</sup>, where T<sub>2</sub> = T<sub>4</sub> = √(T<sub>max</sub>T<sub>min</sub>) and efficiency is 1 − √(T<sub>min</sub>/T<sub>max</sub>), less than Carnot.</li><li><b>(c)</b> "Minimum" is not specific: zero is the exact value.</li><li><b>(d)</b> There is no 50% relation.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Work output is zero at r<sub>p</sub> = 1 and at the r<sub>p</sub> giving Carnot efficiency; it peaks in between.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-16", year: 2017, no: 16, subject: "thermo", topic: "Cycle efficiency from a T–s diagram",
q: `The thermal efficiency of the hypothetical cycle shown is<img class="fig" src="img/me/2017-16.png" alt="T–s diagram: triangle with base at T = 300 K from S = 1 to 5 kJ/K and apex at T = 700 K, S = 3 kJ/K">`,
opts: ["0·6", "0·5", "0·4", "0·3"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 0·4</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> On a T–s diagram the area under a reversible path is the heat transferred; heat is added where s increases and rejected where s decreases. The enclosed area is the net work.</p><div class="formula">Q = ∫T ds (area under the T–s path)</div><div class="formula">η = (Q_in − Q_out)/Q_in</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Heat is added along 1 → apex → 5 (s rises from 1 to 5 kJ/K): Q<sub>in</sub> = area under the two sloping lines = rectangle 300 × 4 + triangle ½ × 4 × 400 = 1200 + 800 = 2000 kJ.</li>
<li>Heat is rejected along the base 5 → 1 at 300 K: Q<sub>out</sub> = 300 × 4 = 1200 kJ.</li>
<li>Net work = triangle area = 800 kJ; η = 800/2000 = <b>0·4</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 0·5 compares the work with heat added only up to the apex.</li><li><b>(a)</b> 0·6 is close to the Carnot limit 1 − 300/700 = 0·571, which no cycle between these limits can exceed.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0·3 underestimates the triangle.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Any cycle between T<sub>max</sub> and T<sub>min</sub> other than Carnot has a lower efficiency because its mean temperature of heat addition is below T<sub>max</sub>.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-17", year: 2017, no: 17, subject: "thermo", topic: "Carnot engine heat rejection",
q: `A heat engine working on the Carnot cycle receives heat at the rate of 50 kW from a source at 1300 K and rejects it to a sink at 400 K. The heat rejected is`,
opts: ["20·3 kW", "15·4 kW", "12·4 kW", "10·8 kW"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 15·4 kW</h3><div class="formula">Q<sub>2</sub>/Q<sub>1</sub> = T<sub>2</sub>/T<sub>1</sub> (reversible engine)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>Q<sub>1</sub> = heat received = 50 kW at T<sub>1</sub> = 1300 K; Q<sub>2</sub> = heat rejected at T<sub>2</sub> = 400 K</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Q<sub>2</sub> = 50 × 400/1300 = <b>15·38 kW</b>; work = 34·6 kW; η = 69·2%.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Q₂ = Q₁ × T₂/T₁ = 50 × 400/1300.</li><li>Q₂ = <b>15.4 kW</b>; W = 34.6 kW, η = 69.2 %.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 20·3 does not satisfy Q<sub>2</sub>/Q<sub>1</sub> = T<sub>2</sub>/T<sub>1</sub>. A real engine would reject <i>more</i> than 15·4 kW, never less.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 12·4 does not satisfy Q<sub>2</sub>/Q<sub>1</sub> = T<sub>2</sub>/T<sub>1</sub>. A real engine would reject <i>more</i> than 15·4 kW, never less.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 10·8 kW does not satisfy Q<sub>2</sub>/Q<sub>1</sub> = T<sub>2</sub>/T<sub>1</sub>. A real engine would reject <i>more</i> than 15·4 kW, never less.</li></ul>
<p>Work output W = Q<sub>1</sub> − Q<sub>2</sub> = 50 − 15·38 = 34·62 kW, and η = 1 − T<sub>2</sub>/T<sub>1</sub> = 1 − 400/1300 = 0·692, which checks: 34·62/50 = 0·692.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Clausius: ΣQ/T = −50/1300 + 15·38/400 = 0 for a reversible cycle; &lt; 0 for an irreversible one.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-18", year: 2017, no: 18, subject: "thermo", topic: "Throttling irreversibility",
q: `An ideal gas is flowing through an insulated pipe at the rate of 3·3 kg. There is a pressure drop of 15% from the inlet to the outlet of the pipe. What is the rate of energy loss because of this pressure drop due to friction, given that R<sub>gas</sub> = 0·287 kJ/kg K and the reference temperature T<sub>0</sub> is 300 K?`,
opts: ["42·62 kW", "40·26 kW", "38·14 kW", "35·13 kW"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 42·62 kW</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Friction in an insulated pipe is a throttling process: enthalpy is constant, and for an ideal gas so is temperature. The loss of available energy is T<sub>0</sub> times the entropy generated (Gouy–Stodola). The rate is taken as 3·3 kg/s.</p>
<div class="formula">ṡ<sub>gen</sub> = ṁ R ln(p<sub>1</sub>/p<sub>2</sub>) &nbsp;&nbsp; İ = T<sub>0</sub> ṡ<sub>gen</sub></div>
<ul class="sym"><li>ṁ = mass flow rate; R = gas constant; p<sub>1</sub>, p<sub>2</sub> = inlet and outlet pressures (p<sub>2</sub> = 0·85 p<sub>1</sub>); T<sub>0</sub> = surroundings temperature</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Exactly: ln(1/0·85) = 0·1625 → İ = 300 × 3·3 × 0·287 × 0·1625 = 46·2 kW, which is not an option.</li>
<li>The options use the small-drop approximation ln(p<sub>1</sub>/p<sub>2</sub>) ≈ Δp/p = 0·15: İ = 300 × 3·3 × 0·287 × 0·15 = <b>42·62 kW</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> is further below even the approximate value; none matches either the exact (46·2) or the linearised (42·62) result.</li><li><b>(c)</b> is further below even the approximate value; none matches either the exact (46·2) or the linearised (42·62) result.</li><li><b>(d)</b> is further below even the approximate value; none matches either the exact (46·2) or the linearised (42·62) result.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> The approximation ln(1 + x) ≈ x is fine for drops of a few percent; at 15% it underestimates the loss by about 8%.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-19", year: 2017, no: 19, subject: "thermo", topic: "Conduction through a lining",
q: `A furnace is provided with an insulating refractory lining. The overall thermal conductivity of the material is 0·03 W/m K. The thickness of the lining is 100 mm. The inner and outer temperatures are 250 °C and 50 °C, respectively. The heat loss to the surroundings will be`,
opts: ["30 J/m²/s", "60 J/m²/s", "60 J/s", "30 J/s"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 60 J/m²/s</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Formula (Fourier's law, plane wall)</b></p><div class="formula">q = Q/A = k (T<sub>1</sub> − T<sub>2</sub>)/L</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>k = 0·03 W/m K; T<sub>1</sub> − T<sub>2</sub> = 250 − 50 = 200 K; L = 0·1 m</li></ul>
<p>q = 0·03 × 200/0·1 = <b>60 W/m²</b> = 60 J/m²·s. The area is not given, so only the heat flux can be found.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>ΔT = 250 − 50 = 200 K, L = 0.1 m.</li><li>q = 0.03 × 200/0.1 = <b>60 W/m² = 60 J/m²·s</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(c)</b> 60 J/s is a total heat rate, which needs an area (it would be right only for 1 m²).</li><li><b>(a)</b> 30 J/m²/s halves the temperature difference or double the thickness.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 30 J/s halves the temperature difference or double the thickness.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Thermal resistance of a plane wall: R = L/(kA). Layers in series add: R<sub>total</sub> = ΣL<sub>i</sub>/(k<sub>i</sub>A).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-20", year: 2017, no: 20, subject: "thermo", topic: "Thermal resistance",
q: `A wall of 0·6 m thickness has normal area of 1·5 m² and is made up of material of thermal conductivity 0·4 W/m K. If the temperatures on the two sides of the wall are 800 °C and 1000 °C, the thermal resistance of the wall is`,
opts: ["1·8 K/W", "1·8 W/K", "1 K/W", "1 W/K"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 K/W</h3><div class="formula">R<sub>th</sub> = L/(kA)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>L = 0·6 m; k = 0·4 W/m K; A = 1·5 m²</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> R<sub>th</sub> = 0·6/(0·4 × 1·5) = <b>1 K/W</b>. The temperatures are not needed: they would give the heat flow Q = ΔT/R = 200 W.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> has the wrong unit: W/K is thermal <i>conductance</i> (1/R).</li><li><b>(d)</b> has the wrong unit: W/K is thermal <i>conductance</i> (1/R).</li><li><b>(a)</b> 1·8 K/W is not L/(kA) for these values.</li></ul>
<p>Resistance depends only on geometry and material, not on the temperatures. Doubling the thickness doubles R; doubling the area or the conductivity halves it.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Electrical analogy: ΔT ↔ voltage, Q ↔ current, R<sub>th</sub> ↔ resistance (K/W).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-21", year: 2017, no: 21, subject: "thermo", topic: "Convection coefficient from Nusselt number",
q: `Heat is lost from a 100 mm diameter steam pipe placed horizontally in ambient air at 30 °C. If the Nusselt number is 25 and the thermal conductivity of the air is 0·03 W/m K, then the heat transfer coefficient will be`,
opts: ["7·5 W/m² K", "15 W/m² K", "25 W/m² K", "35 W/m² K"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 7·5 W/m² K</h3><div class="formula">Nu = h D/k → h = Nu k / D</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>Nu = Nusselt number (ratio of convective to conductive heat transfer); D = characteristic length (pipe diameter, 0·1 m); k = conductivity of the <i>fluid</i> (air)</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> h = 25 × 0·03/0·1 = <b>7·5 W/m² K</b>, a typical natural-convection value.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>h = Nu k/D.</li><li>h = 25 × 0.03/0.1 = <b>7.5 W/m²K</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 15 uses the radius (0·05 m) instead of the diameter.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 25 equates h with Nu.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 35 W/m²K would need Nu ≈ 117.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Typical h: free convection in gases 2–25, forced convection in gases 25–250, liquids 50–20,000, boiling/condensation 2500–100,000 W/m² K.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-22", year: 2017, no: 22, subject: "thermo", topic: "Forced convection from a sphere",
q: `Air at 1 atmospheric pressure and 27 °C blows across a 12 mm diameter sphere at a free stream velocity of 4 m/s. A small heater inside the sphere maintains the surface temperature at 77 °C. With k = 0·026 W/m (kelvin) and with (Nu) = 31·4, the heat loss by the sphere would be`,
opts: ["1·93 J/s", "1·76 J/s", "1·65 J/s", "1·54 J/s"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1·54 J/s</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Forced convection from a sphere.</p><div class="formula">h = Nu k/D &nbsp;&nbsp; Q = h A (T<sub>s</sub> − T<sub>∞</sub>), A = π D²</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>D = 0·012 m; k = 0·026 W/m K; Nu = 31·4; T<sub>s</sub> − T<sub>∞</sub> = 77 − 27 = 50 K; A = surface area of the sphere</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>h = 31·4 × 0·026/0·012 = 68·0 W/m² K.</li>
<li>A = π × 0·012² = 4·524 × 10<sup>−4</sup> m².</li>
<li>Q = 68·0 × 4·524 × 10<sup>−4</sup> × 50 = <b>1·54 W</b> (J/s).</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> comes from rounding h or using a wrong area (for example 4πR² with a slip in R). The given Nu and k fix the answer at 1·54 W.</li><li><b>(b)</b> comes from rounding h or using a wrong area (for example 4πR² with a slip in R). The given Nu and k fix the answer at 1·54 W.</li><li><b>(c)</b> comes from rounding h or using a wrong area (for example 4πR² with a slip in R). The given Nu and k fix the answer at 1·54 W.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Whitaker correlation for a sphere: Nu = 2 + (0·4 Re<sup>1/2</sup> + 0·06 Re<sup>2/3</sup>) Pr<sup>0·4</sup> (μ/μ<sub>s</sub>)<sup>1/4</sup>. The "2" is pure conduction to an infinite still medium.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-23", year: 2017, no: 23, subject: "thermo", topic: "Radiation view factors",
q: `The view factors F<sub>12</sub> and F<sub>21</sub>, for the sphere of diameter d and a cubical box of length l = d as shown in the figure, respectively, are<img class="fig" src="img/me/2017-23.png" alt="Sphere A1 of diameter d inscribed in a cube A2 of side l = d">`,
opts: ["1 and π/3", "π/3 and 1", "1 and π/6", "π/6 and 1"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and π/6</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Radiation view factors.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>The sphere (surface 1) is convex and completely enclosed by the cube, so all radiation leaving it hits the cube: <b>F<sub>12</sub> = 1</b>.</li>
<li>Reciprocity: A<sub>1</sub>F<sub>12</sub> = A<sub>2</sub>F<sub>21</sub>.</li>
<li>A<sub>1</sub> = π d², A<sub>2</sub> = 6 l² = 6 d² → F<sub>21</sub> = π d²/6 d² = <b>π/6</b> (≈ 0·52; the rest, F<sub>22</sub> = 1 − π/6, returns to the cube).</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> π/3 uses the area of 3 faces or a wrong sphere area (2πd²/6).</li><li><b>(b)</b> swaps the two factors: F<sub>21</sub> = 1 would mean the cube sees only the sphere, impossible for a concave enclosure.</li><li><b>(d)</b> swaps the two factors: F<sub>21</sub> = 1 would mean the cube sees only the sphere, impossible for a concave enclosure.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Rules: summation ΣF<sub>ij</sub> = 1; reciprocity A<sub>i</sub>F<sub>ij</sub> = A<sub>j</sub>F<sub>ji</sub>; a flat or convex surface has F<sub>ii</sub> = 0.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-24", year: 2017, no: 24, subject: "icrac", topic: "Knock in SI engines",
q: `Knocking in a spark ignition engine can be reduced by<ol><li>retarding the spark</li><li>supercharging</li><li>increasing the engine speed</li><li>using a fuel of long straight chain structure</li></ol>Select the correct answer using the code given below.`,
opts: ["1 and 4", "1 and 3", "2 and 3", "2 and 4"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> SI knock is auto-ignition of the end gas before the flame front reaches it. It is reduced by lowering end-gas temperature/pressure, shortening the time the end gas is exposed, or using a fuel with a longer ignition delay (higher octane).</p>
<p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul>
<li><b>1 (reduces knock):</b> Retarding the spark makes peak pressure and temperature occur later and lower.</li>
<li><b>2 (increases knock):</b> Supercharging raises the density, pressure and temperature of the charge.</li>
<li><b>3 (reduces knock):</b> Higher speed increases turbulence, so the flame travels faster and the end gas has less time to auto-ignite.</li>
<li><b>4 (increases knock):</b> Long straight-chain paraffins (such as n-heptane, octane number 0) knock readily. Branched chains (iso-octane) and aromatics resist knock.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 4, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 3.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 2, 4, which are incorrect; Omits 1, 3, which are also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> In CI engines most of these act the other way: CI knock falls with supercharging, a higher compression ratio and fuels of high cetane number (straight chains). That is why a good diesel fuel makes a poor petrol.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-25", year: 2017, no: 25, subject: "icrac", topic: "Indicated thermal efficiency (Morse-type test)",
q: `A 4-cylinder diesel engine running at 1200 r.p.m. developed 18·6 kW. The average torque when one cylinder was cut out was 105 N m. If the calorific value of the fuel was 42000 kJ/kg and the engine used 0·34 kg of diesel/kW hr, the indicated thermal efficiency was nearly`,
opts: ["29%", "26%", "31%", "23%"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 29%</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Cutting out one cylinder (at the same speed) removes that cylinder's indicated power; friction stays about the same. The drop in brake power therefore equals the indicated power of one cylinder.</p>
<div class="formula">BP = 2πNT/60 &nbsp;&nbsp; IP<sub>cyl</sub> = BP<sub>all</sub> − BP<sub>one cut</sub> &nbsp;&nbsp; η<sub>ith</sub> = IP/(ṁ<sub>f</sub> × CV)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>N = 1200 rpm; T = 105 N m with one cylinder cut; ṁ<sub>f</sub> = bsfc × BP; CV = 42,000 kJ/kg</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>BP with 3 cylinders = 2π × 1200 × 105/60 = 13·19 kW.</li>
<li>IP of one cylinder = 18·6 − 13·19 = 5·41 kW → total IP = 4 × 5·41 = 21·64 kW (equal cylinders assumed).</li>
<li>Fuel: 0·34 × 18·6 = 6·324 kg/h → heat input = 6·324 × 42,000/3600 = 73·8 kW.</li>
<li>η<sub>ith</sub> = 21·64/73·8 = <b>29·3%</b>. (Brake thermal efficiency = 18·6/73·8 = 25·2%; mechanical efficiency = 86%.)</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 26% is close to the <i>brake</i> thermal efficiency.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 31% comes from using 3 cylinders or forgetting to scale the fuel to the full brake power.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 23% comes from using 3 cylinders or forgetting to scale the fuel to the full brake power.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> bsfc of 0·34 kg/kWh corresponds to η<sub>bth</sub> = 3600/(0·34 × 42,000) = 25·2%.</p>` }
);

// ---- q2017b.js ----
// ESE 2017 Prelims Paper II, Mechanical Engineering, Series A, Q26–50
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2017-26", year: 2017, no: 26, subject: "icrac", topic: "Morse test",
q: `In a Morse test on a 2-cylinder, 2-stroke SI engine, the brake power is 9 kW and the BHP of individual cylinders with spark cutoff are 4·25 kW and 3·75 kW, respectively. The mechanical efficiency of the engine is`,
opts: ["90%", "80%", "52·5%", "45·5%"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 90%</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In a Morse test each cylinder is cut out in turn at the same speed. The fall in brake power equals the indicated power of the cylinder that was cut out (friction is assumed unchanged).</p>
<div class="formula">IP<sub>n</sub> = BP − BP<sub>(n cut out)</sub> &nbsp;&nbsp; IP = Σ IP<sub>n</sub> &nbsp;&nbsp; η<sub>m</sub> = BP/IP</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>BP = brake power with all cylinders firing (9 kW)</li><li>BP<sub>(n cut out)</sub> = brake power with cylinder n not firing: 4·25 kW and 3·75 kW</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>IP<sub>1</sub> = 9 − 4·25 = 4·75 kW; IP<sub>2</sub> = 9 − 3·75 = 5·25 kW.</li>
<li>IP = 4·75 + 5·25 = 10 kW; friction power = 10 − 9 = 1 kW.</li>
<li>η<sub>m</sub> = 9/10 = <b>90%</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 80% treats (4·25 + 3·75) = 8 kW as the indicated power.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 52·5% divides one cylinder's figure by the total; it is not mechanical efficiencies.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 45·5% divides one cylinder's figure by the total; it is not mechanical efficiencies.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> The Morse test suits multi-cylinder SI engines only. It assumes that pumping and friction losses do not change when a cylinder is cut out.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-27", year: 2017, no: 27, subject: "icrac", topic: "Vapour compression cycle chart",
q: `The ordinates and abscissae of the diagram given for the vapour-compression refrigeration cycle represent<img class="fig" src="img/me/2017-27.png" alt="Cycle drawn on a chart with a saturation dome: horizontal evaporation and condensation lines, a vertical expansion line on the left and an inclined compression line on the right">`,
opts: ["pressure and volume", "temperature and entropy", "enthalpy and entropy", "pressure and enthalpy"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) pressure and enthalpy</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The giveaway is the <b>vertical</b> expansion line. Throttling in the expansion valve is at constant enthalpy, so it is a vertical line only on a chart with enthalpy on the horizontal axis. Evaporation and condensation are at constant pressure, so they are horizontal lines: <b>pressure (ordinate) against enthalpy (abscissa)</b>, the standard refrigeration (p–h) chart.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> On a p–v chart throttling is not vertical, and refrigeration charts are never drawn on p–v.</li><li><b>(b)</b> On T–s, throttling is an inclined curve (entropy rises), and compression is the vertical line (isentropic).</li><li><b>(c)</b> On h–s (Mollier) compression is vertical and throttling horizontal.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> The p–h chart lets you read the refrigerating effect (h<sub>1</sub> − h<sub>4</sub>), compressor work (h<sub>2</sub> − h<sub>1</sub>) and heat rejected (h<sub>2</sub> − h<sub>3</sub>) directly as horizontal lengths.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-28", year: 2017, no: 28, subject: "icrac", topic: "Refrigeration basics",
q: `Consider the following statements for refrigeration and air-conditioning :<ol><li>In a refrigerating machine, the heat exchanger that absorbs heat is connected to a conditioned space.</li><li>A refrigerating cycle operating reversibly between two heat reservoirs has the highest coefficient of performance.</li><li>The lower the refrigeration required and the higher the temperature of heat rejection to the surroundings, the larger the power consumption.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Refrigeration basics.</p><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> The evaporator absorbs heat, and it is placed in (or connected to) the space being cooled.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> By the Carnot corollary, no refrigerator working between two reservoirs can have a higher COP than a reversible one: COP<sub>Carnot</sub> = T<sub>L</sub>/(T<sub>H</sub> − T<sub>L</sub>).</li>
<li><b>3 (correct, read as temperatures):</b> A lower refrigeration (evaporator) temperature T<sub>L</sub> and a higher heat-rejection temperature T<sub>H</sub> both lower the COP, so more power is needed per unit of cooling: W = Q<sub>L</sub>(T<sub>H</sub> − T<sub>L</sub>)/T<sub>L</sub> for the ideal cycle.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Each 1 °C rise in condensing temperature or fall in evaporating temperature costs roughly 2–4% in COP. This is why condensers should be kept clean and well ventilated.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-29", year: 2017, no: 29, subject: "icrac", topic: "Carnot refrigerator power",
q: `In a refrigerator, the evaporator and condenser coil temperatures are −33 °C and 27 °C, respectively. Assuming that the maximum COP is realized, the required power input for a refrigerating effect of 4 kW is`,
opts: ["8 kW", "4 kW", "2 kW", "1 kW"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1 kW</h3><div class="formula">COP<sub>max</sub> = T<sub>L</sub>/(T<sub>H</sub> − T<sub>L</sub>) &nbsp;&nbsp; W = Q<sub>L</sub>/COP</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>T<sub>L</sub> = −33 + 273 = 240 K (evaporator); T<sub>H</sub> = 27 + 273 = 300 K (condenser)</li><li>Q<sub>L</sub> = refrigerating effect = 4 kW</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>COP = 240/(300 − 240) = 4.</li><li>W = 4/4 = <b>1 kW</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(c)</b> 2 kW would mean COP = 2; means COP = 0·5. All are below the Carnot COP, which the question says is realised.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 4 kW means COP = 1; means COP = 0·5. All are below the Carnot COP, which the question says is realised.</li><li><b>(a)</b> 8 kW means COP = 0·5. All are below the Carnot COP, which the question says is realised.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Heat rejected to the condenser = Q<sub>L</sub> + W = 5 kW. A real plant between these coil temperatures would need more than 1 kW, because its COP is lower than the Carnot value.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Always convert to kelvin: using °C here gives the nonsense value −33/60. Note also that the coil temperatures, not the room and ambient temperatures, fix this COP; real coils run 5–10 K colder and warmer than the spaces they serve.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-30", year: 2017, no: 30, subject: "icrac", topic: "Volumetric efficiency of compressors",
q: `Consider the following statements :<br>The volumetric efficiency of a reciprocating compressor can be improved by<ol><li>decreasing the clearance volume</li><li>cooling the intake air</li><li>heating the intake air</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["2 only", "3 only", "1 and 2", "1 and 3"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 2</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Volumetric efficiency of compressors.</p><div class="formula">η<sub>v</sub> = 1 + C − C (p<sub>2</sub>/p<sub>1</sub>)<sup>1/n</sup></div>
<ul class="sym"><li>C = clearance ratio V<sub>c</sub>/V<sub>s</sub>; p<sub>2</sub>/p<sub>1</sub> = pressure ratio; n = polytropic index of re-expansion</li></ul>
<p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul>
<li><b>1 (improves):</b> Less clearance means less compressed air re-expanding, so more of the stroke draws in fresh air.</li>
<li><b>2 (improves):</b> Cool intake air is denser, and less heating during suction (from hot cylinder walls) means more mass and a larger effective volume drawn in.</li>
<li><b>3 (worsens):</b> Heated intake air expands and is less dense, which lowers the mass and free-air volume delivered per stroke.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, 2, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Other factors that lower η<sub>v</sub>: higher pressure ratio, leakage past rings and valves, valve throttling (pressure drop during suction) and high speed.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-31", year: 2017, no: 31, subject: "icrac", topic: "Air in condensers",
q: `Consider the following statements :<br>The presence of air inside condensers<ol><li>remains as a non-condensable gas</li><li>reduces the condensing coefficient</li><li>tends to cling to the surface</li><li>introduces large thermal resistance</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1, 2, 3 and 4", "1, 2 and 3 only", "3 and 4 only", "1, 2 and 4 only"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1, 2, 3 and 4</h3><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> Air does not condense at condenser conditions; it stays as a non-condensable gas.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> It lowers the condensing heat transfer coefficient, sometimes by 50% or more with only a few percent of air.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> As vapour condenses, the air left behind collects in a thin layer next to the cold surface.</li>
<li><b>4 (correct):</b> Vapour must diffuse through this air film to reach the surface, which adds a large thermal resistance.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Air also raises the total condenser pressure (Dalton's law: p = p<sub>steam</sub> + p<sub>air</sub>), raising back pressure on the turbine or compressor. It is removed by air extraction pumps or steam-jet ejectors (power plants) and purgers (refrigeration).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3, 4.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 4, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 1, 2, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Vacuum efficiency of a steam condenser = actual vacuum / ideal vacuum (with no air present).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-32", year: 2017, no: 32, subject: "icrac", topic: "Ice plant refrigeration capacity",
q: `The refrigeration system of an ice plant working between temperatures of −5 °C and 25 °C produces 20 kg of ice per minute from water at 20 °C. The specific heat of water is 4·2 kJ/kg and latent heat of ice is 335 kJ/kg. The refrigeration capacity of the refrigeration plant is`,
opts: ["9040 kJ/min", "8750 kJ/min", "8380 kJ/min", "8010 kJ/min"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 8380 kJ/min</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Heat to be removed per kg = sensible heat to cool water from 20 °C to 0 °C plus latent heat of freezing. No specific heat of ice is given, so the ice is taken as delivered at 0 °C.</p>
<div class="formula">Q = ṁ [c<sub>w</sub>(t<sub>w</sub> − 0) + h<sub>sf</sub>]</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>ṁ = 20 kg/min; c<sub>w</sub> = 4·2 kJ/kg K; t<sub>w</sub> = 20 °C; h<sub>sf</sub> = latent heat of fusion = 335 kJ/kg</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Per kg: 4·2 × 20 + 335 = 84 + 335 = 419 kJ/kg.</li><li>Q = 20 × 419 = <b>8380 kJ/min</b> (≈ 140 kW ≈ 40 TR).</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 9040 adds extra heat (for example sub-cooling the ice to −5 °C with an assumed specific heat).</li><li><b>(b)</b> 8750 adds extra heat (for example sub-cooling the ice to −5 °C with an assumed specific heat).</li><li><b>(d)</b> 8010 omits part of the sensible heat.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> 1 TR = 3·5167 kW = 211 kJ/min: the cooling needed to freeze 1 short ton (907 kg) of water at 0 °C in 24 hours.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-33", year: 2017, no: 33, subject: "icrac", topic: "Vapour absorption refrigeration",
q: `Consider the following statements in respect of a vapour-absorption refrigeration cycle :<ol><li>The absorption refrigeration cycle is generally used when waste heat is available from an existing source or when free energy like solar energy is to be used.</li><li>There are no moving parts in the absorption refrigeration plant except a small liquid pump.</li><li>The value of the coefficient of performance is nearly the same in both vapour-absorption and vapour-compression refrigeration plants.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3", "1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Vapour absorption refrigeration.</p><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> VAR runs mainly on heat (in the generator), so it pays off where low-grade heat is cheap: exhaust or waste heat, steam, solar.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> The compressor is replaced by absorber + pump + generator. The only moving part is the small solution pump, which takes very little work because it pumps liquid. (The Electrolux three-fluid system has no pump at all.)</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong):</b> VAR COP (heat basis) is about 0·5–0·8 for single-effect LiBr–water or NH<sub>3</sub>–water and about 1·2 for double-effect. VCR COP is typically 3–5.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Ideal VAR COP = [(T<sub>g</sub> − T<sub>0</sub>)/T<sub>g</sub>] × [T<sub>e</sub>/(T<sub>0</sub> − T<sub>e</sub>)]: a Carnot engine (generator to ambient) driving a Carnot refrigerator.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-34", year: 2017, no: 34, subject: "icrac", topic: "Relative humidity from specific humidity",
q: `Air at 30 °C and 1 bar has a specific humidity of 0·016 kg/kg of dry air. By considering the saturation pressure of water vapour at 30 °C as 4·246 kPa, the relative humidity of the air will be`,
opts: ["66·1%", "60·2%", "58·8%", "56·8%"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 58·8%</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Relative humidity from specific humidity.</p><div class="formula">ω = 0·622 p<sub>v</sub>/(p − p<sub>v</sub>) → p<sub>v</sub> = ω p/(0·622 + ω) &nbsp;&nbsp; φ = p<sub>v</sub>/p<sub>sat</sub></div>
<ul class="sym"><li>ω = specific humidity (kg vapour per kg dry air) = 0·016; p = total pressure = 100 kPa</li><li>p<sub>v</sub> = partial pressure of vapour; p<sub>sat</sub> = saturation pressure at the dry-bulb temperature = 4·246 kPa</li><li>0·622 = ratio of molar masses of water and air, 18·015/28·97</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>p<sub>v</sub> = 0·016 × 100/(0·622 + 0·016) = 1·6/0·638 = 2·508 kPa.</li>
<li>φ = 2·508/4·246 = 0·591 ≈ 59%. The nearest option is <b>58·8%</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 66·1% is far from 59%.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 56·8% is far from 59%.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 60·2% would need a total pressure of about 102 kPa (it is closer if 1 atm = 101·325 kPa is used, giving 59·8%). With 1 bar as stated, 58·8% is nearest.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Degree of saturation μ = ω/ω<sub>s</sub> is slightly less than relative humidity φ; the two are equal at 0% and 100%.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-35", year: 2017, no: 35, subject: "icrac", topic: "Evaporative cooling",
q: `Consider the following statements in respect of an evaporative cooling process :<ol><li>The wet-bulb temperature remains constant.</li><li>The dew-point temperature remains constant.</li><li>The enthalpy remains constant.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3", "1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In evaporative cooling, unheated water evaporates into the air. The latent heat comes from the air's own sensible heat, so the process is (nearly) adiabatic saturation: it follows a constant wet-bulb temperature line on the psychrometric chart.</p>
<p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> Wet-bulb temperature stays constant (the defining feature).</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> Moisture is added, so the vapour pressure and specific humidity rise, and with them the dew-point temperature.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> Enthalpy is very nearly constant. The small enthalpy of the added water, h<sub>f</sub> at the WBT, is negligible.</li></ul>
<p>Dry-bulb temperature falls and relative humidity rises.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Sensible cooling (cooling coil above dew point): ω and DPT constant, WBT and h fall. Evaporative cooling: WBT and h constant, ω and DPT rise.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-36", year: 2017, no: 36, subject: "thermo", topic: "Availability (exergy) of a stream",
q: `For a steady process, the conditions at stage 1 and stage 2 are, respectively, h<sub>1</sub> = 300 kJ/kg, h<sub>2</sub> = 150 kJ/kg, S<sub>1</sub> = 1·25 kJ/kg K and S<sub>2</sub> = 0·8 kJ/kg K. The 'availability' at the ambient temperature 300 K will be`,
opts: ["15 kJ", "20 kJ", "25 kJ", "35 kJ"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 15 kJ</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The maximum useful work obtainable between two states of a steady-flow stream (kinetic and potential energy neglected), with heat exchange only with the surroundings at T<sub>0</sub>, is the change in the flow availability function b = h − T<sub>0</sub>s.</p>
<div class="formula">W<sub>max</sub> = (h<sub>1</sub> − h<sub>2</sub>) − T<sub>0</sub>(s<sub>1</sub> − s<sub>2</sub>)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>h = specific enthalpy; s = specific entropy; T<sub>0</sub> = 300 K (dead state / ambient)</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>h<sub>1</sub> − h<sub>2</sub> = 150 kJ/kg.</li><li>T<sub>0</sub>(s<sub>1</sub> − s<sub>2</sub>) = 300 × 0·45 = 135 kJ/kg.</li><li>W<sub>max</sub> = 150 − 135 = <b>15 kJ/kg</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 20 kJ would need T₀Δs = 130 kJ (T₀ ≈ 289 K).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 25 kJ would need T₀Δs = 125 kJ (T₀ ≈ 278 K).</li><li><b>(d)</b> 35 kJ would need T₀Δs = 115 kJ (T₀ ≈ 256 K).</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Entropy falls from 1 to 2, so the stream must reject heat T<sub>0</sub>Δs = 135 kJ/kg to the surroundings; only the remaining 15 kJ/kg of the enthalpy drop is available as work.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-37", year: 2017, no: 37, subject: "icrac", topic: "Otto vs Diesel at equal peak pressure",
q: `If the maximum pressure in both air-standard Otto and Diesel cycles is the same, then the relations for compression ratio r and the efficiency η between the two cycles are`,
opts: ["r<sub>Diesel</sub> &gt; r<sub>Otto</sub> and η<sub>Diesel</sub> &gt; η<sub>Otto</sub>", "r<sub>Otto</sub> &gt; r<sub>Diesel</sub> and η<sub>Diesel</sub> &gt; η<sub>Otto</sub>", "r<sub>Diesel</sub> &gt; r<sub>Otto</sub> and η<sub>Otto</sub> &gt; η<sub>Diesel</sub>", "r<sub>Otto</sub> &gt; r<sub>Diesel</sub> and η<sub>Otto</sub> &gt; η<sub>Diesel</sub>"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) r<sub>Diesel</sub> &gt; r<sub>Otto</sub> and η<sub>Diesel</sub> &gt; η<sub>Otto</sub></h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In the Otto cycle the peak pressure is reached by constant-volume heat addition after a modest compression. In the Diesel cycle the peak pressure is reached by compression alone, and heat is then added at that constant pressure. So for the same maximum pressure the Diesel cycle must have a much higher compression ratio.</p>
<p>On a T–s diagram, with the same peak pressure and the same heat rejected, the Diesel cycle adds its heat at a higher mean temperature, so it is more efficient. The ranking for equal peak pressure (and equal peak temperature) is η<sub>Diesel</sub> &gt; η<sub>Dual</sub> &gt; η<sub>Otto</sub>.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> rs<sub>Otto</sub> &gt; r<sub>Diesel</sub> is impossible at equal peak pressure.</li><li><b>(d)</b> rs<sub>Otto</sub> &gt; r<sub>Diesel</sub> is impossible at equal peak pressure.</li><li><b>(c)</b> η<sub>Otto</sub> &gt; η<sub>Diesel</sub> holds only at the <i>same compression ratio</i> (and same heat input), the opposite condition.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Same r and same heat input: Otto &gt; Dual &gt; Diesel. Same peak pressure and temperature: Diesel &gt; Dual &gt; Otto.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-38", year: 2017, no: 38, subject: "turbo", topic: "Specific speed and guide vanes",
q: `Which of the following statements are correct?<ol><li>The specific speed of a turbine is the speed at which a homologous turbine develops 1 mhp under unit head at its maximum efficiency.</li><li>The specific speed is a dimensionless parameter used for the selection of turbines.</li><li>The function of guide vanes in reaction turbines is to minimize shock at entry of the fluid onto the runner blades.</li></ol>Select the correct answer using the code given below.`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Specific speed and guide vanes.</p><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> N<sub>s</sub> = N√P/H<sup>5/4</sup> is the speed of a geometrically similar turbine that develops unit power (1 metric hp in the metric definition, or 1 kW) under 1 m head, at its best-efficiency point.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> N<sub>s</sub> as used is <i>dimensional</i>: its value depends on the units of N, P and H (rpm, kW or hp, m). It is used to select turbine type (Pelton below about 30–50, Francis about 50–300, Kaplan/propeller above about 300, in SI kW units), but it is not dimensionless. The true dimensionless form is the shape number ω√(P/ρ)/(gH)<sup>5/4</sup>.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> Guide vanes (wicket gates) direct the water onto the runner at the correct angle, so it enters without shock. They also regulate the flow when opened or closed by the governor.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Pump specific speed: N<sub>s</sub> = N√Q/H<sup>3/4</sup>, using discharge (not power) because a pump's output is flow.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-39", year: 2017, no: 39, subject: "turbo", topic: "Pump overall efficiency",
q: `A centrifugal pump lifts 0·0125 m³/s of water from a well with a static lift of 30 m. If the brake power of the driving electric motor is 5 kW, what is the overall efficiency of the pump-set?`,
opts: ["57·6%", "63·9%", "65·3%", "73·6%"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 73·6%</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Pump overall efficiency.</p><div class="formula">Water power P<sub>w</sub> = ρ g Q H &nbsp;&nbsp; η<sub>o</sub> = P<sub>w</sub>/P<sub>shaft</sub></div>
<ul class="sym"><li>ρ = 1000 kg/m³; g = 9·81 m/s²; Q = 0·0125 m³/s; H = 30 m (static lift; no friction loss given)</li><li>P<sub>shaft</sub> = power supplied = 5 kW</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>P<sub>w</sub> = 1000 × 9·81 × 0·0125 × 30 = 3679 W.</li><li>η<sub>o</sub> = 3·679/5 = <b>73·6%</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 57·6% corresponds to other heads or flow rates. With only the static lift given, ρgQH/P is the only calculation possible.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 63·9% corresponds to other heads or flow rates. With only the static lift given, ρgQH/P is the only calculation possible.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 65·3% corresponds to other heads or flow rates. With only the static lift given, ρgQH/P is the only calculation possible.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Overall efficiency = manometric × mechanical (× volumetric). Manometric efficiency = gH<sub>m</sub>/(V<sub>w2</sub>u<sub>2</sub>).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-40", year: 2017, no: 40, subject: "thermo", topic: "Fins: symmetry",
q: `Two rods, one of length l and the other of length 2l, are made of the same material and have same diameter. Both ends of the longer rod are maintained at 100 °C. One end of the shorter rod is maintained at 100 °C while the other end is insulated. Both rods are exposed to the same environment at 40 °C. The temperature at the insulated end is measured to be 55 °C. The temperature at the midpoint of the longer rod would be`,
opts: ["45 °C", "50 °C", "55 °C", "60 °C"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 55 °C</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The long rod is symmetric: both ends at 100 °C, the same material, diameter and surroundings. By symmetry the temperature gradient at its midpoint is zero, which is exactly the condition at an insulated end (no heat crosses it).</p>
<p>So each half of the long rod (length l, one end at 100 °C, the other end adiabatic) behaves exactly like the short rod. The midpoint temperature therefore equals the insulated-tip temperature: <b>55 °C</b>.</p>
<p><b>Check with the fin equation</b></p><div class="formula">θ(x)/θ<sub>b</sub> = cosh m(l − x)/cosh ml → θ<sub>tip</sub> = θ<sub>b</sub>/cosh ml</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>θ = T − T<sub>∞</sub>; θ<sub>b</sub> = 100 − 40 = 60 K; m = √(hP/kA)</li></ul>
<p>θ<sub>tip</sub> = 15 K gives cosh ml = 4, and the long rod's midpoint satisfies the same equation.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>The long rod's midpoint has zero gradient, like an insulated end.</li><li>Each half (length l) behaves exactly like the short rod.</li><li>Midpoint temperature = insulated-tip temperature of the short rod = <b>55 °C</b> (key).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 45 ignores the symmetry; no other value is consistent with the two rods having the same m and the same base temperature.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 50 ignores the symmetry; no other value is consistent with the two rods having the same m and the same base temperature.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 60 °C ignores the symmetry; no other value is consistent with the two rods having the same m and the same base temperature.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Symmetry planes in conduction act as adiabatic surfaces: use them to halve a problem.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-41", year: 2017, no: 41, subject: "power", topic: "Gas turbine cycles",
q: `Consider the following statements in respect of ideal and practical gas turbine cycles :<ol><li>In the ideal cycle case, the cycle efficiency depends on the pressure ratio only.</li><li>In the practical cycle case (with irreversibilities in the compression and expansion processes), the cycle efficiency depends on the maximum temperature as well as on the pressure ratio.</li><li>In the practical cycle case, at a given maximum temperature, the maximum efficiency and the maximum work done occur at a same pressure ratio.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Gas turbine cycles.</p><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> Ideal Brayton: η = 1 − 1/r<sub>p</sub><sup>(γ−1)/γ</sup>, a function of pressure ratio r<sub>p</sub> only (for a given gas).</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> With compressor and turbine isentropic efficiencies below 1, η depends on r<sub>p</sub> and on the temperature ratio T<sub>3</sub>/T<sub>1</sub>. A higher turbine inlet temperature raises efficiency.</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong):</b> At a given T<sub>max</sub>, the r<sub>p</sub> for maximum specific work is lower than the r<sub>p</sub> for maximum efficiency. Designers pick a value between the two.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Ideal cycle: maximum work at r<sub>p</sub> = (T<sub>3</sub>/T<sub>1</sub>)<sup>γ/(2(γ−1))</sup>, where T<sub>2</sub> = T<sub>4</sub>; the efficiency there is 1 − √(T<sub>1</sub>/T<sub>3</sub>).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-42", year: 2017, no: 42, subject: "fm", topic: "Coefficient of velocity (jet trajectory)",
q: `A jet of water issues from a sharp-edged vertical orifice under a constant head of 0·51 m. At a certain point of the issuing jet, the horizontal and vertical coordinates measured from vena contracta are 0·406 m and 0·085 m, respectively. What is the value of the coefficient of velocity?`,
opts: ["0·975", "0·925", "0·875", "0·825"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 0·975</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The jet is a projectile thrown horizontally from the vena contracta with actual velocity V. From x = Vt and y = ½ g t², V = x √(g/2y). Compare with the theoretical velocity √(2gH).</p>
<div class="formula">C<sub>v</sub> = V/√(2gH) = x/(2√(yH))</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>x = horizontal distance = 0·406 m; y = vertical drop = 0·085 m; H = head over the orifice = 0·51 m</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>√(yH) = √(0·085 × 0·51) = √0·04335 = 0·2082.</li><li>C<sub>v</sub> = 0·406/(2 × 0·2082) = 0·406/0·4164 = <b>0·975</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 0·925 does not satisfy the trajectory equation. Also, C<sub>v</sub> for a sharp-edged orifice is typically 0·97–0·99, so it is unrealistically low.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0·875 does not satisfy the trajectory equation. Also, C<sub>v</sub> for a sharp-edged orifice is typically 0·97–0·99, so it is unrealistically low.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0·825 does not satisfy the trajectory equation. Also, C<sub>v</sub> for a sharp-edged orifice is typically 0·97–0·99, so it is unrealistically low.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Sharp-edged orifice: C<sub>c</sub> ≈ 0·62–0·64, C<sub>v</sub> ≈ 0·97–0·99, C<sub>d</sub> = C<sub>c</sub>C<sub>v</sub> ≈ 0·61–0·62.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-43", year: 2017, no: 43, subject: "icrac", topic: "VCR cycle COP",
q: `In the working of a vapour-compression refrigeration plant, the following enthalpies are recorded at salient points in the cycle :<ol><li>Enthalpy at inlet to compressor (saturated vapour), h<sub>1</sub> = 300 kJ/kg</li><li>Enthalpy at outlet of compressor (after isentropic compression), h<sub>2</sub> = 330 kJ/kg</li><li>Enthalpy at exit of condenser (saturated liquid), h<sub>3</sub> = 150 kJ/kg</li></ol>What is the COP of the plant?`,
opts: ["3", "4", "5", "6"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 5</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Throttling is isenthalpic, so the enthalpy at evaporator entry is h<sub>4</sub> = h<sub>3</sub> = 150 kJ/kg.</p>
<div class="formula">COP = refrigerating effect / compressor work = (h<sub>1</sub> − h<sub>4</sub>)/(h<sub>2</sub> − h<sub>1</sub>)</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Refrigerating effect = 300 − 150 = 150 kJ/kg.</li><li>Work = 330 − 300 = 30 kJ/kg.</li><li>COP = 150/30 = <b>5</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(d)</b> 6 = (h<sub>2</sub> − h<sub>3</sub>)/(h<sub>2</sub> − h<sub>1</sub>) = 180/30 is the heat-pump COP (= COP<sub>R</sub> + 1).</li><li><b>(a)</b> 3 corresponds to no consistent enthalpy difference.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 4 corresponds to no consistent enthalpy difference.</li></ul>
<p>State 4 (after the expansion valve) is a wet mixture at evaporator pressure with the same enthalpy as the liquid leaving the condenser; that is why h<sub>4</sub> needs no separate measurement.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Heat rejected in the condenser = h<sub>2</sub> − h<sub>3</sub> = RE + W = 180 kJ/kg. Refrigerant flow per TR = 211/150 = 1·41 kg/min.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-44", year: 2017, no: 44, subject: "turbo", topic: "Reciprocating compressors",
q: `Consider the following statements for single-stage reciprocating compressors :<ol><li>Isothermal process is the most desirable process for compression.</li><li>The size of clearance volume provided in the compressor has no effect on work done per kg of air delivered.</li><li>The volumetric efficiency of the compressor decreases with increasing pressure.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Reciprocating compressors.</p><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> Isothermal compression needs the least work (smallest area on the p–V diagram). Cooling and multistaging aim to approach it.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct, ideal theory):</b> The air left in the clearance space is compressed and then re-expands, giving back its work. Ideally the work per kg of air <i>delivered</i> is the same with or without clearance: W = n/(n − 1) × mR T<sub>1</sub>[(p<sub>2</sub>/p<sub>1</sub>)<sup>(n−1)/n</sup> − 1], where m is the mass delivered. Clearance reduces capacity, not specific work.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> η<sub>v</sub> = 1 + C − C(p<sub>2</sub>/p<sub>1</sub>)<sup>1/n</sup> falls as the delivery pressure rises, reaching zero at (p<sub>2</sub>/p<sub>1</sub>)<sub>max</sub> = ((1 + C)/C)<sup>n</sup>.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Isothermal efficiency = isothermal work / actual work: the usual figure of merit for reciprocating compressors.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-45", year: 2017, no: 45, subject: "power", topic: "Regenerative Rankine cycle",
q: `Consider the following statements in respect of regenerative Rankine cycle :<ol><li>Regeneration increases the efficiency of the cycle.</li><li>The boiler capacity is increased for a given output.</li><li>The capacity of the condenser is reduced.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3", "1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In regenerative feed heating, part of the steam is bled from the turbine to heat the feed water.</p>
<p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> Feed water enters the boiler hotter, so the mean temperature of heat addition rises and efficiency increases.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> Bled steam does less work, so each kg of steam generated gives less work. The steam rate (kg/kWh) and so the boiler steam generation needed for a given output go up.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> Only the unbled part of the steam reaches the condenser, so the condenser handles less steam and less heat (it can be smaller).</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> With an infinite number of ideal feed heaters, the regenerative Rankine cycle approaches the Carnot efficiency between the boiler and condenser temperatures.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-46", year: 2017, no: 46, subject: "icrac", topic: "Refrigeration unit basics",
q: `Consider the following statements in respect of (l) the temperature of the medium, (m) the refrigerant and (n) the condenser and absorption system—in a refrigeration unit :<ol><li>Temperature of the medium being cooled must be below that of the evaporator.</li><li>Refrigerant leaves the condenser as liquid.</li><li>Any solar thermally operated absorption system is capable only of intermittent operation.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 2 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Refrigeration unit basics.</p><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> Heat flows from hot to cold. For the evaporator to absorb heat, the medium being cooled must be <i>warmer</i> than the evaporator (refrigerant) temperature.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> The condenser removes the superheat and latent heat; the refrigerant leaves as saturated or sub-cooled liquid and goes to the expansion valve.</li>
<li><b>3 (accepted):</b> A solar-driven absorption system works only while the sun heats the generator. Simple solar units therefore run in cycles (regenerate by day, refrigerate by night), so they operate intermittently. Continuous operation needs heat storage or a back-up heater.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 2, 3.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Intermittent absorption refrigerators (such as the solar ice maker) alternate between generation and absorption phases in the same vessel; continuous systems need a separate generator and absorber plus a pump or a third fluid.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-47", year: 2017, no: 47, subject: "thermo", topic: "Partial pressure in gas mixtures",
q: `Volumetric analysis of a certain flue gas gave CO<sub>2</sub> 15%, O<sub>2</sub> 5% and rest as N<sub>2</sub>. The gas was at a temperature of 200 °C and a pressure of 5 bar. The partial pressure of N<sub>2</sub> in the flue gas is`,
opts: ["250 kN/m²", "300 kN/m²", "350 kN/m²", "400 kN/m²"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 400 kN/m²</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For ideal gas mixtures, volume fraction = mole fraction = pressure fraction (Dalton and Amagat).</p>
<div class="formula">p<sub>i</sub> = y<sub>i</sub> × p</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>y<sub>N2</sub> = 1 − 0·15 − 0·05 = 0·80 (mole/volume fraction); p = 5 bar = 500 kN/m²</li></ul>
<p>p<sub>N2</sub> = 0·80 × 500 = <b>400 kN/m²</b>. The temperature (200 °C) does not affect partial pressures at a given total pressure.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>y_N₂ = 1 − 0.15 − 0.05 = 0.80.</li><li>p_N₂ = 0.80 × 500 = <b>400 kN/m²</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 250 corresponds to N<sub>2</sub> fractions of 50, 60 and 70%, which do not match the analysis.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 300 corresponds to N<sub>2</sub> fractions of 50, 60 and 70%, which do not match the analysis.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 350 kN/m² corresponds to N<sub>2</sub> fractions of 50, 60 and 70%, which do not match the analysis.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Mass fractions differ from mole fractions: x<sub>i</sub> = y<sub>i</sub>M<sub>i</sub>/Σy<sub>j</sub>M<sub>j</sub>. For this gas, mass fraction of CO<sub>2</sub> = 0·15 × 44/(0·15 × 44 + 0·05 × 32 + 0·8 × 28) = 21·6%.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-48", year: 2017, no: 48, subject: "power", topic: "Steam condenser performance",
q: `Consider the following statements :<ol><li>The efficiency of heat transfer in a condenser will improve by increase of the overall heat transfer coefficient.</li><li>The efficiency of heat transfer in a condenser will improve by increase of the velocity of flow of water in the tube.</li><li>The difference between the temperature of steam entering the condenser and the inlet water temperature should be maximum for maximum efficiency.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Steam condenser performance.</p><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> Q = U A ΔT<sub>lm</sub>; a higher U transfers more heat in the same area.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> Higher water velocity raises the water-side film coefficient (h ∝ V<sup>0·8</sup> in turbulent flow) and reduces fouling, so U rises. The limit is erosion and pumping power, usually 1·5–2·5 m/s.</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong):</b> Condenser efficiency = (t<sub>w,out</sub> − t<sub>w,in</sub>)/(t<sub>s</sub> − t<sub>w,in</sub>), the water temperature rise divided by the largest rise possible. It is highest when the water leaves close to the steam temperature, i.e. a small terminal difference, not a maximum difference.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Vacuum efficiency = actual vacuum/ideal vacuum (spoilt by air leakage); condenser efficiency = actual water temperature rise/maximum possible rise.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-49", year: 2017, no: 49, subject: "turbo", topic: "Velocity-compounded (Curtis) stages",
q: `The total power developed by a three-stage velocity compounded impulse steam turbine is 900 kW. The power magnitudes developed in the first and the second stages are, respectively`,
opts: ["500 kW and 300 kW", "100 kW and 300 kW", "500 kW and 100 kW", "100 kW and 100 kW"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 500 kW and 300 kW</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For an ideal velocity-compounded (Curtis) wheel with n rows of moving blades at the optimum blade speed ratio (u/V<sub>1</sub> = cos α/2n), symmetric blades and no friction, the work done by successive rows is in the ratio of odd numbers, counting down: 2n − 1, 2n − 3, …, 1.</p><div class="formula">Curtis wheel, n rows: work ratio (2n − 1) : (2n − 3) : … : 1</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>For n = 3 the ratio is 5 : 3 : 1 (sum 9).</li><li>First row = 900 × 5/9 = <b>500 kW</b>; second = 900 × 3/9 = <b>300 kW</b>; third = 100 kW.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> reverses the order: the first row always does the most work.</li><li><b>(c)</b> skips the middle term of the 5 : 3 : 1 ratio.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 100 kW belongs only to the last row.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Two-row Curtis wheel: work ratio 3 : 1, so the last row does only a quarter of the work. This is why more than two or three rows is rarely worthwhile.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-50", year: 2017, no: 50, subject: "power", topic: "Natural-draught cooling towers",
q: `Consider the following statements in respect of natural-draft cooling towers :<ol><li>Theoretically the water can be cooled to even below the dry-bulb temperature of the induced air flow.</li><li>Natural-draft cooling towers are 100 m or more in height.</li><li>The inner and outer surfaces are surfaces of revolution of a segment of a hyperbola about the vertical axis—affording improved strength rather than any thermodynamic augmentation.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3", "1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Natural-draught cooling towers.</p><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> Cooling is mainly by evaporation, so the theoretical limit is the air's wet-bulb temperature, which is below its dry-bulb temperature.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> Large hyperbolic natural-draught towers for power stations are typically 100–200 m tall, and some exceed 200 m. The draught comes from the density difference between warm moist air inside and cooler air outside, so height matters.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> The hyperbolic (hyperboloid) shell is chosen mainly for structural reasons: a doubly curved thin shell is strong and stable against wind with little material, and can be built from straight members. Any thermodynamic gain is secondary.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Range = water inlet − outlet temperature; approach = water outlet − air wet-bulb temperature. Cooling tower efficiency = range/(range + approach).</p>` }
);

// ---- q2017c.js ----
// ESE 2017 Prelims Paper II, Mechanical Engineering, Series A, Q51–75
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2017-51", year: 2017, no: 51, subject: "renew", topic: "Wind energy basics",
q: `Consider the following statements :<ol><li>Wind velocity at about 20 m height above the ground is taken as the rated velocity for design of windmills.</li><li>The total power of a wind stream is directly proportional to the cube of average velocity.</li><li>Wind turbine operates with variable load over a narrow range between cut-in and cut-out velocities.</li><li>Vertical wind machine operates in all wind directions, but it needs yaw adjustment.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2", "1 and 4", "3 and 4", "2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Wind energy basics.</p><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> Wind speeds are measured and quoted at the standard height of <b>10 m</b>. Speeds at hub height are then found with the power law V<sub>2</sub>/V<sub>1</sub> = (h<sub>2</sub>/h<sub>1</sub>)<sup>α</sup>, where α ≈ 1/7.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> Power in the wind P = ½ ρ A V³ (the kinetic energy flux through area A). Strictly the mean of V³ exceeds the cube of the mean speed, but the cube law is the point being tested.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> The machine starts producing at the cut-in speed (about 3–4 m/s), reaches rated power at the rated speed and is shut down at the cut-out speed (about 25 m/s). Between these limits the output varies with the wind.</li>
<li><b>4 (wrong):</b> A vertical-axis machine (Darrieus, Savonius) accepts wind from any direction, which is exactly why it needs <b>no</b> yaw mechanism. Horizontal-axis machines need yaw control.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 1, 4, which are incorrect; Omits 2, 3, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 4, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Betz limit: at most 16/27 ≈ 59·3% of the wind's power can be extracted, when the downstream velocity is one third of the upstream velocity.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-52", year: 2017, no: 52, subject: "renew", topic: "Fuel cells",
q: `Which fuel cell is suitable for spacecraft applications?`,
opts: ["Direct methanol fuel cell", "Proton exchange membrane fuel cell", "Alkaline fuel cell", "Phosphoric acid fuel cell"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Alkaline fuel cell</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The alkaline fuel cell (AFC, KOH electrolyte, about 60–90 °C, Bacon cell) powered the Apollo missions and the Space Shuttle orbiters. In space, pure hydrogen and oxygen are carried anyway, so the AFC's weakness (CO<sub>2</sub> in air poisons the KOH by forming carbonate) does not matter. It gives high efficiency (about 60–70%), and its product water is drinkable for the crew.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> DMFC: low power density, methanol crossover; used for small portable devices.</li><li><b>(b)</b> PEMFC: flew on Gemini and is now dominant in vehicles, but the classic spacecraft answer is the AFC.</li><li><b>(d)</b> PAFC: about 200 °C, used for stationary combined heat and power; heavy, slow to start.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> High-temperature cells: MCFC (molten carbonate, about 650 °C) and SOFC (solid oxide, about 800–1000 °C) suit large stationary plants and can reform natural gas internally.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-53", year: 2017, no: 53, subject: "mech", topic: "Uniform angular acceleration",
q: `A flywheel on a motor speeds up from rest to 1000 r.p.m. in 6 seconds. The number of revolutions made thereby is nearly`,
opts: ["200", "100", "50", "20"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 50</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Assume uniform angular acceleration. Then the angle turned equals the average speed × time.</p>
<div class="formula">θ = ½ (ω<sub>0</sub> + ω) t</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>ω<sub>0</sub> = 0; ω = 1000 rpm; t = 6 s = 0·1 min</li></ul>
<p>Average speed = 500 rpm, so the number of revolutions = 500 × 0·1 = <b>50</b>. In radians: ω = 104·7 rad/s, α = 17·45 rad/s², θ = ½αt² = 314·2 rad = 50 rev.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Uniform acceleration ⇒ average speed = (0 + 1000)/2 = 500 rpm.</li><li>t = 6 s = 0.1 min.</li><li>Revolutions = 500 × 0.1 = <b>50</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 100 uses the final speed for the whole 6 s.</li><li><b>(a)</b> 200 is unit slips (seconds versus minutes).</li><li><b>(d)</b> 20 is unit slips (seconds versus minutes).</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Rotational kinematics mirror linear ones: ω = ω<sub>0</sub> + αt, θ = ω<sub>0</sub>t + ½αt², ω² = ω<sub>0</sub>² + 2αθ.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-54", year: 2017, no: 54, subject: "mech", topic: "Newton's third law",
q: `Two steel balls of 2 kg and 4 kg mass, respectively, are pressed on the two ends of a spring, all pre-placed on a smooth surface. When released, the smaller ball moves with an acceleration of 2 m/s². The simultaneous acceleration of the bigger ball will be`,
opts: ["0·5 m/s²", "1 m/s²", "2 m/s²", "4 m/s²"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1 m/s²</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A light spring pushes on both balls with equal and opposite forces at every instant (Newton's third law; the massless spring transmits the same force at each end). The surface is smooth, so there is no friction.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><div class="formula">F = m<sub>1</sub>a<sub>1</sub> = m<sub>2</sub>a<sub>2</sub></div>
<p>F = 2 × 2 = 4 N, so a<sub>2</sub> = 4/4 = <b>1 m/s²</b> (in the opposite direction).</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>F = 2 × 2 = 4 N.</li><li>a₂ = 4/4 = <b>1 m/s²</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(c)</b> 2 m/s² would need equal masses.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 4 m/s² inverts the mass ratio.</li><li><b>(a)</b> 0·5 m/s² uses the mass ratio squared.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Momentum is conserved too: m<sub>1</sub>v<sub>1</sub> = m<sub>2</sub>v<sub>2</sub> at release, so the lighter ball always moves faster.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-55", year: 2017, no: 55, subject: "mech", topic: "Work–energy principle",
q: `A bullet of mass 0·03 kg moving with a speed of 400 m/s penetrates 12 cm into a fixed block of wood. The average force exerted by the wood on the bullet will be`,
opts: ["30 kN", "20 kN", "15 kN", "10 kN"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 20 kN</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Formula (work–energy)</b></p><div class="formula">F × s = ½ m v²</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>F = average resisting force; s = penetration = 0·12 m; m = 0·03 kg; v = 400 m/s (final speed zero)</li></ul>
<p>KE = ½ × 0·03 × 400² = 2400 J, so F = 2400/0·12 = 20,000 N = <b>20 kN</b>.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>KE = ½ × 0.03 × 400² = 2400 J.</li><li>F = 2400/0.12 = <b>20 kN</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 30 kN comes from forgetting the ½ (40 kN, not offered), using 16 cm or 24 cm, or other slips.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 15 kN comes from forgetting the ½ (40 kN, not offered), using 16 cm or 24 cm, or other slips.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 10 kN comes from forgetting the ½ (40 kN, not offered), using 16 cm or 24 cm, or other slips.</li></ul>
<p>The work–energy method needs only the distance; it gives the <i>average</i> force because the real resisting force varies with depth.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Time to stop = 2s/v = 0·6 ms; impulse = mv = 12 N·s = F × t, which gives the same 20 kN.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-56", year: 2017, no: 56, subject: "mech", topic: "Equilibrium of a hanging ball",
q: `A ball of weight 100 N is tied to a smooth wall by a cord making an angle of 30° to the wall. The tension in the cord is`,
opts: ["200 N", "200/√3 N", "100 N", "50√3 N"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 200/√3 N</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Three forces act on the ball: its weight W (down), the cord tension T (along the cord, 30° to the vertical wall) and the wall reaction R (horizontal, since the wall is smooth).</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><div class="formula">ΣF<sub>y</sub> = 0: T cos 30° = W &nbsp;&nbsp; ΣF<sub>x</sub> = 0: T sin 30° = R</div>
<p>T = 100/(√3/2) = <b>200/√3 ≈ 115·5 N</b>; R = T/2 = 57·7 N.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>T = 100/(√3/2) = <b>200/√3 N</b> ≈ 115.5 N.</li><li>R = T/2 = 57.7 N.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 200 N uses sin 30° in the vertical equation (angle measured from the wrong reference).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 100 N ignores the inclination.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 50√3 N = W cos 30° is a component, not the tension.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Lami's theorem gives the same: T/sin 90° = W/sin 120° = R/sin 150°.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-57", year: 2017, no: 57, subject: "mech", topic: "Elastic constants",
q: `The modulus of rigidity of an elastic material is found to be 38·5% of the value of its Young's modulus. The Poisson's ratio μ of the material is nearly`,
opts: ["0·28", "0·30", "0·33", "0·35"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 0·30</h3><div class="formula">E = 2G(1 + μ)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>E = Young's modulus; G = modulus of rigidity (shear modulus); μ = Poisson's ratio</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> G/E = 0·385 → 1 + μ = 1/(2 × 0·385) = 1·2987 → μ = <b>0·30</b>, typical of steel (G/E = 80/208 ≈ 0·385).</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>G/E = 0.385 ⇒ 1 + μ = 1/(2 × 0.385) = 1.2987.</li><li>μ = <b>0.30</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0·28 gives G/E = 0·391.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0·33 gives 0·376.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0·35 gives 0·370.</li></ul>
<p>Check: E = 2G(1 + μ) = 2 × 0·385E × 1·30 = 1·001E. The relation holds only for isotropic, linearly elastic materials; G is measured in a torsion test and E in a tension test.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Other relations: E = 3K(1 − 2μ) and E = 9KG/(3K + G). For isotropic materials 0 ≤ μ ≤ 0·5; μ = 0·5 means incompressible (K → ∞).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-58", year: 2017, no: 58, subject: "mech", topic: "Poisson's ratio and E",
q: `A bar produces a lateral strain of magnitude 60 × 10<sup>−5</sup> m/m when subjected to a tensile stress of magnitude 300 MPa along the axial direction. What is the elastic modulus of the material if the Poisson's ratio is 0·3?`,
opts: ["200 GPa", "150 GPa", "125 GPa", "100 GPa"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 150 GPa</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Poisson's ratio and E.</p><div class="formula">μ = lateral strain/axial strain &nbsp;&nbsp; E = σ/ε<sub>axial</sub></div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Axial strain = 60 × 10<sup>−5</sup>/0·3 = 2 × 10<sup>−3</sup>.</li><li>E = 300/(2 × 10<sup>−3</sup>) = 1·5 × 10⁵ MPa = <b>150 GPa</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 200 GPa is the usual value for steel, but the data give 150 GPa.</li><li><b>(c)</b> uses μ wrongly (multiplying instead of dividing, for example).</li><li><b>(d)</b> uses μ wrongly (multiplying instead of dividing, for example).</li><li>Using the lateral strain directly would give 500 GPa, which is not offered.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Volumetric strain of a bar in uniaxial tension = ε(1 − 2μ) = 2 × 10<sup>−3</sup> × 0·4 = 0·8 × 10<sup>−3</sup>.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-59", year: 2017, no: 59, subject: "mech", topic: "Economy of beam sections",
q: `In the design of beams for a given strength, consider that the conditions of economy of use of the material would avail as follows :<ol><li>Rectangular cross-section is more economical than square section of the same cross-sectional area of the beam.</li><li>Circular section is more economical than square section.</li><li>I-section is more economical than a rectangular section of the same depth.</li></ol>Which of the above are correct?`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3", "1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Bending strength depends on the section modulus Z = I/y<sub>max</sub>. A section is more economical when it gives more Z for the same area, which means placing material far from the neutral axis.</p>
<p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> A rectangle placed with its depth vertical (d &gt; b) has Z = bd²/6, larger than a square of the same area. For d = 2b and equal area: Z<sub>rect</sub>/Z<sub>square</sub> = √2.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> For equal area, Z<sub>square</sub>/Z<sub>circle</sub> ≈ 1·18: the circle has more material near the neutral axis, so it is <i>less</i> economical.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> An I-section puts most material in the flanges, far from the neutral axis, so for the same depth it carries the same moment with much less material.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Ranking by Z per unit area: I-section &gt; hollow box &gt; deep rectangle &gt; square &gt; circle.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-60", year: 2017, no: 60, subject: "mech", topic: "Strain energy and resilience",
q: `Which one of the following statements is correct?`,
opts: ["The strain produced per unit volume is called resilience.", "The maximum strain produced per unit volume is called proof resilience.", "The least strain energy stored in a unit volume is called proof resilience.", "The greatest strain energy stored in a unit volume of a material without permanent deformation is called proof resilience."], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) The greatest strain energy stored in a unit volume without permanent deformation is proof resilience</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Resilience</b> is the strain energy stored in a body when it is strained within the elastic limit. <b>Proof resilience</b> is the maximum strain energy that can be stored without permanent set, i.e. when stressed up to the elastic limit. Per unit volume it is the <b>modulus of resilience</b>:</p>
<div class="formula">u = σ<sub>e</sub>²/(2E)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>σ<sub>e</sub> = stress at the elastic limit (proof stress); E = Young's modulus</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> confuses strain (a ratio) with strain <i>energy</i>.</li><li><b>(b)</b> confuses strain (a ratio) with strain <i>energy</i>.</li><li><b>(c)</b> says "least", but proof resilience is the <i>greatest</i> energy stored elastically.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Toughness = total energy absorbed up to fracture (area under the whole stress–strain curve). Under a suddenly applied load the stress is twice that of a gradually applied one.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-61", year: 2017, no: 61, subject: "mech", topic: "Bending stress",
q: `A beam of rectangular section (12 cm wide × 20 cm deep) is simply supported over a span of 12 m. It is acted upon by a concentrated load of 80 kN at the mid-span. The maximum bending stress induced is`,
opts: ["400 MPa", "300 MPa", "200 MPa", "100 MPa"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 300 MPa</h3><div class="formula">M<sub>max</sub> = WL/4 &nbsp;&nbsp; Z = bd²/6 &nbsp;&nbsp; σ<sub>max</sub> = M/Z</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>W = 80 kN at mid-span; L = 12 m; b = 0·12 m; d = 0·20 m</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>M<sub>max</sub> = 80 × 12/4 = 240 kN m.</li><li>Z = 0·12 × 0·20²/6 = 8 × 10<sup>−4</sup> m³.</li><li>σ = 240 × 10³/8 × 10<sup>−4</sup> = 300 × 10⁶ Pa = <b>300 MPa</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 400 MPa uses b and d swapped in a slip.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 200 MPa uses WL/8 (the UDL formula) or similar errors.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 100 MPa uses WL/8 (the UDL formula) or similar errors.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The stress is tension at the bottom fibre and compression at the top fibre; it is zero at the neutral axis, 10 cm from each face.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> For a UDL of total load W: M<sub>max</sub> = WL/8, half the point-load value. Maximum shear stress in a rectangle = 1·5 × average shear stress.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-62", year: 2017, no: 62, subject: "mech", topic: "Deflection: point load vs UDL",
q: `A uniform bar, simply supported at the ends, carries a concentrated load P at mid-span. If the same load be, alternatively, uniformly distributed over the full length of the bar, the maximum deflection of the bar will decrease by`,
opts: ["25·5%", "31·5%", "37·5%", "50·0%"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 37·5%</h3><div class="formula">Point load at centre: δ = PL³/(48EI) &nbsp;&nbsp; UDL, total P: δ = 5PL³/(384EI)</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Ratio = (5/384)/(1/48) = 5 × 48/384 = 0·625.</li><li>Decrease = 1 − 0·625 = <b>37·5%</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(d)</b> 50 % would mean the deflection halves, which needs a load ratio of 2.</li><li><b>(a)</b> 25.5 % does not come from the deflection ratio of the two load cases.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 31.5 % does not come from the deflection ratio of the two load cases.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Spreading a load reduces deflection because load placed near the supports bends the beam less than load at mid-span.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Cantilever: end load δ = PL³/3EI; UDL (total P) δ = PL³/8EI, a 62·5% reduction.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-63", year: 2017, no: 63, subject: "mech", topic: "Thin cylinders and spheres",
q: `A thin cylindrical pressure vessel and a thin spherical pressure vessel have the same mean radius, same wall thickness and are subjected to same internal pressure. The hoop stresses set up in these vessels (cylinder in relation to sphere) will be in the ratio`,
opts: ["1 : 2", "1 : 1", "2 : 1", "4 : 1"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 2 : 1</h3><div class="formula">Cylinder hoop: σ<sub>h</sub> = pr/t (longitudinal pr/2t) &nbsp;&nbsp; Sphere: σ = pr/2t (in every direction)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>p = internal pressure; r = mean radius; t = wall thickness</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Ratio = (pr/t)/(pr/2t) = <b>2 : 1</b>.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 1 : 2 is inverted.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 1 : 1 compares the cylinder's <i>longitudinal</i> stress with the sphere's stress.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 4 : 1 has no basis.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> This is why a sphere needs only half the wall thickness of a cylinder for the same pressure and radius, and why cylinders burst along their length.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-64", year: 2017, no: 64, subject: "mech", topic: "Relative velocity",
q: `A boy walks up a stalled escalator in 90 seconds. When the same escalator moves, he is carried up in 60 seconds. How much time would it take him to walk up the moving escalator?`,
opts: ["48 seconds", "36 seconds", "30 seconds", "24 seconds"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 36 seconds</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Let the escalator length be L. Walking speed = L/90; escalator speed = L/60. Walking on a moving escalator, the speeds add.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><div class="formula">1/t = 1/90 + 1/60 = (2 + 3)/180 = 1/36</div>
<p>t = <b>36 s</b>.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>1/t = 1/90 + 1/60 = 5/180.</li><li>t = <b>36 s</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(c)</b> 30 s halves the shorter time.</li><li><b>(a)</b> 48 s does not satisfy the rate addition.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 24 s does not satisfy the rate addition.</li><li>The answer must be less than 60 s (faster than riding alone), which rules out nothing but confirms the method.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Same structure as "two pipes filling a tank": rates add, times combine as the harmonic sum.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-65", year: 2017, no: 65, subject: "mech", topic: "Elastic recovery after yield",
q: `A 10 mm diameter bar of mild steel of elastic modulus 200 × 10<sup>9</sup> Pa is subjected to a tensile load of 50000 N, taking it just beyond its yield point. The elastic recovery of strain that would occur upon removal of tensile load will be`,
opts: ["1·38 × 10<sup>−3</sup>", "2·68 × 10<sup>−3</sup>", "3·18 × 10<sup>−3</sup>", "4·62 × 10<sup>−3</sup>"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 3·18 × 10<sup>−3</sup></h3><p><b>Concept:</b> On unloading from beyond yield, a metal springs back along a line parallel to the original elastic line. The strain recovered is the elastic part, σ/E; the plastic part stays as permanent set.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>A = π × 10²/4 = 78·54 mm².</li><li>σ = 50,000/78·54 = 636·6 MPa.</li><li>Recovered strain = σ/E = 636·6/200,000 = <b>3·18 × 10<sup>−3</sup></b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> corresponds to stresses of 276, 536 and 924 MPa, which do not match 50 kN on a 10 mm bar.</li><li><b>(b)</b> corresponds to stresses of 276, 536 and 924 MPa, which do not match 50 kN on a 10 mm bar.</li><li><b>(d)</b> corresponds to stresses of 276, 536 and 924 MPa, which do not match 50 kN on a 10 mm bar.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> A yield stress of about 637 MPa is high for ordinary mild steel (about 250 MPa); treat the data as given.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-66", year: 2017, no: 66, subject: "materials", topic: "Retained austenite", doubt: true,
q: `On completion of heat treatment, the austenite structure would be retained if`,
opts: ["the rate of cooling is greater than the critical cooling rate", "the rate of cooling is less than the critical cooling rate", "the initiating temperature of martensite formation is above the room temperature", "the finishing temperature of martensite formation is below the room temperature"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) the initiating temperature of martensite formation is above the room temperature (official key)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Martensite forms athermally while the steel cools between the martensite-start (M<sub>s</sub>) and martensite-finish (M<sub>f</sub>) temperatures. Whatever austenite has not transformed when cooling stops at room temperature is "retained austenite".</p>
<p><b>Option by option</b></p><ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Cooling faster than the critical rate avoids pearlite and bainite and gives martensite; on its own it does not decide whether austenite is retained.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Cooling slower than the critical rate gives pearlite or bainite, not retained austenite.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> UPSC's key: M<sub>s</sub> above room temperature means transformation starts on quenching; if it cannot finish by room temperature, some austenite remains.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> If M<sub>f</sub> is below room temperature, transformation is incomplete at room temperature and austenite is retained. This is the standard textbook condition (high-carbon and high-alloy steels).</li></ul>
<div class="note">In doubt (key followed). Our answer was (d): austenite is retained when the martensite-<b>finish</b> temperature M<sub>f</sub> lies below room temperature (common in steels above about 0·6–0·7% C, which is why sub-zero treatment is used). Statement (c) only says martensite starts forming above room temperature, which is true of almost every quenched steel and does not by itself cause retention.</div>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Retained austenite is removed by sub-zero (cryogenic) treatment, which cools below M<sub>f</sub>, or by tempering, where it transforms to bainite or martensite.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-67", year: 2017, no: 67, subject: "materials", topic: "Fe–C and TTT diagrams",
q: `Which one of the following statements is correct?`,
opts: ["Iron-carbon and TTT diagrams are both equilibrium diagrams.", "Iron-carbon and TTT diagrams are both non-equilibrium diagrams.", "Iron-carbon diagram is an equilibrium diagram but TTT diagram is a non-equilibrium diagram.", "Iron-carbon diagram is a non-equilibrium diagram but TTT diagram is an equilibrium diagram."], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Fe–C is an equilibrium diagram; TTT is a non-equilibrium diagram</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The iron–carbon (strictly iron–cementite) diagram shows the phases present under very slow cooling, i.e. at (meta)equilibrium, against temperature and composition. It has no time axis.</p>
<p>The TTT (time–temperature–transformation, isothermal transformation) diagram shows the <b>kinetics</b> of austenite decomposition when steel is quenched to a temperature and held there. It includes non-equilibrium products such as bainite and martensite, which do not appear on the Fe–C diagram.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> TTT diagrams are not equilibrium diagrams: time matters.</li><li><b>(b)</b> The Fe–C diagram is an equilibrium (or metastable-equilibrium) diagram.</li><li><b>(d)</b> reverses the two.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> CCT (continuous cooling transformation) diagrams are the practical version of TTT for real continuous cooling, and the curves shift to the right (longer times).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-68", year: 2017, no: 68, subject: "materials", topic: "Electrical resistivity of materials",
q: `The correct order of increasing resistivity among the following materials is`,
opts: ["nickel, doped silicon, sodium silicate, pure silica", "doped silicon, nickel, pure silica, sodium silicate", "nickel, pure silica, sodium silicate, doped silicon", "sodium silicate, nickel, pure silica, doped silicon"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) nickel, doped silicon, sodium silicate, pure silica</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Approximate room-temperature resistivities (Ω m):</p>
<table><tr><th>Material</th><th>Type</th><th>Resistivity</th></tr><tr><td>Nickel</td><td>metal (conductor)</td><td>≈ 7 × 10<sup>−8</sup></td></tr><tr><td>Doped silicon</td><td>semiconductor</td><td>≈ 10<sup>−5</sup> to 10<sup>−1</sup></td></tr><tr><td>Sodium silicate glass</td><td>ionic insulator (Na⁺ ions can move)</td><td>≈ 10⁸ to 10<sup>10</sup></td></tr><tr><td>Pure (fused) silica</td><td>excellent insulator</td><td>≈ 10<sup>16</sup></td></tr></table>
<p>Adding Na<sub>2</sub>O to silica breaks up the network and provides mobile Na⁺ ions, so soda glass conducts far better than pure silica.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> puts doped silicon below a metal and pure silica below sodium silicate.</li><li><b>(c)</b> places doped silicon above the insulators.</li><li><b>(d)</b> places doped silicon above the insulators.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Resistivity of metals rises with temperature; that of semiconductors and ionic glasses falls (more carriers, more ion mobility).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-69", year: 2017, no: 69, subject: "materials", topic: "Rubber elasticity (entropy)",
q: `Consider the following statements :<br>On heating an elastomer under tensile load, its shrinkage<ol><li>maximizes the enthalpy</li><li>maximizes the entropy</li><li>minimizes the free energy</li><li>avoids breaking</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2", "2 and 3", "3 and 4", "1 and 4"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The elasticity of rubber is mainly <b>entropic</b>. Stretching uncoils the long chains into fewer possible shapes, lowering entropy. The retractive force is about f ≈ −T(∂S/∂L), so it grows with temperature. A stretched rubber band held under load therefore contracts when heated (the Gough–Joule effect).</p>
<p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> Internal energy and enthalpy change very little; the contraction is not driven by enthalpy.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> Contracting lets the chains return to more probable, coiled shapes: entropy increases toward a maximum.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> Equilibrium is the minimum of free energy G = H − TS. At higher T the −TS term dominates, so shrinking (raising S) lowers G.</li>
<li><b>4 (wrong):</b> Shrinkage is a thermodynamic response, not a mechanism to prevent breaking.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 2, 3.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 4, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 1, 4, which are incorrect; Omits 2, 3, which are also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Metals behave the opposite way: their elasticity is energetic (bond stretching), so they expand when heated.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-70", year: 2017, no: 70, subject: "materials", topic: "Uniform elongation",
q: `Which of the following properties will be the meaningful indicator/indicators of uniform rate of elongation of a test piece of a structural material before necking happens in the test piece?<ol><li>Ductility</li><li>Toughness</li><li>Hardness</li></ol>Select the correct answer using the code given below.`,
opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Up to the ultimate tensile strength a ductile specimen elongates uniformly along its gauge length; necking starts at the UTS (Considère condition dσ/dε = σ, which gives uniform true strain = strain-hardening exponent n). Uniform elongation is a measure of <b>ductility</b>: how much the material can be deformed plastically before localised failure begins.</p>
<p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul>
<li><b>1 (meaningful):</b> Ductility (percentage elongation, uniform elongation, reduction in area) is what the uniform elongation describes.</li>
<li><b>2 (not meaningful):</b> Toughness is energy absorbed to fracture: the area under the whole curve, including post-necking. It combines strength and ductility and is not a measure of elongation rate.</li>
<li><b>3 (not meaningful):</b> Hardness is resistance to indentation, related roughly to strength (BHN ≈ UTS/3·45 for steels), not to elongation.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 2, 3, which are incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Total elongation = uniform elongation + necking elongation. The necking part depends on gauge length, so elongation values are quoted with the gauge length (for example 5·65√A).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-71", year: 2017, no: 71, subject: "materials", topic: "Alloying elements in steel",
q: `Which one of the following alloying elements increases the corrosion resistance of steel?`,
opts: ["Vanadium", "Chromium", "Nickel", "Copper"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Chromium</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Chromium, at about 10·5–12% or more, forms a thin, adherent, self-healing chromium-oxide (Cr<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>) passive film. This is what makes stainless steel "stainless". It also raises hardenability and high-temperature oxidation resistance.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Vanadium refines grain and forms hard carbides (tool steels, spring steels); it adds strength and toughness, not corrosion resistance.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Nickel improves toughness and stabilises austenite (in 18-8 stainless it complements chromium). On its own in low-alloy steel it is not the primary corrosion-resisting element.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Copper (about 0·2–0·5%) does improve <i>atmospheric</i> corrosion resistance of weathering steels, but the element that makes steel corrosion resistant in the standard sense is chromium.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Other roles: Mo raises creep strength and pitting resistance; Mn, a deoxidiser, raises hardenability; Si raises electrical resistivity (transformer steel); W gives red hardness (HSS).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-72", year: 2017, no: 72, subject: "tom", topic: "Force-closed kinematic pairs", doubt: true,
q: `Which of the following mechanisms are examples of forced closed kinematic pairs?<ol><li>Cam and roller mechanism</li><li>Door-closing mechanism</li><li>Slider-crank mechanism</li></ol>Select the correct answer using the code given below.`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 2 and 3 only (official key)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A kinematic pair is <b>self-closed</b> (form-closed) when its geometry alone keeps the elements in contact, as in a pin joint or a slider in guides. It is <b>force-closed</b> when contact must be maintained by an external force such as a spring or gravity.</p>
<p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul>
<li><b>1:</b> A cam and roller follower stays in contact only because a spring or gravity pushes the follower onto the cam. This is the textbook example of a force-closed (higher) pair.</li>
<li><b>2 (force-closed):</b> A door closer uses a spring to hold its members together and return the door.</li>
<li><b>3:</b> In a slider-crank, the pin joints and the slider in its guides are held together by geometry (self-closed lower pairs).</li></ul>
<div class="note">In doubt (key followed). Our answer was (a) 1 and 2 only. The cam and roller follower is the standard example of a force-closed pair (Rattan, Ballaney), while the turning and sliding pairs of a slider-crank are self-closed. The key's (c) omits the cam and includes the slider-crank, which does not match the usual definition.</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 2, 3.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> A positive-return (grooved or face) cam makes the cam pair form-closed and needs no spring.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-73", year: 2017, no: 73, subject: "tom", topic: "Grübler's criterion",
q: `A planer mechanism has 10 links and 12 rotary joints. Using Grubler's criterion, the number of degrees of freedom of the mechanism is`,
opts: ["1", "3", "2", "4"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Formula (Grübler / Kutzbach for planar mechanisms)</b></p><div class="formula">F = 3(n − 1) − 2j<sub>1</sub> − j<sub>2</sub></div>
<ul class="sym"><li>n = number of links, including the fixed link = 10</li><li>j<sub>1</sub> = lower pairs with one DOF (revolute or prismatic) = 12; j<sub>2</sub> = higher pairs = 0</li></ul>
<p>F = 3 × 9 − 2 × 12 = 27 − 24 = <b>3</b>.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 1 would need 13 joints.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 2 would need 12·5 joints (impossible), would need 11·5. Neither is an integer case here.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 4 would need 11·5. Neither is an integer case here.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Grübler's equation for constrained motion (F = 1, no higher pairs): 3n − 2j − 4 = 0. For a four-bar chain, n = 4 and j = 4.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-74", year: 2017, no: 74, subject: "tom", topic: "Cam with uniform-velocity motion",
q: `The displacement and velocity diagrams of a cam and follower mechanism are shown :<img class="fig" src="img/me/2017-74.png" alt="Displacement: rise A to B, dwell B to C, return C to D, dwell D to E over one revolution. Velocity: constant positive A to B, zero B to C, constant negative C to D, zero D to E">Which of the following statements is/are correct?<ol><li>The acceleration of the follower at the beginning and at the end of each stroke will be zero.</li><li>The follower remains at rest in the dwell period.</li><li>During period DE, the motion of the follower is retarding.</li></ol>Select the correct answer using the code given below.`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3", "1 only", "2 only", "3 only"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The displacement rises and falls in straight lines, so the follower moves with <b>uniform velocity</b> during each stroke (the rectangular velocity blocks).</p>
<p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> At the start and end of each stroke the velocity jumps suddenly from zero to a finite value (and back). Acceleration = dv/dt is theoretically <i>infinite</i> there, not zero. This is why pure uniform-velocity cams are modified with parabolic or SHM ends.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> During dwells (BC and DE) the displacement is constant and the velocity zero: the follower is at rest.</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong):</b> DE is a dwell at zero lift; there is no motion, retarding or otherwise.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, 3, which are incorrect.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 2.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Peak acceleration ranking for the same lift and angle: uniform velocity (infinite) &gt; constant acceleration (parabolic) &gt; SHM &gt; cycloidal (zero at the ends, no jerk jumps). This makes cycloidal profiles best for high speed.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-75", year: 2017, no: 75, subject: "tom", topic: "Instantaneous centres",
q: `The number of instantaneous centres of rotation in a slider-crank quick-return mechanism is`,
opts: ["10", "8", "6", "4"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 6</h3><div class="formula">N = n(n − 1)/2</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>n = number of links in the mechanism</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The basic slider-crank chain (and its quick-return inversions, such as crank and slotted lever or Whitworth, taken as the basic 4-link chain) has n = 4 links, so N = 4 × 3/2 = <b>6</b>. Four are at the pairs (three pin joints plus the slider's centre at infinity, perpendicular to the guide); two are found with the Aronhold–Kennedy three-centres theorem.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>n = 4 links.</li><li>N = 4 × 3/2 = <b>6</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 10 would need 5 links.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 8 is not of the form n(n − 1)/2.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 4 counts only the primary centres at the joints.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> If the full shaper quick-return mechanism with connecting link and ram (6 links) is counted, N = 15, but that is not among the options.</p>` }
);

// ---- q2017d.js ----
// ESE 2017 Prelims Paper II, Mechanical Engineering, Series A, Q76–100
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2017-76", year: 2017, no: 76, subject: "tom", topic: "Natural frequency of spring–mass system",
q: `A simple spring-mass vibrating system has a natural frequency of N. If the spring stiffness is halved and the mass doubled, then the natural frequency will be`,
opts: ["0·5N", "N", "2N", "4N"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 0·5N</h3><div class="formula">f<sub>n</sub> = (1/2π) √(k/m)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>k = spring stiffness (N/m); m = mass (kg)</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> New f<sub>n</sub> ∝ √((k/2)/(2m)) = √(k/m) × √(1/4) = ½ of the original, so f<sub>n</sub> = <b>0·5N</b>.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>f ∝ √(k/m).</li><li>New: √((k/2)/(2m)) = ½√(k/m) ⇒ <b>0.5N</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> N would need k/m unchanged; here k/m becomes one quarter.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 2N would need k/m to rise four times.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 4N would need k/m to rise sixteen times.</li></ul>
<p>Both changes act the same way: a softer spring gives less restoring force, and a heavier mass gives more inertia, so the frequency falls on both counts.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Static deflection form: f<sub>n</sub> = (1/2π)√(g/δ<sub>st</sub>) ≈ 15·76/√δ<sub>st</sub>(mm) Hz. Doubling the mass and halving k makes δ<sub>st</sub> four times larger and halves f<sub>n</sub>.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-77", year: 2017, no: 77, subject: "tom", topic: "Vibration of a car on springs",
q: `A car of mass 1450 kg is constructed on a chassis supported by four springs. Each spring has a force constant of 40000 N m. The combined mass of the two people occupying the car is 150 kg. What is the period of execution of two complete vibrations?`,
opts: ["0·63 s", "1·59 s", "4·96 s", "1·26 s"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1·26 s</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The four springs act in parallel (they share the body's weight and deflect together), so their stiffnesses add. The force constant is read as 40,000 N/m.</p>
<div class="formula">k<sub>eq</sub> = 4k &nbsp;&nbsp; ω<sub>n</sub> = √(k<sub>eq</sub>/m) &nbsp;&nbsp; T = 2π/ω<sub>n</sub></div>
<ul class="sym"><li>m = 1450 + 150 = 1600 kg; k = 40,000 N/m per spring</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>k<sub>eq</sub> = 160,000 N/m.</li><li>ω<sub>n</sub> = √(160,000/1600) = 10 rad/s.</li><li>T = 2π/10 = 0·628 s per vibration; two vibrations take <b>1·26 s</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0·63 s is the period of <i>one</i> vibration.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 1·59 s is the reciprocal of the frequency in a slip (1/0·628).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 4·96 s uses a single spring for the whole car.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Springs in parallel: k = k<sub>1</sub> + k<sub>2</sub>. In series: 1/k = 1/k<sub>1</sub> + 1/k<sub>2</sub>.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-78", year: 2017, no: 78, subject: "tom", topic: "Vibration isolation",
q: `Consider the following statements :<br>Artefacts to prevent harmful effects resulting from vibrations of an unbalanced machine fixed on its foundation include<ol><li>mounting the machine on springs thereby minimizing the transmission of forces</li><li>using vibration isolating materials to prevent or reduce the transmission of forces</li><li>moving the foundation so as to have only one degree of freedom towards reducing the transmission of forces</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Vibration isolation reduces the force transmitted to the foundation. The transmissibility ratio is</p>
<div class="formula">TR = √[1 + (2ζr)²] / √[(1 − r²)² + (2ζr)²], with r = ω/ω<sub>n</sub></div>
<p>TR &lt; 1 only when r &gt; √2, i.e. when the mounting is soft enough that ω<sub>n</sub> is well below the forcing frequency.</p>
<p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> Soft spring mounts lower ω<sub>n</sub>, so the machine runs above √2 ω<sub>n</sub> and transmits less force.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> Isolating materials (rubber, cork, felt, elastomeric pads) give low stiffness with some damping.</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong):</b> "Moving the foundation" to have one degree of freedom is not an isolation method. Isolation works by choosing stiffness and damping (and sometimes adding an inertia block), not by limiting the foundation's freedom.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Damping helps when passing through resonance during start-up, but in the isolation region (r &gt; √2) more damping slightly <i>increases</i> transmissibility.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-79", year: 2017, no: 79, subject: "tom", topic: "Dunkerley's method",
q: `Two heavy rotors are mounted on a single shaft. Considering each of the rotors separately, the transverse natural frequencies are 100 cycles/s and 200 cycles/s, respectively. The lower critical speed will be`,
opts: ["12000 r.p.m.", "9360 r.p.m.", "8465 r.p.m.", "5367 r.p.m."], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 5367 r.p.m.</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Formula (Dunkerley)</b></p><div class="formula">1/f<sub>n</sub>² = 1/f<sub>1</sub>² + 1/f<sub>2</sub>² (+ 1/f<sub>s</sub>² for the shaft's own mass)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>f<sub>1</sub> = 100 Hz and f<sub>2</sub> = 200 Hz are the frequencies with each rotor acting alone on the shaft</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>1/f<sub>n</sub>² = 1/10,000 + 1/40,000 = 1·25 × 10<sup>−4</sup>.</li><li>f<sub>n</sub> = 89·44 Hz.</li><li>Critical speed = 89·44 × 60 = <b>5367 rpm</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 12,000 rpm exceed the lower individual value (6000 rpm). The combined system must be lower than either rotor alone.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 9360 rpm exceed the lower individual value (6000 rpm). The combined system must be lower than either rotor alone.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 8465 rpm also exceeds 6000 rpm.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Dunkerley's estimate is always slightly <i>lower</i> than the true fundamental frequency; Rayleigh's method gives a slightly higher value.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-80", year: 2017, no: 80, subject: "tom", topic: "Involute gears: centre distance",
q: `Consider the following statements :<br>In the case of gears of involute profiles, increase in the centre to centre distances between the mounting shafts<ol><li>increases the pressure angle</li><li>will not affect the law of gearing</li><li>shortens the path of contact</li><li>increases the contact ratio</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 4", "1, 2 and 3", "1, 3 and 4", "2, 3 and 4"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Involute gears keep a constant velocity ratio even if the centre distance changes slightly (base circles do not change). This is a key advantage over cycloidal gears.</p>
<p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> The working pressure angle grows: cos φ′ = (C/C′) cos φ, where C′ &gt; C is the new centre distance.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> The common normal still passes through a fixed pitch point, so the law of gearing (constant angular velocity ratio = ratio of base radii) still holds.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> The addendum circles overlap less, so the path of contact becomes shorter.</li>
<li><b>4 (wrong):</b> A shorter path of contact means a <i>smaller</i> contact ratio (path of contact / base pitch). Backlash also increases.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 4, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 4, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 4, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Contact ratio should be at least about 1·2–1·4 for smooth running; too large a centre-distance error can drop it below 1, and then contact is lost.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-81", year: 2017, no: 81, subject: "mech", topic: "Statically indeterminate rigid bar",
q: `A rigid bar ACO as shown is hinged at O and is held in a horizontal position by two identical vertical steel wires AB and CD. A point load of 20 kN is hung at the position shown. The tensions in wires AB and CD are<img class="fig" src="img/me/2017-81.png" alt="Rigid bar hinged at O on the right; wire AB at 1·0 m from O and wire CD at 0·6 m from O, both to a fixed support above; 20 kN load at 0·8 m from O">`,
opts: ["15·2 kN and 7·1 kN", "11·8 kN and 7·1 kN", "15·2 kN and 5·0 kN", "11·8 kN and 5·0 kN"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 11·8 kN and 7·1 kN</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> One equilibrium equation (moments about O) has two unknown tensions, so the problem is statically indeterminate. Compatibility supplies the second equation: the rigid bar rotates about O, so each wire stretches in proportion to its distance from O.</p>
<p><b>Equations</b></p><div class="formula">Compatibility: δ<sub>A</sub>/δ<sub>C</sub> = 1·0/0·6, with δ = TL/(AE) → T<sub>A</sub>/T<sub>C</sub> = 1/0·6 (identical wires)<br>Equilibrium: ΣM<sub>O</sub> = 0: T<sub>A</sub>(1·0) + T<sub>C</sub>(0·6) = 20 × 0·8 = 16 kN m</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>T<sub>A</sub>, T<sub>C</sub> = tensions in AB and CD; δ = wire elongation; L, A, E are the same for both wires</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>T<sub>A</sub> = T<sub>C</sub>/0·6 = 1·667 T<sub>C</sub>.</li><li>1·667 T<sub>C</sub> + 0·6 T<sub>C</sub> = 16 → T<sub>C</sub> = 7·06 kN.</li><li>T<sub>A</sub> = 11·76 kN.</li></ol>
<p>So T<sub>AB</sub> ≈ <b>11·8 kN</b> and T<sub>CD</sub> ≈ <b>7·1 kN</b>. The hinge takes the rest: 20 − 18·8 = 1·2 kN.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 15·2 kN comes from wrong lever arms or equal elongations.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 15·2/5·0 comes from wrong lever arms or equal elongations.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 5·0 kN for CD breaks the compatibility ratio 0·6.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> In indeterminate problems always write equilibrium + compatibility + force–deformation (Hooke's law) equations.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-82", year: 2017, no: 82, subject: "tom", topic: "Torques in an epicyclic train", doubt: true,
q: `An epicyclic gear train has 3 shafts A, B and C. A is the input shaft running at 100 r.p.m. clockwise. B is the output shaft running at 250 r.p.m. clockwise. The torque on A is 50 kN m (clockwise), C is a fixed shaft. The torque needed to fix C is`,
opts: ["20 kN m (anti-clockwise)", "20 kN m (clockwise)", "30 kN m (anti-clockwise)", "30 kN m (clockwise)"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 20 kN m (anti-clockwise) (official key)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For a gear train running steadily without losses, two conditions hold.</p>
<div class="formula">Torque balance: T<sub>A</sub> + T<sub>B</sub> + T<sub>C</sub> = 0 &nbsp;&nbsp; Power balance: T<sub>A</sub>ω<sub>A</sub> + T<sub>B</sub>ω<sub>B</sub> + T<sub>C</sub>ω<sub>C</sub> = 0</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>T = external torque applied to each shaft (clockwise positive); ω = speed (clockwise positive); ω<sub>C</sub> = 0 (fixed)</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Power: 50 × 100 + T<sub>B</sub> × 250 = 0 → T<sub>B</sub> = −20 kN m. The load on the output is 20 kN m anticlockwise (resisting): output power = input power = 50 × 100.</li>
<li>Torque balance: 50 − 20 + T<sub>C</sub> = 0 → T<sub>C</sub> = −30 kN m, i.e. a holding torque of 30 kN m anticlockwise.</li></ol>
<p>UPSC's key gives 20 kN m anticlockwise, which is the magnitude of the <b>output-shaft torque</b>.</p><p><b>Other options</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 20 kN m clockwise has the wrong sense: the output load opposes the clockwise rotation.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 30 kN m anticlockwise is what the torque balance gives for the fixed member C (our answer).</li><li><b>(d)</b> 30 kN m clockwise has the right size but the wrong sense: the frame must oppose the net clockwise torque 50 − 20 = 30 kN m.</li></ul>
<div class="note">In doubt (key followed). Our working gives (c) 30 kN m anticlockwise for the fixing torque on C. With the input (50 kN m cw) and the output resisting torque (20 kN m ccw) acting, the frame must supply the difference, 30 kN m, for the three external torques to sum to zero. The key's 20 kN m equals the output torque, not the torque on the fixed member.</div>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Output torque from power: T<sub>B</sub> = T<sub>A</sub> × N<sub>A</sub>/N<sub>B</sub> = 50 × 100/250 = 20 kN m. Fixing torque = T<sub>B</sub> − T<sub>A</sub> in magnitude when input and output turn the same way.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-83", year: 2017, no: 83, subject: "tom", topic: "Planet gear revolutions",
q: `A fixed gear having 200 teeth is meshed with a smaller gear having 50 teeth. The two gears are connected by an arm. The number of turns made by the smaller gear for one revolution of the arm about the centre of the bigger gear is`,
opts: ["1", "2", "3", "5"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 5</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Tabular method</b></p><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Arm</th><th>Big gear (200 T)</th><th>Small gear (50 T)</th></tr><tr><td>Fix arm, give big gear +1 rev</td><td>0</td><td>+1</td><td>−200/50 = −4</td></tr><tr><td>Multiply by −1 (big gear fixed after adding arm motion)</td><td>0</td><td>−1</td><td>+4</td></tr><tr><td>Add +1 to all</td><td>+1</td><td>0 (fixed)</td><td>+5</td></tr></table>
<p>The small gear makes <b>5</b> turns: 4 relative to the arm plus 1 because the arm itself turns once.</p>
<div class="formula">N<sub>planet</sub> = N<sub>arm</sub>(1 + T<sub>sun</sub>/T<sub>planet</sub>) = 1 × (1 + 200/50) = 5</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(c)</b> 3 has no basis.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 2 has no basis.</li><li><b>(a)</b> 1 is the arm's own turn. Relative to the arm the answer would be 4 (not offered); absolute turns are 5.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> This is the "coin rotation paradox": a coin rolled around a fixed coin of the same size turns twice, not once.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-84", year: 2017, no: 84, subject: "tom", topic: "Balancing", doubt: true,
q: `Consider the following statements :<ol><li>Balancing of several masses rotating in the same plane can be effected by a single mass.</li><li>Balancing of several masses in different planes can be done by 2 masses in 2 planes on either side of the reference plane or on the same side.</li><li>Reciprocating masses cannot be completely balanced by rotating masses.</li><li>Secondary unbalanced forces will be negligible compared to primary imbalance forces.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1, 2, 3 and 4", "1, 2 and 3 only", "3 and 4 only", "1, 2 and 4 only"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1, 2, 3 and 4 (official key)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Balancing.</p><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> Masses in one plane give one resultant centrifugal force; a single balancing mass (m<sub>b</sub>r<sub>b</sub> = Σmr, opposite direction) cancels it.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> Masses in several planes need two balancing masses in two chosen planes, to cancel both the resultant force and the resultant couple. The planes may be on either side of the reference plane or on the same side.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> The inertia force of a reciprocating mass acts along the line of stroke and varies; a rotating counter-mass also creates a transverse component. Only partial balancing (usually 2/3 to 3/4 of the reciprocating mass) is possible with rotating masses.</li>
<li><b>4 (key: correct):</b> Secondary force = m r ω² cos 2θ/n, where n = l/r ≈ 4–5, so it is only about a quarter to a fifth of the primary and is often neglected in slow engines.</li></ul>
<div class="note">In doubt (key followed). Our answer was (b) 1, 2 and 3 only: secondary forces are smaller than primary forces by the factor n = l/r, but they are not "negligible" in general. In high-speed and multi-cylinder engines they matter and are balanced deliberately (for example the Lanchester secondary balance shafts in four-cylinder in-line engines, whose primary forces cancel but secondary forces add).</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3, 4.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 4, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 1, 2, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Direct and reverse cranks: the primary force equals the effect of a crank of mass m/2 at radius r turning at ±ω; the secondary force equals one of mass m/2 at radius r/4n turning at ±2ω.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-85", year: 2017, no: 85, subject: "tom", topic: "Static balancing",
q: `A body of mass 10 kg with its CG 200 mm from the axis of rotation is to be completely balanced by another mass B of 5 kg placed in the same plane. The radius at which the CG of mass B should be is`,
opts: ["500 mm", "400 mm", "300 mm", "200 mm"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 400 mm</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For balance in one plane, the centrifugal forces must cancel. ω is the same for both, so the products m × r must be equal and the masses placed diametrically opposite.</p>
<div class="formula">m<sub>1</sub>r<sub>1</sub> = m<sub>B</sub>r<sub>B</sub> → r<sub>B</sub> = 10 × 200/5 = 400 mm</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>r_B = 10 × 200/5 = <b>400 mm</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(d)</b> 200 mm would need equal masses.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 300 mm gives mr = 1500 and 2500 kg mm, which do not equal 2000 kg mm.</li><li><b>(a)</b> 500 mm gives mr = 1500 and 2500 kg mm, which do not equal 2000 kg mm.</li></ul>
<p>The balancing mass must be placed at 180° to the 10 kg mass. A lighter balancing mass needs a proportionally larger radius: halving the mass doubles the radius.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Static balance (Σmr = 0) is enough for masses in one plane. Several planes also need dynamic balance (Σmrl = 0).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-86", year: 2017, no: 86, subject: "tom", topic: "Governors",
q: `Consider the following statements :<ol><li>In stationary constant speed engines, the spring-loaded governor mechanism is fitted on the cam-shaft of the engine.</li><li>Hunting occurs when the governor is not sensitive.</li><li>Isochronous governors have the same speed over a wide range of governor action.</li><li>A governor is said to be unstable if the radius of rotation falls as the speed increases.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1, 2, 3 and 4", "1, 2 and 4 only", "1, 3 and 4 only", "2 and 3 only"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1, 3 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Governors.</p><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> In stationary constant-speed IC engines a spring-loaded centrifugal governor (Hartnell type) is commonly driven from the camshaft and controls the fuel supply.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> Hunting, a continuous fluctuation of speed above and below the mean, occurs when a governor is <i>too sensitive</i>: a tiny speed change makes the sleeve move to an extreme, overcorrecting the fuel supply.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> An isochronous governor has the same equilibrium speed for all radii of rotation within its range; its range of speed is zero. It is infinitely sensitive and in practice tends to hunt.</li>
<li><b>4 (correct):</b> For stability the radius of rotation must increase as speed increases. A governor whose radius falls as speed rises is unstable.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 3, 4.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, 4, which are also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Sensitiveness = (N<sub>2</sub> − N<sub>1</sub>)/N<sub>mean</sub> (smaller is more sensitive). Effort = mean force on the sleeve for a 1% speed change. Power = effort × lift.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-87", year: 2017, no: 87, subject: "tom", topic: "Gyroscopic couple on an aircraft",
q: `An aircraft cruising at 360 kmph takes a right turn on an arc of 100 m radius. The turbines and propellers have a total mass of 500 kg with radius of gyration of 25 cm. The engine rotates at 2000 r.p.m. The magnitude of the gyroscopic couple generated is`,
opts: ["6·55 kN m", "7·65 kN m", "9·81 kN m", "13·1 kN m"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 6·55 kN m</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Gyroscopic couple on an aircraft.</p><div class="formula">C = I ω ω<sub>p</sub></div>
<ul class="sym"><li>I = m k² = moment of inertia of the rotor; ω = spin speed of the engine; ω<sub>p</sub> = precession speed = V/R (rate of turning of the aircraft)</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>I = 500 × 0·25² = 31·25 kg m².</li><li>ω = 2π × 2000/60 = 209·4 rad/s.</li><li>V = 360 km/h = 100 m/s → ω<sub>p</sub> = 100/100 = 1 rad/s.</li><li>C = 31·25 × 209·4 × 1 = 6545 N m ≈ <b>6·55 kN m</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(d)</b> 13·1 kN m doubles the result (for example using the diameter or 2 × mk²).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 9·81 kN m brings in g wrongly.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 7·65 kN m comes from an arithmetic slip.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> The effect (nose up or down) depends on the direction of engine rotation viewed from the front and on the turn direction: the reactive couple tilts the nose up or down.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-88", year: 2017, no: 88, subject: "mech", topic: "Shear stress in a rectangular beam",
q: `The maximum shearing stress induced in the beam section at any layer at any position along the beam length (shown in the figure) is equal to<img class="fig" src="img/me/2017-88.png" alt="Cantilever 1 m long with 2000 kgf at the free end; rectangular cross-section 50 mm wide and 200 mm deep">`,
opts: ["30 kgf/cm²", "40 kgf/cm²", "50 kgf/cm²", "60 kgf/cm²"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 30 kgf/cm²</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For a cantilever with an end load, the shear force is the same (= W) at every section. In a rectangular section the shear stress is parabolic across the depth, zero at the top and bottom and maximum at the neutral axis.</p>
<div class="formula">τ<sub>max</sub> = 1·5 × V/(b d)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>V = shear force = 2000 kgf; b = 5 cm; d = 20 cm</li></ul>
<p>τ<sub>avg</sub> = 2000/(5 × 20) = 20 kgf/cm²; τ<sub>max</sub> = 1·5 × 20 = <b>30 kgf/cm²</b>.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>V = 2000 kgf at every section.</li><li>τ_avg = 2000/(5 × 20) = 20 kgf/cm².</li><li>τ_max = 1.5 × 20 = <b>30 kgf/cm²</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 40 kgf/cm² uses factor 2 (wrong for a rectangle).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 50 kgf/cm² would need V ≈ 3333 kgf.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 60 kgf/cm² is three times the average 20 kgf/cm² (factor 3 instead of 1.5).</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> τ<sub>max</sub>/τ<sub>avg</sub>: rectangle 1·5; solid circle 4/3; thin-walled circular tube 2; for an I-section most of the shear is carried by the web.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-89", year: 2017, no: 89, subject: "design", topic: "Failure criteria for ductile parts",
q: `Consider the following statements :<br>For a component made of ductile material, the failure criterion will be<ol><li>endurance limit, if the external force is fluctuating</li><li>fatigue, if the external force is fluctuating</li><li>yield stress, if the external force is static</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Failure criteria for ductile parts.</p><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> Under fluctuating loads a ductile part fails by fatigue at stresses well below yield. The design stress is based on the endurance limit (corrected for size, surface, reliability and stress concentration).</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> The same idea: the failure mode, and so the criterion, under fluctuating load is fatigue.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> Under static load a ductile material is taken to fail when it yields (permanent deformation), so yield strength is the basis. Brittle materials under static load use ultimate strength instead.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Static theories for ductile materials: maximum shear stress (Tresca, conservative) and distortion energy (von Mises, most accurate). For brittle materials: maximum normal stress (Rankine) or Coulomb–Mohr.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-90", year: 2017, no: 90, subject: "design", topic: "Modified Goodman relation",
q: `A machine component is subjected to a flexural stress, which fluctuates between 300 MN/m² and −150 MN/m². Taking the yield strength = 0·55 of the ultimate strength, endurance strength = 0·50 of the ultimate strength and factor of safety to be 2, the value of the minimum ultimate strength according to modified Goodman relation will be`,
opts: ["1100 MN/m²", "1075 MN/m²", "1050 MN/m²", "1025 MN/m²"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1050 MN/m²</h3><div class="formula">σ<sub>m</sub> = (σ<sub>max</sub> + σ<sub>min</sub>)/2 &nbsp;&nbsp; σ<sub>a</sub> = (σ<sub>max</sub> − σ<sub>min</sub>)/2 &nbsp;&nbsp; Goodman: σ<sub>a</sub>/S<sub>e</sub> + σ<sub>m</sub>/S<sub>ut</sub> = 1/N</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>σ<sub>m</sub> = mean stress; σ<sub>a</sub> = stress amplitude; S<sub>e</sub> = endurance strength = 0·5 S<sub>ut</sub>; S<sub>ut</sub> = ultimate strength; N = factor of safety = 2</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>σ<sub>m</sub> = (300 − 150)/2 = 75 MPa; σ<sub>a</sub> = (300 + 150)/2 = 225 MPa.</li>
<li>225/(0·5 S<sub>ut</sub>) + 75/S<sub>ut</sub> = 1/2 → (450 + 75)/S<sub>ut</sub> = 0·5.</li>
<li>S<sub>ut</sub> = 525/0·5 = <b>1050 MPa</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Yield check (Langer line): σ<sub>max</sub> = 300 MPa against S<sub>y</sub>/N = 0·55 × 1050/2 = 289 MPa. Strictly, a yield check would need S<sub>ut</sub> ≥ 1091 MPa; the question asks for the Goodman relation, which gives 1050.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 1100 MPa is close to the yield-line requirement (1091) rounded up, not the Goodman result.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 1075 does not satisfy either line.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 1025 MPa does not satisfy either line.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Soderberg (uses S<sub>y</sub> instead of S<sub>ut</sub>) is more conservative: 225/(0·5S) + 75/(0·55S) = 0·5 → S<sub>ut</sub> = 1173 MPa.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-91", year: 2017, no: 91, subject: "tom", topic: "Hartnell governor spring stiffness",
q: `In a Hartnell governor, the mass of each ball is 4 kg. The maximum and minimum centrifugal forces on the balls are 1800 N and 100 N at radii 25 cm and 20 cm, respectively. The lengths of vertical and horizontal arms of the bell-crank levers are the same. What is the spring stiffness?`,
opts: ["780 N/cm", "740 N/cm", "720 N/cm", "680 N/cm"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 680 N/cm</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In a Hartnell governor the bell-crank levers convert ball movement into sleeve movement. Taking moments about the fulcrum (obliquity of arms and ball weight neglected) at the two extreme positions:</p>
<div class="formula">S = 2F × (x/y) &nbsp;&nbsp; sleeve lift h = (r<sub>2</sub> − r<sub>1</sub>)(y/x) &nbsp;&nbsp; s = (S<sub>2</sub> − S<sub>1</sub>)/h = 2(F<sub>2</sub> − F<sub>1</sub>)/(r<sub>2</sub> − r<sub>1</sub>) × (x/y)²</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>F<sub>1</sub>, F<sub>2</sub> = centrifugal force on each ball at minimum and maximum radius r<sub>1</sub>, r<sub>2</sub></li><li>x = length of the ball (vertical) arm; y = length of the sleeve (horizontal) arm; here x = y</li><li>S = spring force on the sleeve; s = spring stiffness</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>F<sub>2</sub> − F<sub>1</sub> = 1800 − 100 = 1700 N; r<sub>2</sub> − r<sub>1</sub> = 5 cm.</li><li>s = 2 × 1700/5 × 1² = <b>680 N/cm</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 780 includes extra force terms (such as the ball weights) that the equal-arm simplified relation does not use.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 740 includes extra force terms (such as the ball weights) that the equal-arm simplified relation does not use.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 720 N/cm includes extra force terms (such as the ball weights) that the equal-arm simplified relation does not use.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> The ball mass (4 kg) is not needed once the centrifugal forces are given; it would be used to find the speeds: F = m ω² r.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-92", year: 2017, no: 92, subject: "design", topic: "Pre-tensioned bolted joints",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding the ends of the pressure vessels flanged by pre-tensioned bolts :<ol><li>Pre-tensioning helps to seal the pressure vessel.</li><li>Pre-tensioning reduces the maximum tensile stress in the bolts.</li><li>Pre-tensioning countermands the fatigue life of the bolts.</li><li>Pre-tensioning helps to reduce the deleterious effect of pressure pulsations in the pressure vessel.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3 only", "1, 3 and 4 only", "2 and 4 only", "1, 2, 3 and 4"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1, 3 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> With pre-tension F<sub>i</sub>, an external load P is shared between bolt and clamped members according to their stiffnesses:</p>
<div class="formula">F<sub>bolt</sub> = F<sub>i</sub> + C P, &nbsp; C = k<sub>b</sub>/(k<sub>b</sub> + k<sub>m</sub>)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>k<sub>b</sub> = bolt stiffness; k<sub>m</sub> = stiffness of the clamped members (usually much larger), so C is small, about 0·2–0·3</li></ul>
<p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> Pre-tension keeps the gasket and flanges compressed, so the joint stays sealed under pressure.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> Pre-tension <i>raises</i> the maximum bolt force (F<sub>i</sub> + CP is more than CP); bolts are made strong enough to take it.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct, read as "improves fatigue life"):</b> Because the bolt feels only the fraction C of the fluctuating load, its stress amplitude is small, so its fatigue life is much longer.</li>
<li><b>4 (correct):</b> For the same reason, pressure pulsations cause only small changes in bolt stress and do not open the joint.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 4, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 3, 4.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, 3, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Initial tightening torque T ≈ 0·2 F<sub>i</sub> d. Recommended pre-load is about 75% of proof load for reusable joints and 90% for permanent ones.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-93", year: 2017, no: 93, subject: "mech", topic: "Torsion: solid vs hollow shafts",
q: `Two shafts, one solid and the other hollow, made of the same material, will have the same strength and stiffness, if both are of the same`,
opts: ["length as well as weight", "length as well as polar modulus", "weight as well as polar modulus", "length, weight as well as polar modulus"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) length as well as polar modulus</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> From the torsion equation T/J = τ/r = Gθ/L:</p>
<ul><li><b>Torsional strength</b>: T = τ Z<sub>p</sub>, where Z<sub>p</sub> = J/r<sub>max</sub> is the polar modulus. The same material (same allowable τ) and the same Z<sub>p</sub> give the same strength.</li>
<li><b>Torsional stiffness</b>: T/θ = GJ/L, which depends on G (same material), J and the length L.</li></ul>
<p>The intended answer pairs the same polar modulus (strength) with the same length (stiffness). (Strictly, equal Z<sub>p</sub> does not give equal J for solid and hollow sections of different outer diameter, but among the options (b) is the one consistent with the torsion equation.)</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Equal weight (equal area) does <i>not</i> give equal strength: a hollow shaft of the same weight is stronger and stiffer.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Equal weight (equal area) does <i>not</i> give equal strength: a hollow shaft of the same weight is stronger and stiffer.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Equal weight together with equal polar modulus is impossible for a solid and a hollow shaft of the same material.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> For the same weight, a hollow shaft with d<sub>i</sub>/d<sub>o</sub> = 0·5 has about 44% more torsional strength than a solid one.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-94", year: 2017, no: 94, subject: "design", topic: "Shaft diameter for power",
q: `A solid shaft is to transmit 20 kW at 200 r.p.m. The ultimate shear stress for the shaft material is 360 MPa and the factor of safety is 8. The diameter of the solid shaft shall be`,
opts: ["42 mm", "45 mm", "48 mm", "51 mm"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 48 mm</h3><div class="formula">T = P/ω = 60P/(2πN) &nbsp;&nbsp; τ = 16T/(πd³) → d = (16T/πτ)<sup>1/3</sup></div>
<ul class="sym"><li>P = 20 kW; N = 200 rpm; τ<sub>allow</sub> = τ<sub>ult</sub>/FOS = 360/8 = 45 MPa</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>T = 20,000 × 60/(2π × 200) = 954·9 N m.</li><li>d³ = 16 × 954·9/(π × 45 × 10⁶) = 1·081 × 10<sup>−4</sup> m³.</li><li>d = 0·0476 m = 47·6 mm → <b>48 mm</b> (next size up).</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 42 mm is too small: it would exceed 45 MPa.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 45 mm is too small: it would exceed 45 MPa.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 51 mm is safe but not the minimum.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Shafts are made in standard sizes, so the calculated 47·6 mm is rounded <i>up</i>; rounding down would overstress the shaft. A keyway would weaken it further (about 25%), which is not considered here.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Quick check: d (mm) ≈ 365 × (P/(Nτ))<sup>1/3</sup>, with P in kW, N in rpm, τ in MPa.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-95", year: 2017, no: 95, subject: "tom", topic: "Velocity of a point on a crank",
q: `In the 4-bar mechanism as shown, the link PQ measures 30 cm and rotates uniformly at 100 rev/min. The velocity of point Q on link PQ is nearly<img class="fig" src="img/me/2017-95.png" alt="Four-bar PQRS: crank PQ at fixed pivot P, coupler QR 50 cm, rocker RS 60 cm to fixed pivot S, frame PS 70 cm">`,
opts: ["2·54 m/s", "3·14 m/s", "4·60 m/s", "5·80 m/s"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 3·14 m/s</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Q is on the crank PQ, which turns about the fixed pivot P. Its velocity is simply ω × PQ, perpendicular to PQ. The other links do not matter for this point.</p>
<div class="formula">v<sub>Q</sub> = ω r = (2πN/60) × PQ</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>N = 100 rpm; PQ = 0·30 m</li></ul>
<p>ω = 2π × 100/60 = 10·47 rad/s; v<sub>Q</sub> = 10·47 × 0·30 = <b>3·14 m/s</b>.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 2·54 would be velocities of other points (for example on the coupler or rocker), found from the velocity polygon, or use the wrong radius.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 4·60 would be velocities of other points (for example on the coupler or rocker), found from the velocity polygon, or use the wrong radius.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 5·80 m/s would be velocities of other points (for example on the coupler or rocker), found from the velocity polygon, or use the wrong radius.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> For R or the coupler you need the velocity polygon or instantaneous centres: v<sub>R</sub>/v<sub>Q</sub> = IR/IQ, where I is the instantaneous centre of QR.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-96", year: 2017, no: 96, subject: "design", topic: "Stresses in a flywheel rim",
q: `The rim of a flywheel is subjected to`,
opts: ["direct tensile stress and bending stress", "torsional shear stress and bending stress", "direct shear stress and bending stress", "compressive stress and bending stress"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) direct tensile stress and bending stress</h3><ul>
<li><b>Hoop (tensile) stress</b> from centrifugal force: treating the rim as a free rotating ring, σ<sub>t</sub> = ρ v², where ρ = density and v = rim velocity. This limits rim speed (about 30 m/s for cast iron).</li>
<li><b>Bending stress</b>: the arms stop the rim expanding freely, so the rim between two arms bends like a fixed-ended curved beam under the centrifugal load, σ<sub>b</sub> ∝ ρv²l²/t (l = rim length between arms, t = rim thickness).</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Design commonly takes about ¾ of the hoop stress plus ¼ of the bending stress.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Torsional shear acts on the <i>shaft</i> and, as bending, on the <i>arms</i> (which carry the torque); it is not the rim's stress.</li><li><b>(c)</b> There is no significant direct shear in the rim.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Centrifugal action puts the rim in tension, not compression.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> The arms carry bending from the transmitted torque (as cantilevers), plus tension from centrifugal force.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-97", year: 2017, no: 97, subject: "mfg", topic: "EOQ and total cost",
q: `A stockist has to supply 400 units of a product every Monday to his customers. He gets the product at ₹ 50 per unit from the manufacturer. The cost of ordering and transportation from the manufacturer to the stockist's premises is ₹ 75 per order. The cost of carrying inventory is 7·5% per year of the cost of the product. What are the economic lot size and the total optimal cost (including capital cost) for the stockist?`,
opts: ["989 units/order and ₹ 20,065·80/week", "912 units/order and ₹ 20,065·80/week", "989 units/order and ₹ 18,574·50/week", "912 units/order and ₹ 18,574·50/week"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 912 units/order and ₹ 20,065·80/week</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> EOQ and total cost.</p><div class="formula">Q* = √(2DC<sub>o</sub>/C<sub>h</sub>) &nbsp;&nbsp; minimum (ordering + carrying) cost = √(2DC<sub>o</sub>C<sub>h</sub>) &nbsp;&nbsp; total = purchase cost + √(2DC<sub>o</sub>C<sub>h</sub>)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>D = annual demand = 400 × 52 = 20,800 units</li><li>C<sub>o</sub> = ordering cost = ₹ 75/order</li><li>C<sub>h</sub> = carrying cost = 7·5% × 50 = ₹ 3·75 per unit per year</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Q* = √(2 × 20,800 × 75/3·75) = √8,32,000 = <b>912 units</b>.</li>
<li>Ordering + carrying = √(2 × 20,800 × 75 × 3·75) = ₹ 3420·5/year = ₹ 65·78/week.</li>
<li>Purchase cost = 400 × 50 = ₹ 20,000/week.</li>
<li>Total = ₹ <b>20,065·8/week</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> does not come from the EOQ formula with these data.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 989 units does not come from the EOQ formula with these data.</li><li><b>(d)</b> ₹ 18,574·50/week is below the purchase cost of ₹ 20,000/week, which is impossible.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> At EOQ, annual ordering cost = annual carrying cost (₹ 1710 each here).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-98", year: 2017, no: 98, subject: "mfg", topic: "Centreless grinding feed",
q: `Consider just only the following parameters :<ol><li>Grinding wheel diameter</li><li>Regulating wheel diameter</li><li>Speed of grinding wheel</li><li>Speed of regulating wheel</li></ol>Which of the above parameters will influence the axial feed rate in centreless grinding?`,
opts: ["2 and 4", "2 and 3", "1 and 3", "1 and 4"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 2 and 4</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In through-feed centreless grinding the rubber-bonded <b>regulating wheel</b> is tilted by a small angle α (about 1–8°). It rotates the work and also drives it axially through the gap. The grinding wheel only removes metal.</p>
<div class="formula">f = π D<sub>r</sub> N<sub>r</sub> sin α</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>f = axial feed rate; D<sub>r</sub> = regulating wheel diameter; N<sub>r</sub> = regulating wheel speed; α = tilt angle</li></ul>
<p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul>
<li><b>1:</b> The grinding wheel diameter does not appear in the feed relation.</li>
<li><b>2 (influences):</b> Regulating wheel diameter, through D<sub>r</sub>.</li>
<li><b>3:</b> Grinding wheel speed affects cutting speed and surface finish, not axial feed.</li>
<li><b>4 (influences):</b> Regulating wheel speed, through N<sub>r</sub>.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 2, 4.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 4, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 1, 3, which are incorrect; Omits 2, 4, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> The work speed (rotation) is also set by the regulating wheel: v<sub>w</sub> ≈ π D<sub>r</sub> N<sub>r</sub> cos α.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-99", year: 2017, no: 99, subject: "mfg", topic: "Three-wire method: best wire size",
q: `A metric thread of pitch 2 mm and thread angle 60° is inspected for its pitch diameter using the 3-wire method. The indicated diameter of the wire will be nearly`,
opts: ["0·85 mm", "1·05 mm", "1·15 mm", "2·05 mm"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1·15 mm</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The "best size" wire touches the thread flanks exactly at the pitch line, so errors in thread angle have the least effect on the measured effective diameter.</p>
<div class="formula">d<sub>best</sub> = p/(2 cos(θ/2))</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>p = pitch = 2 mm; θ = thread angle = 60°</li></ul>
<p>d = 2/(2 × cos 30°) = 1/0·866 = <b>1·155 mm</b>.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>d = 2/(2 cos 30°) = 1/0.866.</li><li>d = <b>1.155 mm</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0·85 mm is smaller than best size: still usable, but it do not touch at the pitch line.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 1·05 mm is smaller than best size: still usable, but it do not touch at the pitch line.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 2·05 mm is larger than the pitch; it would sit on the crests.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Effective diameter E = M − d(1 + cosec(θ/2)) + (p/2) cot(θ/2), where M = measurement over the wires. For a metric thread: E = M − 3d + 0·866p.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-100", year: 2017, no: 100, subject: "mechatronics", topic: "NC machines",
q: `Consider the following statements with reference to NC machines :<ol><li>Both closed-loop and open-loop systems are used.</li><li>Papers, tapes, floppy tapes and cassettes are used for data storage.</li><li>Digitizers may be used as interactive input devices.</li><li>Post-processor is an item of hardware.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 4", "1, 3 and 4", "2, 3 and 4", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> NC machines.</p><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> Open-loop NC uses stepper motors with no position feedback (cheap, light duty). Closed-loop NC uses servomotors with encoders or resolvers feeding back position and velocity.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> Classic NC stored part programs on punched paper tape, magnetic tape, floppy disks and cassettes. Modern CNC uses memory, USB and DNC networks.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> Digitizers (tablets, coordinate digitizers) are used as interactive input devices in CAD/CAM part programming.</li>
<li><b>4 (wrong):</b> A post-processor is <b>software</b>. It converts the general CL (cutter location) data produced by CAM into the G-code dialect of a particular machine–controller combination.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 4, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 4, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 4, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> NC: hard-wired control reading tape each run. CNC: a computer stores and edits the program. DNC: a central computer distributes programs to many machines.</p>` }
);

// ---- q2017e.js ----
// ESE 2017 Prelims Paper II, Mechanical Engineering, Series A, Q101–125
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
(function () {
  // Statement (I)/(II) items (Q121–150) share these four codes
  var C = ["Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is <i>not</i> the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Statement (I) is true but Statement (II) is false", "Statement (I) is false but Statement (II) is true"];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2017-101", year: 2017, no: 101, subject: "mfg", topic: "Benefits of CIM",
q: `Consider the following benefits of CIM :<ol><li>Less direct labour</li><li>Less scrap and rework</li><li>Higher machine use</li></ol>Which of the above are correct?`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3", "1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Computer-integrated manufacturing links design (CAD), planning (CAPP, MRP), production (CNC, robots, FMS), handling (AGVs, AS/RS) and quality control through shared computer data.</p>
<p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul>
<li><b>1 (benefit):</b> Automation of machining, handling and inspection cuts direct labour per part.</li>
<li><b>2 (benefit):</b> Consistent programmed operations and in-process inspection reduce variability, so less scrap and rework.</li>
<li><b>3 (benefit):</b> Better scheduling, quick changeovers and unattended running raise machine utilisation (often from about 30–50% to about 80%).</li></ul>
<p>Other benefits: shorter lead times, less work-in-process inventory, faster response to design changes, better quality records. The costs are high capital investment and the need for skilled staff.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Groover: CIM = CAD/CAM plus the business functions (order entry, costing, planning), integrated by a common database.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-102", year: 2017, no: 102, subject: "mfg", topic: "Inventory turnover",
q: `A firm's inventory turnover of ₹ 8,00,000 is 5 times the cost of goods sold. If the inventory turnover is improved to 8 with the cost of goods sold remaining the same, a substantial amount of fund is either released from, or gets additionally invested in, inventory. Which one of the following statements is correct?`,
opts: ["₹ 1,60,000 is released.", "₹ 1,60,000 is additionally invested.", "₹ 60,000 is released.", "₹ 60,000 is additionally invested."], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) ₹ 60,000 is released</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The wording is garbled; it means the cost of goods sold (COGS) is ₹ 8,00,000 and the inventory turnover ratio is 5.</p>
<div class="formula">Inventory turnover ratio = COGS / average inventory → average inventory = COGS / ratio</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>At a ratio of 5: inventory = 8,00,000/5 = ₹ 1,60,000.</li><li>At a ratio of 8: inventory = 8,00,000/8 = ₹ 1,00,000.</li><li>Funds released = 1,60,000 − 1,00,000 = <b>₹ 60,000</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> ₹ 1,60,000 is the original inventory, not the change.</li><li><b>(b)</b> ₹ 1,60,000 is the original inventory, not the change.</li><li><b>(d)</b> A higher turnover means <i>less</i> stock is held for the same sales, so money is released, not invested.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Days of inventory = 365/turnover ratio: 73 days at a ratio of 5, 45·6 days at 8.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-103", year: 2017, no: 103, subject: "mfg", topic: "Standard time from work measurement",
q: `An 8-hour measurement study in a plant reveals that 320 number of units were produced. If idle time = 15% and performance rating = 120%, with allowance = 12% of normal time, the standard time per unit produced will be`,
opts: ["1·823 minutes", "1·714 minutes", "1·645 minutes", "1·286 minutes"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1·714 minutes</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Standard time from work measurement.</p><div class="formula">Observed time = productive time/units &nbsp;&nbsp; Normal time = observed × rating &nbsp;&nbsp; Standard time = normal × (1 + allowance)</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Total time = 8 × 60 = 480 min; productive time = 480 × (1 − 0·15) = 408 min.</li><li>Observed time per unit = 408/320 = 1·275 min.</li><li>Normal time = 1·275 × 1·20 = 1·530 min.</li><li>Standard time = 1·530 × 1·12 = <b>1·714 min</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 1·823 min ignores the idle time (480/320 × 1·2 × 1·12 = 2·016, also wrong) or applies factors differently.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 1·645 min treats the allowance as a share of standard time in a different way.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 1·286 min omits the rating.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Rating above 100% means the worker was faster than normal, so normal time exceeds observed time.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-104", year: 2017, no: 104, subject: "mfg", topic: "Receivables turnover",
q: `An organization's sales during a financial year is ₹ 6,00,000 with 90% of it on credit. At the end of the year, the receivables turnover was found to be 5. Considering 365 days to a year, the average collection period and receivables are, respectively`,
opts: ["81 days and ₹ 1,08,000", "73 days and ₹ 1,08,000", "81 days and ₹ 1,20,000", "73 days and ₹ 1,20,000"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 73 days and ₹ 1,08,000</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Receivables turnover.</p><div class="formula">Receivables turnover = credit sales / average receivables &nbsp;&nbsp; average collection period = 365/turnover</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Credit sales = 0·9 × 6,00,000 = ₹ 5,40,000.</li><li>Receivables = 5,40,000/5 = <b>₹ 1,08,000</b>.</li><li>Collection period = 365/5 = <b>73 days</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(c)</b> ₹ 1,20,000 uses total sales (6,00,000/5) instead of credit sales.</li><li><b>(d)</b> ₹ 1,20,000 uses total sales (6,00,000/5) instead of credit sales.</li><li><b>(a)</b> divides by 4·5 or uses 365 × 1·108/5.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> A shorter collection period means customers pay faster and less working capital is tied up.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-105", year: 2017, no: 105, subject: "mfg", topic: "EOQ: number of orders",
q: `A particular item has a demand of 9000 units/year. The cost of one procurement is ₹ 108 and the holding cost per unit is ₹ 2·40/year. The replacement is instantaneous and no shortages are allowed. What is the optimum number of orders/year?`,
opts: ["7 orders/year", "8 orders/year", "9 orders/year", "10 orders/year"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 10 orders/year</h3><div class="formula">Q* = √(2DC<sub>o</sub>/C<sub>h</sub>) &nbsp;&nbsp; N* = D/Q* = √(DC<sub>h</sub>/2C<sub>o</sub>)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>D = 9000/year; C<sub>o</sub> = ₹ 108/order; C<sub>h</sub> = ₹ 2·40/unit/year</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Q* = √(2 × 9000 × 108/2·4) = √8,10,000 = 900 units.</li><li>N* = 9000/900 = <b>10 orders/year</b> (one every 36·5 days).</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 7 gives lot sizes of 1286, 1125 and 1000 units, all above EOQ and so costlier.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 8 gives lot sizes of 1286, 1125 and 1000 units, all above EOQ and so costlier.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 9 orders gives lot sizes of 1286, 1125 and 1000 units, all above EOQ and so costlier.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Replacement is instantaneous and there are no shortages, so this is the basic (Wilson) EOQ model: stock falls linearly from Q* to zero and the average inventory is Q*/2 = 450 units.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Total relevant cost at EOQ = √(2DC<sub>o</sub>C<sub>h</sub>) = ₹ 2160/year, half ordering (10 × 108) and half carrying (450 × 2·4).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-106", year: 2017, no: 106, subject: "mechatronics", topic: "Microcontrollers",
q: `Which one of the following is correct with respect to microcontrollers?`,
opts: ["Integration of a microprocessor with I/O interfaces and memory and other peripherals in a single IC chip", "A single very large scale integrated (VLSI) chip that contains programmable electronic components that perform control functions", "Digital circuits for data handling and computation", "The primary computation and system control operations"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) A microprocessor with I/O, memory and other peripherals integrated on a single chip</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A microcontroller (for example 8051, PIC, AVR, ARM Cortex-M) puts the CPU, RAM, ROM/flash, I/O ports, timers/counters, serial interfaces and often an ADC on one chip. That makes it a complete "computer on a chip" for embedded control (washing machines, engine control units, robots).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> describes a <b>microprocessor</b> (a VLSI CPU chip that needs external memory and I/O).</li><li><b>(c)</b> is a general description of digital logic or an ALU.</li><li><b>(d)</b> describes what a CPU does, not what makes a microcontroller.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Microprocessor-based systems are flexible and powerful (PCs); microcontrollers are cheap, compact and low-power for dedicated control tasks.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-107", year: 2017, no: 107, subject: "mechatronics", topic: "Seismic transducers",
q: `Which one of the following statements is correct?<br>Seismic transducer working in the displacement mode should have`,
opts: ["weak springs and heavy mass", "stiff springs and light mass", "weak springs and light mass", "stiff springs and heavy mass"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) weak springs and heavy mass</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A seismic (spring–mass–damper) instrument measures the relative motion between its mass and its case. Its response depends on the frequency ratio r = ω/ω<sub>n</sub>.</p>
<ul><li><b>Displacement mode (vibrometer):</b> for r ≫ 1 (ω<sub>n</sub> well below the vibration frequency), the mass stays almost still and the relative displacement equals the case displacement. A low ω<sub>n</sub> = √(k/m) needs a soft (weak) spring and a heavy mass.</li>
<li><b>Acceleration mode (accelerometer):</b> for r ≪ 1 (ω<sub>n</sub> well above the vibration frequency), the relative displacement is proportional to acceleration. This needs a stiff spring and a light mass.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Stiff springs and a light mass give a high natural frequency, which is the accelerometer design (ω ≪ ωn).</li><li><b>(c)</b> A light mass raises ωn; a displacement pickup needs ωn ≪ ω, so it needs a heavy mass.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Stiff springs raise ωn; a displacement pickup needs weak springs.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Vibrometers are therefore large and heavy; accelerometers (often piezoelectric) are small and light.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-108", year: 2017, no: 108, subject: "mechatronics", topic: "Hydraulic actuator velocity",
q: `What will be the velocity of piston movement for a single-acting hydraulic actuator, when the fluid pressure is 100 bar, the diameter of the piston is 50 mm and the flow rate is 0·3 m³/min?`,
opts: ["2·41 m/s", "2·55 m/s", "2·67 m/s", "2·84 m/s"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2·55 m/s</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Piston velocity depends only on the flow rate and the piston area (continuity). The pressure decides the force, not the speed.</p>
<div class="formula">v = Q/A, A = π D²/4</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>Q = 0·3 m³/min = 0·005 m³/s; D = 0·05 m</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>A = π × 0·05²/4 = 1·963 × 10<sup>−3</sup> m².</li><li>v = 0·005/1·963 × 10<sup>−3</sup> = <b>2·55 m/s</b>.</li></ol>
<p>The force available would be pA = 100 × 10⁵ × 1·963 × 10<sup>−3</sup> = 19·6 kN.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 2·41 corresponds to wrong areas (rounding π or the diameter).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 2·67 corresponds to wrong areas (rounding π or the diameter).</li><li><b>(d)</b> 2·84 m/s corresponds to wrong areas (rounding π or the diameter).</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Hydraulic power = p × Q = 10⁷ × 0·005 = 50 kW.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-109", year: 2017, no: 109, subject: "mechatronics", topic: "Stepper motor step angle",
q: `A stepper motor is to be used to drive the linear axis of a certain Mechatronics system. The motor output shaft is connected to a screw thread with a 30 mm pitch. Linear resolution of 0·5 mm is stipulated. What is the needed step angle?`,
opts: ["9°", "8°", "7°", "6°"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 6°</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> One revolution of the screw moves the nut by one pitch (single-start thread assumed). The motor must divide that revolution into enough steps to give the required linear resolution.</p>
<div class="formula">Steps per revolution = pitch/resolution &nbsp;&nbsp; step angle = 360°/steps</div>
<p>Steps = 30/0·5 = 60 → step angle = 360/60 = <b>6°</b>.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Steps/rev = 30/0.5 = 60.</li><li>Step angle = 360/60 = <b>6°</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 9° gives 40, 45 and 51·4 steps per revolution, i.e. resolutions of 0·75, 0·67 and 0·58 mm, coarser than required.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 8° gives 40, 45 and 51·4 steps per revolution, i.e. resolutions of 0·75, 0·67 and 0·58 mm, coarser than required.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 7° gives 40, 45 and 51·4 steps per revolution, i.e. resolutions of 0·75, 0·67 and 0·58 mm, coarser than required.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Common stepper step angles: 1·8° (200 steps/rev), 0·9°, 7·5°, 15°. Microstepping subdivides them further.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-110", year: 2017, no: 110, subject: "mechatronics", topic: "Stepper motor characteristics",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding a stepper motor :<ol><li>The rotation angle of the motor is proportional to the input pulse.</li><li>The motor has full torque at standstill.</li><li>Speed and electric control signal of the motor vary mutually linearly.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Stepper motor characteristics.</p><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> Each pulse turns the rotor by one step angle, so the total angle is proportional to the number of pulses. This is why steppers can position open-loop.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> With the windings energised and the rotor stationary, a stepper develops its full holding torque (its torque is highest at low speed).</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong):</b> Speed is proportional to the pulse <i>frequency</i>, not linearly related to an analogue control signal as in a DC servo. Torque falls as speed rises, and above the pull-out curve the motor loses steps.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Stepper drawbacks: resonance at certain step rates, loss of steps under overload (no feedback in open loop), lower efficiency than servomotors.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-111", year: 2017, no: 111, subject: "mfg", topic: "Critical path method",
q: `The following table lists the tasks in a project and the time duration for each task :<table><tr><th>Task</th><th>Preceding task</th><th>Normal duration (in weeks)</th></tr><tr><td>A</td><td>—</td><td>16</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>—</td><td>20</td></tr><tr><td>C</td><td>A</td><td>8</td></tr><tr><td>D</td><td>A</td><td>10</td></tr><tr><td>E</td><td>B, C</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td>F</td><td>D, E</td><td>12</td></tr></table>The critical path, the project duration and the free float for activity A are, respectively,`,
opts: ["A–C–E–F; 42 weeks and 0 week", "B–E–F; 42 weeks and 1 week", "B–C–D–F; 50 weeks and 2 weeks", "A–C–E–F; 50 weeks and 0 week"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) A–C–E–F; 42 weeks and 0 week</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Forward pass (earliest finish, EF)</b></p><div class="formula">EF = ES + duration</div><div class="formula">ES = max(EF of predecessors)</div><div class="formula">Free float = ES(successor) − EF</div><table><tr><th>Activity</th><th>ES</th><th>EF</th></tr><tr><td>A</td><td>0</td><td>16</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>0</td><td>20</td></tr><tr><td>C (after A)</td><td>16</td><td>24</td></tr><tr><td>D (after A)</td><td>16</td><td>26</td></tr><tr><td>E (after B, C)</td><td>max(20, 24) = 24</td><td>30</td></tr><tr><td>F (after D, E)</td><td>max(26, 30) = 30</td><td>42</td></tr></table>
<p>Project duration = <b>42 weeks</b>. Paths: A–C–E–F = 42, A–D–F = 38, B–E–F = 38. The critical path is <b>A–C–E–F</b>.</p>
<p>Free float of A = (earliest start of its successors C and D) − EF<sub>A</sub> = 16 − 16 = <b>0</b>, as expected on the critical path.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>A 0–16, B 0–20, C 16–24, D 16–26.</li><li>E: ES = max(20, 24) = 24 ⇒ 24–30.</li><li>F: ES = max(26, 30) = 30 ⇒ 30–42.</li><li>Critical path A–C–E–F = <b>42 weeks</b>; free float of A = 16 − 16 = <b>0</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> B–E–F takes 38 weeks and has total float 4.</li><li><b>(c)</b> B–C–D–F is not a valid path: C comes after A, not after B.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 50 weeks wrongly adds all of A, C, D and F.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Free float = ES of successor − EF of activity (delay possible without delaying any successor). Total float = LS − ES (delay possible without delaying the project).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-112", year: 2017, no: 112, subject: "mechatronics", topic: "SCARA robot kinematics",
q: `Consider the following statements with reference to SCARA Robot :<ol><li>It has four degrees of freedom.</li><li>It has only one forward kinematic solution.</li><li>It has two inverse kinematic solutions.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> SCARA = Selective Compliance Assembly Robot Arm (RRP + wrist roll).</p>
<p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> Usually 4 DOF: two horizontal revolute joints (shoulder, elbow), one vertical prismatic (Z) and one wrist roll.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> Forward kinematics (joint values → end-effector pose) always has a unique solution for a serial arm.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> Inverse kinematics (pose → joint values) for the planar two-link part has two solutions, elbow-left and elbow-right (mirror configurations), for any reachable point not on the workspace boundary.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> SCARA is compliant in the horizontal plane but rigid vertically: ideal for pick-and-place and vertical insertion in electronics assembly.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-113", year: 2017, no: 113, subject: "mechatronics", topic: "Laws of robotics",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding the laws of Robotics :<ol><li>A Robot shall not injure a human being or, through inaction, allow a human being harmed.</li><li>A Robot must obey orders given by humans except when such orders conflict with first law.</li><li>A Robot must always protect its own existence.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Isaac Asimov's Three Laws of Robotics (1942, "Runaround"):</p>
<ol><li>A robot may not injure a human being or, through inaction, allow a human being to come to harm.</li><li>A robot must obey orders given by human beings, except where such orders would conflict with the First Law.</li><li>A robot must protect its own existence <i>as long as such protection does not conflict with the First or Second Law</i>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul><li><b>1 (correct):</b> First law as stated.</li><li><b>2 (correct):</b> Second law as stated.</li><li><b>3 (wrong):</b> It says "always", leaving out the essential condition that self-protection is subordinate to the first two laws.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Asimov later added a "Zeroth Law": a robot may not harm humanity, or by inaction allow humanity to come to harm.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-114", year: 2017, no: 114, subject: "mechatronics", topic: "Robot configuration notation", doubt: true,
q: `The number of degrees of freedom in a 3D Robot of TRL : R type configuration is`,
opts: ["4", "3", "2", "1"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1 (official key)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In Groover's notation a robot is written as arm joints : wrist joints, using the letters L (linear), O (orthogonal), R (rotational), T (twisting) and V (revolving). "TRL : R" therefore means an arm with a twisting, a rotational and a linear joint, plus one rotational wrist joint.</p>
<p>UPSC's key gives 1. That would make sense only if the question asked about the <b>wrist</b> alone (the part after the colon, R = one degree of freedom).</p>
<p><b>Option by option</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 4: total joints 3 + 1, by the standard reading of the notation (our answer).</li><li><b>(b)</b> 3: the arm (TRL) alone.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 2 has no basis in the notation.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 1: the wrist (R) alone; the key's answer.</li></ul>
<div class="note">In doubt (key followed). Each letter in Groover's configuration code is one joint with one degree of freedom, so TRL : R has 3 + 1 = 4 DOF, which is option (a). The key's (d) counts only the wrist joint.</div>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Example notations: TLL = cylindrical; TRL = polar (spherical); TRR = articulated (jointed arm); LLL = Cartesian; a two-axis wrist is written RT.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-115", year: 2017, no: 115, subject: "mfg", topic: "Condition monitoring: vibration vs noise",
q: `Which of the following are the basic differences between vibration signature and noise signature?<ol><li>Vibration signature is essentially in the frequency range zero to 100 cps whereas noise signature is in the range 20 cps to 3000 cps.</li><li>Vibration signature has well-defined peaks whereas the noise signal is smeared.</li><li>The intensities of noise signatures are much less than that of vibration signatures.</li><li>Detection of vibration signature calls for a microphone whereas that of noise can do with a pickup.</li></ol>Select the correct answer using the code given below.`,
opts: ["1 and 4", "2 and 3", "1 and 2", "3 and 4"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 2</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Both are used in predictive (condition-based) maintenance to detect faults such as unbalance, misalignment and bearing defects.</p>
<p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul>
<li><b>1 (correct, as intended):</b> Machine vibration signatures of interest are mostly at low frequencies (shaft speed and its harmonics); airborne noise is in the audible band, about 20 Hz upward to a few kHz.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> Vibration spectra show distinct peaks at defect frequencies (1×, 2× running speed, gear mesh, bearing frequencies); noise is broadband and spread out ("smeared") because of reflections and background sources.</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong):</b> The two are measured in different quantities (sound pressure level versus displacement, velocity or acceleration), so "much less intensity" is not a basic difference.</li>
<li><b>4 (wrong):</b> It is reversed: vibration is picked up by contact transducers (accelerometers, velocity pickups, proximity probes); noise is measured with a microphone.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 4, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, 4, which are incorrect; Omits 1, 2, which are also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Vibration severity is usually judged by RMS velocity (mm/s) against ISO 10816 (now ISO 20816) zones.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-116", year: 2017, no: 116, subject: "mechatronics", topic: "SCARA robot features",
q: `Consider the following features relating to Robot kinematics with reference to SCARA Robot :<ol><li>Shoulder and elbow rotational axes are vertical.</li><li>The Robot could perform insertion tasks along the vertical direction.</li><li>Its general configuration is analogous to that of a human arm.</li></ol>Which of the above features are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> SCARA robot features.</p><p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> In a SCARA both revolute joints (shoulder and elbow) have <b>vertical</b> axes, so the arm swings in a horizontal plane.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> A vertical prismatic joint at the end gives straight up-and-down motion: ideal for inserting pins and components, screwing and pick-and-place.</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong):</b> The configuration analogous to a human arm (shoulder, elbow, wrist with horizontal joint axes) is the <b>articulated</b> or jointed-arm robot (such as PUMA). Groover describes the SCARA as similar to the jointed-arm robot <i>except</i> that its shoulder and elbow axes are vertical.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Workspace shapes: Cartesian → rectangular box; cylindrical → cylinder; polar → partial sphere; articulated → irregular sphere-like; SCARA → cylinder-like (kidney-shaped in plan).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-117", year: 2017, no: 117, subject: "tom", topic: "Flywheel kinetic energy",
q: `A flywheel fitted to a steam engine has a mass of 500 kg and radius of gyration 300 mm. The starting torque is 900 N m. What is the kinetic energy after 10 s?`,
opts: ["225 kJ", "450 kJ", "900 kJ", "1800 kJ"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 900 kJ</h3><div class="formula">I = m k² &nbsp;&nbsp; α = T/I &nbsp;&nbsp; ω = α t (from rest) &nbsp;&nbsp; KE = ½ I ω²</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>m = 500 kg; k = 0·3 m (radius of gyration); T = 900 N m (constant starting torque); t = 10 s</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>I = 500 × 0·09 = 45 kg m².</li><li>α = 900/45 = 20 rad/s².</li><li>ω = 20 × 10 = 200 rad/s.</li><li>KE = ½ × 45 × 200² = 9,00,000 J = <b>900 kJ</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Check: work done by the torque = T × θ = 900 × ½ × 20 × 10² = 900 kJ.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(d)</b> 1800 kJ omits the ½.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 450 kJ takes ω or t at half value.</li><li><b>(a)</b> 225 kJ takes ω or t at half value.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Flywheel energy fluctuation ΔE = I ω<sub>mean</sub>² C<sub>s</sub>, where C<sub>s</sub> = (ω<sub>max</sub> − ω<sub>min</sub>)/ω<sub>mean</sub> is the coefficient of fluctuation of speed.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-118", year: 2017, no: 118, subject: "thermo", topic: "Heat exchanger effectiveness", doubt: true,
q: `In a counterflow heat exchanger, hot gases enter at 250 °C and leave at 100 °C. Atmospheric air enters at 50 °C and leaves at 80 °C. The effectiveness of the heat exchanger will be`,
opts: ["0·20", "0·25", "0·30", "0·35"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 0·20 (official key)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Definition</b></p><div class="formula">ε = Q<sub>actual</sub>/Q<sub>max</sub> = C<sub>min</sub>(ΔT of the C<sub>min</sub> fluid)/[C<sub>min</sub>(T<sub>h,in</sub> − T<sub>c,in</sub>)]</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>C = ṁ c<sub>p</sub> (heat capacity rate); T<sub>h,in</sub> − T<sub>c,in</sub> = 250 − 50 = 200 K (the largest possible temperature change)</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Energy balance: C<sub>h</sub> × 150 = C<sub>c</sub> × 30 → C<sub>c</sub> = 5 C<sub>h</sub>, so the hot gas is the C<sub>min</sub> fluid.</li>
<li>Then ε = 150/200 = 0·75, which is not an option.</li>
<li>The keyed value 0·20 equals 30/150, the air temperature rise divided by the gas temperature drop. On the air side alone, 30/200 = 0·15.</li></ol>
<p><b>Other options</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 0·25 comes from no consistent reading of the data.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0·30 comes from no consistent reading of the data.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0·35 comes from no consistent reading of the data.</li></ul>
<div class="note">In doubt (key followed). With the given temperatures, the energy balance makes the gas the minimum-capacity fluid and the effectiveness 0·75. None of the options is the textbook effectiveness; the key's 0·20 is the ratio of the two temperature changes. Our own pick was also (a), as the only value with any basis in the data.</div>
<p><b>Remember:</b> ε–NTU: for counterflow, ε = [1 − e<sup>−NTU(1−C<sub>r</sub>)</sup>]/[1 − C<sub>r</sub> e<sup>−NTU(1−C<sub>r</sub>)</sup>], with C<sub>r</sub> = C<sub>min</sub>/C<sub>max</sub>.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-119", year: 2017, no: 119, subject: "icrac", topic: "Adiabatic mixing of air streams",
q: `Two air streams with mass flow rates of 36 kg/min and 14 kg/min with respective enthalpies of 36 kJ/kg da and 50 kJ/kg da are mixed. The enthalpy of the mixture is nearly`,
opts: ["64 kJ/kg da", "55 kJ/kg da", "46 kJ/kg da", "40 kJ/kg da"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 40 kJ/kg da</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Adiabatic mixing conserves dry-air mass and energy, so the mixture enthalpy is the mass-weighted average.</p>
<div class="formula">h<sub>3</sub> = (ṁ<sub>1</sub>h<sub>1</sub> + ṁ<sub>2</sub>h<sub>2</sub>)/(ṁ<sub>1</sub> + ṁ<sub>2</sub>)</div>
<p>h<sub>3</sub> = (36 × 36 + 14 × 50)/50 = (1296 + 700)/50 = <b>39·9 kJ/kg da</b>.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>h₃ = (36 × 36 + 14 × 50)/(36 + 14).</li><li>= (1296 + 700)/50 = <b>39.9 ≈ 40 kJ/kg da</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 64 exceed both inlet enthalpies, which is impossible for adiabatic mixing.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 55 exceed both inlet enthalpies, which is impossible for adiabatic mixing.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 46 is nearer the smaller stream's value and gives it too much weight.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> On the psychrometric chart the mixture state lies on the straight line joining the two states, dividing it in the inverse ratio of the masses (nearer the bigger stream). Specific humidity and, approximately, dry-bulb temperature mix the same way.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-120", year: 2017, no: 120, subject: "turbo", topic: "Two-stage compressor: minimum work",
q: `Consider the following statements in respect of maximum efficiency of a two-stage reciprocating compressor :<ol><li>The pressure ratios are same for each stage.</li><li>The work done is same in each stage.</li><li>The intercooling is perfect.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Minimum total work (maximum efficiency) for a two-stage compressor with the same polytropic index n in both stages requires all three conditions together.</p>
<p><b>Statement by statement:</b></p><ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> The optimum intermediate pressure is p<sub>2</sub> = √(p<sub>1</sub>p<sub>3</sub>), which gives equal pressure ratios in the two stages.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> With equal pressure ratios and perfect intercooling, each stage does equal work: W = 2 × n/(n − 1) × p<sub>1</sub>V<sub>1</sub>[(p<sub>3</sub>/p<sub>1</sub>)<sup>(n−1)/2n</sup> − 1].</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> Perfect intercooling means the air is cooled back to the initial (suction) temperature before the second stage; it is the basis of the optimum above.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> For z stages: equal pressure ratio (p<sub>final</sub>/p<sub>1</sub>)<sup>1/z</sup> per stage, equal work, equal heat rejected in each intercooler.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-121", year: 2017, no: 121, subject: "fm", topic: "Centre of pressure",
q: `Statement (I) : Depth of centre of pressure of any immersed surface is independent of the density of the liquid.<br>Statement (II) : Centre of area of the immersed body lies below the centre of pressure.`,
opts: C, ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Statement (I) is true but Statement (II) is false</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Centre of pressure.</p><div class="formula">h<sub>cp</sub> = h̄ + I<sub>G</sub> sin²θ/(A h̄)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>h<sub>cp</sub> = depth of centre of pressure; h̄ = depth of centroid; I<sub>G</sub> = second moment of area about the centroidal axis; A = area; θ = angle of the surface to the free surface</li></ul>
<ul><li><b>Statement (I) (true):</b> Density cancels: the total force (ρgAh̄) and its moment both scale with ρg, so the location of the centre of pressure is purely geometric.</li>
<li><b>Statement (II) (false):</b> Since I<sub>G</sub> sin²θ/(A h̄) &gt; 0, the centre of pressure always lies <b>below</b> the centroid. Equivalently, the centroid (centre of area) lies <i>above</i> the centre of pressure, not below. The two coincide only for a horizontal surface.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> II is false.</li><li><b>(b)</b> II is false.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> I is true and II is false.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I is true, so this is not right.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> As the surface goes deeper, h̄ grows and the centre of pressure moves closer to the centroid.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-122", year: 2017, no: 122, subject: "fm", topic: "Reynolds number and flow regime", doubt: true,
q: `Statement (I) : In flow through a pipeline, the nature of the fluid flow depends on the velocity of the fluid.<br>Statement (II) : Reynolds number of the flow depends on the velocity, the diameter of the pipe and the kinematic viscosity of the fluid.`,
opts: C, ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Statement (I) is false but Statement (II) is true (official key)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Reynolds number and flow regime.</p><ul>
<li><b>Statement (II) (true):</b> Re = VD/ν, where V = mean velocity, D = pipe diameter and ν = kinematic viscosity.</li>
<li><b>Statement (I):</b> UPSC's key treats it as false: the nature of flow (laminar or turbulent) is decided by the Reynolds number, a combination of velocity, diameter and viscosity, not by velocity alone. The same velocity can be laminar in a thin tube of viscous oil and turbulent in a large water main.</li></ul>
<div class="note">In doubt (key followed). Our answer was (a): velocity does decide the regime for a given pipe and fluid (raising V raises Re and eventually causes transition, as in Reynolds' dye experiment), so Statement (I) is true in that sense and Statement (II) explains it. The key reads Statement (I) as claiming velocity is the only factor.</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> I is false.</li><li><b>(b)</b> I is false.</li><li><b>(c)</b> I is false and II is true, not the reverse.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> I is false and II is true.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Reynolds' experiment (1883): the dye filament stayed straight at low velocity and broke up into eddies above a critical velocity, the threshold being V<sub>c</sub> = Re<sub>c</sub> ν/D.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-123", year: 2017, no: 123, subject: "thermo", topic: "c<sub>p</sub> greater than c<sub>v</sub>",
q: `Statement (I) : The specific heat at constant pressure for an ideal gas is always greater than the specific heat at constant volume.<br>Statement (II) : Heat added at constant volume is not utilized for doing any external work.`,
opts: C, ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Both true; Statement (II) explains Statement (I)</h3><ul>
<li><b>Statement (II) (true):</b> At constant volume dV = 0, so no boundary work is done. All the heat raises internal energy: Q = m c<sub>v</sub> ΔT.</li>
<li><b>Statement (I) (true):</b> At constant pressure the gas expands and does work pΔV = mRΔT in addition to raising its internal energy by the same m c<sub>v</sub>ΔT. More heat is needed per degree: c<sub>p</sub> = c<sub>v</sub> + R (Mayer's relation), so c<sub>p</sub> &gt; c<sub>v</sub> always.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Statement (II) is exactly why c<sub>p</sub> exceeds c<sub>v</sub>, so it explains Statement (I).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Both true and II explains I (the keyed answer).</li><li><b>(b)</b> II is the correct explanation of I here.</li><li><b>(c)</b> II is true, so this is not right.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I is true, so this is not right.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> For air, c<sub>p</sub> = 1·005, c<sub>v</sub> = 0·718, R = 0·287 kJ/kg K, γ = 1·4. Monatomic gases: γ = 5/3; diatomic: 7/5.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-124", year: 2017, no: 124, subject: "thermo", topic: "Pure substance", doubt: true,
q: `Statement (I) : A homogeneous mixture of gases that do not react within themselves can be treated as a pure substance.<br>Statement (II) : Flue gases can be treated as a homogeneous mixture of gases.`,
opts: C, ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Statement (I) is true but Statement (II) is false (official key)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Pure substance.</p><ul>
<li><b>Statement (I) (true):</b> A pure substance has a homogeneous and invariable chemical composition throughout, even if it is a mixture. A non-reacting homogeneous gas mixture such as air qualifies, as long as no component condenses or separates.</li>
<li><b>Statement (II):</b> UPSC's key treats it as false. Flue gases contain water vapour (and sometimes particulate matter and unburnt components) that can condense or separate as the gas cools, so the composition of the gas phase is not invariable.</li></ul>
<div class="note">In doubt (key followed). Our answer was (b): in combustion analysis flue gas is routinely treated as a homogeneous ideal-gas mixture (Orsat analysis, partial pressures), provided it stays above its dew point. Statement (II) is true in that working sense, though it does not explain Statement (I). The key's (c) treats possible condensation of water vapour as making Statement (II) false.</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> II is false.</li><li><b>(b)</b> II is false.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> I is true and II is false.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I is true, so this is not right.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> A mixture of liquid air and gaseous air is <i>not</i> a pure substance, because the liquid and vapour phases have different compositions (nitrogen boils off first).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-125", year: 2017, no: 125, subject: "icrac", topic: "Air-blast fuel injection", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Statement (I) : Air-blast injection in diesel engines could reduce engine efficiency.<br>Statement (II) : Air-blast injection in diesel engines is not instantaneous but happens when the piston moves outward with the injection valve remaining open for whatever reason.`,
opts: C, ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Both true; Statement (II) explains Statement (I)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In air-blast injection (used by Rudolf Diesel's early engines), fuel is blown into the cylinder by air compressed to about 60–70 bar by an engine-driven compressor.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Statement (II) (true):</b> The fuel–air blast continues while the injection valve stays open during the early part of the outward (expansion) stroke, so fuel burns progressively as the piston moves away. This is the near constant-pressure combustion of the ideal Diesel cycle.</li>
<li><b>Statement (I) (true):</b> Burning fuel later in the stroke means part of the heat is released when the expansion ratio still available is smaller, so less of it is converted to work and efficiency falls (constant-pressure heat addition is less efficient than constant-volume at the same compression ratio). The compressor also absorbs power.</li></ul>
<p>The delayed, prolonged injection described in Statement (II) is the reason given for the efficiency loss, so (a).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Both true and II explains I (the keyed answer).</li><li><b>(b)</b> II is the correct explanation of I here.</li><li><b>(c)</b> II is true, so this is not right.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I is true, so this is not right.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Air-blast injection was replaced by solid (airless) injection with high-pressure pumps (now common-rail at 1500–2500 bar), which removed the compressor and allowed faster, controlled injection.</p>` }
);
})();

// ---- q2017f.js ----
// ESE 2017 Prelims Paper II, Mechanical Engineering, Series A, Q126–150 (Statement I/II items)
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
(function () {
  var C = ["Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is <i>not</i> the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Statement (I) is true but Statement (II) is false", "Statement (I) is false but Statement (II) is true"];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2017-126", year: 2017, no: 126, subject: "icrac", topic: "Zeotropic refrigerant mixtures",
q: `Statement (I) : Use of non-azeotropic mixtures used as the refrigerant in a vapour-compression system improves the coefficient of performance.<br>Statement (II) : The increase in this coefficient is attributable to reduction in volume.`,
opts: C, ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Statement (I) is true but Statement (II) is false</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Zeotropic refrigerant mixtures.</p><ul>
<li><b>Statement (I) (true):</b> A non-azeotropic (zeotropic) blend evaporates and condenses over a temperature <b>glide</b> rather than at one temperature. With counterflow heat exchangers its temperature can follow the falling temperature of the fluid being cooled and the rising temperature of the cooling medium, so the mean temperature differences shrink. The cycle approaches the Lorenz cycle, and the COP can rise.</li>
<li><b>Statement (II) (false):</b> The gain comes from better temperature matching (less irreversibility in heat transfer), not from any "reduction in volume".</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> II is false.</li><li><b>(b)</b> II is false.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> I is true and II is false.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I is true, so this is not right.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Azeotropes (for example R-502, R-507) behave like a single substance with a fixed boiling point. Zeotropes (R-407C, R-410A with near-zero glide) fractionate on leaks, so they must be charged as liquid.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-127", year: 2017, no: 127, subject: "icrac", topic: "Sub-cooling",
q: `Statement (I) : Sub-cooling of a refrigerant liquid increases the coefficient of performance of a refrigeration cycle.<br>Statement (II) : Sub-cooling reduces the work requirement of the refrigeration cycle.`,
opts: C, ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Statement (I) is true but Statement (II) is false</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Sub-cooling.</p><ul>
<li><b>Statement (I) (true):</b> Sub-cooling the liquid below its saturation temperature lowers h<sub>3</sub>, and so h<sub>4</sub> after throttling. The refrigerating effect (h<sub>1</sub> − h<sub>4</sub>) increases, so COP = RE/W rises.</li>
<li><b>Statement (II) (false):</b> The compressor still compresses the same saturated vapour between the same pressures, so the work per kg (h<sub>2</sub> − h<sub>1</sub>) is <b>unchanged</b>. COP improves because the numerator grows, not because the work falls.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> II is false.</li><li><b>(b)</b> II is false.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> I is true and II is false.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I is true, so this is not right.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Superheating the suction vapour does the opposite: it increases both RE (if the superheat is useful) and work, and its net effect on COP depends on the refrigerant.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-128", year: 2017, no: 128, subject: "icrac", topic: "Solution circulation in VAR systems", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Statement (I) : In vapour-absorption system, larger the value of specific solution circulation, more the pump work.<br>Statement (II) : Higher solution circulation rates of poor as well as rich solutions need larger pressure drops in the system.`,
opts: C, ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Both true; Statement (II) explains Statement (I)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Specific solution circulation f = mass of strong (rich) solution pumped per kg of refrigerant generated.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Statement (I) (true):</b> Pump work per kg of refrigerant = f × v × (p<sub>generator</sub> − p<sub>absorber</sub>). A larger f means more liquid pumped and more pump work. It also increases the sensible heat that must be supplied in the generator, so the COP falls.</li>
<li><b>Statement (II) (true):</b> Pumping more rich solution up, and returning more weak (poor) solution through the throttle valve, means larger flows and larger frictional pressure drops in the pipework, heat exchanger and valves, adding to the pressure the pump must develop.</li></ul>
<p>UPSC's key links the two: the higher circulation needs larger pressure drops, hence more pump work.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Both true and II explains I (the keyed answer).</li><li><b>(b)</b> II is the correct explanation of I here.</li><li><b>(c)</b> II is true, so this is not right.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I is true, so this is not right.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> f = (1 − x<sub>w</sub>)/(x<sub>s</sub> − x<sub>w</sub>), where x<sub>s</sub> and x<sub>w</sub> are the refrigerant concentrations in the strong and weak solutions. A wider concentration difference means less circulation.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-129", year: 2017, no: 129, subject: "turbo", topic: "Outward radial flow turbines",
q: `Statement (I) : Outward radial flow turbines do race inherently.<br>Statement (II) : In outward radial flow turbines, the centrifugal head impressed upon the exiting water leads to flow increase.`,
opts: C, ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Both true; Statement (II) explains Statement (I)</h3><ul>
<li><b>Statement (II) (true):</b> In an outward-flow runner, water moves from a small radius to a large one, so the centrifugal head (u<sub>2</sub>² − u<sub>1</sub>²)/2g <b>helps</b> the flow. If the runner speeds up, this head grows and the discharge increases.</li>
<li><b>Statement (I) (true):</b> When load drops and the speed rises, the extra flow gives more power, which raises the speed further: the turbine tends to "race" (run away). This is why outward-flow turbines (such as Fourneyron's) were abandoned.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Inward-flow (Francis) turbines are self-regulating: the centrifugal head opposes the flow, so a rise in speed reduces the discharge.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Both true and II explains I (the keyed answer).</li><li><b>(b)</b> II is the correct explanation of I here.</li><li><b>(c)</b> II is true, so this is not right.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I is true, so this is not right.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> This inherent self-governing is a key reason the inward mixed-flow Francis turbine became standard.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-130", year: 2017, no: 130, subject: "design", topic: "Clutch location",
q: `Statement (I) : Regarding the power transmitted by a clutch, greater the speed, lower the torque to be transmitted for fixed power rating.<br>Statement (II) : The clutch is placed on the low-speed side to transmit larger torque.`,
opts: C, ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Statement (I) is true but Statement (II) is false</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Clutch location.</p><ul>
<li><b>Statement (I) (true):</b> P = T ω, so at constant power a higher speed means a lower torque.</li>
<li><b>Statement (II) (false):</b> Because torque is lower at higher speed, the clutch is placed on the <b>high-speed</b> side (for example between engine and gearbox, before speed reduction). A smaller clutch can then transmit the power. On the low-speed side it would need to carry a much larger torque and be bigger and heavier.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> II is false.</li><li><b>(b)</b> II is false.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> I is true and II is false.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I is true, so this is not right.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Friction clutch torque (uniform wear): T = μ W R<sub>mean</sub> n, where n = number of friction surfaces and R<sub>mean</sub> = (R<sub>1</sub> + R<sub>2</sub>)/2.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-131", year: 2017, no: 131, subject: "turbo", topic: "Clearance volume effect",
q: `Statement (I) : The volume of air taken into the cylinder of a reciprocating air compressor is less than the stroke volume of the cylinder.<br>Statement (II) : Air that has been compressed to clearance volume expands to larger volumes during the suction stroke.`,
opts: C, ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Both true; Statement (II) explains Statement (I)</h3><ul>
<li><b>Statement (II) (true):</b> At the end of delivery, air at delivery pressure remains in the clearance space. On the return stroke it re-expands (V<sub>c</sub> → V<sub>c</sub>(p<sub>2</sub>/p<sub>1</sub>)<sup>1/n</sup>) before the pressure falls to suction pressure and the inlet valve opens.</li>
<li><b>Statement (I) (true):</b> Part of the stroke is used up by this re-expansion, so the fresh air drawn in is less than the swept volume: η<sub>v</sub> = 1 + C − C(p<sub>2</sub>/p<sub>1</sub>)<sup>1/n</sup> &lt; 1.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The re-expansion in Statement (II) is the reason for Statement (I).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Both true and II explains I (the keyed answer).</li><li><b>(b)</b> II is the correct explanation of I here.</li><li><b>(c)</b> II is true, so this is not right.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I is true, so this is not right.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Other causes that further reduce the induced volume: heating of the intake air by hot walls, pressure drop across the inlet valve, leakage.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-132", year: 2017, no: 132, subject: "power", topic: "Reheat in Rankine cycle", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Statement (I) : Providing reheat in a Rankine cycle would increase the efficiency of the cycle.<br>Statement (II) : Reheat in Rankine cycle reduces specific steam consumption.`,
opts: C, ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Statement (I) is false but Statement (II) is true (official key)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Reheat in Rankine cycle.</p><ul>
<li><b>Statement (II) (true):</b> Reheating adds a second expansion, so each kg of steam does more work. The specific steam consumption (kg/kWh = 3600/w<sub>net</sub>) falls.</li>
<li><b>Statement (I) (false as a general statement):</b> The main purpose of reheat is to keep the steam drier at the turbine exhaust (less blade erosion) while allowing higher boiler pressures. Whether efficiency rises depends on the reheat pressure and temperature. Reheating at too low a pressure adds heat at a low mean temperature and can <i>reduce</i> efficiency; a well-chosen reheat (about 20–25% of boiler pressure) gives only a few percent gain.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> I is false.</li><li><b>(b)</b> I is false.</li><li><b>(c)</b> I is false and II is true, not the reverse.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> I is false and II is true.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Reheat: mainly for exhaust dryness (x ≥ about 0·88). Regeneration: mainly for efficiency. Large plants use both.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-133", year: 2017, no: 133, subject: "power", topic: "Natural vs artificial draught",
q: `Statement (I) : Heat carried away by hot gases in chimney draught is much greater than the work required for lifting the same gases through the height of the chimney. Yet artificial draught is not preferred.<br>Statement (II) : Artificial draught involves large initial cost as well as large maintenance cost.`,
opts: C, ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Both true; Statement (II) explains Statement (I)</h3><ul>
<li><b>Statement (I) (true):</b> Chimney (natural) draught is thermodynamically very inefficient. The heat carried away by the hot flue gas is far more than the work needed to lift the gas up the chimney (chimney efficiency is typically below 1%). Yet natural draught is often preferred.</li>
<li><b>Statement (II) (true):</b> Fans for forced or induced draught cost money to buy, consume power continuously and need maintenance. A chimney, once built, needs almost no running cost or upkeep and is needed anyway to discharge gases at height.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The cost argument of Statement (II) is the reason for the preference stated in Statement (I).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Both true and II explains I (the keyed answer).</li><li><b>(b)</b> II is the correct explanation of I here.</li><li><b>(c)</b> II is true, so this is not right.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I is true, so this is not right.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Draught produced by a chimney: h<sub>w</sub> = 353 H (1/T<sub>a</sub> − 1/T<sub>g</sub>) mm of water (approximately), with H in m and T in K.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-134", year: 2017, no: 134, subject: "power", topic: "Oil vs coal combustion efficiency",
q: `Statement (I) : The overall combustion efficiency of a fuel oil based plant is less as compared to that of a coal burning plant.<br>Statement (II) : Fuel oils contain comparatively larger percentage of hydrogen, which produce more moisture per kg of fuel burnt.`,
opts: C, ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Both true; Statement (II) explains Statement (I)</h3><ul>
<li><b>Statement (II) (true):</b> Fuel oils contain about 11–13% hydrogen against about 4–5% in coal. Each kg of hydrogen burns to 9 kg of water vapour.</li>
<li><b>Statement (I) (true, on gross calorific value basis):</b> The water vapour leaves the chimney uncondensed, carrying away its latent heat (about 2400 kJ/kg). The fraction of the gross calorific value lost this way is larger for oil, so the efficiency based on HCV is lower.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The larger moisture loss described in Statement (II) explains the lower efficiency in Statement (I).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Both true and II explains I (the keyed answer).</li><li><b>(b)</b> II is the correct explanation of I here.</li><li><b>(c)</b> II is true, so this is not right.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I is true, so this is not right.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> HCV − LCV = 9H × h<sub>fg</sub> (plus fuel moisture), with h<sub>fg</sub> ≈ 2442 kJ/kg at 25 °C. Efficiencies quoted on LCV basis look higher for exactly this reason.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-135", year: 2017, no: 135, subject: "power", topic: "Proximate analysis of coal",
q: `Statement (I) : Proximate analysis of coal is done to determine its calorific value.<br>Statement (II) : In proximate analysis of coal, the percentages of moisture, volatile matter, fixed carbon and ash are determined.`,
opts: C, ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Statement (I) is false but Statement (II) is true</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Proximate analysis of coal.</p><ul>
<li><b>Statement (II) (true):</b> Proximate analysis determines moisture (heating at 105–110 °C), volatile matter (heating to about 950 °C without air), ash (burning completely) and fixed carbon (by difference).</li>
<li><b>Statement (I) (false):</b> Calorific value is measured directly with a <b>bomb calorimeter</b>, or estimated from the ultimate analysis (C, H, S, N, O) using Dulong's formula. Proximate analysis indicates rank, quality and burning behaviour (for example ignition and smoke tendency), not the calorific value.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> I is false.</li><li><b>(b)</b> I is false.</li><li><b>(c)</b> I is false and II is true, not the reverse.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> I is false and II is true.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Dulong's formula: HCV ≈ 33,800 C + 1,44,000 (H − O/8) + 9270 S kJ/kg, with C, H, O, S as mass fractions.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-136", year: 2017, no: 136, subject: "power", topic: "Cooling water quality", doubt: true,
q: `Statement (I) : Water entering into a condenser from the cooling tower has much dissolved impurities.<br>Statement (II) : In a closed cooling system, the water is continuously aerated, therefore, there is abundant dissolved oxygen in this water.`,
opts: C, ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Both true, but Statement (II) is not the correct explanation of Statement (I) (official key)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Cooling water quality.</p><ul>
<li><b>Statement (I) (true):</b> In a cooling tower, water continuously evaporates, so dissolved salts concentrate (cycles of concentration). The falling water also scrubs dust and gases from the air, so circulating water reaching the condenser carries many dissolved impurities. Blowdown and chemical treatment are needed.</li>
<li><b>Statement (II):</b> UPSC's key treats it as true: water passing through a cooling tower is sprayed into air, so it is saturated with dissolved oxygen, which makes it corrosive.</li></ul>
<div class="note">In doubt (key followed). Our answer was (c): the continuous aeration described in Statement (II) happens in an <i>open</i> (cooling-tower) recirculating system. A truly closed cooling system has no contact with air, so it is not aerated and its dissolved oxygen falls once corrosion consumes it. Statement (II) as worded ("closed cooling system") is therefore false. The key seems to use "closed" to mean recirculating rather than once-through.</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> II is true but does not explain I.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Both true; II does not explain I.</li><li><b>(c)</b> II is true, so this is not right.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I is true, so this is not right.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Cooling-water problems: scale (Ca, Mg salts), corrosion (dissolved O<sub>2</sub>, CO<sub>2</sub>), fouling and biological growth (chlorination is used against it).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-137", year: 2017, no: 137, subject: "renew", topic: "Solar radiation instruments",
q: `Statement (I) : Pyranometer is used to measure diffuse solar radiation by blocking the direct radiation with a shadow band.<br>Statement (II) : Pyrheliometer is used to measure diffuse radiation.`,
opts: C, ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Statement (I) is true but Statement (II) is false</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Solar radiation instruments.</p><ul>
<li><b>Statement (I) (true):</b> A pyranometer sees the whole hemisphere and normally measures <b>global</b> (direct + diffuse) radiation on a horizontal surface. Fitted with a shadow band (or ring) that blocks the sun's disc, it measures diffuse radiation only.</li>
<li><b>Statement (II) (false):</b> A pyrheliometer has a narrow collimating tube aimed at the sun (with a tracking mount), so it measures <b>direct (beam)</b> normal radiation, not diffuse.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> II is false.</li><li><b>(b)</b> II is false.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> I is true and II is false.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I is true, so this is not right.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Sunshine recorder (Campbell–Stokes) measures hours of bright sunshine. Global = beam × cos θ<sub>z</sub> + diffuse.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-138", year: 2017, no: 138, subject: "materials", topic: "Directionally solidified materials",
q: `Statement (I) : Directionally solidified materials have good creep resistance.<br>Statement (II) : Directionally solidified materials may be so loaded that there is no shearing stress along, or tensile stress across, the grain boundaries.`,
opts: C, ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Both true; Statement (II) explains Statement (I)</h3><ul>
<li><b>Statement (II) (true):</b> Directional solidification (as for nickel-superalloy gas-turbine blades) produces long columnar grains aligned with the blade's length. When the blade is loaded along that axis, the grain boundaries run parallel to the stress: there is no tension across them and no shear along them.</li>
<li><b>Statement (I) (true):</b> At high temperature creep proceeds largely by grain-boundary sliding and cavitation at boundaries normal to the stress. Removing transverse boundaries therefore greatly improves creep life.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The loading arrangement in Statement (II) is why the material creeps less, so (a).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Both true and II explains I (the keyed answer).</li><li><b>(b)</b> II is the correct explanation of I here.</li><li><b>(c)</b> II is true, so this is not right.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I is true, so this is not right.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> The next step is the single-crystal blade, which has no grain boundaries at all, giving still better creep and thermal-fatigue resistance.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-139", year: 2017, no: 139, subject: "design", topic: "Shaft materials",
q: `Statement (I) : The ideal material for shafts transmitting power is CI.<br>Statement (II) : CI resists compression well.`,
opts: C, ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Statement (I) is false but Statement (II) is true</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Shaft materials.</p><ul>
<li><b>Statement (II) (true):</b> Cast iron has high compressive strength (about 3–4 times its tensile strength) and good damping and machinability.</li>
<li><b>Statement (I) (false):</b> Power-transmitting shafts carry torsion, bending and fluctuating (fatigue) loads, often with shock. Cast iron is brittle and weak in tension, shear and fatigue, so it is unsuitable. Shafts are made of plain carbon steels (C40, C45) and alloy steels (Ni–Cr, Cr–Mo) for heavy duty.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> I is false.</li><li><b>(b)</b> I is false.</li><li><b>(c)</b> I is false and II is true, not the reverse.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> I is false and II is true.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Cast iron suits machine beds, frames and housings (compression, damping); shafts, axles and spindles use steel.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-140", year: 2017, no: 140, subject: "materials", topic: "Jominy hardenability",
q: `Statement (I) : Hardenability curves are developed based on the fact that any given steel item always develops the same microstructure under a standardized cooling rate.<br>Statement (II) : Industry employs Jominy hardenability test to measure hardenability.`,
opts: C, ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Both true, but Statement (II) is not the correct explanation of Statement (I)</h3><ul>
<li><b>Statement (I) (true):</b> For a given steel, the microstructure (and so the hardness) at a point depends only on the cooling rate there, whatever the part's size or shape. This is why a hardenability curve measured on a test bar can be transferred to real parts by matching cooling rates.</li>
<li><b>Statement (II) (true):</b> The Jominy end-quench test (a 25 mm bar heated to austenite and water-sprayed on one end) is the standard industrial test. Hardness is measured along the bar.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Statement (II) only names the test; it does not explain why hardenability curves work, which is the principle in Statement (I).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> II is true but does not explain I.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Both true; II does not explain I.</li><li><b>(c)</b> II is true, so this is not right.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I is true, so this is not right.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Hardenability (depth of hardening) depends on alloy content and grain size; hardness (maximum achievable) depends mainly on carbon content.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-141", year: 2017, no: 141, subject: "tom", topic: "High-speed cam profiles",
q: `Statement (I) : Cams used in high-speed application should have displacement, velocity and acceleration curves of the follower in continuity.<br>Statement (II) : Abrupt changes in these curves will cause high contact stresses at the bearings and make the operation noisy.`,
opts: C, ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Both true; Statement (II) explains Statement (I)</h3><ul>
<li><b>Statement (II) (true):</b> A jump in velocity means infinite acceleration (an impact); a jump in acceleration means infinite jerk. Either produces shock loads on the cam, follower and bearings, vibration and noise, and the effect grows with the square of speed.</li>
<li><b>Statement (I) (true):</b> High-speed cams therefore use profiles whose displacement, velocity and acceleration are all continuous, such as cycloidal, polynomial (3-4-5, 4-5-6-7) and modified trapezoidal profiles.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Statement (II) gives the reason for the design rule in Statement (I).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Both true and II explains I (the keyed answer).</li><li><b>(b)</b> II is the correct explanation of I here.</li><li><b>(c)</b> II is true, so this is not right.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I is true, so this is not right.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> SHM has continuous velocity but jumps in acceleration at the ends of the rise; cycloidal motion avoids both, which is why it is preferred at high speed.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-142", year: 2017, no: 142, subject: "tom", topic: "Resonance",
q: `Statement (I) : Resonance is a special case of forced vibration in which the natural frequency of the body is the same as the impressed frequency of the external periodic force whereby the amplitude of the forced vibration peaks sharply.<br>Statement (II) : The amplitude of forced vibration of a body increases with increase in the frequency of the externally impressed periodic force.`,
opts: C, ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Statement (I) is true but Statement (II) is false</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Resonance.</p><div class="formula">X/X<sub>st</sub> = 1/√[(1 − r²)² + (2ζr)²], r = ω/ω<sub>n</sub></div>
<ul class="sym"><li>X = amplitude; X<sub>st</sub> = F<sub>0</sub>/k (static deflection); ζ = damping ratio; ω = forcing frequency; ω<sub>n</sub> = natural frequency</li></ul>
<ul><li><b>Statement (I) (true):</b> At r = 1 (strictly near it when damped) the amplitude peaks; with little damping the peak is sharp and large.</li>
<li><b>Statement (II) (false):</b> Amplitude rises with frequency only up to resonance. Beyond it, amplitude <i>falls</i>, tending to zero as r → ∞ (the mass cannot follow fast forcing). So it does not simply increase with frequency.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> II is false.</li><li><b>(b)</b> II is false.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> I is true and II is false.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I is true, so this is not right.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Peak amplitude for damped systems occurs at r = √(1 − 2ζ²), slightly below ω<sub>n</sub>; phase lag is 90° at r = 1.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-143", year: 2017, no: 143, subject: "design", topic: "Worm drives",
q: `Statement (I) : All worm drives (worm and worm wheel) are reversible.<br>Statement (II) : The worm and worm wheel are made of different materials.`,
opts: C, ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Statement (I) is false but Statement (II) is true</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Worm drives.</p><ul>
<li><b>Statement (I) (false):</b> A worm drive with a small lead angle (single-start, lead angle less than the friction angle, roughly below 5–6°) is <b>self-locking</b>: the wheel cannot drive the worm. This is useful in hoists and lifts. Only multi-start worms with large lead angles are reversible.</li>
<li><b>Statement (II) (true):</b> The worm is usually case-hardened steel and the wheel phosphor bronze. The dissimilar pair reduces friction, wear and seizure in the heavy sliding contact.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> I is false.</li><li><b>(b)</b> I is false.</li><li><b>(c)</b> I is false and II is true, not the reverse.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> I is false and II is true.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Worm efficiency η = tan λ/tan(λ + φ), where λ = lead angle and φ = friction angle. A self-locking worm has η &lt; 50%.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-144", year: 2017, no: 144, subject: "tom", topic: "Balancing of reciprocating engines", doubt: true,
q: `Statement (I) : There is no balancing methodology in the case of reciprocating engines.<br>Statement (II) : Balancing of dynamic forces is achieved mostly by resorting to multi-cylinder engine concept.`,
opts: C, ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Both true; Statement (II) explains Statement (I) (official key)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Balancing of reciprocating engines.</p><ul>
<li><b>Statement (II) (true):</b> In practice most reciprocating unbalance is removed by arranging several cylinders and suitable crank angles (for example in-line six, V8 with cross-plane crank, opposed twins), so that primary and secondary forces and couples cancel.</li>
<li><b>Statement (I):</b> UPSC's key accepts it in the sense that the reciprocating inertia force of a single cylinder cannot be completely balanced by any simple counter-mass; the remedy is the multi-cylinder arrangement of Statement (II).</li></ul>
<div class="note">In doubt (key followed). Our answer was (d): Statement (I) as written is false. Well-established balancing methods for reciprocating engines exist: partial primary balancing with crank counterweights, the direct and reverse crank method, secondary balance shafts (Lanchester) and multi-cylinder firing orders. Statement (II) describes one of those methods, which itself shows that a methodology exists.</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Both true and II explains I (the keyed answer).</li><li><b>(b)</b> II is the correct explanation of I here.</li><li><b>(c)</b> II is true, so this is not right.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I is true, so this is not right.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> In-line four-cylinder engines are primary-balanced but have large secondary forces; in-line six is fully balanced for primary and secondary forces and couples.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-145", year: 2017, no: 145, subject: "mech", topic: "Moment of inertia of discs",
q: `Statement (I) : Two circular discs of equal masses and thickness made of different materials will have same moment of inertia about their central axes of rotation.<br>Statement (II) : Moment of inertia depends upon the distribution of mass within the body.`,
opts: C, ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Statement (I) is false but Statement (II) is true</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Moment of inertia of discs.</p><ul>
<li><b>Statement (II) (true):</b> I = ∫r² dm, so it depends on how far the mass lies from the axis, not only on the total mass.</li>
<li><b>Statement (I) (false):</b> With equal mass and equal thickness but different densities, the denser disc must have a smaller radius: m = ρπR²t gives R ∝ 1/√ρ. Since I = ½mR², the less dense (larger) disc has the larger moment of inertia.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> I is false.</li><li><b>(b)</b> I is false.</li><li><b>(c)</b> I is false and II is true, not the reverse.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> I is false and II is true.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> This is why flywheels concentrate mass in a heavy rim: for a given mass, larger radius means much more I (∝ R²).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-146", year: 2017, no: 146, subject: "turbo", topic: "Governing of water turbines",
q: `Statement (I) : The speed of a governed water turbine will remain constant irrespective of load.<br>Statement (II) : In governing, the water supply is regulated to maintain the speed constant.`,
opts: C, ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Both true; Statement (II) explains Statement (I)</h3><ul>
<li><b>Statement (II) (true):</b> The governor senses speed and adjusts the flow: the spear (needle) and deflector in a Pelton wheel, the guide vanes (wicket gates) in Francis and Kaplan turbines (plus runner blades in Kaplan). Power output then matches the load.</li>
<li><b>Statement (I) (true, in principle):</b> Because the alternator must run at synchronous speed (constant frequency), the governed turbine is held at (nearly) constant speed whatever the load. Strictly there is a small speed droop, but the intent is constant speed.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The regulation of water supply in Statement (II) is how the constant speed of Statement (I) is achieved.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Both true and II explains I (the keyed answer).</li><li><b>(b)</b> II is the correct explanation of I here.</li><li><b>(c)</b> II is true, so this is not right.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I is true, so this is not right.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Pelton wheels also use a deflector to divert the jet quickly on sudden load loss, so the spear can close slowly and avoid water hammer in the penstock.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-147", year: 2017, no: 147, subject: "design", topic: "Interference fits",
q: `Statement (I) : In sugarcane crushing rollers, the fit between the cast roll and the forged steel shaft is of interference type.<br>Statement (II) : This helps in removing the roll from the shaft whenever not needed.`,
opts: C, ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Statement (I) is true but Statement (II) is false</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Interference fits.</p><ul>
<li><b>Statement (I) (true):</b> Sugarcane mill rollers transmit very large torque under heavy, shock-prone crushing loads. The cast-iron roll shell is shrunk or pressed onto the forged steel shaft with an interference fit, so friction at the interface carries the torque without keys loosening.</li>
<li><b>Statement (II) (false):</b> An interference fit makes removal <b>difficult</b>: it needs heating, a press or hydraulic (oil-injection) methods. Easy removal is a feature of clearance or transition fits.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> II is false.</li><li><b>(b)</b> II is false.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> I is true and II is false.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I is true, so this is not right.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> ISO fits: clearance (H7/g6), transition (H7/k6), interference (H7/p6, H7/s6). Shrink-fit contact pressure follows from Lamé's thick-cylinder equations.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-148", year: 2017, no: 148, subject: "mfg", topic: "Solidification of thick sections", doubt: true,
q: `Statement (I) : Thicker sections of casting take longer to solidify than thinner sections.<br>Statement (II) : Thicker sections of casting carry residual stresses.`,
opts: C, ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Both true; Statement (II) explains Statement (I) (official key)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Solidification of thick sections.</p><ul>
<li><b>Statement (I) (true):</b> Chvorinov's rule: solidification time t<sub>s</sub> = B (V/A)². Thick sections have a larger volume-to-surface ratio, so they lose heat more slowly and solidify later (and are the usual sites of shrinkage cavities, hence risers are placed there).</li>
<li><b>Statement (II) (true):</b> Because thick sections solidify and contract after the thin sections have already become rigid, their contraction is restrained, leaving residual stresses (tension in the thick section, compression in the thin).</li></ul>
<div class="note">In doubt (key followed). Our answer was (b): both statements are true, but the cause and effect run the other way. Slower solidification of thick sections (Statement I) is the <i>cause</i> of the residual stresses (Statement II); residual stresses do not explain why thick sections take longer to solidify. The key's (a) treats Statement (II) as explaining Statement (I).</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Both true and II explains I (the keyed answer).</li><li><b>(b)</b> II is the correct explanation of I here.</li><li><b>(c)</b> II is true, so this is not right.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I is true, so this is not right.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Remedies for thick-section problems: chills, padding, risers placed on heavy sections, uniform section design, and stress-relief annealing of the casting.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-149", year: 2017, no: 149, subject: "mfg", topic: "Moulding sand grain shape", doubt: true,
q: `Statement (I) : Sand with grains of uniform round shape is preferred for preparing moulds.<br>Statement (II) : If grains are large and regular in shape, the air-venting property of the mould prepared with them would be better.`,
opts: C, ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Statement (I) is false but Statement (II) is true (official key)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Moulding sand grain shape.</p><ul>
<li><b>Statement (II) (true):</b> Large, regular (rounded, uniform) grains leave larger, connected voids, so permeability (venting of air, steam and gases) improves.</li>
<li><b>Statement (I):</b> UPSC's key treats it as false. Rounded uniform grains give high permeability and good flowability but low strength and poor bonding, because they have less contact area. Angular or sub-angular grains with a range of sizes pack more tightly and give stronger moulds. In practice a balanced, sub-angular, graded sand is preferred over uniformly round grains.</li></ul>
<div class="note">In doubt (key followed). Our answer was (a): many foundry texts recommend rounded grains for their better permeability, flowability and lower binder demand, and Statement (II) gives exactly that reason. The choice depends on whether strength or permeability is put first, so the item is debatable. The key puts strength first and treats Statement (I) as false.</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> I is false.</li><li><b>(b)</b> I is false.</li><li><b>(c)</b> I is false and II is true, not the reverse.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> I is false and II is true.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Moulding sand properties: permeability, green and dry strength, refractoriness, collapsibility, flowability, cohesiveness and adhesiveness. Grain shape: rounded gives permeability; angular gives strength.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-150", year: 2017, no: 150, subject: "mfg", topic: "Bar (Gantt) charts",
q: `Statement (I) : Bar chart plots in the time dimension the planned performance of various activities of a project.<br>Statement (II) : One advantage of a bar chart is that the inter se sequence and linkage of all activities is indicated therein.`,
opts: C, ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Statement (I) is true but Statement (II) is false</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Bar (Gantt) charts.</p><ul>
<li><b>Statement (I) (true):</b> A bar (Gantt) chart shows each activity as a horizontal bar against a time scale, giving its planned start, duration and finish. Actual progress can be shaded in for comparison.</li>
<li><b>Statement (II) (false):</b> The main <b>limitation</b> of a bar chart is that it does <i>not</i> show interdependencies (which activity must precede which), so the effect of a delay on other activities and the critical activities cannot be seen. Network methods (CPM, PERT) were developed to show these links.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> II is false.</li><li><b>(b)</b> II is false.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> I is true and II is false.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I is true, so this is not right.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Milestone charts add key events to bars. Linked bar charts partly show dependencies, but only networks give floats and the critical path.</p>` }
);
})();

